window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['metaphysik-mrongovius/kemp'] = {"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-001":["Prolegomena Metaphysices",["Latin heading kept as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-002":["Our modes of knowledge are connected in a twofold manner. First, as an aggregate, when one is added to another in order to make up a whole. For example, a heap of sand is in itself no connection of things; they are put together arbitrarily (nothing is determinate here). Secondly, as a series of grounds and consequences. The parts of a series are entitled members, because we can know one part only through another; in the human body, for example, one part exists through another. We readily see that the connection of modes of knowledge as an aggregate yields no determinate concept of the whole. It is as if I added one little piece to another until a mountain arises, and so on, until a planet or earth-body results; at least we can think of it so. In the series there is something that makes the connection accord with a rule, namely the grounds and consequences.\n\nIn the case of grounds and consequences we must think limits *a priori*, that is, a ground that is no further a consequence, and limits *a posteriori*, that is, a consequence that is no ground. Take, for example, the generations of men. Men are members of the series; here we must think of a man who begets but is not himself born, hence a *terminus a priori*; and of one who is born but begets no one, hence a *terminus a posteriori*. We consider here (in metaphysics) not things as they cohere as grounds and consequences, but modes of knowledge, which also descend from one another as men or other things do. I can represent to myself a mode of knowledge that is no consequence, and so is the supreme ground; and one that is no ground, and so is the last consequence.",["Erkenntniße rendered 'modes of knowledge'; the long sentence was divided and the second paragraph break is mine, for clarity.","'Glieder' rendered 'members' (also 'limbs'), as the context of series and human body suggests.","Source has 'derReihe' (OCR run-together); silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-003":["The last consequence is immediate experience, e.g. something — body — stone — limestone — marble — marble column.",["Dashes follow the OCR; the stray double dash was normalised."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-004":["We have, then, an idea of a connection of modes of knowledge as grounds and consequences. The grounds of what grounds in accordance with a certain rule are entitled *principia*. Inasmuch, then, as modes of knowledge stand in a series, there must also be *principia*. It is noteworthy that I can make the consequence into the ground, from which, however, the other does not follow, but through which I have merely come to the knowledge of the other. These are therefore not *principia essendi* but *cognoscendi*. For example, I know the existence of God from the world. The world is not the ground of God; the reverse holds. But through the world I can arrive at the concept of God, and to that extent I can proceed from the *principiata* to the *principia*. Consequences that are used as grounds, when we go back from them to their grounds, are entitled *principia a posteriori*.\n\nIf I begin from the consequences, I know something *a posteriori*; if I begin from the grounds, I know *a priori*. When something is given to me, I can try whether I could have known it *a priori* from grounds. That sunlight melts ice, for example, experience teaches; *a priori* we should hardly have known it. The knowledge that is taken from experience is *kat' exochen a posteriori*, and when in future we call modes of knowledge *a posteriori*, we always mean those that derive from experience, because experience contains the last consequence of our knowledge, for which we seek grounds in reason. If we take experience as *principium*, the principle is empirical. For example, experience teaches that all bodies are heavy (so far as we know them); we can adopt this as a principle and say: since all bodies are heavy, it follows that . . . Principles *a priori* are those that are borrowed from no experience. Whether there are any must soon be investigated.",["The text of the first sentence is corrupt in the OCR ('Efkenntniße', 'Erkenntniß' after 'Folgen'); I translate what can be made out and treat the stray 'Erkenntniß' as dittography.","'Die Gründe von gründen nach einer gewißen Regel sind' is awkward; I read it as 'the grounds of what grounds according to a rule', which is a judgment.","Sentence division and paragraph break are mine. 'kat' exochen' (Greek) kept in transliteration, italicised."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-005":["From the linking together of our knowledge a unity can arise, that is, science; or a connection can be met with that is systematic. A We can think a connection of our modes of knowledge as science, from the first grounds to the last consequences, and this would comprehend within itself the whole of all inferred modes of knowledge; but it exceeds our powers.",["The letter 'A' is a stray mark in the source (probably an editorial or marginal sign); retained as it stands.","'Kräfte' rendered 'powers'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-006":["The first ground is simple, namely [the concept of] something; but to have the last consequences we should have to run through the whole field of experience, which cannot be done.",["'[the concept of]' is my supplied gloss on 'von Etwas'; the German says only 'of something'.","'mäßen' read as 'müßten'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-007":["Since, then, this will not do, we can B take the material away from it. I want to have only a piece of the system of the whole of human knowledge, namely the science of the highest principles of human knowledge; and such a project is modest. Men have long had it in mind to work out such a science, and have called it metaphysics. Metaphysics is therefore the science of the first principles of the whole of human knowledge, and so contains the first members of the series. But this concept C is not determinate enough as to how much or how little belongs to it. For when I give a concept of a science, it must be determined what belongs to it and what does not; and men also cannot know (by the concept given) how far these principles reach down towards experience or not, and where we are to stop.\n\nEvery series can be divided into two parts; the one that precedes the other is comparatively the first, as, for example, a church history of the first centuries. Nothing is determinate here; it may be the third, seventh, eighth, or following century of our reckoning. The limit *a priori* of metaphysics is determinate, namely that there are no principles from which it could be derived. One speaks of the possible, the impossible, something and nothing; higher man cannot climb. Of world, contingency, fate, the natural, the supernatural, cause of the world, existence, God, one has so far spoken through mere concepts. Only about the soul of man was it thought that something could be said, if he did not attend to himself. So the body, too, may belong here, and now I do not know whether the whole of natural science does not. From this it is clear that I cannot set limits to metaphysics in the series of consequences and grounds as to degree, if I regard it as homogeneous with and related to other sciences; I must rather think of it as heterogeneous and wholly different from the others.",["'B' and 'C' are stray marks in the source; retained. 'projeet' read as 'Projekt'.","'den Stoff davon wegnehmen' rendered literally as 'take the material away'; the sense (abstracting from empirical matter) is not certain.","'Nur von der Seele ... wenn er nicht auf sich attendirte' is elliptical; rendered closely, and the sense is doubtful.","Paragraph break and sentence division are mine."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-008":["Principles *a priori* always carry necessity with them; those *a posteriori* do not [ozi: reading doubtful], e.g. 'Everything that happens must have a ground' is a proposition *a priori*, for experience teaches only that it is so, but cannot deny that something might not also at some time happen without a ground.",["'ozi' is an OCR garble; probably 'nicht' or an abbreviation. I translate what can be made out and leave the token marked.","'e. gr.' = *exempli gratia*, rendered 'e.g.'.","The final clause is syntactically loose in the German; I have smoothed it, and the sense (experience cannot rule out an exception) is my reading."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-009":["There are five kinds of modes of knowledge *a priori* and *a posteriori*, which are wholly distinct from one another. For example, God can be known only *a priori*. Now we say that metaphysics *est scientia principiorum cognitionis humanae a priori* [is the science of the principles of human knowledge *a priori*]. Does mathematics not belong to metaphysics, although it too is *cognitio pura* [pure knowledge]? No; but the principles of the possibility of mathematics do belong to it, for metaphysics contains the principles of the possibility of all modes of knowledge *a priori*. The concept is now determined and fixed. All modes of knowledge which require experience in order to arrive at what they know lie outside the field of metaphysics; in order to determine the concept I must add a special species of principles. A mode of knowledge which is burdened with no experience is pure; or metaphysics is *scientia principiorum cognitionis purae* [the science of the principles of pure knowledge], or simply *philosophia pura* [pure philosophy]. This is a proper touchstone. For example, the soul likewise does not belong to metaphysics; psychology is merely patched on. The use of reason must be *pura*, that is, isolated, i.e. freed from all experience and independent of it. Natural science is also philosophy, but *philosophia applicata* [applied philosophy]: it is an application of reason to objects of experience, in which we lay empirical principles at the foundation. We have two fields for the use of reason. Reason can proceed by having *principia a priori*, or *principia a posteriori*, in that it consults experience. The first part of this use is entitled metaphysics. Here there must be two principal parts:",["The opening 'Es sind 5 Arten' is doubtful: the number may be an OCR or transcription slip, and the sentence is left as it stands. Stray letters ('D', 'E') in the text are OCR marginal marks and have been omitted.","'gehörtauch' corrected to 'gehört auch'. 'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge' (plural); in the singular, 'knowledge'.","The sentence 'Eine Erkenntniß, die mit keiner Erfahrung behaftet ist, ist rein' is restated with a semicolon to carry the 'oder' clause smoothly.","The passage breaks off after the colon; the two parts follow in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-010":["1) We must consider reason itself; or the first part is the science which has reason for its object. It would treat of the sources, extent and limits of our pure reason, or of its nature, that is, of the possibility of judging *a priori*. First, we shall examine whether our rational knowledge can know anything *a priori*; the possibility of knowledge *a priori* must first be established. 2) Next, its extension: how far it can go, and to what objects it can attain without experience. 3) Finally, the limits which it must not transgress if it wishes to judge without experience. I do not say, 'This is substance, that accident'; for in so doing I should not be examining reason itself, but should be dealing with objects. Transcendental philosophy is therefore very different from metaphysics proper, i.e. the system of knowledge through reason. We naturally ask how we arrive at such modes of knowledge *a priori*, in which the principles are certainly wholly *a priori*: for example, whether the world has a beginning or not. No experience can teach that, and yet reason asks about it. It is greatly concerned to settle it. It is therefore a question addressed to pure reason, not to reason supported by empirical principles, for these can give us no answer to it. Here the question naturally arises, how reason proposes to know this independently of experience. The answer to this question belongs to transcendental philosophy, where we see that there are principles *a priori*. Now again I do not know how far they will carry me. I must therefore seek to become acquainted with the whole of its [reason's] capacity, without falling into delusion and error. This first part might be entitled transcendental philosophy, or critique of pure reason. Pure reason is that which judges independently of all experience. This part might also be entitled *metaphysica pura* [pure metaphysics]; and the second part, the application of the *principia a priori* to objects of experience, would be *metaphysica applicata* [applied metaphysics]. What this is we shall show later.",["The numerals '1.', '2.' and the stray 'c' in the source are partly OCR marks; I have rendered the sequence as 1), 2), 3) on the evidence of 'Zuerst… Ferner… Endlich'. This is a supplied ordering.","Several sentences were divided for clarity. 'daraJ' read 'daran'; 'ohnabhängig' = 'independently'; 'a prion' read 'a priori'; the stray '_' and '2' omitted.","'ihr ganzes Vermögen': 'ihr' resolved as reason's; supplied in brackets. 'Vermögen' = capacity."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-011":["Modes of knowledge *a priori* are of two kinds: (a) mathematical, (b) philosophical, with the difference [lacuna: the text is incomplete]: Logic — we here take only the latter. Mathematics therefore does not belong to metaphysics, although it too is *cognitio pura* [pure knowledge]; but the principles of the possibility of mathematics do belong to it, for it contains the principles of the possibility of all knowledge *a priori*. We can, however, philosophize about mathematics.",["'mit dem Unterschied: Logic —' is broken or elliptical in the transcript; I have not filled the gap and mark it as such.","The initial 'E' and 'F' are marginal letters/OCR marks, omitted."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-012":["Something is *a priori* (a) *simpliciter* [absolutely] and *a priori* (b) *secundum quid* [in a certain respect]. It is *a priori secundum quid* when I know something through reason, but from empirical principles: for example, when I throw a stone horizontally, it does not fall straight. The arc can be determined *a priori*, but according to laws of gravity, which we know *a posteriori*.",["The dash after 'nicht gerade' is read as a sentence break; the stray initial 'F' is omitted.","'Schwere' rendered 'gravity'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-013":["Pure philosophical modes of knowledge are metaphysical. What is philosophy? The system of rational knowledge through concepts. Metaphysics is therefore the system of pure rational knowledge through concepts.",["'Vernunft Erkenntniße' rendered 'rational knowledge' (*Vernunfterkenntnis*), 'reine Vernunft Erkenntniße' as 'pure rational knowledge'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-014":["A system must have an idea at its foundation, whereby the parts, the connection of the parts, and the completeness of the parts are determined.",["OCR 'T heile' and 'I heile' read as 'Theile' (parts)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-015":["How modes of pure rational knowledge are possible, mathematics teaches, for it is itself such a mode of pure rational knowledge. Since its propositions always carry their own evidence with them, and moreover each of them can be confirmed by experience, it would not be necessary to fetch the possibility of them from so far afield. But our pure reason now has a special field of modes of knowledge in which it is not as it is in mathematics: they are not so fortunate as to possess evidence, nor to be confirmed by experience. They are, moreover, of such a kind that, although man may soon believe himself convinced of one or another of them, there is none that has not been disputed. Since universal human reason, i.e. the sum of the reason of all men, is in conflict with itself in the philosophy of pure reason, that is, in metaphysics, it is necessary to investigate the possibility of pure rational knowledge. So we shall also investigate the possibility of pure metaphysics, not for its own sake, but to see whether we may not thereby at the same time learn the possibility of pure philosophy,",["The long, loosely punctuated sentence is divided into several. 'lach' read 'Fach' (field); 'Inbegriff' = 'sum' (also 'totality').","'ihr zum besten' rendered 'for its own sake' (literally 'for its benefit'), a judgment of sense; 'ob wir nicht… lernen möchten' kept tentative.","The passage ends with a comma and breaks off mid-sentence; it is not completed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-016":["Applied metaphysics (*Metaphysica applicata*), which contains the knowledge of objects *a priori*, constitutes a system of pure reason; and the system of pure rational knowledge is entitled metaphysics in the strict sense. Transcendental philosophy is the *propaedeutic* to metaphysics proper. Here reason determines nothing, but speaks only of its own faculty; in metaphysics proper it makes use of this faculty, and it is in this latter sense that metaphysics is always understood.\n\nA true transcendental philosophy has not hitherto existed. The word has been used, and ontology understood by it; but we do not take it so, as is easily seen. In ontology one speaks of things in general, and so properly of no thing at all. One is occupied with the nature of the understanding in thinking things. Here, on the other hand, we have the concepts through which we think things, namely the pure concepts of reason; and transcendental philosophy is therefore the science of the principles of the pure understanding and of pure reason. That, however, was also transcendental philosophy, and ontology therefore belongs to it. But it has never been well treated; men dealt at once with things in general, without inquiring whether such pure modes of knowledge, whether of understanding or of reason, or such pure scientific modes of knowledge, were possible at all.\n\nIn ontology I already speak of things, of substances and accidents, which are properties of things that I know *a priori*. In the critique I cannot speak in this way. Here I shall say: among the concepts which are *a priori* there occur also those of substance and accident. How do I come by them? What can I accomplish with them? What is it possible to know *a priori* of objects, so far as they are substances? I therefore examine the origin of the concept, [to see] how far it could extend, and [likewise] the origin of these concepts, in order to learn how far they reach and what their limits are. That is the business of the critique of pure reason.\n\nSuppose we paid no attention to the establishment of the principles, and made a system, as it were, by taking principles *a priori* whose validity has arisen merely through use. We should then have a metaphysics proper, but with what assurance could we employ it? We should in the end fall into difficulties from which we could no longer extricate ourselves, and reason would destroy its own products. The critique is therefore in the highest degree necessary. When reason errs, no experience can set it right.\n\nIf we consider the critique of pure reason independently of all experience, it is true pure philosophy; or the consideration of the faculty of reason itself is pure philosophy. In all modes of knowledge in which principles of reason occur, and indeed from concepts, there is science only in its pure, that is, experience-free, use. This can be regarded more as critique than as doctrine; in the latter we are dogmatic. A mode of knowledge is dogmatic when it is presented in its connection with grounds. In the critique we do not present a connected body of knowledge, but first investigate the sources of the possibility of such knowledge without experience.\n\nThe second part of metaphysics is now the system of pure reason. Philosophy signifies a system of rational knowledge from concepts. Here pure philosophy is to be presented as a system.\n\nOur reason is to have objects, and these are of two kinds: they belong either to nature or to freedom. Both are subject to rules and laws. Our reason knows them by bringing them under rules. Reason weighs necessary laws, and everything that can be known through reason is necessary. We are to know the objects through reason, and so according to what is necessary to them and pertains to them; our modes of rational knowledge must therefore bear the character of necessity.\n\nWe can know either (1) what is, or (2) what ought to be. The former belongs to nature, the latter to freedom. Nature is the sum-total of what is, and the sum-total of what ought to be is morals [*Sitten*]. We have therefore two parts of philosophy. The philosophy of nature considers things that are; the philosophy of morals concerns free actions that ought to take place.\n\nSince we said above that every science has a metaphysics, we can also think a metaphysics of nature, which contains the principles of things so far as they are, and a metaphysics of morals, which contains the principles of the possibility of things so far as they ought to be; the 'ought', however, concerns only free actions.\n\nThe metaphysics of nature has various parts. It is the philosophy (a) of corporeal nature, (b) of thinking nature, (c) of nature as a whole, (d) of the supreme ground of all nature. The objects of metaphysics are (a) nature or the world, (b) the author of the world; hence *Cosmologia generalis* and *Theologia rationalis*. We know the objects either through outer sense, that is, *Physica generalis*, or through inner sense, that is, *Psychologia rationalis*, according to Wolff's division. Though this is incorrect, one reckons to cosmology only *Physica generalis*, and treats *Psychologia rationalis* apart.",["The first paragraph is restated in separate sentences; 'in dem Verstände ist Metaphysic immer genommen' is rendered 'in this latter sense' by judgment, the sentence being loose.","'Vernunft Erkenntniß' rendered 'rational knowledge'; plural 'Erkenntniße' as 'modes of knowledge'.","The sentence 'Ich examinire also den Ursprung des Begriffs … und ihren Ursprung' is irregular (singular 'Begriff', then plural 'sie'); I smoothed it and supplied bracketed words. The text may be corrupt or the transcript careless.","'Man kann sie mehr ansehen als Critic denn als Doctrin, da sind wir dogmatisch': the antecedent of 'sie' and 'da' is unclear; I resolved 'da' as referring to doctrine, which is a judgment.","'reine Vernunftoder reine Wissenschaftliche Erkenntniße' is an OCR run-together; read as 'Vernunft- oder reine wissenschaftliche'.","'Sitten' rendered 'morals'; 'Vermögen' as 'faculty'; 'Gränzen' as 'limits'.","OCR 'l' after 'bedienen' (a stray mark for '?') and 'Obiecte' silently corrected; the opening 'die Erkenntnis der Gegenstände a priori' kept as in the text."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-017":["All our modes of knowledge are either theoretical, in that they consider things as they are, or practical, in that they say how something ought to be and have necessity in themselves, and so forth. We are here treating the metaphysics of nature. From the metaphysics of morals (which sets aside the rules of life that we abstract from experience of the nature of the soul) we shall take only something from *theologia naturalis*, where we think God as lawgiver.",["The parenthesis on the metaphysics of morals is awkwardly placed in the German; I kept it in position but the sense (that practical rules drawn from experience are excluded) is a judgment.","'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge'; 'etc.' kept as 'and so forth'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-018":["Metaphysic of morals is pure morals, which we set wholly aside. Metaphysic of nature contains the principles of the speculative use of reason, and metaphysic of morals the principles of the practical use of reason. Everything that is can be divided into (A) that which is given to the senses, and (B) that which, though it transcends the use of the senses, is connected with the objects of the senses. — *Ad A*: the objects of the senses are either objects of outer sense, that is, of the five bodily senses, or objects of inner sense, that is, the soul. We have therefore the doctrine of body and the doctrine of soul. *Ad B*: here we consider the sum-total [*Inbegriff*] of all objects of the senses, that is, the whole of nature. We can also think the whole series of things, and then we must assume a supreme author of nature, that is, God. That is the last part of metaphysic.\n\nThe metaphysic of corporeal nature is *Physica rationalis*; that of thinking nature is *Psychologia rationalis*; that of the whole of nature, or of the world, is *Cosmologia rationalis*; and that of the supreme author of nature and of all beings is *Theologia naturalis*. The concept of a being, in so far as it contains the ground of the possibility of the whole of nature, is the concept of God. The last part of metaphysic is therefore *Theologia rationalis*.\n\nNow we readily see that everything empirical must drop out, for otherwise it would not be *Philosophia pura*. The concept of body, however, is an empirical concept, and so also [is that] of the science [?]; this is entitled that which is a product of reason and is separated from all experience.\n\nIn all sciences we must, so far as possible, separate off the elements that are heterogeneous, because they spring from different sources of knowledge, [and show] how much rests on mere philosophy and how much on mere experience. In natural science, for example, much is *a priori*: nothing happens without a sufficient ground; substance does not perish, only the form; and so on. If I take this (apart, and separated from what experience teaches) with all the consequences that can be drawn from it, that is the metaphysic of natural science. So there is also a metaphysic of politics, of legislation, and so forth; but it can obtain only where there are principles of reason. A metaphysic of history is nothing. Such a metaphysic is very good; for if I go astray in a science, I can see at once whether the fault lies in reason, or in experience and the deception of the senses.\n\nIn the system of metaphysic the object of philosophy must lie. It is the compass of all that pure reason can think, and it contains together all that, as said above, is distributed among the various sciences. Metaphysic is the greatest culture of human reason. We learn to know all the illusions, to perceive their cause and to avoid them. It sets forth the elementary concepts, for example substance and necessity, and the principles [*Grundsätze*] which reason everywhere employs. In every science, then, where reason rules, a metaphysic must be possible; in natural science, for example, namely [a metaphysic of] what principles reason has without experience. Even mathematics gives rise to a metaphysic: it relates to objects only in so far as they have a magnitude, and the general use of reason's principles upon all objects lies at the ground of mathematics and is its metaphysic. — Mathematics, one may say, is the philosophy of all philosophy. —\n\nTranscendental philosophy stands in regard to metaphysic as logic stands in regard to the whole of philosophy. Logic contains the general rules for the use of the understanding, and is in so far an introduction to all philosophy. Transcendental philosophy is an introduction to *philosophia pura*, which is a part of philosophy as a whole. In transcendental philosophy we consider not objects but reason itself, just as in general metaphysic [sic] we consider only the understanding and its rules. One could therefore also call transcendental philosophy transcendental logic. It occupies itself with the sources, the extent and the limits of pure reason, without concerning itself with objects. Hence it is wrong to call it *Ontologia*; for there we already consider things according to their general properties. Transcendental logic abstracts from all that; it is a kind of self-knowledge. Reason tries whether it cannot, on the wings of ideas, raise itself above experience. It speaks, for example, of spiritual being. — What sources has it, then, for establishing a system of such things? And when it has explained this, the question arises, to what things can this be applied? It is not directed, therefore, to the object but to the subject; not to things, but to the source, extent and limits of reason.",["Restated the long opening sentence and the later runs of clauses as several shorter sentences; the paragraph breaks are mine, added for the reader.","\"und so auch der Wissenschaft; dies heißt das, was product der Vernunft ist…\" is garbled or defective in the transcript. I translate what can be made out, supplying \"[is that]\", and mark the doubt with [?].","\"Mathematic … die Philosophie aller Philosophie\" is as printed; the transcript may be corrupt, but I do not emend it.","\"allgemeinen Metaphysic\" in the parallel with transcendental philosophy is odd (one expects general logic); kept as written and marked [sic].","*Inbegriff* = sum-total; *Grundsätze* = principles, to distinguish it from *principien*. *veranstaltet* rendered \"gives rise to\" (literally \"arranges, occasions\").","\"ad A\", \"ad B\" and the Latin titles are kept in Latin and italicised; OCR spacing (\"dasistder lezteTheilder\") silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-019":["Concept of the soul.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-020":["By soul, namely, we mean that of which I am conscious in that I think. How, then, can *Physica* and *Psychologia rationalis* be brought in here? We note that the object may be given through experience, provided we consider it only according to principles that are *a priori*. For example, the concept of a thinking and of a corporeal being is given through experience. But if I do not take the *principia* of its properties from experience, then the object is given, but the principles are not, and it can therefore belong to metaphysic. Thus *Physica* and *Psychologia rationalis* lie in the field of metaphysic. Empirical doctrine of body is what is properly called natural science, *Physica*; this does not belong here. There is also a *Psychologia empirica*, where I must presuppose observations in order to say anything about the soul. — This too does not belong here. Psychology as it is treated here has, however, both a rational and an empirical part. The latter [*Dieser*] considers the soul from experience, the former [*jener*] from concepts.",["\"feil\" in the OCR is read as \"Teil\" (part).","\"Dieser … jener\" resolved as empirical part … rational part, by the sense of the last clause; the German pronouns could be taken the other way only by violating the argument.","Restated the \"Wenn ich aber…\" sentence slightly for clarity; \"die gehört nicht her\" rendered \"this does not belong here\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s02-021":["How, then, does the empirical part come into metaphysic? From the poor definition of *physica empirica* cited above they do not gather it, but neither do they see that *Psychologia empirica* too does not belong here: joy, pleasure and all the movements of the mind are surely pure observations. A psychology of observations might be called anthropology. — We shall, however, treat it here, because the sciences are divided not only as reason pairs them but as academic instruction requires. It has not yet grown so large that a special course of lectures could be made of it. It was therefore pushed into metaphysic. Here there is a *metabasis eis allo genos* [a passage into another kind]. From it one can still distinguish anthropology, if by that one understands a knowledge of man in so far as it is pragmatic.",["\"dei\" read as \"der\"; \"Gemüths\" rendered \"mind\".","The subject \"sie\" in the second sentence is unspecified (the textbook writers? the hearers?); I render \"they\" and do not resolve it.","The Greek (written in transliteration) is kept in italics; the bracketed gloss is mine.","\"Sie\" in \"Sie ist noch nicht so angewachsen\" is taken to refer to empirical psychology, not to anthropology."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-001":["History of metaphysic.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-002":["Metaphysical inquiries are as old as reason itself. It is remarkable that men began to judge of what transcends the senses sooner than of what is given to them. Natural science was but poorly cultivated. The reason is probably this: to philosophise about nature demands sustained industry, observation, and the collection of many kinds of empirical laws, whereas the ideas of the understanding and of reason each man finds in himself, and one traces them, as it were, out of oneself. Without any doubt the human understanding is also driven by natural needs to know that in which all its ends culminate. It is not content with what the world of sense supplies, but must know what awaits it in the future; he must have a poor conception of his life who believes that with death everything ends. These needs, to know God and the other world, which are so closely bound up with the interest of human reason, passed nature by, since nature has far less interest for men. Although all this was very difficult, men were nevertheless driven by the importance of the objects to further inquiries, even though some of these miscarried.\n\nBefore the Greeks no peoples had metaphysics, any more than they had philosophy. If we begin to reckon correctly, we find a time in which the Greek language was so restricted, and so ill adapted to expressing philosophical reflections, that everything had to be expressed poetically. Orpheus, Hesiod and others have in their poems many sparks of philosophy. At that time the way of expressing ideas was to clothe them in images, so that philosophy is as a rule found delivered poetically; this was done in part also in order the better to impress religion on the memory. But poetry is always for us a play of sensibility.\n\nIt is said that Pherecydes was the first philosopher to express himself in prose. But we have nothing of his, nor of Heraclitus, whose writings were exceedingly obscure. Socrates, who knew them (for afterwards they were lost), said that what he had understood was excellent, and so he believed that what he had not understood would be so too. (Heraclitus found it so hard that he said: *Delio natatore opus est* [a Delian swimmer is needed].) This came about because there were not words enough, and those which were invented were new and for the most part therefore unknown.\n\n*Impossibile* is not a Latin word. Cicero says *fieri nequit*, or one used [a Greek word, illegible in the OCR]. To invent new words is not so easy as one supposes, because they offend against taste, and taste is in this way a hindrance to philosophy.\n\nPythagoras clothed philosophy still more in the language of pure reason. But the fault of clothing ideas and concepts in images, in order to give them their proper meaning, clung to Greek philosophy for a long time, whereby a concept loses much of its purity. Aristotle carried this furthest: for the most abstract ideas he invented words, for which the Greek language was very pliable. German is similar to it in this respect. It has many apt expressions. If one word does not fit, another does, and one always expresses more than another. This does not hold at all of French.\n\nProfessor Kant derives this from the spread of religion in Germany. The missionaries had no proper words for expressing their thoughts and concepts, and so invented new ones, closely related to Latin because that was what they knew. For example, *einfältig* properly means that which has a fold (*Falte*); in Latin *simplex*, which comes from *simplus* and *plica*, the fold. By this means our language has been greatly enriched.\n\nIn the time of Pythagoras and the Eleatic sect there prevailed a philosophical system in which objects of the senses and objects of the understanding were distinguished. The first were entitled *sensibilia* and *phaenomena*, the second *intelligibilia* and *noumena*. The Eleatic sect said: *in sensibus nihil ineße ven[…]* [text garbled]. They give appearances (*Erscheinungen*), not as things are, but as they affect us.",["Restated several long runs of sentences as shorter ones and divided the passage into paragraphs for clarity; the German is a single block.","The Greek word after 'oder man brauchte' is garbled OCR ('ocSuvoctoi;'); probably a Greek word for 'impossible' (e.g. ἀδύνατον), but I do not supply it.","'fieri necjuit' is read as *fieri nequit*; the Eleatic Latin tag 'in sensibus nihil ineße ven…' is cut off or corrupt, perhaps *nihil inesse veri*, and is left incomplete.","'Philosojihie' read as Philosophie; 'Hauptererkennen' not at issue here. 'Pherekydes' rendered Pherecydes.","'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Verstand' = understanding; 'Erscheinung' = appearance, with the German added at the one place where the sense turns on it.","'Delio natatore opus est' is bracket-glossed by me as translation; the gloss is supplied, not in the text.","'Aber ... der muß von seinem Leben einen geringen Begriff haben' is rendered in the third person to keep the shift from 'er' (the understanding) to a general 'he' intelligible; 'afficiren' rendered 'affect'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-003":["Things ought not to have been divided into *intelligibilia* and *sensibilia*, or *noumena* and *phaenomena*. It should rather have been said that our knowledge is twofold (1. intellectual, 2. sensitive). That would have prevented the rise of a mystical concept of the intellectual, which departs from the logical, and through which metaphysics fell into fanaticism (*Schwärmerey*). They ought not to have divided philosophy with respect to objects.",["'Erkenntniß' = knowledge.","'Schwärmerey' rendered 'fanaticism'; 'enthusiasm' is an alternative.","'sensitiv' rendered 'sensitive', following Kant's Latin parallel *sensitivus*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-004":["Here two parties arose, of whose very famous leaders we know two, shortly before or in the time of Alexander: namely Epicurus, philosopher of sensibility, and Plato, philosopher of ideas. The latter said that there was no reality in the senses; sensible appearances could be otherwise if we had other senses, without the object being altered thereby.",["'Häupterkennen' in the OCR is read as 'Häupter kennen' (we know leaders).","'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Gegenstand' = object; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-005":["Remark. Sense and appearance (*Erscheinung*) have been confused. Illusion (*Schein*) lies in the understanding; in the senses there is no false illusion. They give appearances, and the understanding judges about them. Now the understanding can judge falsely through the combination of appearances, and this produces illusion. The senses are not properly to blame for it, but the understanding, because it does not examine closely enough, before it judges, that about which it judges. The partisans of the intellectualists said that truth is only in the understanding, and some added that in the senses there is false illusion.\n\nIn ancient times there was a method, still derived from the Eleusinian mysteries, of delivering some doctrines exoterically, that is, for everyone's use and comprehension (*captu*), and others esoterically, for confidants and the initiated, under the seal of secrecy, so to speak *intra vela*. Examples are the doctrine of the one invisible Godhead, and of the groundlessness of their theogonies and mythologies. At a certain time, for instance at the origin of such doctrines, this may be good; but afterwards it may cease to be so, and must cease, because they are not entitled to carry on a monopoly of wisdom for themselves alone. In Plato's time some esoteric doctrines were [made public]; the exoteric, however, were always held back.\n\nIn his time also arose the question: through what do our intellectual modes of knowledge arise? Sensitive modes of knowledge need not be explained; they spring from the senses. We note that either our understanding has a faculty of intuition (*Anschauung*) of a different kind from the sensible, which yields only appearances of things, whereas the former yields them as they are; these would then be *intellectualia per intuitus*. Or it has the faculty of concepts, of forming concepts for itself from the intuitions of things by reflection; these would be *intellectualia per conceptus*. *Intellectualia per intuitus* are objects which the understanding alone can intuit; *per conceptus* they would be concepts which the understanding makes for itself. The first is a mere chimera: the understanding cannot intuit, only the senses can; so there are only *intellectualia per conceptus*. The first Professor Kant calls mystical, the other logical intellectuals.",["Sentence 'die von der Seite der Intellectualisten sagten ...' is syntactically broken; I render it as a clause about the partisans, as the transcript has it.","'Richtigkeit ihrer Theogonien' is odd; I read it as ironic ('groundlessness'), a judgment; the sense could be 'correctness'. Doubtful.","'Einige Esoterische Lehren; Exoterische jedoch aber immer zurückgehalten' appears corrupt; '[made public]' is my supplied word, in brackets, and the contrast may be reversed in the original.","'Denn wären es Intellectualia per intuitus' is rendered 'these would then be ...'; 'Denn' read as a loose connective.","'Erkenntniße' = modes of knowledge (plural); 'Anschauungs Vermögen' = faculty of intuition.","'Intellectualia per intuitus / per conceptus' kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-006":["According to this the philosophers divided themselves. Plato maintained mystical *intellectualia*, Aristotle logical ones.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-007":["Aristotle accepted *noumena*, not as objects of intuition by the understanding, but as intellectual concepts.",["'Obiect' rendered 'object' (Gegenstand)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-008":["Plato says that we have concepts which are not borrowed from the senses, for example the concept of the original being [*Urwesen*], and that we obtained these through a higher intuition which our understanding formerly possessed, an intuition of things that transcend the senses, and therefore intuitive. We had, he says, an intuition of God, from which we derived all our other ideas. Of these we now have only faint recollections, which occur to us on the occasion of sensible appearances. We no longer have that intuition, because our soul is shut up in the body as in a prison. All concepts of the Deity are only copies of the intuition which the soul had before it was united with the body.\n\nLogical intellectualia arise through reflection of the understanding. The understanding reflects upon the objects of the senses; if it leaves the object aside, the concept is a logical intellectual [concept]. For example, that water runs off when there is no other hindrance, we see from experience. The understanding knows this as necessary. If it now leaves the water out of account, it has the concept of the necessary, which is a logical intellectual [concept].\n\nAristotle held everything to be logical intellectualia. This division gave rise to a philosophical dispute whether the *conceptus intellecti* are *conati* [innate] or *acquisiti* [acquired]. According to Plato they would be *conati*, for we have not acquired them, at least not now; rather everyone brings them along as renewed ideas. (Remark: Plato's ideas relate to mystical intuition, and are to be distinguished from the *conceptus* of Aristotle.) We observe that our understanding can think but cannot intuit. If the latter is assumed, then they are all *conati*.\n\nAristotle says that the concepts of the understanding are not *conati* but *acquisiti*: we have obtained them on the occasion of experience, when we reflect upon objects of the senses.\n\nNowadays it is said that our concepts are innate, but not in Plato's sense, as renewed ideas which we originally acquired. Rather, God has implanted in each of us certain fundamental concepts in order afterwards to guide the understanding. To these belongs the concept of cause and effect. Whether we take the intellectualia logically or mystically, we can still ask whether they are innate in us, in that we could not and need not have arrived at them by reflection, or whether nothing is innate in us except the capacity of understanding.\n\nIn more recent times Locke was the follower of Aristotle. He said: *Nihil est in intellectu quod non antea fuit in sensu* [nothing is in the understanding which was not previously in sense].",["'Z.E.' (zum Exempel) rendered 'for example'. The OCR 'reflectieit' is read as 'reflectirt'.","'Bei gab läuft' and 'noth wendig' are garbled OCR; read as 'bergab läuft' [runs downhill] and 'nothwendig'. 'Water runs off' is a smoothing; the literal sense is 'runs downhill'. The following sentence, 'Läßt er nun das Wasser weglaufen', is probably a corruption of 'weglassen' (leaves the water out); I have translated it according to that sense.","'conati' is the transcript's form, probably for 'connati' (connatus, innate); the bracketed glosses 'innate' and 'acquired' are mine. 'Intellectualia' kept in Latin as a technical term.","The sentence 'Wir merken, unser Verstand kann denken, aber nicht anschauen' and the one after it are obscure in the transcript; 'the latter' is taken to mean intellectual intuition.","The sentence 'Dazu gehört … Wirkung' and the passage on 'Jetzt sagt man so' is divided into separate sentences for clarity; the 'Anmerkung' parenthesis is kept in place."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-009":["Locke asserted that a practised understanding could even see the way in which something could come to be out of nothing, and yet he wished to derive all knowledge from experience. He proceeded very inconsistently.",["'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-010":["Leibniz was the follower of Plato and believed in *ideas connatas* [innate ideas], but he left out the mystical element. Crusius also maintained this, although he does not express himself so obscurely about it. One may say that Plato's school has still retained something of the mystical *intellectus*. This opinion, however, is fanatical; anyone can imagine a great deal there, for example that he stands in communion with spirits, and so on.\n\nIf we take the logical intellectualia as our ground, the question arises how we come by them. The concepts of the necessary, the contingent, and so on, are for example pure concepts of the understanding. Now two ways may be assumed. Either they have arisen from the understanding, in which case one adheres to Plato, though not in his mysticism but only in so far as he asserts their origin [in the understanding]; or, as Aristotle said, *nihil est in intellectu quod non antea fuit in sensu*. There is, however, a misunderstanding here. Our concepts never arise otherwise than on the occasion of the objects of the senses, upon which the understanding reflects. In this Aristotle is right, for if nothing is given to us, we can reflect upon nothing. Plato, on the other hand, says that they are not borrowed from the senses, and in this he was also right. For could our senses bring about the concept of the necessary or the possible? In which sense should it lie: in smell, in taste, and so on? The concepts of the understanding are nothing but acts of reflection. But since I cannot possibly reflect if I have no object, and since the senses supply us with it, the understanding would not reflect if the senses gave no material. The pure understanding produces the concepts, but they would not occur if there were no material. Plato, then, was also right.\n\nAristotle's intention was to maintain that the matter comes from the senses, but not the form. Had he expressed himself so, and had Plato in turn said that the form of reflection is what the understanding has independently of the senses, no dispute would have arisen, and the two kinds of system could very well have been united. Here the one cannot dispense with the other. All concepts of the understanding would signify nothing if the senses supplied no objects and examples. If, for example, I explained ever so thoroughly what a substance is, and could give no example, all would be nothing; and again, without understanding we should have no concepts and should merely gape at everything.\n\nThrough concepts of the understanding we think the form, [that is,] how the understanding makes concepts from appearances. I can now leave the appearances aside, but the concept would nevertheless have no meaning without them. It is quite correct, however, that the concepts of the understanding are not borrowed from the senses, for they arise from reflection, and the senses do not reflect.\n\nOne can err by saying that all concepts of the understanding are borrowed from the senses, and Aristotle is to blame for this; he did not express his opinion definitely enough. Plato, for his part, goes too far and says that we have representations of the understanding independently of the senses; that the understanding would know things without any senses, indeed far better, since the senses, instead of helping us, still hinder us. Here, however, he already lapses into fanaticism: he says that we see only the shadows of things, the understanding the things in themselves.\n\n[If] he had said that we have concepts which we do not draw from the senses but which occur in the understanding independently of the senses, and had admitted that these alone do not give the objects unless the senses supply them [...]. Aristotle, with his symbolic *Nihil* etc., nevertheless rises to things which wholly transcend the senses, for example the world-whole and the original being. About these the senses can teach us nothing.",["The last-but-one sentence ('Hätte er gesagt … liefern') has no main clause in the transcript; I translate it as it stands, supplying '[If]' and marking the break with [...], and do not complete it.","'Dochzu' is OCR for 'doch zu'. 'Fehlen' in 'Nun kann man fehlen' is read as 'one can err'.","'Aristoteles schwingt sich ... doch zu Dingen' is as printed; the sense that even Aristotle's maxim leads to supersensible objects is left as the text has it.","'Gegenstände und Beyspiele' rendered 'objects and examples'; 'Stof' rendered 'material' (matter, in the following sentence, translates 'Materie').","'Mißverständniß' etc. are smoothed; the long paragraph is divided into several paragraphs and sentences for readability, in accordance with the method."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-011":["Aristotle was right that there are no *conceptus connati* [innate concepts], but that did not entitle him to the proposition: *Nihil est in intellectu quod non antea fuerit in sensu*. All metaphysics is built upon this, and Aristotle is the greatest among the ancient metaphysicians known to us.",["The remark 'Darauf baut alle Metaphysic' is ambiguous: it may mean that all metaphysics is built on the proposition (as translated) or that metaphysics rests on denying it; I follow the plain construction.","'berechtigte' rendered 'entitled'; the transcript's wording 'unter den uns bekannten alten Metaphysicern' is kept literally."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-012":["We shall show that all concepts are *acquisiti* [acquired], only not all of them from the senses; many we have also through the pure use of reason. A scholar proceeds inconsistently if he acts contrary to the principles he has adopted. Those who take all concepts of the understanding from experience Professor Kant entitles empiricists; and we now say that almost all empiricists, Aristotle, Locke and so on, act inconsistently. For if all concepts are borrowed from experience, they can assume nothing that does not rest upon experience. But God is given in no experience. We could therefore say nothing of him.\n\nAmong those who proceed very consistently is Epicurus. In regard to the gods Epicurus seems to have been dissembling, and to have done so in order not to fall under the censure of the priests. Plato is the philosopher of the intellect, Epicurus of sensibility. Although his propositions were strange, he must nevertheless be excused, since perhaps he has not been rightly understood. By his philosophy he sought to limit the proofs of things, but not the things themselves. That is always a great difference. He said that no concept has a meaning unless an object corresponding to it can be given in experience. We have no writing of his, but we have his opinions from the poet Lucretius; and in philosophy one must not put much trust in a poet. He said that we can reflect only upon the objects of the senses. He also made himself gods, but these were wholly sensible, being composed of the finest atoms in the world.\n\nLeibniz was a follower of Plato. He began to go further than the senses reach, but he did not say how it comes about that concepts of the understanding, which we have without the senses, are valid in respect of the objects of the senses. Plato and Aristotle had a metaphysics; Epicurus had none. With him everything was physics, natural science. He said there could be no principles other than those which experience confirms. Whether he was right in this or not, he was at any rate the anti-metaphysician of the ancients.\n\nTo distinguish the philosophical inquiries we note the following. We can distinguish physiology, critique of pure reason, and system of science. Physiology of pure reason is the investigation into the origin of concepts. It is an inquiry into a *re facti* [matter of fact]; it is, as the jurists say, a *quaestio facti*. How did it come about?",["Restated the long run from 'Ein gelehrter…' into shorter sentences and paragraphs; the content is unchanged.","'nennt … Empiristen' rendered 'entitles'; 'Verstandesbegriffe' = concepts of the understanding.","'Wir können also nichts von ihm reden' rendered 'We could therefore say nothing of him', the subjunctive being supplied by sense.","'heuchlen' (heucheln) rendered 'dissembling'; the ironic sense is that Epicurus only pretended to hold views about the gods.","'daß ... die Beweise der Dinge einzuschränken' kept literal: proofs of things versus things themselves.","'Physic' and 'Metaphysic' rendered 'physics' and 'metaphysics'; 'Critic' rendered 'critique'.","'von einer re facti' is ungrammatical in the transcript (probably 'res facti'); translated by sense, Latin bracketed. 'Wie ist er dazu gekommen?' ('he' = the concept? the mind?) is left with the vague pronoun 'it'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-013":["This investigation may be very pretty, but it does not belong in metaphysics. But since we have such concepts once for all, we must also ask by what right we make use of them. This question has a far more important influence on metaphysics, for it is critique, and so a *quaestio iuris*. That [first] question has been the occupation of philosophy, of Locke and Leibniz: the former wrote a book *de intellectu humano*, and the latter brought out one in French under this title. Locke adheres to Aristotle and maintains that the concepts are derived from experience through reflections. Leibniz adheres to Plato, though not to his mysticism, and says that the concepts of the understanding existed prior to the knowledge of all sensible objects.\n\nPhysiology is the consideration of the nature of reason, of how reason generates concepts of the understanding in us; it is properly a part of psychology. No one has so far thought of a critique of pure reason. The authors presented a system without physiology, as for example Wolff, who likewise presents a system without indicating how all this arose. He investigates only the content. But how far does the use of pure rational knowledge extend, and by what right do we make use of it? That is critique, and so a *quaestio iuris*. Critique is therefore widely distinguished from physiology and from system.\n\nFor example, one proves that a spirit thinks; with regard to these grounds various investigations can be undertaken. 1. Whether these grounds produce conviction in us; and Wolff also permits that investigation. [The second:] How it can be possible that reason can know properties of things that are given in no experience; this belongs to critique. In many cases critique seems unnecessary, for it seems superfluous to see, of modes of knowledge which we have, whether they are possible. But when we see to what illusions reason is subject, we shall recognise its necessity.\n\nWe have before us, then, an undertaking of a kind that has never yet been pursued. If we look at the result of all the knowledge acquired through metaphysics, we see that it cannot continue to stand as it is. Systems enough have been drawn up, which, even where they agree, cannot withstand even the assault of a malicious scepticism. They contain no utility, if we except the *principium contradictionis* [principle of contradiction], which is of such a character that it can withstand and resist the attacks of an opponent who is no systematiser. Men who did not wish to have laboured at their pure reason in vain have either given it up, or have gone on with it until they found the philosopher's stone.",["Divided the transcript's run-on paragraphs into shorter sentences. 'Erkenntniße' in the plural is rendered 'modes of knowledge'.","'iene Frage' is ambiguous: I take it as the physiological question of origin (the quaestio facti) that occupied Locke and Leibniz, hence '[first]'.","'Die Untersuchung erlaubt auch Wolff' and the numbered '1.' break off without a '2.'; I supplied '[The second:]' for the next sentence, which evidently is the second inquiry. This is conjectural.","'Zusammen' in 'es ist eigentlich ein Teil der Psychologie' is ordinary; 'Beschäftigung' rendered 'occupation' in the first instance and 'undertaking' where Kant speaks of the new task.","'die Verstandesbegriffe vor der Kenntniß ... gewesen': 'Kenntniß' rendered 'knowledge' for lack of a better distinction.","'Systematicer' rendered 'systematiser'; the OCR spacing around commas and 'Sys tematicer' silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s03-014":["What path shall we now take? The first? That is impossible, because we cannot break the understanding of the habit of these questions. They are so interwoven in the nature of reason that we cannot be rid of them. All the despisers of metaphysics too, who wished thereby to give themselves the air of clear heads, even Voltaire, had their own metaphysics; for everyone will surely think something about his soul. This resolution, then, cannot hold. Reason would sooner give up all other sciences than this one. These questions concern its highest interest, and to say that reason is no longer to put them to us is to say that it is to cease to be reason.\n\nThere remains to us, then, critique of reason. It criticises the pure use of reason, from which reason draws its principles independently of experience, [to determine] how far it can go; and critique can be wholly satisfying. Hitherto we have had nothing satisfying in metaphysics, for all systems can be shaken. The teacher of metaphysics may proceed so as to conceal from his pupils the weakness of the proofs and merely boast of this semblance of wisdom. In the future, when they read and think more about it, they will see how little of all that stood the test, by which many minds were spoiled.\n\nOur reason can take mighty strides without critique; it assures itself of the correctness of its use through the thoroughness of its progress. In mathematics, for example, we can use reason securely without first criticising it. The cause is that it can exhibit its concepts in intuition; further than this it need not go. Intuition assures it of the correctness of its use. In philosophy I may not venture to bring about modes of knowledge which have a supposed conviction, for many illusions can take place there. In mathematics, as said, intuition prevents the illusion; in metaphysics, critique does. We thus see the necessity of critique. The difficulties which meet us in the discovery of a critique must therefore be overcome.",["'wie weit sie gehen kann' is loosely attached in the German; I supplied '[to determine]' to carry it. The pronouns 'sie/diese' (reason, use) are resolved by sense.","'dem Verstand die Fragen nicht abgewöhnen können' rendered as breaking the understanding of 'the habit of these questions'.","'Schein Weisheit' rendered 'semblance of wisdom'; 'heiterer Köpfe' rendered 'clear heads' (conjectural: 'cheerful', 'enlightened').","'wie wenig alles Stich hielte' is irregular; rendered 'how little of all that stood the test'. 'Erkenntniße' = modes of knowledge.","Sentences were divided and the OCR spacing before commas silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-001":["The Utility of Metaphysics",["Heading only; 'Nutzen' rendered 'utility'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-002":["We now ask, as in every science: what is its end? The end of every science may be speculative or practical. Our aim is either to extend our modes of knowledge, or [to attain such] as bear upon our interest. If we consider the first case, we find that the objects of speculation are of two kinds: either they belong to experience, or they go beyond experience. To become acquainted with the former we need no metaphysics. We therefore ask: would it be worth the trouble to draw up a metaphysics for this purpose? As regards physics, we note that it admits no principles other than those which are (1) borrowed from experience, (2) mathematical, or (3) philosophical, in accordance with common sense (*sensus communis*) and with experience. Put more briefly, its principles must be borrowed from experience or confirmed through experience. This holds of mathematics, whose principles are *a priori* but find their confirmation in experience. That everything has its cause physics admits, since experience confirms it; but it does not inquire further into the grounds. Physics wants no metaphysical principles. Just as little does metaphysics serve to give us a deeper empirical acquaintance with the soul. We can discover various things about it and draw inferences from them, but without any metaphysical principles; these inferences must rather be confirmed in experience. Metaphysics can therefore contribute nothing considerable to knowledge of nature. For in order to explain the appearances of the soul and of the body, it is best to take the principles from experience, since otherwise they are unreliable.",["The sentence 'oder die auf unser Intereße geht' is elliptical; I supplied '[to attain such] as bear upon our interest' (presumably 'Erkenntniße' understood). The sense is the speculative/practical contrast.","'1. aus der Erfahrung entlehnt sind' is a relative clause grammatically governing only the first item; I smoothed the list into (1), (2), (3) with 'principles which are...'.","'Daß iedes Ding seine Ursach hat' etc.: 'Ursach' rendered 'cause'; 'Gründe' rendered 'grounds'.","'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Erfahrungs Kenntniß' = 'empirical acquaintance' (Kenntnis distinguished from Erkenntnis).","'Folgerungen daraus ziehen... sondern sie müßen' — 'sie' taken to refer to the inferences/discoveries; a judgment call in a loose transcript."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-003":["The connection between friends is more certain than that between citizens of a state. The ground of the connection yields a degree. Order is a quality; nevertheless it can be as great in a few things as in many; it rests on the truth of the rules for connecting the manifold. Transcendental perfection is the same. The ground of it rests on how a manifold agrees in several [things]: the more [such things], the more perfection. *Unum ad quod varium consentitur est socius* [the one with which the various agree is a companion]; the more companions there are, therefore, the greater the perfection. If I do not take it metaphysically, it is only relative. Metaphysical perfection is the degree of reality, and this we cannot estimate, because we have no concept of the highest degree of reality. The author goes on to speak of the magnitude of change. Sameness [*Einerleyheit*] too can be great or small. *Existentia determinationum oppositorum* [existence of opposed determinations] is change. The opposed determinations cannot exist at the same time, but only successively. This is therefore *existentia successiva*. A changeable thing is that in which *determinatio oppositorum* [determination of opposites] is possible; the greater the determination, the greater the change.",["The opening sentences are fragmentary in the transcript; the passage begins mid-discussion of Baumgarten's perfection and magnitude (§§ on quantity). 'Der Grund ... giebt einen Grad' kept literal.","'wie in mehreren ein Mannigfaltiges zusammenstimmt, desto mehr sind Vollkommenheit' is irregular; I restated it with supplied '[things]' and '[such things]'.","The Latin gloss 'unum ad quod varium consentitur est socius' is kept in Latin; the English in brackets is my supplied gloss. 'socii' rendered 'companions'; the Latin is probably corrupt or loosely transcribed.","'Einerleyheit' = sameness (identity); 'die oppositi' is the transcript's form for the opposed determinations."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-004":["This transcendent use of pure reason has the drawback that in it nothing can be confirmed through experience, but the advantage that no one can refute it through experience either. Metaphysics is concentrated in the transcendent use of reason, because the most interesting objects lie there and no satisfaction is to be found in experience. On account of the immanent use of reason we should not give ourselves the trouble of proving and analysing the principles, and of making a science of them, if this did not seem to us a preparation for ascending to the ideas. We have many modes of knowledge *a priori* which we cannot dispense with in experience. For example: if something happens, there must be a cause. This proposition is *a priori* because of the 'must', and necessary in experience, since otherwise we should have no connection in the series of experiences. For the principles that are necessary in experience we need no metaphysics, although it is good, and a cultivation of the understanding, to separate what in experience belongs to reason and what to the senses. First, then, we must examine the use of pure reason in respect of experience, for this is the simplest use. This takes place in the transcendental analytic (transcendental philosophy is that which investigates the possibility of metaphysics), where we analyse the concepts *a priori* that are necessary for the sake of experience. But this is of no particular utility; for example, 'everything that happens', etc., is already serviceable as a proposition true only *per inductionem*. But reason does not find its satisfaction in experience; it asks the Why, and cannot always, though for a time, find the Because. It therefore ventures not a step out of the field of experience, and [yet] arrives at ideas, and here reason cannot be satisfied except through knowledge of its own nature and through answers that can be given by knowledge of its sources, extent and limits. This part, to which we reckon the concepts *a priori* that go beyond experience, is entitled the transcendental dialectic, and here metaphysics is needed; for beyond the limits of experience lie the most interesting objects, and metaphysics will derive its value from the necessary relation of our reason to objects beyond experience.",["'Sie wagt daher keinen Schritt aus dem I elde der Erfahrung': OCR 'I elde' corrected to 'Felde'. The 'keinen' is probably a slip for 'einen' (ventures a step) given 'kommt auf Ideen'; I translated the text as it stands ('ventures not a step') and supplied '[yet]'. Doubtful reading.","'Dieser Satz ist wegen Müßen a priori': rendered 'because of the \"must\"'.","'und Cultur des Verstandes, beides abzusondern' is elliptical; I rendered it as 'it is good, and a cultivation of the understanding, to separate...'.","'Zu den Begriffen... Befriedigung': 'Kenntniße' rendered 'knowledge' where it is not the plural of Erkenntnis; 'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge'.","'Umfangs -und Gränzen' read as 'Umfangs und Gränzen' (extent and limits). The parenthesis on transcendental philosophy is kept in place.","The sentence 'wenn uns dies nicht eine Vorbereitung schiene' and 'wegen des immanenten Gebrauchs' were kept close; 'Vernunft' without article ('hier kann man Vernunft nicht befriedigen') rendered 'reason cannot be satisfied'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-005":["There are two kinds of use of the pure understanding. The immanent is that in which the modes of knowledge *a priori* have their objects in experience. So too there are principles *a priori* that apply to objects of experience; this is pure reason, but in its immanent use; and if we separate these from experience, it is analytic. The transcendent use is that in which the modes of knowledge *a priori* do not have their objects in experience. Metaphysics, which expounds the transcendent use of reason, occupies itself with ideas, and this is the field of necessities. In the immanent use there are indeed some also, but none concerning the correctness of the propositions, the definitions and the proofs. For example, up to now a proof of the principle of sufficient ground has been sought, yet it is nevertheless used, and no one doubts it; but in the transcendent use there is no proposition whose truth has not been doubted. No metaphysician has up to now thought of distinguishing this use of reason. For in cosmology, and also in ontology, there are propositions that have their objects in experience and also some that do not, and that is worthless; for the critique of reason must adopt quite different principles in regard to the immanent use than to the transcendent.",["The opening speaks of 'reinen Verstandes' but then 'reine Vernunft'; the transcript uses them loosely and I kept each as written.","'die ist das Feld der Notwendigkeiten' and 'beym immanenten sind zwar auch welche' are obscure; 'Notwendigkeiten' possibly a miswriting for 'Nothwendigkeiten' or 'Streitigkeiten' (disputes), which would suit the following contrast with doubt. Translated as written; doubtful.","'und sondern wir die von der Erfahrung ab, so ist das analytic' kept literal; 'die' taken to refer to the principles.","'und das ist nichtig' rendered 'that is worthless' (literally 'null, void')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s04-006":["We have divided metaphysics into the part that contains the immanent use of reason and the part that contains the transcendent use. 'Transcendent' and 'transcendental' must be carefully distinguished. 'Transcendental' belongs not to concepts but to the science, that is, to the use of the understanding. Transcendental philosophy is that which considers the pure use of the understanding, and to it belong all concepts and principles of pure understanding and of pure reason. Some concepts, however, are of immanent use, others of transcendent use. Transcendental philosophy considers the whole faculty of pure understanding and of pure reason to know anything *a priori*. The concept of ground and cause, and of God, belongs to transcendental philosophy; the former, however, is immanent.",["'Vermögen ... a priori was zu erkennen' rendered 'faculty ... to know anything a priori'.","'Der Begriff von Grund und Ursache ... iener ist aber immanent': 'iener' (the former) is singular and so refers to the concept of ground and cause taken together; I rendered 'the former'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-001":["We now come to the concepts of substance and accident.",["The initial '40' is a stray line or page number and is omitted.","*Substanz* and *Accidens* are kept in their Latin-derived English forms, following Kant's own Latin terms."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-002":["In dealing with the category we have spoken of the relation of substance to accident, of cause to effect [*causa* to *causatum*], and of the active to the passive. The second we shall entitle the category of the principle [*categoria principii*], the first the category of the subject [*categoria subiecti*], the third the category of community [*categoria commercii*]. These three are possible only of real relations. In all judgments there is a relation of the predicate to the subject. If this relation is merely that, the judgment is categorical; if it is the relation of ground to consequence, the judgment is hypothetical; if several judgments are considered as together in one whole of knowledge, the judgment is disjunctive. We know that the table of the categories is analogous to the table of the functions of the understanding in judging. In the latter we find categorical judgments, which underlie all the others; they contain the relations of the subject to the predicate. That which cannot exist otherwise than as subject is substance; that which cannot exist otherwise than as predicate is accident [*accidens*]. Here, then, everything is of one pattern: as categorical judgments are the foremost, so the category of substance is also the foremost, and the concepts of accident are, as it were, only secondary concepts [*conceptus secundarii*]. Substance is therefore also entitled the substratum of appearances, and it is first, for I can think substance or subject without accident or predicate, but not conversely. Hence substance is also entitled the substrate of the accidents. Accidents are merely modes of the existence of substance and cannot be outside that substance; for they exist as predicates, and these cannot be outside the subject. The ancients therefore said: *accidentia non migrant e substantia in substantiam*; [to suppose that they did] would indicate that they had an existence of their own. If we go back, we find that reality underlies substance and accident alike. (Remark: the *accidens praedicabile* is a contingent accident, as opposed to the necessary; the *accidens praedicamentale* is contradistinguished from substance.) An accident is also an existence, but only as inherence, and for that there must be something really positive. Negative predicates are therefore not accidents; nor are logical predicates, which relate merely to the actuality of a thing, e.g. A [*is^* in the text] is not a substance, and three angles are not an accident. By the expression 'inherence' we represent substance as bearing the accidents, as though they were separate existences needing only a basis; but this is a mere misuse of speech: they are only ways in which things exist. In so far as a thing is positively determined, accidents inhere in it; in so far as it is negatively determined, they do not inhere in it. They do not exist for themselves, nor are they merely supported by substance, as a book is in a bookcase.",["'Categorie com m er ei i' is an OCR break; corrected to *commercii* (community, *Gemeinschaft*). 'caußa/caußato' normalised to *causa/causatum*.","Sentence 'Die zweyte … die erste … die 3te': Kant's order of the three relations is subject, principle, commerce; I kept the German's ordinals as written and did not rearrange.","'das würde anzeigen' is elliptical; I supplied '[to suppose that they did]' for the antecedent.","'A is^ keine Substanz' is a garbled reading; I translate 'A is not a substance' and flag the stray mark. 'und 3 Winkel kein accidens' kept literally.","The sentence on logical predicates ('auch nicht logische praedicate, die gehen blos auf die Wirklichkeit') is compressed; sense retained, probably some words lost in the transcript.","'die Tafel der Categorien'/'Verhältniße': 'relations' for *Verhältnisse*; 'a whole of knowledge' for *Ganzen von Erkenntniß*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-003":["That which exists without being the determination of another is substance; that which exists only as determination is accident [*accidens*]. We consider first the category of substance: that which cannot be represented otherwise than as subject is substance, and that which cannot be represented otherwise than as predicate is accident. These are taken from the logical concepts of subject and predicate. Anything can be a logical predicate. For example, 'man', considered really, cannot be the predicate of a thing.",["'Z. E.' rendered 'For example'. 'real betrachtet' rendered 'considered really' (i.e. in the real, not logical, use).","'Wir erwägen' rendered 'We consider'; the OCR spacing before colons and 'vorgestellet. werden' silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-004":["Learning [*Gelehrsamkeit*] cannot exist by itself alone. — Categorical judgments underlie hypothetical and disjunctive judgments. Since without judgments we can know nothing, and every concept is itself a judgment, categorical judgments constitute an essential condition of experience. In every experience the real element is the relation of substance to accident. The category of substance is therefore the basis of all other knowledge. All determinations [*determinationes*] are either positive or negative. The latter are not accidents, because accidents involve an existence: every existence is a subsistence; that whose existence is inherence is an accident [*omnis existentia est subsistentia — cujus existentia est inhaerentia, est accidens*]. In a substance we can have two respects [*respectus*]. In respect of the accidents [*respectu accidentiae*] it has force [*Kraft*], in so far as it is the ground of their inherence; and in respect of the first subject, without any accidents at all, it is the *substantiale*. Force is therefore not a new accident; rather the accidents are effects produced by force. Sometimes the accidents are not really different, and then the forces too are only logically different, e.g. giving light and giving warmth. All forces which are only logically different from others, or whose difference can be removed by analysis, are entitled derivative [*derivationes*]. We have, then, primitive and derivative forces in every substance; it is assumed, and with much probability, that there must be one primitive force from which all the others come. But we cannot reduce all forces to the one, because the accidents are so various that we cannot take them as one and the same. If we leave out all accidents, substance remains, that is, the pure subject in which everything inheres, or the *substantiale*, e.g. the I. Here all forces are set aside. The other respect is that of the substance with its accidents to the *substantiale*, i.e. to the subject, which is distinguished from all other accidents. As to force, it is to be noted that the author defines it as that which contains the ground of the inherence of the accidents; since accidents inhere in every substance, he concludes, every substance is a force. That is contrary to every rule of usage. I do not say that substance is a force, but that it has force; the respect of substance to the accidents, in so far as it contains the ground of their actuality, is force. For example, I cannot say that the faculty of thinking [*Denkungsvermögen*] in us is the substance itself, for that belongs to it [as an attribute]; nor is it an accident, [for the accident is the thought]. We have, then, something that is neither substance nor accident. What, then, is the faculty of thinking? It is the relation of the soul to the thought, in so far as the soul contains the ground of the thought's actuality. The *substantiale*, i.e. the subject in which no accidents inhere, and which must necessarily be distinguished from the accident, we do not know at all; for if I remove all positive predicates I have no predicates and can think nothing at all. Substances are sometimes mistaken, through an error, for predicates; this is *substantia praedicata*. But this is rare; more often predicates are turned into substances, e.g. original sin by some theologians.",["The opening sentence 'Gelehrsamkeit kann vor sich nicht allein existiren' seems a stray fragment (a continuation of the preceding passage's example about predicates); translated as it stands.","'Autor' is Baumgarten, the text-book author; not named in the passage. 'wieder allen Regelgebrauch' rendered 'contrary to every rule of usage'.","Doubtful: 'auch nicht ein accidens der Gedanken ist das accidens' is garbled. I rendered it as '[for the accident is the thought]'; 'das kommt ihr zu' supplied '[as an attribute]'. Both bracketed supplements are conjectural.","'sind die Kräfte auch nur logisch verschieden' follows 'denn'; I read 'denn' as 'then'. 'Der andre respectus … von allen andern accidentien unterschieden' is awkward and kept close to the German.","'derivationes' kept in Latin as the transcript has it; 'Kraft' rendered 'force', 'Vermögen' as 'faculty'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-005":["The categories are, indeed, pure concepts of the understanding, but they can also be applied to objects of experience. They then retain their name with the addition *phaenomenon*, and so here also: there is a *substantia phaenomenon*. For example, body, which is itself an appearance, but is so as the *substratum* of other appearances. That which is nothing but a sum of *accidentia*, and yet presents itself as substance, is a *phaenomenon substantiatum*; for example, the rainbow. Substance proves its existence only through reason. This is a strange consequence, and it arises from the false activity of force. [Force] is not a thing but a *respectus*, and is therefore an *accidens*. If I take subject and *accidens* together, there is substance; if I leave out the *accidentia*, the *substantiale* remains. Of this we can form not the least concept; that is to say, we know nothing but *accidentia*. For our understanding knows everything through predicates (*praedicata*); that which underlies the predicate we never know. For example, 'All men are mortal' means: whatever I know under the predicate of man, I also know under the predicate of mortality. Man is the essence of what is corporeal, animal, eats, thinks, wills, and so on, and these are nothing but *accidentia*. The concept of man is therefore composed of nothing but predicates; the concept of substance is that of something in space to which the *accidentia* belong, but which we do not know. If I speak of myself, 'I will', 'I act', these are *accidentia*; the substantial element is the 'I'. Can we, then, know anything otherwise than through *accidentia*? We otherwise have not the least concept of it. When we speak of appearances more specially, we shall see that we know merely forces. The thing in which the forces inhere remains unknown to us. Negations are not *accidentia*, and real [accidents] can be known only according to their reality, that is, according to the real that is in them. Forces are derivative if their *accidentia* are identical with the *accidens* of another force. A force whose *accidentia* are not identical with the *accidens* of another force, or which cannot be reduced to a higher force, is a primitive force. All natural philosophy is occupied with reducing forces to a fundamental force, and this we cannot explain further, namely, that because something is, something else thereby follows. All fundamental forces must be given through experience.\n\n*Coexistentia mutabilium cum fixis est status.* In every thing there is something abiding; this is the *fixum*. The changeable, so far as it coexists with the existence, is state [Zustand]. State is therefore nothing but the determination of a substance in time. Only changes are possible in time. If the determinations at different times are different, the state is changed; if they are the same, the state is not changed. The unchangeable [Unveränderliche] therefore has 2 states, [and] consequently the *ens reale* does not. Inner state is the coexistence of the inner changeable determinations with the existence; outer state is the coexistence of the relations with the existence. *Modification* is change of the inner state; if someone receives more ills, he is not thereby modified. The outer state can be changed without the inner, and the inner without the outer.\n\nWe now come to more important concepts. From force, action can be derived, and from both, other things. Corresponding to this is *passio*, suffering. The possibility of acting is *facultas*; the possibility of suffering is *receptivitas*. A substance, so far as it contains the ground of that which belongs to the existence of a thing, acts; so far as the ground of that which belongs to its own existence is contained in another substance, it suffers passively. Every substance acts, because the subject subsists. The predicates inhere in every substance; the *accidentia* (though we call them so merely [by name]) cannot exist except in the substance. Substance therefore contains the ground of something that belongs to existence, and therefore acts. In natural science there is great ground for regarding the *vis attrahens* and the *vis pollens* [sic] as primitive forces. Can there be many fundamental forces in one substance, or only one? Our reason also … must be several [Auch unsre Vernunft müßen mehrere seyn] <",["'Pollentem' is printed thus in the OCR; it is probably a misreading or slip for *repellentem* (repulsive force), paired with attractive force. I have kept the reading as found and marked it [sic].","'Sie kommt her aus…' and 'Sie ist kein Ding': the antecedent is ambiguous. I took the first 'sie' as the strange consequence and supplied '[Force]' (Kraft) for the second, since a relation (*respectus*) and hence *accidens* fits force, not substance.","'Real [accidents]' supplied in 'wirkliche': the German has only an adjective after 'Negationen sind keine accidentien'.","'Das Unveränderliche hat daher 2 Zustände, mithin das ens reale nicht' is probably corrupt or the transcriber omitted a 'nicht' (the sense wants 'not 2 states'). Translated as it stands.","Zustand = 'state'; Bestimmung = 'determination'. The Latin *modification* is retained in italics. 'Übel' rendered 'ills'; the example is obscure.","The closing sentence ('Auch unsre Vernunft müßen mehrere seyn') is garbled, perhaps with words missing. The translation is a literal reading with the gap marked by an ellipsis, not filled in.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the editors' '< [bricht ab)' apparatus is left out as the task instructs, except for the '<' mark kept to show the break.","Long passage divided into shorter sentences in places for clarity; the editors' page and line marks and any OCR debris are silently omitted."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-006":["... are unreliable, and we can also get on quite well without any metaphysical principles. Yet it may be said that a metaphysics, or critique of reason, is of use here. If we have not examined the first sources of judgment, we run the risk of judging falsely in a metaphysical way. The use is therefore negative, to warn against false metaphysics, and not positive, to extend science. The object of metaphysics does not lie in corporeal or thinking nature. What, then, is it with which metaphysics is concerned? It must be an object above the senses. Metaphysics is entitled the science beyond nature, μετὰ τὰ φυσικά, that is, *trans physicam*. Some have held this name to be unsuitable. They have supposed that Aristotle so named it because in his bundles of manuscripts it came after physics. But μετά means not 'after' but 'beyond', and the name is very suitable and appears to have been carefully devised by Aristotle. Metaphysics is the science of the things that go beyond all appearances, of what lies beyond nature. Here we take nature not *sensu toto* but *proprio*; for then it is entitled the sum total of all objects of the senses. Here I can think of nothing but God and another world: a being that is distinct from all others, and a life that follows upon this one and is distinct from it. These two propositions govern the whole of metaphysics. If we are indifferent to them, we may be indifferent in every respect. If these two propositions are the *cardines* on which the whole of metaphysics turns, the question is why we need to answer these two questions at all. We may entitle the part of metaphysics that treats of the original being *Theologia rationalis*, and the part that treats of the future life (since this presupposes the immortality of the soul) *Psychologia rationalis*. Then metaphysics has only these two parts, *Psychologia rationalis* and *Theologia rationalis*. But what use have they? They must somehow interest us.\n\nSo far as speculation is concerned, all this is a matter of indifference to us. I shall explain the appearances of nature as if they sprang from the constitution of nature, and I cannot appeal to God, for that would be to set all philosophizing aside. It is the same with the immortality of the soul; here I can say, 'Let us wait until we come to where we can make experiments.' By speculation we can accomplish and settle nothing. The aim must therefore be a practical one. What shall I do if there is a God and another world? Now it becomes clear that this interests us greatly. I must now conduct myself quite differently than if I saw that only the world of sense exists. In that case I make use of things as accords with my purpose, as I can promote my pleasure and still my desires.\n\n(All men wish to be happy. Reason prescribes laws and conditions under which alone one is worthy of happiness. Morality, which contains these conditions, does not teach us the way to happiness, but only the conditions under which we are worthy of it; and this is the practical interest of reason in the principles of the conduct of life. Religion, however, is also connected with a speculative interest. I ask: can I also hope to partake of happiness if I am not unworthy of it? Here we must presuppose a wise ruler of the world. If it were of no concern to us that the rules of morality should have influence on us and moving force, we could be relieved of all speculation on this matter. As soon as we have no practical interest, that is, as soon as we are not concerned about being worthy of happiness, all this falls away.)\n\nIf I have ground for presuming that there is another world and an Author of the world, quite another interest opens up. What have I to do? In the practical respect, then, these two propositions are of the utmost importance, and more important than all other ends. They concern our final ends. If there is yet another world, and an Author of the world on whom my fate in that world depends, what have I to do in order to partake of lasting happiness? Suppose now that all speculation can give us no adequate and satisfying instruction on the two questions; what use would metaphysics then bring? We must see whether there is not another way of arriving at it. Let us consider: what ought I to do if there is a God and another world? We see that morality teaches what I must do if I am not to be contemptible in my own eyes and wish to partake of happiness.\n\n(We do not find that fortune is always distributed in right proportion. Vice triumphs, and the virtuous man must not show his grief. If, then, our present life concludes our existence, we cannot say that we should be happy in the measure in which we are worthy. We must therefore assume yet another world. From these two needs, God (for from a blind necessity we cannot expect that it should make us happy in so far as we are worthy of it; for that we must assume a wise ruler of the world) and another world, metaphysics springs. To these, everything in the immanent world is related; and among all the questions that speculative philosophy can ever raise, none is so pressing and so interesting that it should move us to entangle ourselves in such difficult speculations. Both these objects lie wholly beyond the field of experience (God and another world, that is, the world which lies outside the compass of all experience now possible to me). Empirical principles can therefore be of no use to me here; pure reason alone must decide. Theology and psychology are thus the proper parts of metaphysics. In the latter we seek to learn from the nature of the soul enough to see that it can live on when animal life ceases, and indeed as a spiritual life.)\n\n(Pure philosophy has yet another use: it enlightens the understanding, since here we set our concepts apart from one another. This use is logical, but much too small for the labour we must expend upon it. Its chief use is to free us from errors with regard to these objects. It does not serve to procure them for us. For it is easy enough to see that modes of knowledge which answer to such great needs will not lie so high that the loftiest speculation is necessary for them. Common human understanding must also be able to convince itself of them, and so it is in fact. It finds so many traces of wisdom in the world that it infers that there must be a wise Author of the world. If it combines with this the command which reason, or rather this wise Author, gives it, it must hope for another world, because it finds that virtue and vice do not receive their reward as they should.",["The passage opens and closes mid-argument ('... are unreliable'); the final parenthesis is left unclosed, as in the German. I have kept the transcript's parentheses and dashes.","Silent OCR corrections: 'sejm' = seyn; 'psTa toc «puaixa' and 'pe t<x' = μετὰ τὰ φυσικά and μετά; 'J ragen' = Fragen; 'Beine Philosophie' = Reine Philosophie; 'unwiüdig' = unwürdig; 'Gliickseeligkeit' = Glückseeligkeit; 'Denn heißt es' read as 'Dann heißt es' (then it is entitled). The Greek is a reconstruction of garbled OCR.","'Denn heißt es der Inbegriff...' is read as 'dann' (nature in the proper sense is then the sum total of objects of the senses). 'sensu toto' is kept as printed; it may be a slip for a different Latin phrase, but the contrast with *proprio* is clear.","The sentence 'Wenn diese 2 Sätze, die die cardines sind... so ist die Frage' is irregular (a protasis with no proper apodosis); I smoothed it into 'If... the question is why...'. The following 'Wenn wir... nennen, so hat Metaphysic...' I rendered with 'may entitle'.","'Sie dienet nicht dazu, sie uns zu verschaffen': the antecedent of 'sie' is uncertain (the objects, or the knowledge of them); I took it as 'these objects'. 'Er' in the final sentences is common human understanding (gesunder Menschen Verstand), so I used 'it'.","'Wissenschaft' rendered 'science'; 'Convoluten' 'bundles of manuscripts'; 'Welt-Urheber' 'Author of the world'; 'Weltregierer' 'ruler of the world'; 'immanenten Welt' 'immanent world'; *Theologia rationalis* and *Psychologia rationalis* left in Latin."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-007":["It is true enough that speculative reason is not needed to produce these concepts. Yet there will be many who do produce them; for speculation, and this speculative reason, can make us so uncertain and so wavering in faith, and can also bring errors in upon us (as common reason too can), that we must of necessity have weapons of the same kind against it, and these are provided by metaphysics. Everyone readily sees that it is unreasonable to demand happiness without conducting oneself so as to be worthy of it. This is what morals teaches. Morals therefore does not rest on metaphysics, since I can lay at the foundation a morality which does not presuppose metaphysics; thus I have a law of conduct for becoming worthy of happiness, and this morals teaches apodeictically. But [in this], we see, something is still wanting. Can I also hope to become a partaker of happiness, if I am worthy of it? If my disposition and conduct are framed according to morals and rest upon it, then it is necessary to connect with the moral principle, that no actions are to be performed save those that accord with the law of duties, a belief in a benevolent ruler of the world, who is willing to make us partakers of happiness, and in another world in which we can become partakers of it. Can metaphysics, then, enlarge this belief? No. This belief is not only supported by the contemplation of nature as a whole, but metaphysics also secures it against all objections of speculative reason, and sets limits to that reason. Metaphysics is therefore not the foundation of religion (how else would it fare with those who are not of metaphysical bent?), but the bulwark against the attacks of speculative reason. For that reason is very dangerous, and its attacks are unavoidable. Its utility is therefore negative: to prevent errors; but it is immeasurable, because such errors are so pernicious.",["\"Irrthiimer\" in the OCR is read as \"Irrthümer\"; \"lener\" is read as \"Jener\" and rendered \"[in this]\", the antecedent (the moral law taught apodeictically) being supplied by judgment; the sentence is slightly uncertain.","\"Glaube\" is rendered \"belief\" here, as the context (practical, not theoretical, assent) suggests; Kant's own contrast with Wissen appears in the next passage.","The sentence beginning \"Kann ich denn auch hoffen\" and the long sentence on the moral principle were kept close to the German, though restated in somewhat shorter clauses.","\"sie sichert ihn\" and \"sie setzt derselben Grenzen\": \"sie\" taken as Metaphysic (not Betrachtung), and \"derselben\" as speculative reason.","\"Moral\" is rendered \"morals\" / \"morality\" as the sense requires."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-008":["The knowledge of what I must unfailingly do according to the principles of reason which concern my whole end is entitled morals. This is already given to us, and needs no metaphysical principles, nor need it presuppose God and another world. The moral incentives, however, are strengthened the more when I see that there is a God and another world. The moral laws must also precede the theological; these present to us the most perfect will of God. But first we must know the perfection of our own will, and then set the divine will before us as a model. Morality is a canon. A canon is any legislation in so far as it commands absolutely; it must presuppose no condition, not even that of God and another world. From the nature of the action it must be known what is to be done and what to be left undone. Morals teaches what I ought to do. But it puts into our hands no springs [Triebfedern] to do what reason prescribes as duty. The sum of the springs to actions is happiness, which no mortal can dispense with; and this is lacking in morals. I cannot hope, or I cannot see whether I can hope, to become a partaker of happiness. This morals does not teach, but only how to become worthy of happiness. The latter can be seen a priori, without knowledge of the propositions concerning God and another world. Laws are not complete which do not carry with them, besides the fulfilment, a threat and a promise. The moral laws have no threat or promise that we shall become partakers of happiness or forfeit it. In this world this cannot be, for here well-being and well-doing are not connected together. Yet there remains for morals a belief, that there may perhaps be a wise ruler of the world and another world. Were this not so, the moral laws would be without effect.\n\nNow we can represent to ourselves a practical dilemma, that is, a proposition which shows that, unless one concedes something, one falls, turn where one will, into sheer absurdities. A practical dilemma is one in which, if I do not presuppose something, I plunge myself always into a *absurdum practicum*. This is of two kinds: 1. A principle by which I give up all claim to honour, honesty and conscience. 2. A principle by which I give up all claim to happiness. If we adopt moral principles without presupposing God and another world, we entangle ourselves in a practical dilemma. For if there is no God and no other world, then either I must steadfastly follow the rules of virtue, and then I am a virtuous visionary [Phantast], since I expect no consequences worthy of my conduct; or I shall throw away the law of virtue, despise it, trample all morality underfoot, because it can procure me no happiness; I should give myself up to my vices, enjoy the pleasures of life when I have them, and thereby adopt a principle through which I become a scoundrel. We must therefore resolve to be either fools or scoundrels. This dilemma indicates that the moral law, which is written in our reason, is inseparably bound up with a belief in God and another world.\n\nAmong the ancients no virtue so pure as ours was taught; but they could practise it without believing in God and another world. They practised only a political virtue, which procured for them esteem, happiness and the advantages of life, and which is merely prudence. Here the question is whether anyone can adhere to the purest virtue, in which many pleasures of life must be sacrificed, without belief in God and another world. That is quite impossible. In his own eyes he would always be worthy of reverence, but for all that a visionary. With the knowledge of our duty, which reason prescribes, and with living in accordance with its moral maxim, belief in God and another world is inseparably bound up. The mere beauty of the action cannot impel us to it; it is a great motive, but we sacrifice it nevertheless to advantage. If, then, we cannot attain knowledge [*Wissen*] through metaphysics under logical conditions, there remains only a moral belief, which the contemplation of nature as a whole not only does not controvert but rather confirms. If we do not satisfy reason in its speculative use, we do so in its practical use; and this moral belief is as unshakeable as the greatest speculative certainty, indeed firmer still, since what is grounded on the principles of resolution is far above the principles of speculation. And now we obtain freedom to examine impartially, and in the sharpest manner, all the proofs of God and of the immortality of the soul; to strip them of their semblance when they pretend to do more than they actually do and can do; to throw away everything sophistical; and even if not a single proof remained, these great truths will lose nothing, since if we are not sufficiently convinced in speculative respect, we are so in practical respect. Here we have the right freedom to philosophize without any constraint or partiality. In most of our philosophizing we are supposed to find nothing other than what others have already found, to approve what others have held true, and not to make the least alteration. With these matters we now wish to occupy ourselves at greater length.",["Passage divided into paragraphs and some long sentences restated more shortly, as the method permits; no content added.","OCR slips silently corrected: \"voi auszusetzen\" as \"voraus­zusetzen\", \"nrüßen\" as \"müssen\", \"\\\"V ollkommenheit\\\"\" as \"Vollkommenheit\", \"Bios\" as \"Blos\", \"philosohiren\" as \"philosophiren\", \"ohnmöglich\" as \"unmöglich\", \"Ehre\" etc. unchanged; \"-Dieses\" stray dash dropped.","\"a *absurdum practicum*\" should read \"an *absurdum practicum*\"; Kant's Latin term kept in italics.","\"Wissen\" supplied in brackets for the sense of knowledge under \"logical conditions\"; Erkenntniß is otherwise \"knowledge\".","\"die nicht eine Drohung und Verheißung außer der Erfüllung bei sich führen\": \"außer der Erfüllung\" rendered \"besides the fulfilment\"; the sense (beyond mere fulfilment) is somewhat uncertain.","\"wurden\" (\"theilhaftig wurden\") is read as a subjunctive; \"verlustig gehen\" rendered \"forfeit\".","\"Dieselbe läßt sich a priori einsehen\": \"dieselbe\" is ambiguous; I take it as the worthiness of happiness (the preceding noun), rendered \"the latter\".","\"Entschließung\" rendered \"resolution\" (the will's determination); the sentence \"weit über die Principien der speculation hinweg\" is translated literally as \"far above\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-009":["The whole of metaphysics is nothing but a chain of systems that have been built up and overthrown. No book has yet appeared in which there is anything lasting. There is no science that has the fate of being lasting. The more the understanding begins to be enlightened, the more value metaphysics acquires; yet no one can rid himself of metaphysical questions, since they are too closely bound up with the interest of human reason. It will be objected that Wolff and Crusius have published metaphysics. But one need only look at the outcome, without examining the matter itself. These systems have all collapsed already. Some propositions were true, but not the whole. With regard to such metaphysical propositions one can take the dogmatic way, that is, one can seek to know one's pure reason as far as possible. The treatment of a science is dogmatic when one does not trouble to inquire from which powers of the mind the knowledge springs, but lays down certain general propositions, held to be true, as a foundation, and infers the rest from them. The treatment is critical when one seeks to discover the sources from which that knowledge springs. In mathematics the latter is not necessary, for everything that mathematics says can be confirmed through experience. In philosophy this is not so, and there we often find great difficulties and contradictions in the use of various modes of knowledge, although they seem to be immediately certain, just like the fundamental principles of mathematics, e.g. everything that exists is in time. We must therefore investigate the powers of the mind from which the modes of knowledge spring, in order to see whether we can trust them, although they seem manifestly true; and then [we must investigate] on what the capacity to know anything *a priori* is founded at all. The critical method examines the proposition not *objectively*, that is, according to its content, but *subjectively*. — The method of metaphysics is accordingly critical-dogmatic: its aim is to find a *criterium* by which to distinguish the modes of knowledge that arise legitimately from the understanding and from reason, from those that arise through an illusion or through the understanding's deceiving itself. In metaphysics we shall not ask whether the propositions 'There is a God and another world' are true, but whether the understanding can get so far by mere speculation. By all the rules of a rational faith one can be practically so convinced of something that no reason will deny it, but not in a speculative respect. — On the first question, whether the propositions are universally true, we shall show that no one will deny them without abandoning all rational principles; for this we shall need only common human understanding, merely enlightened a little. On the second question, whether we can get so far by mere speculation without a practical aim, we shall show that this cannot be done. — This we shall call logical truth; so we cannot become logically certain of these propositions. — In critical investigations we shall find great weaknesses of the understanding, and that it pretends to some knowledge which it does not have. Here metaphysics has no positive use but a great negative one: hitherto propositions have always been criticized, but no one has criticized reason itself, and therefore nothing lasting has been achieved here. (In no science is the negative use so great as in metaphysics, for in none are the errors so great and so dangerous. In no science, then, is critique so necessary as here, for here everything has a negative use. We can show that such principles as the opponent argues with against me, and with which he wishes to shake belief in God and in another world, are not grounded in reason at all — and so [we can show] the incapacity of reason to do any harm to religion.) To invent propositions and bring them into a system, that was the first way; then, when it was seen that this caused discord, men began to examine reason itself, not to dispute about things, but to inquire what sources reason has and how far it can get without experience. These were investigations of the subject, and this resolution was very reasonable. For if we make no progress with the object, it is well to direct our attention to the subject itself, and this was bound to arise at some time; for if I cannot be speculatively certain about things, I must at least be certain about the sources, boundaries and principles of reason. In many matters we cannot give the solution, as for example in natural science, and here we cannot judge whether it is in any way possible, because all our insights in natural science depend upon experience, and we cannot know how far we shall get in it. Here it is quite impossible to determine the boundaries of human reason. In regard to mathematics also it is impossible, because everything there is grounded on intuitions from which consequences flow; and since the intuitions may be infinite, the consequences too may be infinite. One cannot, therefore, determine whether many more properties of the circle will not yet be discovered. In metaphysics we need pure reason, without basing ourselves on intuitions. It is pure philosophy, which knows objects through pure concepts. It is a special science of the use of reason through concepts. If we have not yet got far here, as is actually the case, we can nevertheless determine something with certainty with regard to reason. The boundaries of pure reason can be determined with certainty, because the objects are to be reflected upon [reflectiren] from intuitions; for if objects are not given through experience, we have no objects of reflection. We shall therefore be able to enumerate all the concepts of pure reason, and likewise to list the fundamental principles, and to show how far the pure use of reason extends and within what limits it must be held. We can then show whence the illusion [Schein] arises, which has prevailed in so many systems, and which questions have been either answered or merely raised. We shall be able to secure morality and religion against the apparent objections of speculative reason, and thereby human reason attains complete satisfaction; and such a work is possible. (In the previous century, when all the sciences underwent a great revolution, when experimental physics came into vogue, men began also to examine reason, but physiologically, not critically. This was properly an explanation and investigation of the origin of concepts. Locke and Leibniz did not think of any critique of the concepts of reason; they inquired merely how we come by concepts. Locke said, all concepts are borrowed from experience. Leibniz said no: we have some also through pure reason. This is easily distinguished; for example, can experience indeed give the concept of cause and effect, of God, etc.? But Leibniz did not ask how reason, independently of all experience, comes to the concept — on what is the capacity to know anything *a priori* at all founded? How far does it extend? Something similar to a critique of pure reason is found in David Hume, who however fell thereby into the wildest and most desolate scepticism; and this came easily, because he did not study reason wholly and entirely, but only this or that concept.)",["Long passage divided into shorter sentences in places (e.g. the definitions of 'dogmatic' and 'critical'); the parenthetical passages are kept in parentheses as in the transcript.","Erkenntniß rendered 'knowledge' and, in the plural, 'modes of knowledge'; Gemüths Kräfte as 'powers of the mind'; Vermögen as 'capacity'.","'We must investigate ... and then [we must investigate] on what ...': 'und dann' continues the investigation; the verb is supplied in brackets. Likewise '[we can show]' in the parenthesis on religion.","'d.h. seine reine Vernunft so weit zu kennen als möglich' is loosely constructed in the transcript (infinitive after 'man'); rendered as 'one can seek to know'; 'seek' is supplied.","'die ohne eine Illusion entstehen oder dadurch, indem er sich selbst hintergeht': the 'ohne' is probably a slip for 'durch' or 'mit' (the sense requires 'through an illusion'); I translate by the sense. Flagged as doubtful.","'Das wollen wir nennen logische Wahrheit' is cryptic; the antecedent is probably the universal truth of the propositions or their speculative demonstrability; translated literally. The context is unclear.","'Die Grenzen... weil die Gegenstände aus Anschauungen zu reflectiren sind': the transcript is compressed; kept close to the German. 'der in so vielen Systemen geherrscht' refers to the Schein, resolved by judgment ('which').","OCR slips silently corrected (e.g. 'miiße', 'V ernunft', 'enummeriren', 'dem in wildesten'); 'Wolf' is rendered 'Wolff' and 'Leibnitz' as 'Leibniz'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-010":["An inquiry into the *factum*, into how we come to knowledge, whether from experience or through pure reason. Locke accomplished much in this, and such an inquiry may also be of use; but it is not so very necessary, and indeed hardly possible. It is better to ask: How many concepts of pure reason are there? What significance have they? That is, to what objects can they relate, how can they be employed, and within what limits must they keep? This is a critique of pure reason. We here see by what right we make use of concepts without experience, and whether we do not do so unlawfully. By means of critique I do not arrive at certainty in regard to the questions, so far as they are to be answered dogmatically; what I arrive at is certainty as to what reason can accomplish in respect of all metaphysical questions. Such a critique has never yet been published, except by Professor Kant, Riga, 1781.",["'Oritic' in the OCR is read as 'Critic' (Kritik); rendered 'critique'.","'mäßen sie sich halten' read as 'müssen sie sich halten'.","The dash in the German is replaced by a division into separate sentences and a colon, to bring out the contrast between the Lockean inquiry and the critical question; 'Gewißheit ... dessen, was' is completed with 'what I arrive at is certainty', a supplied construction.","'H. Professor Kant' rendered 'Professor Kant'; 'Riga 1781' is the first edition of the Critique of Pure Reason.","*factum* is kept in Latin as Kant's technical term (the question quid facti, as against quid juris)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-011":["(Remark. Metaphysics can be divided into metaphysics as natural disposition and metaphysics as science. The sum total of all our modes of knowledge through concepts, knowledge which belongs to every human being and which he employs in experience, is *metaphysica naturalis*. Every rational knowledge, so far as it is not speculative or *in concreto*, is natural, and of such knowledge there is a great deal. The common use of the understanding therefore also has its metaphysics, and this metaphysics well deserves to be raised to a science. Not every natural use of reason need always be transformed into a scientific one, since the ends are attained in the natural way. In morals, for instance, we need not represent the rule *in abstracto*, but only *in concreto*. It is, for example, difficult to prove *a priori*, or to show *in abstracto*, the inadmissibility of lying; *in concreto* it can well be done. In metaphysics, however, it is necessary. We have various modes of knowledge *a priori* which find their confirmation in experience. But we also have transcendent concepts, and in their case material metaphysics can no longer help, since the confirmation of the general principles in experience is lacking. I then flutter, as it were, in empty space; and unless a science *a priori* confirms itself through itself, I run the risk of falling into mere brain-work [*Hirn Geschäfte*]. The natural use of reason has its place so long as we remain within experience, but it does not suffice as soon as we go beyond experience; and since this is unavoidable, metaphysics as science is necessarily inevitable in reason. Reason seeks whether it cannot there find solutions of the intricate questions which true reason itself raises. Even practical reason looks about for someone who treats metaphysics as a science, or else tries it for itself. No mode of knowledge so urgently needs to be a science as metaphysics. People mock at it as a useless thing, easily dispensed with; but the same might have been done with the theory of the circulation of the blood. Nor can we excuse ourselves by saying that it goes beyond reason and our modes of knowledge. In nature there are mysteries; but the things are outside me and I am to study them, and there veils are drawn before them. But if I think anything, if I think to myself things *a priori* which are only creatures of my understanding, then it must be possible to give an account of them, whether my assertions have ground or not. What is in question is not the investigation of a thing but of an understanding; its principles and concepts must be capable of being investigated, for everything lies in myself.)",["Several OCR slips silently corrected: 'Eifahiung' as Erfahrung, 'dei' as der, 'verwikt kelten' as verwickelten, 'Unzuläßigkeit' retained as 'inadmissibility', 'entbehrendeSache' split.","'Hirn Geschäfte' is doubtful in sense; rendered 'mere brain-work' with the German in brackets.","'selbst die wahre Vernunft' kept as 'true reason'; possibly a transcriber's slip for 'reine Vernunft' (pure reason).","'oder probiert es bei sich selbst' is obscure; rendered 'or else tries it for itself'.","'hier sind Decken vorgezogen' rendered 'there veils are drawn before them'; 'hier' is read as referring to the mysteries of nature.","Long run-on sentences divided; 'Erkenntniße' = 'modes of knowledge'. 'Itzt' in 'itzt kann die materiale Metaphysic nichts helfen' rendered 'in their case ... can no longer help'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s05-012":["Meanwhile let us see to it that we do not lose the dogmatic method; we shall therefore present metaphysics as our author does. We shall accordingly not establish metaphysics as a doctrinal edifice, as a doctrine, since it will afterwards be found that all this is illusion; but we shall add the critique of pure reason, by which all dogmatic boasting will fall away. Critique is thus to produce a system of the self-knowledge of our reason. In the system of metaphysics we shall first go through all the concepts analytically and merely explain what our reason understands when it makes use of this or that concept. This is of great utility, even though we here leave undecided whether the questions of metaphysics are answered or not. This elucidation (not extension) of our cognitions is very useful, for wherever there is reason at all there are metaphysical concepts. Metaphysics is the sum total of all modes of pure rational knowledge, which is greatly clarified by analytic. The answering of all the questions of reason is the synthetic part of metaphysics, where we seek to extend our knowledge through pure reason and to acquire pure knowledge. This part is of a different kind from the analytic; for in the latter the concepts of the understanding are dissected but no questions are answered, so that our knowledge is not extended but only elucidated. In the synthetic part, however, our concepts are extended, and there we require critique, because there we make attempts without going out beyond experience with reason. Even if not all philosophers at present take the path of the critique of pure reason, they will nevertheless be compelled some day to arrive at it. Necessity will teach them this, since critique alone gives complete satisfaction, and without it our reason will be in constant conflict with itself.",["'1 heil' in the OCR read as 'Theil' (part).","'Kenntniße' rendered 'cognitions' to distinguish it from 'Erkenntniße' ('modes of knowledge').","'wo wir ... Versuche machen, ohne mit der Vernunft über die Erfahrung herauszugehen' is awkward; rendered as it stands, and the sense (attempts made without leaving experience, or being careful not to) is doubtful, perhaps a transcriber's slip for 'um nicht ... herauszugehen'.","'sie' in 'werden sie erweitert' refers to the concepts/cognitions; I have rendered 'our concepts' by judgment.","'Analytic' is kept as the noun for the analytic part; 'Critic' rendered 'critique'. Some sentences restated."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-001":["End of the Prolegomena; Ontology [follows]",["'Ontologia' is the heading of the next section; 'follows' is supplied in square brackets sense only, since the German/Latin gives just the heading words.","Latin *Finis Prolegomenorum* kept in sense as 'End of the Prolegomena'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-002":["We now begin the science of the properties of all things in general, which is entitled ontology. (Ontology is supposed to be the science that deals with the general predicates of all things, that is, with such predicates as are common to the greater number of things. If the predicates are not *universalia*, i.e. common to all things, one does not know at all what ontology is. They must belong to all things: if not copulatively, that is, so that both belong to them, then at least disjunctively, so that one of the two belongs, e.g. composite and not composite. These predicates belong to all higher [things], but not copulatively, that is, not so that both predicates should belong to them, but disjunctively, one of the two. When I speak of general predicates, an exception still always occurs, and how far does it extend?) It is easy to see that this science will contain nothing but all the fundamental concepts and fundamental principles of our knowledge *a priori* in general. For if it is to consider the properties of all things, its object is nothing but a thing in general, i.e. every object of thought, and so no determinate object. Nothing is left to me, then, but the knowing which I am contemplating. (The science that deals with objects in general will deal with nothing but the concepts through which the understanding thinks, and so with the nature of the understanding and of reason, in so far as it knows anything *a priori*. That is transcendental philosophy. It does not say anything *a priori* about objects, but investigates the faculty of the understanding or of reason to know something *a priori*. It is therefore a self-knowledge of the understanding or of reason as regards content, just as logic is a self-knowledge of the understanding and of reason as regards form. Criticism of pure reason belongs necessarily to transcendental philosophy. But ontology used to be treated without criticism [gap in the text]. What was ontology then? An ontology which was not transcendental philosophy. Men philosophised, therefore, now here, now there, without asking: Can this be done at all? Transcendental philosophy is the result of the critique; for if I can represent the extent and the sources in their connection, then the connected representation of the principles *a priori* is transcendental philosophy; and if I take all the consequences that flow from it, that is metaphysics. Without a critique I do not know whether the concepts of pure reason and of pure understanding are all there, or whether some are not still lacking, since I have no principles. No limits were set to reason, and men went as far as they could. They see well enough that in matters of experience they cannot mix everything together, but *a priori* they can have insight into everything, and [they do so] because no one can refute them.)",["The first parenthesis is restated in shorter sentences; its repetition (\"These predicates belong to all higher…\") is kept, though it is obscure. \"Higher\" (höheren) may be an error for \"things\"; the text is doubtful.","\"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge; \"das Erkennen\" rendered \"the knowing\".","OCR \"h orm\" read as \"Form\"; the mark \"■\" after \"abhandelte\" marks an illegible or garbled place, shown as [gap in the text].","\"Critic\" rendered \"criticism\" (first occurrence) and \"critique\" (later), as the context suits.","The last sentence breaks off awkwardly (\"und daher, weil sie keiner wiederlegen kann\"); I supplied \"[they do so]\" and the sense is uncertain, possibly corrupt.","\"Die\" in \"wie weit erstreckt sich die?\" taken as referring to the exception."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-003":["(Metaphysics has this peculiarity, that it can be completed entirely; the faculty of reason can be measured out according to its sources, extent and limits. Metaphysics cannot hope to make discoveries in the nature of things; reason must teach everything. I can therefore determine which concepts lie in reason independently of experience. It is here as with a grammar, which can be complete, but not with a lexicon; for while the author is writing it, new words are being made again.)",["\"Vermögen der Vernunft\" rendered \"faculty of reason\".","\"Lexicon\" kept as in the German; \"ohnabhängig\" rendered \"independently\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-004":["(In morals, too, something can be completed, namely what Herr Kant calls the metaphysics of morals, that is, the first principles of morality; these are from pure, but practical, reason.)",["\"Herr Kant\" is the transcriber's third-person reference to the lecturer, kept as it stands.","\"Sittlichkeit\" rendered \"morality\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-005":["(The author's ontology is a *farrago*, a gathering together of knowledge that is not a system but merely rhapsodic, although in other respects he was one of the most acute of philosophers. The reason is that nothing was yet known of criticism [*Critic*].) The science which is to treat of the properties of all things in general must be a science *a priori*. It is therefore a knowledge from mere reason, and cannot be drawn from experience. For experience does not reach so far that it can be applied to all things. It does not teach us what must belong to things in general, but only what the senses show us. Since ontology has no determinate object, it can contain nothing but the principles of knowing *a priori* in general. The science of all the fundamental concepts and fundamental principles upon which all our knowledge from pure reason rests is therefore ontology.\n\nStrictly speaking, however, this science will not be entitled ontology. To have a thing in general as object is the same as to have no object at all, and to deal only with a mode of knowledge, as in logic. The name, however, suggests that the science has a determinate object. But it has no object distinct from the essence of reason. It considers understanding and reason themselves, namely their fundamental concepts and fundamental principles in their pure use (that is, of pure reason and pure understanding). The most fitting name would be transcendental philosophy.\n\nA mode of knowledge is entitled a pure knowledge of understanding or of reason in so far as it is possible *a priori*, and it is to be distinguished from the empirical. But a pure knowledge of reason is not therefore transcendental. What is transcendental is the consideration of the nature and possibility of such a pure reason, of knowledge through pure reason. For example, the concept of cause and effect is pure but not transcendental, whereas the consideration of the possibility of such a concept is transcendental. — Between two points only one straight line is possible: this is a proposition *a priori*, but not a transcendental proposition, for it deals with the object. But when I consider how it is possible to arrive at such a knowledge *a priori*, that is transcendental.\n\nTranscendental philosophy contains the principles of the possibility of knowledge *a priori*. It contains all the fundamental concepts of our pure reason and pure understanding, and in general all the fundamental principles of the possibility of knowledge *a priori*, [and] the extent and limits of all reason *a priori*. It determines how far pure reason can go without experience. Before that can be done, we must know the content of pure reason: the fundamental principles, fundamental concepts, principles, and conditions of the use of the concepts of pure reason.\n\nIt is very necessary, in extending our knowledge, to know the limits beyond which we cannot go; otherwise we run the risk of labouring at a loss. We venture to extend our knowledge, and do not know whether we are in the field of truth or of mere figments of the brain, and even our true modes of knowledge become doubtful. The limits of empirical knowledge, as was said above, cannot be determined. Physics, that is, the philosophy of the appearances of nature, therefore has no determinate limits either, and the same holds of mathematics, because it can multiply its intuitions without end.\n\nThe objects of pure reason are such as can be given in experience, e.g. God, world, whole, and so forth. What, then, are the limits of pure reason? It must be possible to determine them. For in pure reason the object is not given to me at all; I need only study reason itself, since that is given to me. In natural science, on the other hand, the knowledge is obtained from elsewhere, namely from experience. In the modes of knowledge of pure reason I have to spin everything out of myself; here experience and intuition can be of no help to me. Since, then, the whole can be drawn in advance from my knowledge, I have reason in myself. The limits of pure reason must therefore admit of being determined, and transcendental philosophy is the determination of the limits of the use of pure reason.\n\n— We now proceed to actual knowledge. Before we consider it and its elements, that is, its limits, sources and extent, we must first premise something, namely the distinction between analytic and synthetic judgments. We can judge in two ways. Either in judging we attribute to the subject a predicate that lies in the concept of the subject; or in judging we go beyond the concept of the subject and attribute to it a predicate that does not lie in its concept. The former is analytic, the latter synthetic.\n\nFor example, 'Every body is extended' is an analytic judgment. When I think a body, I think at the same time something extended. Here I can find the predicate by analysis of the subject. Such judgments are entitled judgments of dissection [*Zergliederungs Urtheile*]. Herr Kant calls them explicative judgments [*Erläuterungsurtheile*], because they elucidate our knowledge. — 'Every body is heavy' is a synthetic judgment; weight does not lie in the concept of body at all.\n\n'Everything that happens has a cause.' The concept of cause is not contained in the concept 'it happens'. To happen means to become what was not before, and the concept of cause does not lie in this at all. This judgment is ampliative [*erweiternd*]. 'Everything that happens exists in time' is analytic. 'It happens' means: there was a time in which it arose, preceded by another time in which it was not. I need only dissect the concept 'everything happens' in order to bring out the judgment.\n\nThe possibility of analytic judgments is easy to see, for I know only what I already had in the concept of the subject; I need only dissect the concept. Whether synthetic judgments are possible is a question. Synthetic judgments *a posteriori* are easily possible; for example, 'Every body is heavy' is taught by experience. What remains is to show the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*. This is hard to answer, but it constitutes the spirit of the whole of transcendental philosophy. Had philosophers investigated this earlier, they would have got further in many matters.\n\nMathematics has a multitude of synthetic judgments *a priori*. They must therefore be possible. We must now see how they are possible, since reason makes a great multitude of such synthetic judgments beyond experience. I have a concept, of which I am to say not something that lies in it but something outside it, and this *a priori*, without the aid of experience. How is that possible? For example: everything that happens has a cause, that is, something must precede what happens, upon which it follows according to a rule, and what happens is regarded as effect. How can I extend a knowledge in this way *a priori*?\n\nThis *quaestio* has never been raised in such generality, although it is so exceedingly necessary. Indeed it may be said that the whole of transcendental philosophy is an investigation of the possibility of synthetic judgments *a priori*. Before we speak at length of the answer to this question, we shall go back once more to analytic judgments.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'V issenschaft' (Wissenschaft), 'Eifaluung' (Erfahrung), 'jjriori' (priori), 'Hie Mathe matic' (Die Mathematik), 'Beyhiilfe' (Beyhülfe), 'W ißenschaft'. Stray punctuation spacing removed.","The long sentences were broken into several, and the passage divided into paragraphs for readability (the transcript is continuous). The opening parenthesis is kept as in the German.","'Die Objecte der Reinen Vernunft sind solche, die in der Erfahrung gegeben werden können' is translated as it stands. The argument (God, world, whole; 'der Gegenstand gar nicht gegeben') requires 'nicht gegeben werden können', so this is probably a slip of Mrongovius or of the OCR.","The sentence 'da das ganze also voraus aus meiner Erkenntniß gezogen werden kann, habe ich in mir Vernunft' is obscure and possibly corrupt; I translated it closely and did not smooth it further.","'Grund Begriffe / Grund Sätze' rendered 'fundamental concepts / fundamental principles'; 'Principien' is kept distinct as 'principles'. 'Erkenntniß' is 'knowledge', and 'a mode of knowledge' where the sense is particular.","'Zergliederungs Urtheile' and 'Erläuterungsurtheile' are given with the German; 'erweiternd' is rendered 'ampliative' (Kant's Latin *iudicia ampliativa*, in the Critique 'Erweiterungsurtheile').","'Hirngespinste' is 'figments of the brain'; 'auf Verlust zu arbeiten' is 'to labour at a loss'. 'Quaestio' is left in Latin.","'Critic' in the opening parenthesis is rendered 'criticism' with the German in brackets, since it refers to the Critical philosophy."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-006":["All judgments must have a principle, which is the criterion of truth; for without one there is no difference at all between true and false judgments. (The metaphysicians did see that synthetic judgments must have a different principle. Leibniz supplied for this the *principium rationis sufficientis*. Everyone believes this proposition, but how do we come by it? Wolff ventured to add a proof of it from the principle of contradiction. He saw well enough, however, that the proof was not adequate, and so remarked in a note that, even if the proof were not stringent, one could here appeal to common human understanding. — That is true enough. But if the proposition is to be one *a priori*, it must also be proved *a priori*, and completely; and this proposition cannot be the principle, that is, the principle of judgment, since it itself requires a proof.) What, then, is the principle of analytic judgments? For everyone readily sees that synthetic judgments must have an altogether different one. — It is one thing to say what lies in the concept, and another to go beyond the concept. The principle of analytic judgments is the proposition of contradiction: *cuilibet subjecto non competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*. All analysis is nothing other than the consciousness that a certain mark is contained in the concept of the thing. The mark contained in the concept of the thing is in part one and the same with the thing. Every analytic mark is identical, not with the whole concept, but with a part of it. For example, 'Every body is extended' is in fact a judgment of identity. All analytically affirmative judgments rest on the proposition of identity: *cuilibet subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum*. (The predicate can be identical *ex parte*, as in 'Every body is composite'; or it can be wholly identical, in which case the proposition is empty.) 'Man is an animal': here I say nothing but what I already think in the concept of man. The proposition of identity indicates nothing but the analysis of the concept. (All analytic judgments are easy, and are propositions *a priori*; for we need only run through our concepts and see what lies in them. They are wrongly passed off as propositions of experience.)\n\nThere are also analytically negative judgments. An analytically negative judgment is one in which I find, by dissection, that a certain mark conflicts with the thing; for example, 'No body is simple'. The simple conflicts with the composite. The principle is: whatever contradicts the concept of the thing must be denied of the thing. The *principium contradictionis* expresses the negation in which I must not go out beyond the concept of the thing.\n\nAre these the principles of all analytic judgments? Yes. All analytic judgments, affirmative as well as negative, stand under the proposition of contradiction. For if a predicate is identical with the subject, its opposite contradicts the subject; and by the *principium contradictionis* I shall at once recognize the falsity, once I have recognized the truth by the *principium identitatis*. Conversely, I shall at once recognize the truth by the *principium identitatis*, as I have recognized the falsity by the *principium contradictionis*. Since here I can infer from the one to the other, I shall adopt the *principium contradictionis* as the universal principle of all analytic judgments. For example, 'A body is simple' means that the simple, not the composite, contradicts the concept of body; the composite therefore belongs to it. It is the same whether I say, 'A concept contradicts the thing, or its opposite is identical with it', or, 'A concept is identical with the thing, or its opposite contradicts it'. The *principium contradictionis* is therefore the principle of all analytic judgments.\n\nCan we not also say that it is the principle of all human knowledge? *Responsio*: Yes; but then it is not a principle from which all judgments can be derived. It is a criterion of truth, with which no true knowledge may conflict, since otherwise the thought is self-contradictory. But what does not conflict with the *principium contradictionis* is not on that account true; it is therefore a negative criterion of truth. The thought of a thing may be possible, but the thing is not on that account possible. For example, we can think a power of the mind by which we think the thoughts of others [*andrer Gedanken denken*]; there is nothing contradictory in this, yet the thing is not thereby possible. Here the *principium contradictionis* can be no criterion of truth. But it is false, according to the *principium contradictionis*, that I think and do not think at the same time, — or that I stand in immediate connection with a distant object. The *principium contradictionis* is a universal principle of all human knowledge, but only for deriving falsity from it. It is universal, but not sufficient. There may be no contradiction, and the thing may nevertheless be false. The *principium contradictionis* is the universal negative criterion of all truth: what conflicts with it is false.\n\n(The *impossibile* in the principle of the author [Baumgarten] can be nothing but the modality; it is meant merely to indicate the apodictic character of the proposition, which it is not always necessary to express, here as elsewhere, since it goes without saying.) Our author expresses it thus: *Impossibile est aliquid simul esse et non esse*. How can we use this, when we have as yet said nothing of time? In this many acute authors have erred. (We must first ask, or rather can ask, how far time is possible.) The author believes this to be at the same time the definition of the impossible: *impossibile quod est et non est*, or that which contradicts itself. (As we have expressed it, *nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*, the *simul* drops out, and the principle still holds universally; for a predicate opposed to a subject can never belong to it. But a predicate may well belong to a subject if it is opposed to another predicate of the same subject, only not at the same time. For example, a moved body that is simple is a contradiction, but a body that is at rest and in motion is no contradiction, namely when the two are successive. Whether a predicate can belong to one subject which is *oppositum* to another predicate of the same subject, can be known only through experience; or rather, that a thing can be changed is a possibility which experience teaches.)",["Restated: the long opening and the 'analytic mark' sentences are divided; the parenthesis on Leibniz and Wolff is closed after 'proved completely… requires a proof', the OCR having lost the parenthesis, and 'Welches ist das princip…' is set as a new sentence outside it.","Latin tags silently corrected from OCR/transcript forms ('cuilibet subjecti… praedicato' → *subjecto… praedicatum*; 'Cuilibet subjecti competit praedicato' → *subjecto… praedicatum*). 'ltespensio' is read as *Responsio*; 'impoßibile', 'eße' are normalised.","'Jedes analytische Merkmal ist mit dem (nicht ganzen) Begriff identisch, sondern…' is an irregular construction; smoothed to 'not with the whole concept, but with a part of it'.","'Dem Begriff des Körpers wiederspricht das Einfache, nicht das Zusammengesetzte' is kept as it stands; the sense is that the predicate 'simple' is excluded and 'composite' therefore belongs to body.","'andrer Gedanken denken' is doubtful (perhaps 'anschauen' or 'andrer Gedanken' as genitive of persons); rendered as 'think the thoughts of others' with the German in brackets.","'kein wahres Erkenntniß zuwieder seyn muß': rendered 'may conflict' on the sense (no true knowledge may contradict it); 'unser Autor' is taken to be Baumgarten, supplied in square brackets.","Last sentence: the closing clause ('oder die Möglichkeit… lehrt die Erfahrung') is awkward in the transcript; I made it a restatement with 'or rather'. The passage's bracketing of the final parenthesis is as the transcript has it; 'ex parte' and 'oppositum' left in Latin."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-007":["(The *principium contradictionis* is the critique of possibility, and one may say also of truth; yet it is not the sufficient criterion but only the *conditio sine qua non*.) If that were so, I could reverse it and say: what does not contradict itself is not impossible (*cui competit definitio competit definitum*). But this is false, for then all fantasies that involve no contradiction would be possibilities. — The *principium contradictionis* is to precede all knowledge, and so cannot depend on the concept of time. Some have expressed it thus: *ut aliquid sit et non sit*. This too is false, for it is possible that a thing should be and not be, provided it is successively. All things, as we know, can be changed, and change is precisely the *successio praedicatorum contradictorie oppositorum*. The principle of identity he has expressed thus: *quicquid est illud est*. (The principle of identity enunciates positively what the principle of contradiction enunciates negatively. For if predicates identical with the subject did not belong to it, the opposite would have to belong to it; that would be a *praedicatum oppositum*, and this is contrary to the *praedicatum contradictum* [i.e. the predicate that the principle of contradiction excludes]. — This principle of contradiction is the supreme logical negative principle, and that of identity is the supreme logical positive principle.) (From the combination of these two principles a third arises, namely the *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria*. That is, of two predicates that are opposed, one must always belong to a subject; for if I ascribe both to it at once, the one cancels the other and I think nothing; if I deny both, I likewise think nothing (logically), that is, where one is A, the other non-A. In the *principium exclusi medii* we do not compare two contradictorily opposed judgments, but one thing with all possible predicates of things in general. The understanding sets out to furnish a given concept with predicates drawn from all possibilities.) This is an empty proposition. All empty propositions are no means of attaining distinctness, since *idem per idem* is explained, and so no distinct concept arises. For example, \"a body is\" is a true proposition, but empty; and so likewise is the proposition *quicquid est aliud est*. If I explain the concept through itself, I have thought nothing else. In our explanation we did not say: *praedicatum subjecto per tale identicum*. That would also be empty, e.g. \"body is body\"; we said rather: *ex parte identicum*. The predicate is a *criterium falsitatis* of all human knowledge, but [it is] a positive, real predicate of all analytic judgments, which can be derived and proved from it. (An analytic judgment is one in which subject and predicate are identical. If I now attach to the subject a predicate identical with it, I attach to it an *oppositum* and so contradict myself — but not in the synthetic judgment, because there subject and predicate are not identical.) Now the truth of analytic judgments cannot be known in any other way than that either the predicate is identical with the subject, or its opposite contradicts the subject. In synthetic judgments, however, there is no contradiction, and therefore I cannot judge them by the *principium contradictionis*. For example, that something happens without anything preceding it involves no self-contradiction, and yet it is false. A contradiction arises only when that is cancelled which is posited in the concept of the subject. The truth of synthetic judgments does not rest on the principle of identity. For the synthetic judgment consists precisely in this, that I posit something other than what is identical with the thing; for example, everything that happens has its cause. Cause is not identical with what happens, but is something quite different.",["Restated the opening 'Wenn das ist' as 'If that were so' (subjunctive of argument): the claim reversed is that the principle is a sufficient criterion of possibility.","'Der Autor' / 'er' (Baumgarten) is not named in this passage; 'he has expressed' refers to the author of the textbook, as the context suggests.","The sentence 'Das praedicat ist ein criterium falsitatis…' is loose in the transcript; 'predicate' presumably means the predicate (principle) of contradiction/identity. I supplied 'it is' in brackets and render it as it stands.","'das wäre praedicatum oppositum, das ist aber dem praedicatum contradictum zuwieder' is obscure in the transcript; I rendered it literally and added a bracketed gloss. Doubtful.","'Critic' rendered 'critique' (Kritik); 'Erkenntniß' as 'knowledge'; 'Deutlichkeit' as 'distinctness'.","Latin technical terms kept in italics; 'e. gr.' / 'z.E.' rendered 'for example'. 'Patenter' etc. not in this passage.","I rendered 'ut aliquid sit et non sit' as a bare quotation since the German does not complete it; 'succeßive' as 'successively', supplying 'provided it is'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-008":["The author says that *nihil negativum* is as much as *impossibile*; this is the usual view, and quite right. It is distinguished from *nihil privativum*, which signifies a lack; for example, light is something positive, darkness something negative. Bright darkness is a *nihil negativum*. *Nihil negativum* is that of which no thought at all is possible. I can think something affirming and something denying, but I think nothing at all if I think the affirming and the denying together, for example, bright darkness. Here I cannot think darkness, because I think brightness, and cannot think brightness, because I think darkness — hence nothing at all. If I think opposed things, I have two thoughts; if I put them together, I think nothing at all. In the progress of knowledge one must take care not to pair real thoughts together in such a way that in the end nothing at all is thought.",["'Der Autor' is Baumgarten; 'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'.","'Mangel' rendered 'lack'; Latin terms kept in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-009":["Contradiction is divided into *veram et apparentem* [real and apparent]. For example, *pia fraus* [pious fraud] seems to involve none and yet is a real contradiction, for one who is pious cannot deceive, and conversely. That the earth attracts the moon immediately, without touching it, is an apparent contradiction. Further, contradictions are latent where the contradiction can be recognised only through analysis; for example, a permitted lie from necessity is a hidden contradiction; one must first show the concept, for necessity can give no permission to lie. They are patent where no analysis is needed. One must not at once accuse someone of manifest contradiction, for if it were known to him he would not contradict himself. The patent contradictions are therefore only relatively patent. If one wishes to charge anyone with absurdity, that is, with a contradiction that is patent, one must turn the latent into the patent; and if the opponent nevertheless persists in it, he thereby assumes an absurdity.",["'u.v.v.' (und vice versa) rendered 'and conversely'.","'Schein Wiederspruch' rendered 'apparent contradiction'; 'Nothlüge' rendered 'lie from necessity'.","Latin 'latentem in patentem', 'patentis' etc. normalised as 'latent'/'patent'; the OCR 'iemanden' read as 'jemanden'.","The sentence 'Ferner sind sie latenter…' is slightly irregular; I restated it in plain English, adding 'contradictions' for 'sie' and 'They are patent' for the bare 'Patenter'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-010":["In passing we may also cite the *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria* [principle of the excluded middle between two contradictories]. Two opposed predicates, A and not-A, cannot be posited together; that would be a contradiction. Nor can two opposed predicates be removed together; one of them must belong to the thing. That is what the *principium exclusi medii* says. For example, round or not round: by the *principium contradictionis* both cannot belong to the subject, but by the *principium exclusi medii* both cannot be denied of the subject either. This latter principle cannot be derived from the *principium contradictionis*. For if two opposed predicates cannot both belong to one thing, and one of them must be denied, then the other is thereby posited in its place. [Yet this proves nothing against the priority of the *principium contradictionis*:] the *principium contradictionis* is, after all, the first. By this principle we can make many analytic judgments *a priori*. We need only analyse the concept, see what is contained in it, and affirm one of the marks or deny its opposite. Take, for example, body: in it I think to myself something extended. I therefore say, a body is extended, or, no body is not extended. This is an analytic judgment *a priori*. From all concepts that admit of analysis we can form analytic judgments *a priori*; if they are simple they cannot be analysed, for example, being, something.\n\nWe now come to synthetic judgments. Experience is nothing other than a chain of synthetic judgments *a posteriori*. We do not, however, need to ask how synthetic judgments *a posteriori* are possible, for that is clear. Suppose, for example, that I have gold and want to know more of it than lies in my concept of gold. I then call experience to my aid and place the gold in various circumstances, whereby I become aware of more and more in it and notice, for example, that it is malleable and fire-resistant, and so on. Then I have a synthetic judgment *a posteriori*. The whole of experience is nothing but the synthesis of perceptions. Consciousness of sensation is perception. Of pure sensations we can form no concepts, nor communicate them to others, since a sensation is the way one finds oneself affected by something, and another finds himself affected otherwise. Of the synthesis of perceptions, however, we can form concepts. Since in this we are not passive, everything that we may be minded to do can be present to us in consciousness.",["The sentence 'Dieses läßt sich nicht herleiten... Mithin ist das principium contradictionis doch das Erste' is compressed; I supplied the bracketed clause to make the connection of 'doch' plain. The sense is that the excluded middle presupposes the principle of contradiction.","'Wiederspruch' rendered 'contradiction'; 'Praedicate' as 'predicates'. 'Ausgedehntes' rendered 'something extended'.","'Ich habe Gold' read as 'suppose I have gold'; the passage is split into shorter sentences, as the method permits.","'Weil wir da nicht paßiv sind, kann uns also alles das bewußt seyn, was wir thun möchten' is loose in the transcript; 'möchten' is rendered 'may be minded to do', and the sense is somewhat uncertain.","'Wie sind synthetische Urtheile a posteriori möglich, das ist deutlich': I restated this as a statement that the question need not be asked because the answer is clear.","'Befindet' ('wie man sich bey etwas befindet') rendered 'finds oneself affected', to bring out the reference to feeling."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-011":["Experience gives synthetic judgments *a posteriori*. Can we not have synthetic judgments *a priori*? On the answer to this question rests the possibility of the whole of metaphysics. (Many deny synthetic judgments *a priori*, and thereby the whole of metaphysics. If metaphysics is possible, then such synthetic judgments *a priori* are also possible, and whether they are so is the first cardinal question in metaphysics. Here we at once find great difficulties. We attribute to a subject a predicate that did not lie in its concept, for example, 'All substances are persistent, all accidents changeable'. That is a synthetic proposition. In the concept of substance there lies nothing except that it is not a property of another thing, but is itself a thing. Is the proposition *a priori* or *a posteriori*? To distinguish this, we must see whether the proposition contains necessity or not. For experience teaches only that things are, not that they ought to be. In the case of this proposition there is necessity, and so it is *a priori*. When something comes to be that was not before, we say that it must have a cause. This is a synthetic proposition, for happening is something other than having a cause. It is therefore a synthetic proposition, and indeed *a priori*.)\n\nWe first ask: are not certain synthetic judgments *a priori* necessary for the possibility of experience, or of synthetic modes of knowledge *a posteriori*? Must there not be certain synthetic judgments *a priori* through which the synthetic judgments *a posteriori* are possible? And these would certainly be true, because they underlie experience, and experience is true. We see first, then, whether an experience is possible without laying synthetic judgments *a priori* at its foundation.",["The long parenthesis is retained as in the transcript, but divided into shorter sentences; the dashes are replaced by full stops.","'accidencen' (accidents) is silently corrected in spelling; 'Cardinal Frage' rendered 'cardinal question'.","'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge' (plural of Erkenntnis).","'und die waren gewiß wahr' is read as 'and these would certainly be true' (waren = wären); 'die' at the end refers to experience, as the argument requires.","'Das Geschehen' rendered 'happening', i.e. an event's occurring, as distinct from 'having a cause'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-012":["Experience has matter, that is, data, and form, that is, the connection of the data. The matter is constituted by the perceptions. Experience is the unity of manifold perceptions, and this unity is the form of the perceptions. There must, then, be a rule of connection of the perceptions through which all experience is possible. This rule cannot in its turn be *a posteriori*, for it must precede all experience, and is therefore *a priori*. There must accordingly be a rule *a priori* of the unity of perceptions, which makes experience possible. There will be synthetic propositions *a priori* which contain the principles of the possibility of experience. This presupposes that experience is not an aggregate of perceptions but a unity of perceptions ordered according to certain rules, a point we shall illustrate further later on.\n\nWe have now got so far as to see, from afar and as in twilight, that in a certain way synthetic judgments *a priori* must be possible, since some of them must themselves underlie experience. We must now inquire which synthetic propositions may precede all experience *a priori*.\n\n— I note two things in my experience. One is intuition, which rests on the senses and is entitled empirical intuition. The other is concept, which does not rest on the senses but comes about through categories, and therefore rests on the understanding. I have, then, intuition, which belongs to the senses, and concepts, which belong to the understanding. The question now is whether there are intuitions and concepts *a priori*, or whether everything is *a posteriori*.\n\nEmpirical intuitions are representations of an object as our senses are affected by it. Empirical intuition has two constituents, matter and form; and so too have empirical concepts. — The matter of everything empirical, that is, of empirical intuition, is sensation; the form is shape. The concept has matter, that is, content, representations, data which are given. Its form is reflection of the understanding, whereby the understanding so combines the sensations that it thereby thinks something universal. The concept is a sum of sensations worked up by the understanding. The empirical constitutes the matter in perceptions, intuitions and concepts. — The empirical rests on sensations, and these are *a posteriori*. The matter of all representations is sensation, and is given to us *a posteriori*.\n\n— If I leave out everything from the intuition, I still retain the form, that is, the shape. Prior to all empirical intuition there is something empirical, namely sensation, and something that can be represented *a priori*. In every empirical concept there is matter, that is, sensation, and form; and the form belongs to the understanding, for it is logical. I can now retain the intellectual element, the form, when I leave out everything empirical. For example, in the case of the piece of chalk, if I leave out the intuition, there remains only the form, magnitude and shape, and that is *a priori*. The modes of knowledge *a priori* of the form of intuition and of the form of concepts are the ground of synthetic judgments *a priori*. We must therefore have concepts which are possible prior to all experience, underlie it, and are synthetic.\n\nSensation constitutes the matter of all experience. If we leave all that out, shape still remains. In empty space I can feign a thousand sorts of shape. In geometry real shapes, for example the cone, are thought *a priori*. What remains of extended beings when I leave out all the matter of perception I shall therefore entitle the form of intuition. In the case of a body I think nothing but space and shape, that is, the form of intuition. Everyone has the representation of space *a priori*: that it is extended in length, breadth and height, and that between two points a straight line is possible. Hence these are absolutely necessary. We have, then, an intuition of space.\n\n— If we leave out everything empirical, such as weight, density and colour, form and shape remain to me. I now ask whether I can leave that out also. Yes, but then no body remains to me. Through body I think a substance, and so the concept still remains to me. Through substance I think a subject which is the predicate of no other. Here I have already come upon concepts. It is a concept that remains when I leave out everything else from the object. Every body has a force in itself, that is, a ground of action; this again is a concept. It has form, a multitude of parts, or it is a whole; and here I need not think space. Finally there remains the concept of a thing which is substance, has force, has parts, is a whole, and this presupposes no shape or figure.\n\nIn the end, therefore, pure intuition remains; and if that is left out, the pure concept. The latter is pure because it contains nothing empirical and also has no intuition; it is therefore transcendental. All pure concepts belong to the understanding. Intuitions underlie them; they supply the object. Our understanding reflects, but does not intuit.\n\nWhat are intuitions? They are nothing but ways in which our senses are affected by the object. We have no *intellectus archetypus*, which would be the producing cause of things, so that the object would arise together with the representation. Since there is none, how can we represent to ourselves objects which the understanding does not produce? Every representation must agree with its object; otherwise it is no knowledge. This agreement is possible in two ways: either my representation produces the object, or the object produces my representation. Now our knowledge is not of such a kind that it itself produces objects. There remains, then, only that the things themselves produce the knowledge. These are therefore modes of knowledge which rest on the way in which we are affected by objects. On this all our knowledge rests. Were we affected by no thing, we could have no concept of any thing.\n\nThe property of the power of representation which shows us how we are affected by things is sensibility. Sensibility is therefore a receptivity (*Empfänglichkeit*), in accordance with which we are affected by things. Consequently all intuitions are sensible. For if nothing affects us, we also have no representation; if we are to know objects which are given to us, we can never have the slightest representation of them unless they affect us. Empirical intuitions therefore belong to sensibility, and even pure intuitions will belong to it. For example, the empirical intuition of a body contains warmth and cold; but if we take merely the space, the extension, of the body, this, as pure intuition, will be nothing other than the form of our sensibility, [the form] of being affected by things through outer circumstances. Space is therefore nothing but the form of outer intuition.\n\nIt is the same with time. We find in our soul alterations and inclinations of the will. When we take all these together, there is always a connection present, in that either something is simultaneous with another or one thing follows upon another. Time is therefore being simultaneous and being successive, and is nothing but the form of inner intuition, or of inner sense.\n\nThe pure form of sensibility, then, precedes all experience. We can know nothing of sensations *a priori*; who, for example, can tell by looking at the aloe that it is bitter? But we can know *a priori* how we shall be affected by things that are mere shapes. For example, if I leave out everything empirical from a cube, I still retain the shape; and if I look at it from a corner I see three squares. That I can know *a priori*, for it is pure intuition. There are, therefore, modes of knowledge *a priori* which are possible in respect of pure intuition. We find in our sensibility, then, a rule by which we are capable of intuitions *a priori*, though of nothing more, to be sure, than the form of intuitions.\n\nOn the other side we also have pure concepts of reason [*Vernunft Begriffe*]. Every object of experience must be a subject of a substance. We can therefore think various concepts *a priori* through the mere understanding, for mere thinking can be considered apart from all pure intuition, apart from all intuition and sensible sensation. The properties of things, so far as they are objects of pure thought, I can know *a priori* just as well as the objects of pure intuition. Here it is clear that we are capable of no knowledge *a priori* of things of experience, for everything rests on the form of sensible intuition. This form I can know *a priori*, for I can say something *a priori* about space without an object being present. I can say much *a priori* without experience, in so far as the objects of experience are thought by me; and if I did not think what I intuit, I could not say at all that I had experience. I have, then, intuition *a priori*. The intuitions are forms of sensibility which I can know beforehand; for before I am affected, the mind must represent to itself a form of how it is to be affected. But we should also get no concept of things which are intuited if we could not think any intuition. We can form concepts of things in general only through the understanding, even though no object is given, since we represent to ourselves only the way in which we can think the object.\n\nWe see, then, that at the basis of all experience there lies *a priori*, first, an [*Verstandes Anschauung*, \"intuition of the understanding\"], which is nothing but the form of our sensibility, through which it is possible for us to perceive anything, and [through which] all empirical intuitions are finite; and, secondly, pure concepts *a priori*. For since experience is not possible through perception alone, but concepts must be added to it, concepts *a priori* must lie at the basis, whereby I can bring perceptions under concepts. These, for example substance, lie at the basis of experience; and had we no concepts *a priori*, we should get none. — Sensations make no concept.\n\nThis knowledge *a priori* will therefore have two parts. The first contains the form of sensibility *a priori*; the second, the form of understanding or of thinking *a priori*. If, then, we wish to consider the first source of experience, we have two: the one is aesthetic, and shows what belongs to the senses and how we are affected by objects; the other is logical, and considers the form of thinking. We have, therefore, a transcendental aesthetic, which considers intuition *a priori* and the conditions of sensibility *a priori*, and through it we shall recognise the possibility of the aesthetic judgments. When we have gone through this synthetic knowledge *a priori* in its whole extent, we can say that we have principles *a priori* on which rests the possibility of experience, which extends to all objects. We shall show that they are certain, because experience is certain and rests on them. We shall be able to determine the limits of knowledge *a priori*. Since all modes of knowledge *a priori* have no significance except that they are conditions of the possibility of experience, they cannot hold beyond the field of experience. The endeavour of transcendental philosophy will therefore be to show that all our modes of knowledge *a priori* can extend no further than to objects of experience, and so to hinder our reason from overstepping the limits of experience and from venturing attempts which aim at possible objects of experience [sic]. —",["Several very long sentences are divided into shorter ones (e.g. the opening sequence on matter and form, and the 'We see, then…' sentence), as the method allows; the content is unchanged. Paragraph breaks are mine, for readability.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Empfindüngen' to Empfindungen, 'lOOOley' to 1000erlei ('a thousand sorts'), 'dei Aloe' to 'der Aloe', 'wrenn' to 'wenn', 'weide' to 'werde', and the stray '■' and quotation mark before 'Übereinstimmung' omitted.","'Erkenntniß' is rendered 'knowledge'; the plural 'Erkenntniße' as 'modes of knowledge'. 'Wahrnehmung' is rendered 'perception', 'Gestalt' 'shape', and 'Vorstellungskraft' 'power of representation'.","'Eine Verstandes Anschauung' is doubtful: it stands oddly with the surrounding argument, which calls this the form of sensibility. I keep the words in brackets with the German, and the clause 'daß alle empirische Anschauungen endlich sind' is syntactically loose, so I supplied '[through which]'. The numeral '2' is read as 'secondly' (zweitens).","Supplied in brackets: '[the form]' in the sentence on space, and the German and Latin tags where the sense turns on them. The phrase 'wir von Dingen afficirt zu werden durch äußere Umstände' is rendered literally and is somewhat loose in the German.","'aesthetischen Urtheile' ('aesthetic judgments') is kept as written; it may be a slip for synthetic judgments, but the transcript is not emended.","The last clause, 'Versuche zu wagen, die auf mögliche Gegenstände der Erfahrung abzwecken', is translated as it stands. The sense seems to require 'nicht mögliche' (not possible objects), so the text may be corrupt or the student may have slipped. I marked it [sic].","The passage ends with a dash, as in the source; 'heißt' is rendered 'is entitled'. Editorial apparatus and page numbers are absent from the supplied text, so none is carried over."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-013":["The manner in which we are affected by things makes sensible representation possible. All sensible intuition has a certain form which is peculiar to human nature. Outer sensible intuition has the form that all outer things appear to us in space. That is a particular way in which we intuit things. Space is no thing in itself, but a form of sensibility, such that relations arise out of the representations, and these are the relations of space. Time also is the form of pure intuition; it is nevertheless not something that we know immediately; and in accordance with this twofold form of sensibility all representations are possible. These forms we must know beforehand, before we have impressions. We shall therefore have intuitions *a priori* without objects being given to us. We need have nothing but the form of intuition, and this rests not on the things but on ourselves.\n\nAccording to the difference of subjects, the same things will affect them in different ways. The raven, for instance, is agreeably affected by a rotting carcass, while we run away from it. Each subject has its own way of being affected. Its representation therefore does not rest on the object, but on the particular mode of intuition. Our human nature is of such a kind that, when we are affected by outer things, it represents them to us in space. This form of intuition alone can be considered *a priori*, because it underlies every representation and therefore precedes them. In the same way time can be considered *a priori*; that is, the form through which we know our inner state. All this is possible because our intuition is sensible. It rests upon receptivity (*Receptivität*, *Empfänglichkeit*), the receptivity to being affected by things.\n\nWe can think of a being that intuits through spontaneity, from its own power, through itself, without being affected by objects. This is how God is represented. How this is possible we cannot understand, unless perhaps in this way, that [his] knowledge by its power produces the things; for then he would affect them, not they him. Of this, however, we have no concepts. Our intuition is receptivity.\n\nBy intuition we understand sensible intuition through all the organs, not merely through sight. In every way in which we are affected there are two constituents: matter, that is, the impression of sensation [*Empfindungs Eindruck*]; and form, that is, the manner in which the impressions are united in my mind. Otherwise I should have millions of impressions, but no intuition of a whole object. Intuition rests on our nature just as sensation does; and sensation presumably differs in each person, although we have the same words for it.\n\nAll objects of our intuition are appearances. We never see things as they are, but as they present themselves to our senses. If a being had intellectual intuition, as we think of God as having, it would intuit beings as they are, not as they appear. Our intuitive representations are only representations of the appearances of things. We therefore know only the appearances of things.\n\nTo our experience there belong, then, first, intuition; second, thinking, which does not belong to the senses. To intuit is not to have experience. Experience is a mode of knowledge which we have of the object of intuition. Thinking therefore belongs to it, and this can be considered separately. Thinking makes up one part of experience, in so far as the understanding has a share in it. Intuiting is a part of experience, in so far as sensibility has a share in it. We can leave out everything empirical, and pure intuition then remains, that is, space and time. If we leave this out too, pure thinking remains. When we think objects in general, and the conditions under which they are, we call this pure concepts of the understanding, or categories.\n\nThey derive from Aristotle. But since he had no principle by which to enumerate them with certainty, and could not deduce whence we have them, they finally fell into decay, because there was no certainty to be had here. He also called them *praedicamenta*. We wish to sketch in advance a system of the categories, which exhausts all the concepts that the understanding employs in experience. (The inquiry how synthetic modes of knowledge *a priori* are possible is entitled the critique of reason.) Every system of knowledge is entitled dogmatic if no critique precedes it. We can therefore think a dogmatic metaphysics, and all metaphysics has so far been of that kind. All dogmatic procedure without critique, which might also be called anti-critical procedure, is however a hazardous venture of reason.\n\nThis question divides into two: first, how are speculative concepts possible *a priori*? second, how are judgments? The concept of ground is a synthetic concept, for by it I think something upon which something else follows according to a rule. Before we can answer this question (namely, how our reason can connect a manifold without the rule of identity) we must set out the table of the concepts *a priori* which everywhere lie at the foundation. First that of the judgments; with this all division of concepts is connected.",["Restated several long run-on sentences as shorter ones and added paragraph breaks; the text has none.","'Die Art, wie wir von Dingen afficirt werden' etc.: 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Empfänglichkeit' = receptivity, with the German/Latin forms given where the transcript doubles them.","'Die müßen wir zuvor erkennen': 'Die' taken as referring to the two forms (space and time); 'Eindrücke' = impressions.","'denn afficirte er sie, nicht sie ihn': 'he' supplied as God; '[his]' in 'his knowledge' is my gloss, and the sentence is obscure in the transcript.","'Wir dürfen nichts als die Form der Anschauung haben': rendered 'We need have nothing but…'; 'dürfen' may be a slip for 'dürfen' in the sense 'need'.","'Vorkommen' (capitalised in OCR) read as 'vorkommen'; 'ltens/2tens' read as 'first/second'; 'Untersucliung' corrected to 'Untersuchung'; stray 'j' after 'ltensj' dropped.","'Wie sind speculative Begriffe, 2tens Urtheile möglich a priori': the verb is shared; I supplied 'how are judgments' for clarity.","The passage breaks off mid-division; the OCR 'Erst der Urtheile' is translated as it stands, and 'Erkenntnis' is rendered 'knowledge' / 'mode of knowledge'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-015":["4)",["A bare numeral (heading of the fourth class of the table); translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-016":["Modality: problematic, assertoric, apodeictic. There are therefore as many categories as there are moments of the understanding in judging. By this table we gain the advantage that we see the origin of the categories. Logic deals with the connections of concepts; metaphysics with their origin. We see from this that the understanding performs the same action when it makes concepts for itself as when it connects them.",["'aßertorische' corrected to 'assertoric'; 'Modalitaet' = modality.","'Verknüpfungen' rendered 'connections'; 'Vortheil' as 'advantage'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-017":["All actions of the understanding reduce to judgments. As many moments as each judgment has, so many moments must there be in respect of the thinking of objects in general; that is, as many moments of judgments as there are, so many categories must we have.",["'Handlungen' rendered 'actions'; 'obiecte überhaupt' = objects in general."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-019":["*Omnitudo*, limitation, *actio mutua* or *commercium*.",["Fragment of the table of categories (probably quantity, quality, relation); the preceding items are not in this passage. Latin terms kept in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-020":["4)",["A bare numeral; translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-021":["Modality: *possibilitas*, *existentia*, *necessitas*.",["Latin spellings 'poßibilitas', 'neceßitas' normalised."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-022":["These categories may be entitled *praedicamenta*. Under these fundamental concepts stand derived ones, which are entitled *praedicabilia*. Under *omnitudo* lie whole [*Ganzes*] and perfection; under *multitudo*, *numerus*; *infinitudo* [lies] under *ordo*, etc. And so we obtain a complete system of pure thinking, without regard to the intuition that is to be thought.\n\nSpace, time and sensation belong to sensibility, and so not to the categories. We therefore divide transcendental philosophy into two parts. The first is transcendental aesthetic, that is, *scientia sensuum*, the science of sensibility and its representations *a priori*. The science of sensible modes of knowledge *a priori* deals, then, with nothing but space and time, for there are no other sensible modes of knowledge. It is therefore an entirely separate part. The second is transcendental logic, which contains pure thinking *a priori*, or the pure form of the understanding.\n\nThis transcendental logic differs from the common logic in the following way. The latter concerns knowledge without troubling itself whether the objects are *a priori* or *a posteriori*; the former considers the modes of knowledge of the understanding in so far as they cannot be *a posteriori*, or the possibility of pure knowledge of the understanding *a priori*. To this [logic] belong the categories.\n\nAristotle had ten (many are ours): 1. substance and accident; 2. *qualitas*; 3. *quantitas*; 4. relation; 5. *actio* [and] *passio*; 6. *quando* (belongs to time); 7. *ubi*; 8. *situs* (these two belong to space); 9. *habitus* (that is, skill for something, in that a thing is receptive of a form; *habilitas*).",["The sentence on derived concepts ('unter ordo etc') is elliptical and probably corrupt in the transcript; 'infinitudo' placed under 'ordo' follows the wording but the grouping is doubtful.","'Ganzes' given in German as the sense turns on it; 'gemeine Logik' rendered 'common logic'.","'hieher 35 gehören': '35' is a stray line number and is omitted.","'einganz' read as 'ein ganz'; 'Actio paß io' read as 'actio passio'; '(i. Quando' read as '6. Quando'. I supplied '[and]' between actio and passio.","'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge'; the passage breaks off after item 9 (the tenth, *positio*/other, is not in the text)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-023":["In making trials he found that something was still lacking to him, and so he adopted four further *postpraedicamenta*: *oppositum*, *prius et posterius*, *simul*, *motus* (which is altogether empirical), and *habere* (which also cannot be a category). By means of this table we gain the advantage that we do not gather up concepts at random; here, on the contrary, we can count off the elements of our pure understanding, so that no gap is left. (We also have the proper order. We shall prefer to begin with quantity, because its concepts are the clearest, rather than with modality, as the author does; the latter are much harder, yet here we must follow him.) The *praedicabilia* are likewise pure concepts of the understanding, but derived from the categories. The concept of the whole is a *praedicabile* which stands under the category of *omnitudo*, and so we can have many predicates under this category. When one speaks nowadays of categories, *praedicamenta* and *praedicabilia*, one seems to be warming up the old scholastic philosophy. In fact, however, nothing is left of Aristotle but the names. His design, to enumerate the pure concepts of reason and bring them under a register, was an excellent one and worthy of a philosopher, but it did not succeed. In every system the slightest deficiency shows itself as a gap, because an idea of the whole underlies it. In a heap of sand we do not notice it if a few grains are missing, but in a pyramid we notice it at once. The same holds when we draw up a system of the categories. We can then know which are the concepts that make up the whole extent of pure reason.\n\nWhat are categories? They are pure concepts of reason, that is, concepts which are wholly *a priori*. A concept of experience has its seat in the understanding just as the category has; take, for example, the concept of body. In it, however, there is still much that is empirical, that is, what contains nothing further than sensation; and there is also pure intuition, such as space, which, as has been said, can be represented *a priori*. If we leave all this out, substance, force and so forth remain. The pure concept of reason lies in experience, where I must set aside everything else that belongs to sensibility. This is, first, sensation; second, intuition, through which the object appears. Nothing then remains here but the pure thinking of the appearance, and these are the categories. They are thus the pure thinking of objects, so far as these are given through intuition. Our experience consists in intuiting and thinking. Empirical intuition is the manner in which we are affected by things. Through it nothing is given but appearance, which can be considered in respect of matter and form. Space and time belong to the Aesthetic. Thinking also belongs to experience; the concept *a priori* through which alone thinking is possible in experience is the category. Here we abstract from the difference of all objects of experience, in respect both of sensation and of intuition. The categories are preconditions of appearances, through which concepts of experience arise, although they are not themselves concepts of experience.\n\nOur common language already contains all that transcendental philosophy extracts with labour. These categories are all already contained in us, for without them no experience would be possible. For example: \"Snow has fallen.\" This implies that snow is, [which is] substance; \"fallen\" signifies an accident; \"on the earth\" signifies an influence, that is, *actio*, and so belongs to causality. \"Today\" relates to time, \"fallen\" to space. If we leave out all sensations, and also space and time, there remains substance which acts in a certain way; these must therefore be combined, so that the concept of experience arises. Suppose we had no such pure concepts of the understanding: we could then neither think nor speak at all.\n\nExperience is the ordering of appearances and of the concepts; the concepts are categories, and they are therefore the conditions of the possibility of experience, in so far as thinking belongs to it, just as space and time are the conditions of the possibility of experience in so far as intuition belongs to it. Categories are pure concepts of the understanding without which there would be no concepts of experience, and consequently no experience. Through them the intuitions are brought into a concept of experience, and impressions of sensibility must then still be added.\n\nThe transcendental Aesthetic contains the elements of our modes of knowledge that lie in sensibility; the transcendental Logic, the elements of our knowledge that lie in the understanding. It is divided into transcendental Analytic and transcendental Dialectic, as in general logic. The former contains all pure modes of knowledge of the understanding, so far as they hold only of objects of experience, and is entitled the logic of truth of the pure concepts of the understanding; it has to do with modes of pure knowledge of reason which relate only to objects of some possible experience. The transcendental Dialectic contains pure concepts of reason, so far as they are supposed to go further than to objects of all possible experience, that is, beyond the limits of experience, and this is the logic of illusion (*Schein*). For they cannot go beyond experience, since they have no other significance than that through them experiences arise from given appearances. They have truth in so far as they relate to objects of possible experience, for they are nothing but the thinking which must be added to appearance in order that experience may come about. They are nothing but concepts through which given appearances are expounded, that is, made into a concept which is a concept of experience. In so far as a part of transcendental philosophy contains nothing but the principles of possible experience, it is a logic of truth. The proposition *a priori* which precedes all experience is certain; for what is more certain than experience? and only so far as it concerns experience is it certain.\n\nThe understanding is here in a quite peculiar position. With the very concepts which it employs to make experience possible, it tries to soar beyond experience. What results? They must lose all significance; for since beyond the limits of experience the categories have no object, all knowledge through categories, when it goes beyond experience, is illusion, and the Dialectic is the logic of illusion. As is easily seen, we shall not be showing how to contrive an illusion, but how the illusion can be detected; and the Dialectic is the greatest aim of transcendental philosophy. The Analytic, however, must come first. We shall analyse the concepts and here proceed dogmatically. When we come to the synthetic propositions, we shall show that what is supposed to be proved from pure concepts alone can be proved only in reference to possible experience. The Dialectic we shall leave out here, and reserve what belongs to it for places where it can suitably be inserted; and here we shall be practised in the critique.",["Long passages were divided into shorter paragraphs and sentences; the transcript's dashes were mostly replaced by sentence breaks.","\"Es ist Schnee gefallen\" example: \"Hierin liegt, daß Schnee ist, Substanz\" rendered with bracketed [which is] for clarity. The next clauses are loosely arranged in the transcript ('auf die Erde' is taken as the ground of the influence); \"gefallen auf den Raum\" is as given.","\"Der Reine Vernunft Begriff\" / \"Reine Vernunft Begriffe\" for the categories is the student's wording (elsewhere Verstandesbegriffe); kept as \"pure concept(s) of reason\".","\"Das ist ltens Empfindung, 2 Anschauung\": OCR for \"1stens\" and \"2\"; rendered 'first… second'.","\"Erfahrung ist die Anordnung der Erscheinungen und der Begriffe\": text as transmitted; sense somewhat loose.","\"Der Satz a priori … ist gewiß, denn was ist gewißer als Erfahrung, und nur um so weit willen ist sie gewiß\": 'sie' is ambiguous; taken as the proposition, certain only so far as it concerns experience.","\"Es ist hier ganz besonders mit dem Verstand\" is elliptical; rendered by judgment as 'in a quite peculiar position'.","\"wrie\"/\"wrir\" are OCR slips for \"wie\"/\"wir\", silently corrected; \"Aesthetic\" and \"Logic\" etc. capitalised as titles of parts. *Schein* rendered \"illusion\"; Erkenntnisse as \"modes of knowledge\" where plural."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-024":["Let us now return to our author. We cannot possibly proceed according to a system without laying a foundation beneath it. Our author, like all the others, had the intention of sketching a system, but he wholly lacked freedom [of thought], or the principle for ordering the manifold. In general the idea of metaphysics was lacking: it was no system, but an aggregate. Hence the author also cannot give an account whether these were all the pure concepts. — Since, then, we have no system, we shall analyse all the concepts which he presents, even though they do not have the order of the categories. Meanwhile we shall appeal to the categories, and note under which of them this or that concept belongs.\n\nIn the Critique of Pure Reason, which Professor Kant has published, the extent of pure reason is analysed, and the idea is established from which all concepts of the understanding can be classified. The concepts themselves are not all analysed there, for that was not necessary: since our only concern was to determine the extent and the limits of pure reason, the parts could be omitted. They are analysed only so far as was necessary in order to comprehend them in the system of pure reason. We must now undertake this work of analysis, though not in the order of the categories. We shall, however, refer to them.\n\nWhat we have before us is analytic, and here something essential at once fails us, namely a principle of all synthetic propositions *a priori*. It may be said that the sum of metaphysics amounts to showing the possibility, and the criterion of the truth, of all synthetic propositions *a priori*. A principle of all synthetic judgments *a posteriori* is experience; but that of synthetic judgments *a priori* we do not yet have. All the concepts we have contain a synthesis, and if we have seen how this is possible, we shall soon also have a criterion of truth.\n\nWe shall criticise our author, who sets forth synthetic propositions *a priori*; yet strictly not him, for that would yield little profit, but the whole of human reason, for example in the proposition of sufficient ground. We shall show that it is impossible to prove such propositions *a priori* through pure concepts, and we shall assign to each of them a place in the system according to the categories.\n\nWe take first the concept. Here the author has followed Wolff, who saw well enough that the *principium contradictionis* is not sufficient, and therefore added to it the *principium rationis sufficientis*. Hence the author deals here at once with ground. We take first the concept [Begrif—]",["Restated for readability: the long run of sentences is divided into paragraphs, with no content added; the dash after 'pure concepts' is kept as in the source.","'Freiheit' in 'die Freiheit fehlte gänzlich' is rendered literally; the supplied '[of thought]' is a guess at the sense, and the reading may be doubtful (the transcript could mean freedom from the old schools).","'Hie Begriffe' is an OCR slip for 'Die Begriffe'; corrected silently. 'festzusezzen' etc. normalised.","'Critic der Reinen Vernunft' rendered 'Critique of Pure Reason'; 'Herr Professor Kant' kept as a third-person reference, as in the student's transcript.","'Analytic' is kept as 'analytic' (the Baumgarten-based division of the text); 'Urtheile' = judgments, 'Sätze' = propositions.","'criticise' for 'critisiren'; 'Grund' = ground; 'zureichender Grund' = sufficient ground.","The passage breaks off at 'Begrif' (Begriff); translated as it stands and not completed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-025":["Second Chapter",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-026":["On Ground and Consequence",["'Grund und Folge' rendered 'Ground and Consequence' (Latin *ratio* and *rationatum*, if wanted)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-027":["*Ratio*, *respectus* [relation], is a manifold in so far as, through one [member] of it, another is posited or removed. Hence every relation is a *relatio* either of *nexus* or of opposition. For example, the citizens of a republic stand in a relation of *nexus*, since through the one a benefit is posited for the other; or they stand in a relation of opposition, in so far as the interest of one conflicts with that of another. (The relation of ground and consequence, and conversely, is *nexus*. If one thing were not a ground of another, and this other its consequence, things would be completely separated. A thing as ground is related to its consequence, i.e. *nexus a posteriori*, and conversely, that is *nexus a priori*. Anything that stood in no such *nexus*, and so was neither ground nor consequence, would be isolated. That is an Italian word and means *isola*, an island. For we should not know at all how we had come by it, and should have no mark whereby to tell whether it belongs to our knowledge. Hence all our modes of knowledge are in *nexus*.) (*Nexus* and *oppositio* alike are either analytic, when the connection accords with the principle of identity, or synthetic, when it does not accord with it. *Oppositio* is analytic when it accords with the *principium contradictionis*, and synthetic when it does not. The latter may be entitled *nexus* and *oppositio realis*, the former logical. Connection and contradiction rest on grounds, and are logical or real. They may be *rationis ponendi*, where something is posited according to the rules of identity, or not according to them; or *tollendi*, where something is removed according to the principle of identity, or not. For example, extension is a ground of divisibility, and the latter is posited through the former according to the rules of identity. But in 'every body has attractive force', this force is posited through body, though not according to the rules of identity. The *nexus* here is real, in the former case logical. Real *nexus* can be known only *a posteriori* [text: 'ist nur Möglichkeit, a posteriori zu erkennen']. Conflict is logical when it occurs [?] the *principium contradictionis*, and real even without that. Logical *opposita*, *simul sumta dant nihil negativum*, i.e. the impossible. Really opposed things, *realiter opposita simul sumta dant nihil privativum*, i.e. lack, and this can certainly be thought. If two opposed grounds are in the same subject, the result is zero. If, therefore, there are two logically opposed grounds, the result is the impossible. What is logically opposed is that *quo posito tollitur aliud*; what is really opposed is that *quo posito tollitur ratio realem* [of the other], not the *aliud* itself.) We can regard opposition as the connection with the contrary. For example, if something is opposed to motion, it is connected with the rest of the body.\n\nBy *connexum* we shall understand every relation, *nexus* as well as *oppositum*, and we say: *connexa sunt, quorum uno posito ponitur aliud*. For example, when the horses move, the wagon too is moved. In any other connection there is a *respectus* of two, namely *unum, quod ponitur, et aliud, quod uno posito ponitur*. *A ponitur*, *B aliud quod posito A ponitur*; thus in every *nexus* there are two *correlata*. The *unum quo posito ponitur aliud* we call ground, the *aliud* consequence. *A est ratio, B rationatum*. In the relation of ground and consequence we distinguish the ground, namely that through which I posit something, from the consequence, which is that which is posited. By the definition I cannot distinguish ground and consequence, for I can just as well say: *posito B, ponitur A*. (We say this in hypothetical judgments: *posito antecedens, ponitur consequens*, but not conversely; rather: *posito consequens ponitur quod* [?] the antecedent.)\n\nWe must now return to logic, which contains the forms of our understanding without distinction of objects. There we see that from the ground we can infer to the consequence, but not conversely from the consequence to a determinate ground. The *nexus* of the consequence with the ground is certain, but not with the particular ground named. Our definition is now at once put in order: *Ratio est id, quo posito aliud determinate ponitur, rationatum quod non ponitur nisi posito alio*. One can also say: *rationatum est quo posito ponitur aliud*, but *indeterminate*. For if there is a consequence, there must always be a ground, and if something is a ground, there must always be a consequence. Only in the first case it is *indeterminate*, in the second *determinate*. In the case of many things we can see that they are a consequence, but we cannot know the ground.\n\nWe now have a criterion of ground, namely: *quo posito determinate ponitur aliud*. *Determinate* means *secundum regulam generalem*. Every ground yields a rule, and hence the *nexus* of ground and consequence is necessary. (A logical ground is a mode of knowledge on which another follows according to a rule. For example, necessity is the ground of unchangeableness. The one is not the cause of the existence of the other. A real ground is the thing on which another follows.) If something is posited, something else is thereby posited according to a universal rule. The expression 'consequence' is indeterminate, and is often used of time; we shall therefore often make use of the expression *rationatum*. So it signifies the connection with what precedes. (Ground contains that on which [something] follows according to a universal rule.) For example: he always had the fever, but when he took cinchona it disappeared; so cinchona is the ground of the fever's disappearance. The criterion of ground is not that something follows upon it, but that something follows according to universal rules.\n\nTo what [class] does the concept belong? To the category of relation. [Objection:] But to presuppose the concept of ground to the concept of cause would make it the *praedicamentum* of the latter, and have we not left it out of the table of categories? *Responsio*: The two concepts of ground and consequence are logical, but not transcendental. Cause and effect are things. Cause is that from which the existence of another follows. In logic existence is not spoken of at all; what is considered is not how one thing is the ground of another thing, but how one concept is the ground of other concepts.",["Very long passage divided into several sentences; the parenthetical series is kept as Kant's student has it, but its long sentences are restated more shortly.","Supplied in brackets: 'relation' for *respectus*; 'member'; 'of the other' after *ratio realem* (the Latin 'realem' is ungrammatical, perhaps an OCR or transcript slip, and the antecedent is a judgment); 'Objection' before 'But to presuppose…', since the German question is a pupil's objection answered by *Responsio*.","Erkenntniß = knowledge; plural 'modes of knowledge'. Verhältniß and *relatio* both given as 'relation'; Folge = consequence; Grund = ground; Wiederstreit = conflict.","'Der reale nexus ist nur Möglichkeit, a posteriori zu erkennen' is probably corrupt (perhaps 'nur möglich'); I give the evident sense and mark it.","'ob principium contradictionis' is doubtful (OCR or transcript; perhaps 'nach' or 'ohne'); translated with [?].","'posito consequens ponitur quod dem anteceden«' is garbled; translated as far as it can be made out, with [?]. 'posito alia' silently read as *alio*.","Silent OCR corrections: 'LTnterschied' → Unterschied; 'Grandes' → Grundes; stray 'I' after 'Die Opposition' and a stray hyphen before 'sed' dropped; 'Mangel' = lack (privation).","*Nihil negativum* and *nihil privativum*, *praedicamentum*, *determinate/indeterminate* left in Latin as technical terms."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-028":["We have here spoken of a relation of concepts in which one can infer determinately from the one to the other. But that there are such things as allow me to infer from one to the other belongs to metaphysics. Let us now consider the possibility of the real ground (*Realgrund*): that, when one thing is posited, another is thereby posited as determined, and this not in the logical manner, in which I can infer from one concept to another. If the thing to be inferred is really (*realiter*) distinct from the other, then no human reason can see how it is possible that one thing should be the ground of another thing. Experience teaches that it is so, but reason cannot make it comprehensible to us.\n\n(All grounds are either *rationes cognoscendi*, when one mode of knowledge is a ground of others (for example, 'composite' is a ground of knowing 'divisible'); or, when one sees a man's footprints, one says that men have been there. The footprint is therefore a ground of knowledge, not a *ratio fiendi*; otherwise the footprints would have to be the ground of the man's existence. A *ratio essendi* is the ground of what belongs to a thing when it is considered according to its possibility. For example, the three sides in a triangle A are the ground of its three angles; here I speak merely of the possible triangle A. Considered in actuality, for example, ink and pen are the *ratio fiendi* of the A [i.e. of the letter A, as written]; and the *ratio fiendi* is cause.)\n\nBut such grounds must be really, not merely covertly, distinct; otherwise they are after all one and the same. If, for example, I posit A and B is nevertheless posited, this is not to be understood at all. Yet I can in fact infer in this way, or logic would be in the wrong. I cannot do so, however, with things that are really distinct. Things are logically distinct when they are in fact one and the same but the identity is concealed; and the connection (*nexus*) of ground and consequence is then analytic, according to the rule of identity. (What exists as *rationatum* is *dependens*.) For example, because something is a body, it is divisible.\n\nThe inference from ground to consequence which one has in general logic, which is a *nexus logicus*, is easily comprehended. But the real *nexus*, which is synthetic, is not comprehended at all, where the consequence is really distinct from the ground; for example, snow with salt causes frost. Here there is no *nexus* according to the rule of identity. A consequence is added to the ground which did not lie at all in the concept of the ground, and this synthetic *nexus* cannot be understood by any man's reason. It is a remarkable property of the understanding that it infers from ground to consequence, and yet cannot see the possibility of this; and there is more of the same kind to come.\n\nAll relation is, as was said, a *nexus oppositorum*. Let us first think of a logical opposition; it is analytic. Real opposition is synthetic. Logical opposition is contradictory (*contradictoria*). A square circle [*eckigter Zirkel*, literally 'angular circle'] is a contradiction. Two logical *opposita* wholly cancel each other, and nothing remains (*nihil negativum*). Two real *opposita* do not cancel each other; it is their consequences that cancel, and what arises from their combination is zero, nought, *nihil privativum*. For example, I receive an inheritance, which causes pleasure; I must pay off just as much in debts, which causes displeasure. The *opposita* can very well exist together; only the consequences cancel, and I remain in a state of equality. Two real *opposita* can therefore be in one thing at the same time, since only the consequences are cancelled; but logical *opposita* cannot be in one thing at the same time.\n\n(Logical *opposita* are either *contradictorie opposita*, as for example A and not-A, or *disparata*, as for example 'every body is either red or green'. The *disparata* contain, besides the contradictory opposition, something further that is added. For example, 'something is either red': the contradictory opposite would be 'not red'. In the disparate opposition there is something more besides, namely green. The *principium exclusi medii* does not say that one of two disparate predicates belongs to things, but [speaks] of contradictory opposition [*contra Opposition*; reading doubtful].)\n\nOne may say that the whole play of change in the universe comes from real oppositions. Since by these only consequences are cancelled, the same thing is not cancelled, but another, namely the consequence. Real oppositions can therefore exist together. We come to the concept of",["Restated a few long sentences as several (e.g. the opening of the *Realgrund* discussion and the *ratio cognoscendi* parenthesis); the transcript's parentheses are kept as separate paragraphs.","'Erkenntniß' in 'wenn eine Erkenntniß ein Grund von andern ist' rendered 'mode of knowledge'; 'Erkenntniß Grund' as 'ground of knowledge'.","The *ratio cognoscendi* parenthesis is broken in the transcript: the 'either … or' is not properly completed, and the example 'zusammengesetzt … theilbarem' is awkward. I have translated it as it stands, with the sense of 'ground of knowing' supplied.","'die 3 beiten im A' is OCR/transcript garble, probably 'Seiten' (sides) of a triangle A; I silently read 'sides' and supplied 'triangle' in the sense of the example. Likewise 'Tinte und Feder ist ratio fiendi des A': the letter A is my bracketed gloss.","'ratio eßendi' = *ratio essendi*; 'rationatum' and 'dependens' are left in Latin as technical terms.","'Realgrund' for 'real Grund' is a technical coinage in italics; 'eckigter Zirkel' glossed in brackets.","'sondern contra Opposition' near the end of the second parenthesis is doubtful (possibly 'contradictorie Opposition'); I translate what can be made out and bracket the doubt.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('Wir kommen zum Begriff der'); it continues in the next passage, 'Möglichkeit und Unmöglichkeit'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-029":["possibility and impossibility",["Completes the previous sentence: 'We come to the concept of possibility and impossibility.' Here a heading-like fragment, translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-030":["The author speaks first of impossibility, then of something (*Etwas*) and of the possible.",["'Der Autor' is Baumgarten, the textbook author; 'Etwas' is rendered 'something' (*aliquid*)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-031":["Remark. When two concepts are opposed to each other, as here [for example] the possible and the impossible, they always stand under a higher concept; for opposition always represents a disjunctive proposition. Now there must be a divided concept which has the opposed concepts as members of the division, and this is a higher concept. What is possible or impossible? The object. (Yet one can also think an object with impossible predicates.) The object is accordingly presumably the highest concept in ontology. The possible we call thing, something [*Etwas*], and to this the impossible, nothing, is opposed. But nothing is opposed to the object; hence it would seem to be a still higher concept than something. For example, by 'thing' one understands: 1. object in general; 2. the possible; 3. the positive, or reality; 4. that which is actual.)\n\nPossibility is wrongly defined if one says: the possible is that which contains no contradiction. What contradicts itself is impossible, but not conversely. The thought of it is possible, but whether the thing is objectively possible is not yet certain. That whose thought does not contradict itself is thereby not [shown to be] possible, nor yet impossible. It is difficult to define possibility so that it applies at once to things and to thoughts. What agreed with all possible rules of thinking would be possible; but then one would have to know all possible rules.\n\n[The following sentence is too garbled to render with confidence:] No possible is that of which the thought of a possible metaphysics is possible. What is such that the thought of it is possible, but the thing in and for itself, without regard to experience, is not possible (for example, a four-cornered circle), is logically impossible, for the thought is impossible. Logical possibility is possibility of the concept, and the principle of contradiction (*principium contradictionis*) is a sufficient criterion of it. Different from this is real possibility, for which the principle of contradiction does not suffice. What is logically impossible is also really impossible; but what is logically possible is not therefore also really possible.\n\n(The impossible is twofold: I. when either the concept itself is nothing, for example the four-cornered circle; II. or when no possible object corresponds to it, for example fairy tales.)\n\nLogical possibility is that in which there is no contradiction. Metaphysical possibility obtains when the thing is possible in and for itself, without relation to my thoughts. No man can have insight into this. How am I to judge of a thing what it is in and for itself, without relation to experience? The possibility of the thought does not constitute the possibility of the thing. We know the thing itself solely through concepts, and then no mark of possibility remains to us. Logic we can have insight into, but that settles nothing in regard to the thing.\n\nWhat does not conflict with the laws of experience is physical possibility; this one can indeed have insight into. For example, that a great palace should have been finished in four weeks is physically impossible. Morally possible is that which is possible according to the rules of morals and does not conflict with the universal law of freedom.\n\nThese distinctions need to be noted. Many philosophers have confounded logical with metaphysical possibility. For example, no logical philosophy can demonstrate the possibility or impossibility of ghosts; yet no reasonable man ought to believe in them, because he has no concept of their possibility or impossibility. — One must not build on experience here, for those who have such experiences are commonly weak people, and they experience much that is not true. —",["Restated for clarity: the parenthetical about 'object' and the list of senses of 'Ding' are kept in their places; the closing parenthesis after 'wirklich ist' has no opening mark in the OCR text, so it is retained as printed.","'Etwas' kept in German beside 'something'; 'Gegenstand' = object, 'Ding' = thing.","'v.v.' (vice versa) expanded as 'conversely'.","'Wovon der Gedanke sich nicht widerspricht, das ist nicht möglich, auch nicht unmöglich': the German as printed reads flatly 'is not possible'; I supplied '[shown to be]' to make the sense required by the argument (non-contradiction does not establish possibility). Possible transcript slip.","'Kein Mögliches ist das, wovon der Gedanke einer möglichen Metaphysic möglich ist' is garbled or corrupt in the transcript/OCR; rendered literally and flagged, not repaired.","'ohiective' silently read as 'objective'; 'Wiederspruch' and 'pur' (= purely, 'solely') normalised; 'Feen Märchen' rendered 'fairy tales'.","'Merkmal' rendered 'mark'; 'einsehen' rendered 'have insight into'; 'Sitten' rendered 'morals'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-032":["If a synthesis is thought in the concept, and not according to the principle of contradiction or of identity, it cannot be known at all by the principle of contradiction. For it is not logical but real, and here we have no logical mark of possibility. For example, 'Every body is extended' can be known according to the principle of contradiction. A body that is not extended is an absurdity [*Unding*], or impossible. Here we know the possibility *a priori*, but analytically; and the possibility of an analytic ground can easily be seen *a priori*. For example, every body has changeability. The analytic ground is this: a body is a multitude of parts that can be separated; the concept of body therefore contains the analytic ground of change. The possibility of an analytic ground and of its consequence flows from the principle of contradiction. But if I think a ground whose consequence is really [*realiter*] different from it, this cannot be known from the principle of contradiction. — It is not in the least self-contradictory that, because A is posited, B is posited also. But we cannot on that account assume it to be possible, since it is a synthetic judgment and we have not the slightest concept of how this comes about. — Real possibility cannot be seen *a priori* without pure concepts. We shall see in what follows that the criterion of the possibility of things is this: the synthesis which contains the conditions of experience is possible. But this applies only to the objects of experience.\n\n(If something is considered apart from the *nexus*, one says: *spectatur per se* or *interne*; of something in the *nexus* one says: *interne spectatur*.) — Something is possible inwardly, that is, in and for itself, and [it is possible] relatively, in relation and connection with other things. (The internally impossible is a *nihil negativum*; a nothing arising through a mere lack of something is called a nothing. — Many things are internally possible which are not externally possible, in the connection; that is, [they are] also conditionally possible, the condition here being equivalent to a ground. For example, it is possible in itself that a man may become rich, but also conditionally possible, for his parents are rich; that is, there is in addition a ground for it. — To take 'condition' in this way is not in accordance with linguistic usage. Strictly it signifies a restriction; for example, many a commander can give orders [only] if the people agree to it.)\n\nThe inner criterion of things is the principle of contradiction, but it is far from sufficient. [Possibility] in the relative sense is the possibility of a thing in relation to its grounds or consequences. For example, it is possible that a man should attain great riches, but on account of laziness, unfitness and want of rich relatives it is impossible. — What contradicts the conditions under which something is possible is hypothetically impossible; what contradicts itself is absolutely impossible. Something may be possible in itself and yet hypothetically impossible, whether under its logical or under its real hypothesis. Hypothetical impossibility tacitly presupposes absolute possibility; for what in itself is nothing at all can [only] be considered in a logical relation.\n\nWe now come to the famous principle of sufficient reason (which is the first synthetic proposition *a priori*). The author is speaking here not of existing but of possible things. Expressed in its whole generality it runs: *nihil est sine ratione*. Here we may substitute other words: *id quod habet sine rationem aliquam est rationatum*; thus *omne possibile est rationatum* is entirely the same. (So the author expresses it; yet he afterwards asserts a being which is ground but not consequence. He then helps himself out by saying that it has the ground in itself. That is absurd: the ground must always be something other, and if it is not, there is no ground. It is as if I say, 'I will something.' Why? 'Because I will it.' That is, there is as yet no ground at all; and the [*veile*] must be entitled *originarium*, as likewise the supreme being [is entitled] *ens originarium*. Now the proposition strikes us as very self-contradictory: we see nothing but consequences and no sufficient ground that would be merely a ground. The sum-total of all things would be something *quod non poneretur nisi posito nihil*. The ground of the possibility of all things would thus be an absurdity. We shall therefore have to make a restriction, namely: everything contingent has a ground. Contingent is that whose opposite is possible; and this we cannot know from pure concepts. The logically possible we can, but not the real. Logical contingency can be known from pure concepts, but the real possibility of the opposite can in no way be seen. Since the contingency of things cannot be known *a priori* through pure concepts, we must say that everything empirically contingent has its ground, that is, the contingent in appearance [*Erscheinung*]. Empirically contingent is that whose opposite is empirically possible, as experience shows that something is which was not before. 'Something happens' always signifies empirical contingency. Hence the proposition also runs: everything that happens has a ground. Happening contains within it an arising or a passing away; these are the *species*, that [happening] the *genus*.\n\nThis principle of sufficient reason is to be proved, but not from pure concepts. For it is a synthetic proposition, in which I go out beyond my concept in order to add another that was not contained in the concept given to me. Yet we do find in ourselves a certain partiality for this proposition as the author presents it. For every assertion that is groundless is false. Here lies the illusion. If we must give a ground for everything that happens, it does not yet follow that things in themselves must have a ground; and it is here that men become confused. For example, the supreme being has no ground, and yet we must adduce grounds for [the fact] that it exists. The proposition may also be expressed thus: everything that follows in sensibility, or in sensible intuition, follows in the concepts of the understanding. Or: what can be represented as consequence by sensibility can be represented as consequence by the understanding. This commends the proposition. But since, as just said, it cannot be proved from pure concepts, it will have to have another proof. — We shall see in what follows that synthetic modes of knowledge [*Erkenntniße*] *a priori* have validity only in so far as they are principles of the possibility of experience.\n\n(All our judgments have a ground why the predicate belongs to the subject; in analytic judgments it is a ground of analysis, in synthetic judgments a ground of synthesis. — We have also judgments *a posteriori*. These are either judgments of perception or judgments of experience, and the latter always presuppose the former. [The reading before 'die erstem' is marked ■ in the text.] — The former have only subjective validity; I say, for example, 'I feel cold.' The latter have objective or universal validity. What has only subjective validity, then, if it is to have objective validity, or to be a judgment of experience, requires that the sequence of the perceptions be determined according to rules, that is, be necessary. Then, one says, there is in the object also a sequence, in that one thing follows upon another according to a rule; this is ground. The principle of sufficient reason is therefore the ground of the possibility of experience; without it there would be no experience.)\n\nHow is experience possible, or how does the understanding make out of perceptions modes of knowledge of things? It must have principles; these are synthetic propositions, and to them this proposition also belongs. (Perceptions can follow one another without the things on that account following one another. In a house, for example, I can perceive first the roof, then the foundation, then the windows, and so on, without the things following in this order; for they are simultaneous.) The proposition holds of all objects of experience, and we shall see that it cannot be proved otherwise than as a proposition holding of all objects of experience, but not beyond them. So it goes with all synthetic propositions *a priori*; and the whole fault of metaphysics consists in this, that propositions which concern only experience are used beyond it, although they hold only of all possible objects of experience, not of things in themselves. They are rules of the synthesis of appearances, by which experience alone is distinguished from dreaming; and so likewise is the *principium rationis sufficientis*.\n\nThe author has proved it, but in a very strange fashion. He argues: if a thing had no ground, its ground would be nothing; but then nothing would be the ground of something, and that is a contradiction. In this he has confused the logical and the metaphysical nothing, which can scarcely be forgiven him. The proof can easily be refuted by parodying it, that is, by proving from it something absurd. For example: you have money in the chest; for if you had not, there would be nothing of money in the chest, then nothing would be money; therefore you must have money. The fault is that *nihil* is taken once as a negation and another time as a concept. It is not the same whether I say *nihil est sine ratione* or *nihil est ratio*.\n\n(From a pure concept this cannot be proved; we must take a third [thing] with which the concepts agree, namely the concept of a possible experience. Empirical knowledge, in so far as it is considered as universally valid, that is, necessary, is entitled experience; empirical knowledge in so far as it is subjectively valid is entitled perception. The latter is indisputable, but only for me. For example, if the proposition 'Warmth follows upon sunshine' is to be a proposition of experience, it must run: for everyone, or necessarily, warmth follows upon sunshine. But what is that upon which something follows necessarily? A ground. If the above perception is therefore to become experience, I must represent to myself that the ground of the warmth lies in the sunshine; then it holds for everyone. These principles of the necessary connection of perceptions are synthetic principles *a priori*, and our proposition belongs to them; without it no experience can take place. If the above proposition is to become a proposition of experience, it must stand under the principle of the necessary connection of perceptions. The chief connection is that of ground and consequence. — If connections of things are to be universal, there must be a constant rule; otherwise I cannot say that the sequence of things is to hold universally, and then again it is not experience.",["The text breaks off at the end without a closing parenthesis (the parenthesis opened at '(Aus purem Begriff…' is never closed); I have closed nothing and added nothing, and the passage is untranslated beyond 'not experience.'","Long sentences are divided in places; the argumentative and parenthetical structure of Mrongovius's notes is otherwise kept. Words in square brackets are supplied to complete the sense or give the German/Latin.","OCR corrections made silently: 'Pertheylichkeit' read as *Partheylichkeit* (partiality); 'I olge ist. un hilft' as 'Folge ist. Nun hilft'; 'lenes' as 'jenes'; 'sme rationem' as 'sine rationem'; 'restiiction' as 'restriction'; 'beständigeRegel' as 'beständige Regel'.","'das veile muß man originarium nennen' is garbled (perhaps 'das Erste' or 'die Reihe'?); I have left *veile* untranslated in brackets rather than guess.","The parenthesis on *spectatur per se / interne* and 'in nexu … interne spectatur' looks corrupt or miswritten in the transcript (one would expect *externe* for the second); translated as it stands.","'Ding … Unding': rendered 'absurdity', with the German given; *Unding* literally 'non-thing'. *nihil negativum* is kept in Latin.","'Bedingung … restriction': Kant (as noted by the student) distinguishes the loose sense of 'condition' as ground from its proper sense as restriction; 'only' in the commander example is supplied in brackets.","A stray mark '■' after 'iene voraus' is an OCR artefact or an apparatus sign; I have indicated it in a bracket rather than dropping it silently. 'der Autor' is Baumgarten; 'Erkenntniße' rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s06-033":["We said above, however, that empirical modes of knowledge, or experiences, contain no necessity. Experience does not teach that anything is necessary, apart from all perceptions. — Experiences presuppose principles that are necessary, and under which alone experience is possible. Experience does not teach the necessity of things. — All principles of synthesis that I think *a priori* are principles of the possibility of experience. They apply solely to objects of experience, and yet they are *a priori*, because they precede all experience, which is possible only through them. They are also synthetic, just as experience is; for experience is nothing but a synthesis of perceptions, one that holds necessarily and that I can know through concepts *a priori*. A synthesis that is given in no experience is here simply impossible. But these synthetic principles *a priori* I can have insight into, and I do actually have insight into them, since otherwise there would be no experience. I can have insight into them because they are merely modes of knowledge that lie in my understanding. The author says that if everything has its ground and its consequence, then everything is connected *a priori* and *a posteriori* [*connex*]. That is correct, but it does not hold of all things, since otherwise there would be no highest ground.)",["The closing parenthesis ')' stands in the source without an opening one in this passage; it is carried over as it stands.","'Sie lehrt' / 'Sie setzen … voraus, … unter denen sie allein möglich ist': the number of the pronoun varies in the German; I take it to refer throughout to 'Erfahrungen' / experience, and have rendered it so for clarity.","'connex' is left as Kant's Latinism, with the English supplied in brackets; 'platt unmöglich' rendered 'simply impossible'.","'Erkenntniße' in the plural rendered 'modes of knowledge'; 'einsehen' rendered 'have insight into'.","'wirkleich' in the OCR corrected silently to 'wirklich'. 'Der Autor' is Baumgarten, the textbook author."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-001":["On the Sufficient and the Insufficient.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-002":["A ground is sufficient in two ways. First, we can represent the ground as a whole, as an aggregate; second, as a series. The completeness of a ground as aggregate makes it sufficient, and as series it is likewise sufficient. The ground by which everything in the consequence is determined is sufficient as aggregate; hence everything must have a sufficient ground. An insufficient ground is also true [*wahr*]; it is a part of the sufficient ground. The ground in a series of grounds subordinated to one another is sufficient only if we trace everything back to the first ground. It is commonly entitled sufficient when it is capable of enabling us to understand the consequences immediately from it.",["'Der Grund … ist zureichend als aggregat': I read this as the complete ground taken as an aggregate; 'wahr' kept literally though the sense may be 'real' (an insufficient ground is still a genuine ground).","'gemeinhin zureichend genannt' rendered 'commonly entitled sufficient', following the method's rendering of 'genannt werden'.","The sentence 'Wir können uns … Reihe' was divided into two sentences with 'First … second' for clarity."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-003":["The author proves the proposition that everything has its consequences in the same way as the proposition that everything has its ground; but it cannot be proved from pure concepts either. We consider grounds in a series when one is the *rationatum* of another. In a series there is always one ground that is immediate, and each is a ground of the consequence in part. — We can therefore represent grounds as complete, coordinated and subordinated. All our grounds are connections: coordination, that is, the connection of the parts in an aggregate; and subordination, that is, the connection of the parts in a series. A ground is sufficient *simpliciter* or *secundum quid*. Something is a mediate ground of something else if it is the ground by means of another; it is an immediate ground if it is the ground not by means of another. [The *ratio mediata* is] the *remota posita*, or the *ratiocinium* of something through many *rationes intermediae*. In the coordination of grounds one is the *complementum* of the other toward the sufficient ground; in subordination one ground is the consequence of the other. In a series there are *rationes immediatae*, *mediatae* and *simpliciter talis*. The coordination of grounds is the connection of them into one immediate sufficient ground; the subordination of grounds is the connection of them into a mediate sufficient ground.) (A ground can be sufficient with regard to its consequences and with regard to itself: with regard to its consequences, if it determines everything contained in them; with regard to itself, if it presupposes no other ground. If I take the sum total of all world-causes, they contain the complete ground of the present state of the world; but this sum is not sufficient for itself, that is, *simpliciter talis*, but only with regard to its consequences, that is, *secundum quid*. In the world we do not find *rationes simpliciter talis*. The relation of ground and consequence contains only logical inference, that is, if the one is posited, so is the other, and necessarily; and in so far it contains the ground of the possibility of connected perceptions, that is, of experience. In the series of subordinated concepts, what is sufficient in a certain respect is sufficient *secundum quid*, and what is sufficient in every respect, *simpliciter*. We must adduce the following *canones*:",["The passage breaks off at a colon; the *canones* follow in the next passage. Parentheses ')' and '(' are carried over as they stand; the first ')' has no opening in this passage.","'remota posita oder ratiocinium…' is elliptical; I supplied '[The *ratio mediata* is]' in brackets as the evident subject, and the reading is doubtful.","Long sentences were divided; 'Begriffe' in 'Reihe subordinirter Begriffe' is kept as 'concepts' though 'Gründe' may be meant (possible slip in transcript).","'sufficient' before 'secundum quid' in the German is Latin 'sufficient'; rendered as English 'sufficient'. 'Weltursachen' rendered 'world-causes'.","Latin technical terms (*rationatum*, *complementum*, etc.) kept in italics as instructed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-004":["*Posita ratione, ponitur rationatum* is already evident from the definition of the ground; further, *posito rationato, ponitur ratio quaedam non certa*. For example: if someone has been strangled, he is dead; but if he is dead, he need not have been strangled.",["'quaedam non certa' left in Latin: 'some ground, not a determinate one'. 'erwürgt' rendered 'strangled'.","'sein' in 'erwürgt sein' read as the infinitive of the transcript; no change of sense."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-005":["To determine is to attribute to a thing one of two contradictorily opposed, contrary predicates. I can compare anything with some possible opposite of a predicate, and with the predicate itself. If I attribute the predicate to the thing, the opposite is excluded. Of two opposed predicates (*praedicata opposita*) one must belong to the thing; hence every thing must be determinable. (The author's inferences belong to logic, where they all occur; only there the ground is entitled *antecedens* and the consequence *consequens*. *Sublato rationato tollitur ratio*: for if the ground remained, the consequence would remain also. But it is not the case that *sublata ratione tollitur rationatum*, for the consequence can have another ground.) (Since, of two contradictorily opposed predicates, one belongs to every consequence, every [thing] is thoroughly determined. Yet we do say that a thing is undetermined in respect of certain opposed predicates when, through the concept we have of it, neither of the two belongs to it. For example, every man who exists is either learned or unlearned, but through the concept I have of man it is undetermined whether he is learned or unlearned. There is only one being which is thoroughly determined through its concept, namely that to which all reality belongs. Otherwise things are, in respect of their concepts or through them, undetermined in many respects.)\n\nA universal concept is not in itself determined. For example, 'a man is either learned or unlearned' is undetermined; but 'a learned man' is determined. We can determine only through synthesis, not through analysis, for in respect of what lies in the concept it was not undetermined. Thus I can determine the concept only if I add something to it. Every ground of determination is entitled *ratio determinans*. Our knowledge, if it is to be determined, must have a ground; and so it is with things also. If two things are undetermined in respect of two predicates, they can be determined through a determining ground. Determination is to be distinguished from logical predicates. (The logical predicate can be analytic, but determination is always synthetic. For example, it is said that the concept of body is determined through extension. I determine the concept through a predicate if without that predicate it would have been undetermined. But the concept of body is not undetermined without extension, for extension already lies in its concept; heaviness, however, is a determination.) (Must every ground be determining, or only the sufficient one? Most people still make a distinction here, but that is false. A ground consists precisely in this, that *quo posito, determinate ponitur aliud*. The insufficient ground determines something in the consequence; the sufficient ground determines everything.)\n\n'A determination is a predicate of a thing whereby the opposite is excluded.' That is the usual way of thinking determination; that is, it is regarded as a predicate added to the thing, whereby the opposite is excluded. That is not enough. Determination is a synthetic predicate. For example, 'a body is extended': by this the body is not yet determined. A learned man is determined, for learnedness does not lie in the concept of man.\n\nA predicate belongs to a thing internally if it already lies in its concept, externally if it belongs to it in relation to others. (Internal predicates are those which belong to the thing in itself. They are otherwise also divided into absolute and respective. The latter are respective either *ad interna* or *ad externa*. The *respectus ad externa* is relation. For example, that a man is master of his passions is respective, but *ad interna*; here I consider the relation of a power to what is subjected to it. There is thus here a *respectus* [...]; it is respective *ad externa* when I consider him as master and subject.\n\nThe author explains what the same (*idem*) and the diverse (*diversum*) are: A and B are *eadem* in so far as the representation of B is nothing but a repetition of the representation of A, that is, if I can represent B along with it through the representation of A. We see, however, that two things which we call one and the same can be *eadem* only in some respect, since otherwise we could not say 'the other'. More on this will come up below.\n\nThe author speaks *de attributis, modis et affectionibus*; all this belongs to logic.)\n\nWe note the following. In most philosophies we find a fluctuating distinction between nature and essence. What is the difference? One is not accustomed to attribute nature to a thing which does not subsist for itself; for example, of an A one says that it has a nature. In the case of things that subsist for themselves, no distinction is made. Above we distinguished the analytic relation between ground and consequence, in which the ground is logical, from the synthetic relation between ground and consequence, in which the ground is a real ground. The relation of ground and consequence is analytic if the predicate can be known analytically, synthetic if it cannot be so known. That which is analytically first would be the ground of all the predicates of a thing, and is entitled the logical essence. That which is synthetically first would be the ground of all the predicates of a thing, and is entitled the real essence or nature.\n\n(The first inner ground of all that belongs to the possibility of a thing is essence; the first inner ground of all that belongs to its actuality is nature. For example, one will not ascribe nature to the A in geometry. In the concept of every thing there is always something separable from it and something inseparable. The first is said to be *ad possibilitatem rei seu ad essentiam pertinens*; the second is *extra essentiale*, what is not necessarily connected with the concept or possibility of the thing. These [two] again can be *ut rationis*, that is *essentia*, or *ut rationata*, that is *attributa*, [together] *complexus essentialium*. That is, the first true higher ground of all that belongs to the existence of a thing is nature.)\n\nOr, [put otherwise]: the first logical inner ground of all the determinations of a thing is essence; the first inner real ground of the determinations of a thing is nature. To find the complete inner real ground of all determinations is impossible. The logical ground is easy: I need only analyse my concept until I come to concepts which are no longer derived from others.\n\n(When we complain that we do not know the essences of things, this concerns only nature; essences we can know very well. For if something is not actual, it lies merely in my concept. If, then, I deal with what belongs to the possibility of a thing, I deal with what lies in my concept of the thing, and that I can surely know. If essence is divided, as [Professor?] Kant does, into logical and real essence, the latter is nature.)\n\n(The attribute which can flow only from the whole essence of the thing is *proprium*, for another thing would have another essence. That which is always a *consectarium* from a part of the essence is *commune*, for the part can occur in other things also. For example, man is a rational animal. By reason of the first he has reason, and in this he is not distinguished from spirit; by reason of the second he has senses, and in this again he is not distinguished from the animal.)\n\nBut what man will undertake to indicate the whole real essence of things that are given through experience? Comparatively, we may call something the real essence, or in a certain respect we may stop at the investigation of the real essence and be content with it. We often hear the complaint that the essences of things are unknown and that we know only the surface. That is quite correct, but holds only of the real essence. Much that belongs to it we can know, though not all. This real essence is nature.",["Restated as shorter sentences in places and the paragraphing is mine; the parentheses of the transcript are kept, since they mark the student's inserted glosses on Baumgarten.","Bestimmen/Bestimmung = determine/determination; unbestimmt = undetermined; durchgängig bestimmt = thoroughly determined; Wesen = essence; Natur = nature; Grund/Folge = ground/consequence. The Latin technical terms are left in Latin.","'Every [thing] is thoroughly determined': the German has 'iedes' with no noun; 'thing' is supplied. 'Of an A' ('von einem A') and 'the A in geometry' are as printed; the letter A is probably an OCR or transcript slip for another word, and I cannot tell which.","Garbled passage: 'Es ist also hier ein respectus ;' has a gap in the text, marked [...]. 'Die Deren können wieder seyn' is probably corrupt; I read 'Diese' and supplied '[two]' and '[together]' for 'complexus eßentialiam' (corrected to *essentialium*). The sense is uncertain.","'Avie H. P. Kant' is read as 'wie H. P. Kant'. The abbreviation 'H. P.' is unclear; I tentatively give '[Professor?]' and flag it as doubtful.","Silent OCR corrections: Polge→Folge; 'zureichen -de'→zureichende; 'Denn kann ich'→'dann kann ich'; 'das lte/2te'→das 1te/2te; 'Denn so etwas nicht wirklich ist'→'wenn etwas nicht wirklich ist'; eßentia, eßentiale, poßibilitatem→essentia, essentiale, possibilitatem.","'Oder der erste logische innre Grund…' is rendered 'Or, [put otherwise]' to show that it resumes the definition after the long parenthesis; the bracketed words are supplied.","'*Sublato rationato tollitur ratio*' and its counterpart are left untranslated, as the German that follows explains them."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-006":["We now come to the concept of",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-007":["Existence.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-008":["Among the categories we listed, under the fourth heading, that of modality, possibility, actuality and necessity. We then found that these are in no way determinations of a thing, or synthetic predicates. They are concepts through which a thing, with all its predicates, is posited. Possibility is nothing but the agreement of the thing with the conditions of thought. These conditions are either analytic or synthetic. If the thing does not agree with the former, it is impossible, by the principle of contradiction. But it must also agree with the synthesis. The principle which contains the synthetic conditions of thought is, provisionally, this: all synthesis must contain the conditions under which the manifold is brought into a unity; or, no synthetic predicate can be attributed to any thing except in so far as it is a possible experience. All synthetic judgments *a priori* express nothing but the conditions of a possible experience. For example: everything that exists in the world of sense is in space, that is, in so far as it is in experience.\n\nWhen I say that a thing is possible, I think nothing more in addition. Possibility is not a predicate belonging to the thing, but belongs to the positing [*position*] of the thing, in so far as this agrees with the laws of thought. Actuality is *positio absoluta*. Necessity is present when the *positio absoluta* is of such a kind that its removal contradicts the laws of thought. Possibility, actuality and necessity are not concepts of things in themselves. Possibility already presupposes the thing with all its predicates, and is the comparison of the thing with the laws of thought, to see whether it can be thought or not. (Actuality is that to which an object in experience corresponds; actuality is a necessity which follows from possibility.) (In actuality the object is added to a concept, but nothing is added to the object.) [Likewise actuality [*Wirklichkeit*]; they are distinguished only in the manner in which a thing is posited.]\n\nWe have indeed said that they are not predicates of things in themselves, but *modi* of the way in which a thing is posited. Yet we must admit that all concepts, modal concepts included, can be made predicates, though not real predicates or determinations, but logical ones; for example, God is possible. Possibility, actuality and necessity presuppose the thing, but are not themselves determinations of it. Possibility is a positing [*position*] in respect of our thought; actuality is *positio absoluta*. More is contained in actuality than in possibility. This does not mean that actuality represents more in the thing than possibility does, but that more is added to our thinking. First I posit a thing in respect of my thought; then I posit it absolutely, and thereby I evidently think more. By way of illustration let us note the following: — (breaks off)",["OCR corrected silently: Modahtaet to Modalität, rnodi to *modi*, Noth-Wendigkeit to Nothwendigkeit.","Restated the long opening sentences and the passage on modal predicates as several shorter sentences.","The sentence 'Ebenso die Wirklichkeit; sie sind nur unterschieden…' is bracketed: 'die Wirklichkeit' is probably a slip (for Nothwendigkeit?) in the transcript, and 'sie' has no clear antecedent. Doubtful reading.","*position* (German form of Latin *positio*) rendered 'positing'. 'Bestimmung' is rendered 'determination' throughout.","'(bricht ab)' is the editors' note that the text breaks off; kept as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-009":[". . . because we cannot reduce everything to one; but the unity of every substance requires that there be only one fundamental power [*Grund Kraft*]. A substance, in so far as it contains not merely the ground of the accidents but also determines the existence of the accidents, acts. Or: a substance, in so far as accidents inhere in it, is in action; and in so far as it is the ground of the actuality of the accidents, it acts. The substance in which accidents inhere through another power suffers (is passive).\n\nHow is passion possible, when it has been said before that a substance is active in so far as accidents inhere in it? Every substance is active in so far as accidents inhere in it, but also passive in so far as they inhere in it through a [separating, *trennende*] power. This involves no contradiction. For example, a representation of the sound of a trumpet inheres in me through a foreign power, but not through that power alone; for if I had no *vis repraesentativa*, the trumpet might be blown for ever and I could have no representation. From the union of one substance with another an effect arises, namely the representation of the trumpet's sound. We can never be merely passive; every passion is at the same time action.\n\nThe possibility of acting is a *facultas*, that of suffering a *receptivitas*. The latter always presupposes the former. Every substance is self-active; without that it could not be substance. In one respect it can be passive, and yet in the same respect also active. A merely passive substance is a *contradictio*, for otherwise it could have no accidents. Actions are either *immanentes*, which a substance produces in itself, or *transientes*, which act upon another substance, or *influxus*.\n\n*Substantia patiens* is *eo ipso agens*; for the accident would not inhere unless the substance had a power through which it inheres in it, and consequently the substance also acts. *Influxus* is therefore an unsuitable expression; it means as much as that the accident migrated out of one substance. What, then, is passio properly? The *substantia agens* determines the power of the *substantia patiens* to the production of this accident; consequently all passio is nothing but the determination of the power of what suffers by an external power.",["'trennende Kraft' is probably an OCR misreading of 'fremde Kraft' (foreign power), as the following example suggests; translated as read, with the likely reading bracketed. Doubtful.","OCR corrected silently: mhaerirt to inhaerirt, handlen to handeln.","The passage begins mid-sentence ('. . . weil'), and the sentence about substance acting is split into shorter ones.","Kraft is rendered 'power' here (cf. 'force' in the later passages on *lebendige Kraft*); Vermögen as 'capacity'/'faculty'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-010":["*Actio transiens*, when I make something actual outside myself, is of two kinds: *actio substantiae vel accidentiae extra se*. (*Actuare* means to make actual.) The first, when I make a substance actual outside myself, is entitled *creatio*. When I make accidents actual outside myself, [it would be determined,] this is entitled *influxus*. An action which consists of many actions is *composita*; one which is not composed of smaller actions is *simplex*.",["'wäre es bestimmt' ('it would be determined') is obscure and perhaps corrupt in the transcript; bracketed and left as it stands.","'wirklich außer mir wirklich' is doubled in the German; rendered once."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-011":["Force [*Kraft*] is the capacity [*Vermögen*] in so far as it suffices for the actuality of an accident. The distinction between force and capacity is hard to determine. Capacity, in so far as it is determined in respect of an effect, is force; and in so far as it is indeterm[inate]",["Breaks off mid-word ('unbestimm'); 'indeterm[inate]' marks the unfinished word. Continued in the next passage.","OCR corrected silently: hmreicht to hinreicht, Wirklic keit to Wirklichkeit, m Ansehung to in Ansehung."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-012":["... is, it becomes capacity [*Vermögen*]. Force contains the ground of the actuality of an action, capacity the ground of the possibility of an action.",["The running head ('6 Kant's Schriften XXIX 1,2') is omitted. The fragment continues the previous passage (presumably '[unbestimmt] ist'); nothing has been supplied."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-013":["A force which contains the internally and externally sufficient ground of an action is a living force [*lebendige Kraft*]; in so far as it contains only the internally sufficient ground of an action, it is a dead force. In comparison with the living forces, the dead forces in the world are infinitely many. The combination of two forces which resist one another is *confluens*. In itself every force is living; for if it exists, the effect must also exist.",["OCR corrected silently: Hie to Die, iede, exist iren to existiren.","Kraft is rendered 'force' here, in contrast to Vermögen, 'capacity'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-014":["Reaction is not counter-action [*Gegenwirkung*] but reciprocal action [*Wechselwirkung*]. Counter-action is the reciprocal action which is opposed to the action of the other, or *actio opposita patientis in agentem*; this is also called *impedimentum* [German text: *impediment*]. It is of two kinds. (1) Negative: here there are no efficient causes, or there is a lack in the cause of that which would constitute its complement. (2) Positive: here there are efficient causes belonging to the *patiens* which are opposed to the actions of the *agentes*.",["OCR corrected silently: eomplement to complement, patient is to patientis.","'impediment' is left in the German form of the transcript, with the Latin form bracketed.","The second clause ('Mangel von dem an der Ursache…') is awkward; rendered by judgment as a lack in the cause of its complement."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-015":["The author now speaks of presence, distance, absence and contact. The first three may still belong here, but not the last. Contact concerns extended beings which have a surface and are impenetrable; for it is *contactus* when two bodies in space oppose each other through their impenetrability. One substance, it may be said, is present to another when it exerts influence upon it. This can happen mediately or immediately. *Praesentia* is either *bilateralis*, when two substances act upon each other reciprocally, or *unilateralis*, as God acts upon all things, but they do not act upon him. All things in the world are in [his] presence. The influence is *unilateralis* where, in respect of one substance, another is merely *patiens*; it is *bilateralis* where the substances can be regarded as *reciproce agentes*. In *influxus* the substance must be present. The inwardly sufficient ground of the action of a substance is force. Force is distinguished from *patientia*; something must be added to the latter if it is to become force. Force which is sufficient inwardly but not outwardly is dead force; force which is sufficient both inwardly and outwardly is living force. Dead force is a ground by which an effect can exist but does not exist, on account of external circumstances. If the ground is inwardly sufficient and the effect nevertheless fails, there must be a real force [...], whose effect is an equally strong [counter-]object, or an obstacle, that is, something opposed to the effect of a given force. If the ground of the opposite of an effect is a force, this is entitled resistance (*Widerstand*). *Vires mortuae* are those which have no actuality because another force resists them. If one increases such a force inwardly, or removes the obstacles, it may well become outwardly sufficient. Between dead and living forces there is in itself no difference, but only a difference due to the obstacles. If we remove the obstacles, many effects will occur in the world without any increase of the active forces.",["OCR 'A\\ eit' in 'in praesenz sind alle Dinge in der A\\ eit' is too garbled to be certain; 'Welt' is a conjecture, and the supplied '[his]' (God) is my reading of the sense.","A word seems to be missing after 'reale Kraft' (the OCR leaves a gap), and 'Gegenstand' ('object') is perhaps a slip for something like 'Gegengrund'. I translate what is there and supply '[counter-]' in brackets.","Silently corrected OCR: Her/Hie→Der/Die, Hinge→Dinge, eontaetus→contactus, liiere→mere, hinzukonunen→hinzukommen.","'heißt es resistenz' rendered 'is entitled'; Wiederstand = Widerstand."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-016":["The author now speaks of *contactus*. Two bodies touch each other when they have a common boundary. He believes this to be the same as *praesentia immediata*. One substance is present to another when it acts upon it. If this is immediate, then according to the author it is *contactus*. But this holds only of space and of what is extended, and is a physical concept which presupposes impenetrability. *Praesentia* is either *unilateralis* or *bilateralis* or *mutua*. In every *contactus* there is *influxus mutuus*; but conversely, where there is *influxus mutuus* there is not always *contactus*. In sensible beings there is, but not in spiritual beings.",["OCR 'umlateralis' corrected to *unilateralis*.","The triad 'unilateralis vel bilateralis vel mutua' is as printed; the text may be slightly off, but I have kept it."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-017":["We now speak of the",["Breaks off; continued in the next passage (the simple and the composite)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-018":["simple and the composite.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-019":["*Quantum* and *compositum* have already been discussed above. In the case of a *quantum* one thing must be taken several times; in the case of a *compositum* this is not required. We represent to ourselves that many things make up one; but without *identitas plurium* nothing can be regarded as a *quantum*. The *compositum* is divided into the real, that is, *compositum ex substantiis*, and the ideal, that is, the *compositum* which is composed of something that is not substance, or of accidents. The real is also called [the composite] *strictius dictum*. If the parts exist by themselves, then composition is an action by which the parts are combined. If the parts can exist prior to the composition, it is a *compositum reale*; if the concept of the *compositum* precedes the concept of the parts, it is a *compositum ideale*. For example, space: I cannot think it as composed of small spaces; on the contrary, I must think the small space within the great. The composition is thought later than the *compositum*. We here consider the *compositum substantiale*, in which the following is to be noted.",["OCR repairs: 'exiMiren'→existiren, 'die lei e'→'die Theile', 'z' → 'zusammengesetzt', 'mei 'en'→'merken'; 'strictus dictum' read as *strictius dictum*.","The sentence 'Wenn die Theile vor sich existiren, so ist die Zusammensetzung eine Handlung' is somewhat irregular in the transcript; I have kept its sense and smoothed it slightly.","'[the composite]' is supplied."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-020":["1) Matter, or the parts of which the *compositum* consists;",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-021":["2) The manner in which the parts are composed, that is, the form. The essence of every *compositum* consists in the form, that is, in the *modus compositionis*. The ancients said: *forma dat esse rei*. By this they understood that the matter in each thing is something real, but that the form constitutes the diversity of things; and we must think the form prior to the material.",["OCR 'clat eße rei' read as *dat esse rei*; 'compositi onis' as *compositionis*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-022":["In the case of substance much was already represented, in so far as it is contained in one [subject]; but these were not parts. For example, the soul does not consist of thoughts; these inhere in it. There is thus a *nexus* which is not a *nexus* of composition but of inherence. We now take the former. The *compositum*, in so far as it is not regarded as part of something else, is a *totum*. A *totum* and a *compositum* both relate to parts. — A manifold, in so far as it becomes an aggregate in one, is entitled a *compositum*, and, if the manifold is homogeneous, a *quantum*. Now we speak only of the *compositum quatenus est compositum*.",["'in einem enthalten' is ambiguous; I supply '[subject]'.","The sentence 'Vieles, insofern es in Eins ein aggregat wird — da heißt das eine compositum' is loosely constructed in the transcript; I have smoothed it."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-023":["A *compositum reale* is that whose parts can also exist by themselves, apart from the *nexus* with others. That whose parts can never exist apart from connection with others is ideal. For example, an inference is a *compositum ideale*; its parts can exist only in the soul. A body is a *compositum reale*.",["'Schluß' rendered 'inference' (syllogism/conclusion); a choice."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-024":["The author still speaks of coming to be and passing away, and says that change from non-existence into existence is *ortus*, and conversely *interitus*. *Ortus ex nihilo* is the origin of substance; of accidents it cannot be said that they have arisen from nothing. *Ex nihilo ortum est* is that whose parts would have had pre-existence. *Annihilatio* likewise concerns only substance; accidents are mere *modi existentiae* of substance. But this is not change of a thing itself, for change can take place only in existing things. *Ortus* signifies a being which a non-being precedes, and *interitus* a being which a non-being follows. In appearance that is substance which remains while the determinations alternate. This is the condition under which we entitle something the first substantial, namely that it persists. For were there nothing persisting, which neither came to be nor passed away, we could have no experience of change. There must be a substantial, in respect of which alone we can perceive the change. In what persists the state was A and is now B; it is therefore changed by something or other, and this *perdurabile* we entitle substance. When it is said that everything in the world is changeable, this means that nothing comes to be and passes away except accidents and combinations of bodies.",["'Ex nihilo ortum est, ist das, dessen Theile praeexistenz hätten' is obscure and possibly corrupt (a negation may have dropped out); translated as it stands.","'das erste substantiale' kept literally as 'the first substantial'.","Erscheinung = appearance; Bestimmungen = determinations."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-025":["The parts of a *compositum reale* are in *commercium*, and all substances, in so far as they stand in *commercium*, constitute a *compositum reale*. *Commercium* is *influxus mutuus*; for how is the *commercium* of different substances possible, except by one determining something in the other, since the substances act upon one another? For example, in a body all the parts are in *commercium*; what is not in *commercium* does not belong to it. The connection of the supreme cause with its *causata* connects nothing and is no *commercium*. The cause accordingly does not belong to the *causata*.",["'denn wirken die Substanzen in einander' is loosely joined in the transcript; rendered as a subordinate clause.","OCR 'XHe Ursache' read as 'Die Ursache'; 'caußatis' = *causatis*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-026":["Every whole consisting of substances consists of simple substances. We would further note that the author, contrary to Leibniz, maintains that a composite (*compositum*) should not be entitled a substance, even though its parts are substances, since composition is only an accident. This is indeed true, but it is mere subtlety. Leibniz says that bodies are substantial phenomena; they seem to be [substances], but only the parts are substances. In saying this he presumably had in mind something other than what Wolff and the author attribute to him; otherwise he could always have kept this to himself, as anyone readily sees. (Monad means the unity of an indivisible substance, that is, of one that cannot be thought further as an aggregate of many substances. Now the question arises: can we say of every *compositum substantiale* that it consists of simple substances, that is, is it *monadatum*? Yes, in so far as it is a noumenon; for all connection is nothing but relation. Since substances, by definition, can have an external existence for themselves, all relation can be removed and the substances remain and are simple. For if they were not simple, the composition would not have been removed. But how is it with a real composite that is a phenomenon (*compositum reale Phaenomenon*)? Where something is represented through the senses alone as a *compositum substantiale*, and where even the substances are substances only for the senses, this falls away, for the proof falls away. The appearance of a substance is not the substance itself, and what holds of the latter does not hold of the former. Suppose a body consisted of simple parts; its shadow need not therefore consist of simple parts. If we take away all composition, nothing remains.) If in the composite of substances nothing is substance except the parts, in respect of which we abstract from all composition, which is only an accident of their reciprocal relation, then the substances remain, which are without composition; accordingly in every *compositum substantiale* the substances are the simple parts. If I set aside all relation, the simple remains. This is quite correct if I speak of composites *quatenus sunt Noumena*; for through the understanding I cannot think any composite without first thinking simple substances. The simple is the *substratum* of everything composite in the case of noumena. Composition (*compositio*) is the relation of substances in so far as they stand in community; but this does not obtain in the case of a composition that is phenomenon. The real composite, in so far as it is appearance, is considered in space. Space does not consist of simple parts, and therefore neither does the composite. Of an enduring appearance I cannot say what holds of substances in so far as they are thought through the understanding. Bodies are appearances and in space, and therefore do not consist of simple substances. If we were to know the unknown [something] which underlies bodies and produces the corporeal appearances, we should know nothing but simple [beings] which would stand in *commercium*. In the appearance we make the phenomena into substances; but we must then keep to the concept of substance, for the enduring appearance admits of it [?]. To speak of simple beings we must depart from the world of sense; but then we have no proof of the objective reality of our concept, for we can give no example of it; whereas for all appearances this can be done. Composites of which I can give examples are *phaenomena substantiata*. But what holds of noumena does not hold of them.",["Square-bracketed 'substances' and 'something', 'beings' are supplied to complete the sense; the German has only 'es' / 'das Unbekannte' / 'Einfaches'.","OCR corrections made silently: 'cl.h.' = d.h.; 'Nouinenon' = Noumenon; 'fälltdas' split; 'trägt es sich :' normalised.","'Monas ... Einheit einer untheilbaren Substanz': kept close; 'monadatum' left in Latin as a technical term.","'denn die beharrliche Erscheinung leidet' is obscure and possibly corrupt; the rendering 'admits of it' is conjectural.","'Phaenomenon' in 'composito reali Phaenomenon' rendered as in apposition ('that is a phenomenon'); the German is elliptical.","Parenthesis marks of the transcript retained as in the source; one long sentence was divided where the German runs on."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-027":["Have we, then, gained any new insight through this doctrine? No; for through the category of substance we know no things. Experience must give us examples, and these are appearances. We can just as little see how substances are supposed to make up a whole; we can do so of appearances which are in space, but not of substances in themselves, since here we must leave space aside, because it is the form of sensible intuition. We are now speaking of",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence; translated as it stands.","'Eorin' read as 'Form' (OCR slip).","'einsehen' rendered 'see/gain insight'; 'wohl von Erscheinungen' supplied with 'we can do so' for the ellipsis."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-028":["Space and Time",["Heading only."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-029":["Space and time are not determinations of things in so far as these are thought through the understanding, but in so far as they fall within our senses; they are therefore forms of intuitions. (We now come to the important concepts, which are of such a kind that, if we have been able to develop their nature, they will alter the whole plan of metaphysics and banish all the contradictions which have deprived metaphysics of its credit.) Space is outer sense, time inner sense. The matter of all appearances is sensation, and what corresponds to this is real. Philosophy: the real we know only *a posteriori*, but the formal *a priori*. The representation of the impression of the object upon us is sensation, and is therefore something subjective; all of these we must know *a posteriori*. In our knowledge through the senses there is something of which we have a representation *a priori*; this is the form of appearance, outer space, inner time. They are the form in which objects will appear to us when they affect us; hence we can represent to ourselves much that we have never seen, for example cones and pyramids, as in geometry; accordingly space and time can be known *a priori*. Besides appearance, something more belongs to experience, for experience is not mere perception but the unity of perceptions connected with one another according to universal rules. The matter must be given; the form consists in the concepts of the understanding. These are the categories; they constitute the form of all human experience, and the unity of appearances is *a priori*, and this too is [the work of] the categories, for example substance; for they are the connection of the manifold of appearance according to rules. — We can therefore know *a priori*:",["'Philosophie :' left as it stands; its function in the transcript is unclear (perhaps a heading or aside).","'Eindruck des Gegenstandes auf uns' kept literal; 'Gegenstand' = object.","'[the work of]' supplied for the elliptical 'das sind auch die Categorien'.","'Anschauungen' rendered in the plural as in the German; 'wird/werden erscheinen' rendered 'will appear'.","Sentence about the unity of appearances divided/smoothed slightly."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-030":["1.) The form of intuition, that is, space and time, and",["Breaks off; the continuation is not given."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-031":["2.) The form of the understanding, in so far as it is applied to sensibility; or the form of the thinking of the objects that appear to us. This is a concept of the understanding, and constitutes the form of experience. Space and time, which belong to sensibility, and the categories, which belong to the understanding, make up the concepts which we can have *a priori*, and to which we can give corresponding objects in experience. We have also other transcendent concepts, to which, however, no object in experience can be given; these must be distinguished from the transcendental. The latter are those through which I represent merely the possibility of synthetic knowledge *a priori*. *Transcendent* can be expressed in German by *überschwenglich* [extravagant, over-reaching]. Concepts are immanent if an example of them can be given from experience, or according to the analogy of an experience. — These concepts are the ground of the possibility of knowledge *a priori*, and of synthetic knowledge *a priori*. They have always been a stumbling-block.\n\nOf space it is said that it is something in itself. But then it is nothing at all without things, for in space there are places; and the place, or the way in which things are side by side, does not exist if no things exist. Yet our understanding represents it as preceding all things, and it is regarded as an all-embracing *receptaculum* which contains nothing but the places of things. Nor can it be relations, for we cannot represent to ourselves that these could exist without things. Space is not a thing, yet it is a something in which I can represent to myself all relations. What, then, is space?\n\nIt went the same way with time. Time is that in which alterations subsist; it is not a thing, but things are in it. It does not presuppose the existence of things; rather I must have a time in which I posit the thing. If I take away all things, there remains a time in which nothing is, but in which something can come to be. What, then, is it? A flux of moments. And what are these in turn? Since [these philosophers] were compelled to regard space and time as something, yet not as a thing, they did not know what to make of them.\n\n(The author says: *Ordo successivorum est tempus — post se invicem posita coniuncta succedunt*. Time is thus the connection of things in so far as they follow one another. But he omits being simultaneous, and that is an important relation. When are things simultaneous? When they are in the same time; and successive, when they are things in different times. And no one has grasped succession except by presupposing time; the definition is therefore false. — Space is *ordo simultaneorum extra se positorum*; that things can be outside one another presupposes space. It is said that things can be represented as outside one another without being in space; for example, I say that the one substance is other than the other. True, but being posited outside one another also presupposes a space. The author says this too: *simultaneorum extra et intra se posita*; otherwise I cannot see what relations the different things have to one another.)",["OCR corrected silently: 'piiori' to *a priori*; 'succeßivormn' to *successivorum*; 'Verstand' in 'stellt sich unser Verstand vor: Er gehe...' kept as written, though the masculine 'Er' suggests the antecedent is Raum (the sense: space precedes all things).","In 'Gleichwohl stellt sich unser Verstand vor: Er gehe vor allen Dingen vorher' I took 'Er' to refer to Raum, as the argument requires, and rendered it 'it'.","'Das ist ein Verstandes Begriff' follows a stray quotation mark after 'erscheinen;' in the OCR; I punctuated with a full stop. The sentence may refer to the second item of a list whose first item lies before this passage.","Restated: the long run on space and on time is divided into short paragraphs and sentences; 'Sie' in 'Was ist sie nun?' etc. refers to time.","Supplied in brackets: 'these philosophers' for the German 'sie' in 'Indem sie genöthigt waren', whose antecedent is not in the passage (those who held space and time to be something in themselves); also the gloss on *überschwenglich*.","'Der Autor' is Baumgarten (Metaphysica); the Latin quotations are kept in italics as given, with the transcript's own text (e.g. 'simultaneorum extra et intra se posita'), which may be garbled.","'Stein des Anstoßes' rendered 'stumbling-block'; 'Verhältniße' as 'relations'; 'Erkenntniß' as 'knowledge'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-032":["The author explains space by *ordo extra se positorum*. Things are *extra se posita* when they are in different places. The concept of place presupposes the concept of space, and that concept is already assumed as known. *Ordo plurium, quatenus post se existunt, est tempus*: to be one after another is to be at different times, and so the explanation defines *idem per idem* [the same by the same].",["'Der Autor' is Baumgarten; the Latin definition is kept as in the transcript and may be a loose quotation.","'der Begriff' in 'und der Begriff wird schon als bekannt angenommen' is taken to refer to the concept of space, as the argument requires; the German could also be read as the concept of place.","Bracketed gloss on *idem per idem* is supplied; the passage is complete as given."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s07-033":["The chief question is whether they [space and time] are things subsisting in and for themselves; for then they would be distinct from all the possible things that are in them. Most of the ancient philosophers rejected this. Some, however, believed that space is a determination of things. Since space and time consist and are contained wholly in relations, it was believed that this relation of things, in so far as they are connected, constitutes space, and that time is constituted by the relation in so far as one thing is determined by another in a series. They would then be genuine relations, which would lapse if there were no things, such as can be thought through the understanding as possible. Others held that space and time are nothing but a general concept of relations as such. Let us see whether this is so.\n\nIf they were relations, we could have no concept of them *a priori*, which yet we have in geometry. Or else we should have to be able to know the properties of space from the relations of things; and then, throughout the whole of geometry, synthetic propositions *a priori* would be impossible. We might indeed know the properties *a posteriori*, but in that case there would be no apodeictic propositions. Space is not a concept but an intuition. The concept presupposes intuition. We cannot prove properties of any [concept? the reading is garbled] without drawing them. We have only one space, and when we speak of many spaces we mean parts of the one infinite space. This is not so with a concept. For example, a particular virtue is not a part of virtue in general; but with space, which comprehends everything, it is otherwise.\n\nTo intuit a thing *a priori* means to intuit a thing that is not given at all. So I can intuit nothing but the form of sensibility, the way in which I intuit things; and this is in me. Space must be something of this kind, namely the form of outer appearance. It is not what lies in the things, but what lies in ourselves. Now I can explain it: it is the formal element of outer appearances, and so we can know properties of space *a priori*. This condition, under which appearances are possible, is pure outer intuition. It is now comprehensible how space and time can precede the things, or rather the appearances. They concern merely the nature of sensibility, in conformity with which alone it can be affected.\n\n(Space and time give us modes of knowledge *a priori*, prior to all experience; consequently they cannot be borrowed from experience. We recognise propositions *a priori* by their necessity. Such necessary propositions we have in geometry, e.g. between two points only one straight line is possible. Time also yields such necessary propositions, e.g. two times cannot be at once. Hence space and time cannot be properties of things in themselves. To have insight into things in themselves *a priori* is, in this regard, flatly impossible. Since, then, they are not determinations of the object, they must be determinations of the subject, that is, forms of our sensibility. Space is the form in which we intuit outer things; time, the form in which we intuit ourselves. What we know through outer and inner sense is mere appearance, not things in themselves. It might be objected: surely we can know ourselves? Yes; where we are self-active, one may say that this does not belong to sensibility; but when we observe ourselves, this is nothing but a series of inner appearances. We can observe everything only as we are inwardly affected.)\n\n(We can now also see how we know time and space *a priori*. Things in themselves we cannot look at *a priori*; but how can we look at appearances *a priori*? Because I can know how they will affect me, since they are not things in themselves. For example, I shall see no body unless the rays of light from a point of the body meet in a point on my retina; this I can see *a priori*.)\n\n(Space and time will therefore not hold of things in themselves either; but things as phenomena are in space and time. This is the foundation of the transcendental aesthetic. Aesthetic is the doctrine of sensibility. The ancients divided everything into *aistheta* and *noeta*; now the word is used of sensible pleasure, and is entitled the doctrine of taste. Since this cannot properly be raised to a science, it is better to keep to the ancients' meaning. Transcendental aesthetic is the consideration of things as objects of the senses, in so far as we can know them *a priori*. It shows the grounds of the possibility of sensible intuitions *a priori*, which rests on space and time.)\n\n(When I say that something is extended, this is appearance, for extended is a determination of space. When we speak of coming to be and passing away, alteration and so forth, we speak merely of phenomena. This has no meaning if we speak merely of the *noumenon*.)\n\nThe concept of time we cannot have *a posteriori*. For how should we set about drawing it *a posteriori*? We could have no experience that one thing follows upon another, if the concept did not underlie it. Time is not a concept of relations of things; otherwise we could have no properties *a priori*, and no apodeictic propositions, about time, e.g. between two instants there is only one time; two times are not at once; time has only one dimension. But if these properties were concepts of relation, we could not have all these propositions. Time too is intuition. All things are in the same time; all times are parts of the same time. Intuition contains not the constitution of the thing, but the subjective form of sensibility, the way in which I am affected. The form of inner sense is time. I can see *a priori* how my inner sense intuits itself, and can say much about it *a priori*.\n\nHave these concepts [space and time] also objective reality, or are they *entia rationis*? They certainly have objective reality, but are restricted to mere objects of the senses; they hold of all appearances. Crusius says: all things are somewhere or somewhen. This must be restricted to appearances. The sum-total of all possible objects of the senses is the world of sense; the concepts therefore hold only of the world of sense. World is the aggregate of appearances. I can indeed think an intelligible world, just as we represent a world of sense, which can be known only through the understanding. But the understanding has not the power [Vermögen] of intuiting; it has that of reflecting upon appearances. It cannot therefore know what things in themselves are. It can gain experiences, but thereby it has known only the connection of appearances, not things in themselves.\n\nAppearance [Erscheinung] and illusion [Schein] must be distinguished. Appearance, as appearance, can be true. Illusion is a perception from which I have wanted to form a concept without taking in any other perceptions. Appearance does not judge. If we wish to judge about appearances, we judge truly when the judgment is made from all the perceptions; if I wish to judge from one perception only, that is illusion.\n\nGod knows things in themselves, for his power of knowledge brings the things forth. We know only the appearances, that is, the way in which we are affected; and things must affect us, otherwise we know nothing of them at all.\n\nSpace and time are nothing but representations of things, and now we have a key to many problems. If appearances are nothing but representations, and all sensible things are appearances, then all sensible things are nothing but representations. Appearances therefore exist only in the representation itself. We can accordingly say: only so much exists in the world of sense as lies in our representation. For example, suppose that in the midst of music men lost their hearing; then no music would exist for them either, and likewise for those who have no musical ear. Objects in themselves do not exist. Hereby we are relieved of many errors. For example: is the world finite or infinite? The question, which has caused so many difficulties, now falls away, and becomes: shall I proceed without end in the series of my perceptions, or not? And to this the answer is determinate: without end.",["Silent OCR corrections: 'Wed' read as 'Weil'; 'Iheil' as 'Theil'; 'Rischeinungen' as 'Erscheinungen'; 'Zusammenstößen' as 'zusammenstoßen'; 'mäßen' as 'müssen'; 'lealitaet' as 'realitaet'; 'Geschmackslehr 3' as 'Geschmackslehre'; 'vor die' as 'für die'. Stray page numbers and marks omitted.","'Wir können von keinem \\ Eigenschaften beweisen': the OCR is garbled at the backslash. I supplied '[concept?]' in brackets as a conjecture only; the reading is doubtful.","'Wed Raum und Zeit in lauter Verhältnißen bestehen': 'Wed' taken as 'Weil'; I rendered it causally and divided the long sentence into several.","'Das ist aber nicht bei einem Begriff … Einigen unendlichen Raumes': 'Einigen' taken as 'einzigen/einigen', i.e. the one infinite space.","'glatt unmöglich' and 'hierfür' are rendered literally ('in this regard, flatly impossible'); the transcript may be slightly corrupt here.","'Vermögen' rendered 'power' in 'the understanding has not the power of intuiting'; 'Erscheinung' and 'Schein' given in brackets where the distinction turns on them; Kant's parentheses retained as in the transcript.","'Sinnen Welt' and 'sensual Welt' rendered 'world of sense' (Latin *mundus sensibilis*), 'intelligible Welt' as 'intelligible world'; 'aistheta', 'noeta', *noumenon*, *entia rationis*, *a priori*, *a posteriori* left untranslated."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-001":["Of the Finite and the Infinite.",["Heading only. *Vom Endlichen und Unendlichen* is rendered literally; the neuter substantives are given as 'the Finite' and 'the Infinite'. The page reference (AA 29:834) is omitted from the translation as apparatus."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-002":["A magnitude that cannot be intuited immediately as a magnitude is estimated through its consequence. I represent it to myself as I do a quality. *Quantitas qualitatis* is degree. This is the definition of the author [Baumgarten], and it is quite correct. That is, such a magnitude is not represented immediately as quantity, but it is represented mediately, namely through its consequence. In the same way we can also say that *quantitas rationis* (of a ground) is degree. Degrees are the opposite of extensive magnitudes, and these are space and time and everything in them. Of inner magnitude we use the expression 'degree', not 'magnitude'; the latter holds only of extensive magnitudes. All reality has degrees. There are degrees from sensation up to the thought in which I think myself in respect of the understanding, that is, up to apperception. Something may have so small a degree that I can scarcely notice it, and yet I am still conscious of it. Strictly speaking, there is in experience no greatest and no smallest. We have three concepts, that of the *maximum*, the *illimitatum* and the *infinitum*. They are very closely related, yet distinct from one another.\n\nA *maximum* is that beyond which nothing greater is possible. The *illimitatum* is the negative representation of the greatest. In the *maximum* we have the concept of a totality to which nothing is lacking that is required for a certain kind of thing. (What contains everything of a certain kind is *illimitatum*; what does not contain everything, and so on, is restricted. The *limitatum* is therefore opposed to the *omnitudo*.) The *illimitatum* can accordingly signify the *maximum*, so far as the latter is represented through a negative concept. Something can be *illimitatum* comparatively, but not absolutely. The *maximum* is a positive, the *illimitatum* a negative, concept of totality.\n\nIn the *infinitum* I represent to myself the relation of the magnitude of a thing to its essence. The relation of the magnitude to the possibility of measuring it determines either the magnitude or the infinity. [In the latter case] I do not say how great the thing is; I say [only that it is] greater than all the concepts with which I otherwise am accustomed to measure. The infinite has been confused with the *illimitatum*. We estimate magnitudes successively, *unum rei addendo* [by adding one to another]. All counting is a progress in the construction of a magnitude. A magnitude whose construction is possible through a finite progress is a *quantitas finita*; one whose progress is infinite is *infinita*. A thing in itself I cannot call *infinitum*. Common usage brings it about that the *illimitatum* is often called the infinite, but in themselves the two are not the same. Of appearances (*Phaenomena*) we can say that they are both *illimitatum* and *infinitum*.\n\nThe *ens summum* we represent to ourselves as *illimitatum*. Can we then call it *infinitum*? That is not fitting. For by 'infinite' I do not say as much as by *ens summum*, that is, that it contains everything that a being can contain, or, put generally, that nothing is lacking to it. By *infinitum* I say not what the being itself is, but only that its magnitude cannot be determined in relation to a measure. In this way I do not know how great it is itself, or whether it is great or not. I know only that it is too great for each of my concepts. For that reason, however, it need not be the greatest among all things.\n\n(The infinite does not say how great a thing is in itself; in respect to a measure it is greater than every number. All estimation of the greater in space and time is relative. For example, the sun is far from the earth, but relatively to the fixed stars it is near to the earth; and the smaller the measure, the greater the number. — Absolute magnitude is allness [*Allheit*]. If we take something in relation to allness, it is restricted. Absolute metaphysical magnitude is that of the *ens realissimum*. Through allness we know things in themselves, also through restriction of the *omnitudo*.) (The concept of the infinite concerns only appearances, therefore only space and time and everything possible in them, and to these alone must we apply it. If I say that God is *ens infinitum*, this means *illimitatum*.) (In the case of *noumena* there is a *maximum*, but in the case of appearances neither a *maximum* nor a *minimum* has place.)\n\n'Infinite' signifies the advance to infinity in the measurement of magnitude. The *infinitum*, as distinguished from the *illimitatum*, is entitled *infinitum mathematicum*; and so far as it is the same as the *illimitatum*, it is entitled *infinitum reale*. *Quantum relative ad unitatem aliquam dabilem omni numero maius* is infinite. If it is greater than every number, it exceeds my concept, for I can form a determinate concept of a magnitude only through number. A being that has a determinate concept of magnitude without number can also comprehend the *infinitum*. In the case of the highest being we cannot make use of this word, for it presupposes that we have a measure. This requires homogeneity, and the highest being has nothing homogeneous with it. In the *illimitatum* I compare it with no measure. The concept of the infinite can be applied only to appearances, for it presupposes that the progress from one [member] to another is greater than every number. The progress takes place in time.\n\nIn the case of appearances, is it the object that is infinite, or the progress? I cannot call the appearance infinite, for it is nothing outside me. If the world of sense were a thing in itself, one could ask whether it were infinite or not. As it is, I cannot ask: is the world infinite in respect of space? I can ask only: must I in the world proceed onward to infinity? Yes, progress and regress *in infinitum* are possible; but, on the other side, we cannot represent to ourselves that a *quantum* is infinite. A future eternity is infinite. It is not an *infinitum datum* [infinite given], but an infinite possible progress of changes that can follow one upon another. It is an infinite possibility, not an actuality.\n\n(Infinity is either progressive, which concerns potential magnitude, or collective, which concerns actual magnitude. An actual *infinitum* cannot be thought, but a potential one can. For example, a future eternity is not something actual, but the composition of several times, and this can be infinite. — If we wished to think a filled collective [infinite] as a whole [*collective tale*, reading doubtful], we should have to represent to ourselves the parts as all actual. We should have to run through them and take them together, but that is again the definition of the infinite. Hence the collective infinite is not in itself impossible, for we say nothing further than that the magnitude cannot be known as determinate without number. Since we go on to infinity, we can express it by no number. But with an understanding that need not posit magnitude by numbers, it would be otherwise.)\n\n(The concept of the *infinitum* ought at the same time to be the concept of the *maximum*, beyond which no greater is possible. But that is the *illimitatum*; whether the infinite thing contains everything does not yet follow from this. Various things have been sought to be proved from it. An *numerus infinitus*, it is said, is impossible. That is apodictically true. It would be a number greater than all numbers. But, it was inferred further, a *multitudo infinita* is not therefore impossible, for example an infinite space. We say it is a number of miles, but here there is no number at all. It was proved in this way: the *multitudo infinita* would, by its definition, be the greatest; but to every magnitude a further unit can still be thought; hence the greatest, and so the infinite, magnitude is impossible. If one whittles definitions in this fashion, one can prove whatever one wishes.)\n\nSome authors say: because there is no infinite number, there is also no infinite. *Summus infinitus est contradictio*, but not an infinite multitude. One must distinguish an actual and a potential *infinitum*. The potential is that in which a progress *in infinitum* is possible, which is not given; the actual is that which is given. The unrestricted belongs to the *noumena*. I represent to myself that no reality is lacking; [the boundary] bounds a concept that applies to appearance [*sic*, reading doubtful]. Boundaries (*Grenzen*) we think in space and time; limits (*Schranken*) in the case of *noumena*. That which contains in itself the ground of a limit, either in time or in space, is a boundary, and this can therefore be found only in appearances.\n\n*Infinitum mathematicum non est dabile*; that is, it cannot be represented to me as existing as a whole. For example, a future eternity cannot be given as a whole, otherwise it would not be an eternity. By what means do we represent magnitudes to ourselves as existing as wholes? Only by the progress of addition of one to one. An infinite magnitude cannot be given for this reason, that the progress *in infinitum* would also have to be given as a whole, that is, the progress would have to be completed, and this it is impossible to represent. [We can] just as little [represent] an infinite space, though the progress in space we can represent as infinite. If we wished to represent it as existing prior to the progress, we should have made a concept with a property with which we cannot think it at all, for we can think no magnitude except through the progress. (A measure is a magnitude that we can know intuitively.)\n\nProgress *in infinitum* has place, for I can continue a magnitude further *in infinitum* and proceed ever further in addition; it can be thought also in the coming eternity. Progress *in indefinitum* is less than *in infinitum*. Here merely no determinate limit is set for me. We now speak",["OCR slips silently corrected: Beyrn→Beim, giößer→größer, müßcn→müssen, findt→findet, größem→größeren, Progeßus→Progressus.","Long sentences, especially the opening of the paragraph on the *infinitum* and the 'ens summum' sentence, are divided into several; parenthetical passages are kept in parentheses as in the transcript.","'Die Grade sind entgegen gesetzte extensive Größen' is rendered 'the opposite of extensive magnitudes'; the sense (intensive versus extensive) is supplied by the context, not by the words.","'Das Verhältniß … bestimmt entweder die Größe, oder die Unendlichkeit; ich sage nicht, wie groß…' is elliptical; I supplied '[In the latter case]' and '[only that it is]'. Doubtful.","'collective tale' is probably garbled (perhaps 'collective totale'); I give '[collective tale, reading doubtful]'. Likewise 'aliquant' is read as *aliquam*.","'Man kann sich ebensowenig einen infiniten Raum' lacks a verb in the transcript; '[We can] just as little [represent]' is supplied in brackets.","'begränzt einen Begriff, der aufs Phaenomen geht' has no clear subject; I supplied '[the boundary]' and marked the reading doubtful. It may be an omission or a garbled clause.","'durch Allheit erkennen wir Dinge an sich selbst auch durch Einschränkung der omnitudo' is obscure and possibly corrupt; translated as it stands.","Grenze/Schranke rendered 'boundary'/'limit'; *Allheit* 'allness'. The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('Wir reden itzt') and is not completed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-003":["Of Agreement and Difference",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-004":["The author now speaks *de praedicatis entium relativis* [of the relative predicates of beings]. He calls them predicates of connection (relation), which is not good. Predicates serve to enable us to know identity and diversity, but for that there must be no relation. For example, two men may be very similar, or agree in many marks, without having the slightest relation to one another. We note the following. The definition of quality is *similitudo*; that of quantity is *aequalitas*. Things are *vel partialiter vel totaliter similia* [similar either partially or totally]; *aequalitas totalis* is equality in respect of all predicates. Let us suppose that two total equalities are also *similia*; for if they are to be totally equal, they must also be totally similar. The *aequale* is identical quantity. But *homogenea* can be compared only in respect of magnitude. For example, I cannot compare a year and a mile. If they were completely equal, they would also have to be completely homogeneous; for if they are to be equal in respect of all determinations, they must also be homogeneous.\n\nIdentity is threefold: (a) qualitative; (b) quantitative; these two together constitute inner identity; (c) identity *relationum* [of relations], that is, outer identity, for example, that a thing exists at different times. One would suppose that things *totaliter congruentia* might still differ *ratione relationis* [in respect of relation]; and likewise that things *totaliter similia* might be distinguished. Hence the author also says: *Quantitas est discrimen internum*; *mere similium totaliter eadem tanquam ad internos et externos determinationes* [as regards inner and outer determinations], the author says, *sunt numerice eadem*. Or, *non plure plura totaliter eadem sunt impoßibilia*; otherwise there would be no inner difference of things. Outer difference does not make a difference of the things, but only of the outer relation. So they are not different in kind, for I have no ground for saying that the one is something other than the other.\n\nFurther: *perfecte aequalia sunt impoßibilia*. For, by what precedes, they would have to be completely similar, and hence *totaliter congruentia*; and these are impossible. Neither are *mere similia* possible, for if they were completely similar, they would still differ in magnitude, one being greater than the other. The one is then completely equal and similar to a part of the other, and so *totaliter congruentia*, and therefore impossible. These propositions were asserted by Wolff, Baumgarten, and before them Leibniz, who had the *principium indiscernibilium*. (This *principium indiscernibilium* does not hold of the world of sense. It is also absurd to suppose that God must have created differently every little particle which was to produce the same effect as others, and so on.)",["The Latin sentences in the middle (*Quantitas est discrimen internum…*, *non plure plura…*) are corrupt or garbled in the transcript and probably in the OCR; I give them as they stand without emending. 'mere similium' and 'non plure plura' are doubtful readings.","The parenthesis at the end opens with ')' in the OCR ('Leibniz; er hatte das principium indiscernibilium.)'): I have closed the sentence and kept one parenthesis; the stray bracket is an apparatus or OCR slip.","'Qualitaet…similitudo, der Quantitaet aequalitas – —': the dash is kept out as a stray mark. 'Gleichheit' is rendered 'equality', 'Ähnlichkeit' 'similarity'.","'Totaliter congruent.ia' silently corrected to *congruentia*. 'Wolf' rendered Wolff.","The sentence 'Stellen wir uns vor 2 totale aequalitates…' is obscure; I have translated it literally, supplying no further sense.","The 'Der Art nach' clause: 'Art' rendered 'kind'; the point that mere outer relation does not differentiate things is kept as in the German."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-005":["*Totaliter eadem* are those between which there is no diversity; *partialiter eadem* are those which differ in respect of single relations. Things are inwardly *eadem* if they agree in inner determinations, outwardly *eadem* if they agree in relations. Things that are altogether one and the same are entitled *numero eadem*, for it is one and the very same thing, or *unum idemque*. Where, then, is numerical unity or total identity, and where *pluralitas numerica*, or numerical difference?\n\nLeibniz wished to bring into metaphysics a proposition concerning things which agree in respect of quantity and quality and also in their inner determinations: these, he held, are *eadem*, but if they are merely in different places, they are already different. Here it is to be noted that if we regard objects as *noumena*, the proposition is true, since we are then considering two things solely through the understanding, and the understanding does not distinguish them at all, even if one places them in different relations, A with B and C with D; it is then all one whether A is compared with D or C with B. Two *entia noumena* that differ in nothing are the same. But if I regard things as phenomena, the case is otherwise. They are not *eadem*, however equal they may be in quantity and quality, if they are in different places. For then they are already *plura* and *diversa*; for that is what our representation of the space of things signifies, namely things which are outside one another and therefore different.\n\nLeibniz calls this the *principium indiscernibilium*, and wished to use it to prove that in the world two things could not be, and are not, equal and similar. The first [that they are not] is contingent and may well be true, but not absolutely so; for example, that God could not make, in one place, a thing similar to one existing in another place. That is absurd. Accordingly we call things *numero diversa* which are in different places. But difference of time gives no numerical diversity; for example, I live at different times and am nevertheless the same. We are speaking of things which are side by side.",["The paragraph break after 'numerische Verschiedenheit?' and before 'Leibniz calls…' is mine, following Kemp Smith's practice of dividing long passages; the German is continuous.","'Das erste ist zufällig' is ambiguous; I take 'the first' to be the claim that two such things are not in fact found in the world, and supply '[that they are not]'.","'Denn sind sie schon plura und diversa' (OCR) is rendered 'For then they are already…'; 'Denn' for 'Dann' is a probable OCR/transcript slip, as is the clause 'denn das bedeutet nicht unsre Vorstellung vom Raum', which I read as 'denn das bedeutet unsre Vorstellung…' dropping 'nicht' is a possibility; I have translated it as printed, with 'nicht' read as an intensifier; the reading is doubtful.","The phrase 'wie sehr sie auch… gleich sind, und sind an verschiedenen Orten' is smoothed into a conditional; the sense is that difference of place alone suffices.","*noumena* and *phaenomena* kept in Latin as technical terms; 'Verstand' = understanding, 'Vorstellung' = representation.","'A mit B und C mit D … A mit D oder C mit B': text as printed; the point is that the understanding finds no difference in the exchange."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-006":["Simultaneity (*simultaneum*) is a mathematical concept. Place is a *positus determinatus*, that is, a relation to other things in space; there is also a *positus* in time, namely *aetas*. A *positus* is nothing but the determinate relation of a thing to others, and it is referred to space and time. The *positus* in time is determined (1) by what is simultaneous, (2) by what precedes, (3) by what follows upon something. Place is not space; rather, places are in space, and one place cannot have many places. That which is in space and is not itself a space is a point; hence every place is a point. Since place must always be the simple in space, space cannot consist of points. (Place is always something simple. If, for example, I want to determine the place of Paris, it is not a matter of indifference which street I walk into; the determination is made at the observatory, but there again is a space. *Formaliter*, because it is relatively very small, it is taken as a point.) For if space consisted of places, the places would have to be capable of being thought beforehand, and that is not possible. (A thing cannot be in different places at the same time; for if it could, it would be as far removed from itself as the one place is from the other, which is absurd.) In the same way time does not consist of moments, for these are *positus determinationis in tempore*. (Motion is continuous change of place, in which I pass successively through all the intervening spaces. If I went from one place to another not through the intervening places but in the intervening time, so that I was nowhere, this would be *mutatio interrupta*. Rest is the abiding presence of a thing in one place, that is, it is [present] throughout a period of time.) In a boundary (*Gränze*) there is something positive; in limits (*Schranken*) something negative, for there a reality is lacking. The boundary of a line is a point, that of a surface a line, that of a body a surface. (*limites sunt negationis entis alicuius quatenus non est. maximum*; a boundary is a space and time, and the positive which contains the ground of the limits and by which they are determined.) (The space that is not the boundary of another space is entitled corporeal or complete space; it can abut on the boundary of another space, but is not itself that boundary. One can conceive a superficial space which can be only the boundary of another (the corporeal). A line is the boundary of superficial space, and a point the boundary of the line. From this it is also evident that space cannot consist of points, because points are boundaries. (What follows does not properly belong here. For the actuality of any accident one must think a force. Moving force is the *oppositum* of the force of inertia; this is a *contradictio in adjecto*: a force that is the ground of nothing's being done is no force. One may say *vis resistens*, but that is not *vis inertiae*: what resists is also material. Space is distinguished from matter; it is nothing but the form. Matter is that which can be intuited in space. One distinguishes matter and body. By matter people have always understood something passive, which underlies all appearances; body would be the active principle. That is false. No substance is merely passive. If matter is substance, and this lies in its concept, then it is active, otherwise no motion could inhere in it. They must be distinguished thus: *materia quo ad figuram et quantitatem determinata est corpus*.)",["Restated the opening clause 'Ort ist determinatus positus' as *positus determinatus*, keeping Kant's Latin as transcribed; the OCR order may be garbled.","'aetas' retained in Latin; 'wo ich alle Zwischen Räume succeßive durchgehe' rendered as a relative clause.","'die eine Zeit über ist' is elliptical; I supplied '[present]' to complete the sense.","'Gränze' = boundary, 'Schranken' = limits, to preserve Kant's distinction between positive and negative.","'ist ein Raum und Zeit' is odd; translated literally ('is a space and time'), possibly corrupt in the transcript.","'iecles accidens' read as 'jedes accidens'; 'Kaum' read as 'Raum'; 'Cörper' rendered 'body'. The parentheses are left as in the source, including an unclosed one."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-007":["Complete space is corporeal space. Motion is the continuous change of the place of things. Things that are external to one another cannot be in the same place, and one thing cannot be at different places at the same time, for one would have to see it at the other places too and call it another thing. The act by which I form a distinct concept of magnitude through the successive addition of the unit is measuring. The force that begins a motion, continues it, and resists it is moving force. The force of resistance is entitled *vis inertiae*. The name, however, is unsuitable.",["'denn man würde das an andern Orten auch sehen und ein ander Ding nennen müßen' is compressed; rendered with 'one would have to see it', supplying 'it' by judgment.","'Begriff' = concept; 'deutlichen' = distinct."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-008":["On the relations of time. These concern only appearances, since time is not the representation of the constitution of a thing in itself. The concept of time is not the concept of things in themselves, but an order in appearances. In time the present, the past and the future are to be distinguished. Strictly, we can represent to ourselves two relations of things in time, namely so far as they are simultaneous and so far as they are successive. The present is that which is together with the representation of time; the past is what precedes the present; the future is what follows upon the present. In themselves things are neither present, nor past, nor future; these are relations according to which things have come to be in time. To the concept of time there belongs also the concept of existence, namely in the present, the past and the future. Temporal existence can be considered as a magnitude, and the magnitude of existence is duration; by this I do not understand the magnitude of things in themselves. Of the existence of the thing in itself ... (*entia in potentia* are those that can come to be in the future and the ground of whose existence is already found now.) (Ground of existence is duration. — The existence of God cannot be measured in time, nor compared with it.)",["'wie die Dinge in der Zeit geworden sind' is rendered 'according to which things have come to be in time'; the 'wie' clause is loose.","'Von der existenz des Dinges an sich selbst' breaks off; the ellipsis marks the incomplete sentence, not completed.","'ent.ia' read as *entia*; 'dei Dinge' read as 'der Dinge'.","'Grund der existenz ist Dauer' is as transcribed and seems doubtful; kept literally."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-009":["How can we form a concept of the magnitude of existence? If existence is considered in the case of phenomena, it is posited in time: if the time is long, the duration is great; if the time is short, the duration is short. We cannot represent to ourselves duration without time. This is indeed no *contradictio* of the concepts, but it has no objective reality, and is therefore problematic. — (*Perdurabile* is that whose existence endures, or whose existence is equal to a time. *Existentia non perdurabilis est instantanea.* The moment is that in time which is itself no time, and so is to time what the point is to space. What is a point? It is always the boundary of a line, where one line begins and another ends, and therefore no part [of it]; and so too the moment. The moment that closes one year at the same time begins the next.) The duration of a thing in time is the measure of the magnitude of the thing's being [*Daseyn*], in so far as it is a phenomenon. That whose being has no duration is strictly *instantaneum*. Hence we must not say that something endures, but that it exists for a moment. *Instantaneum* and *durabile* are therefore distinct. We can represent a moment as a boundary of time, but not as a part of time, for parts presuppose the time [of which they are parts]; just as little are points parts of space. Of beginning and end: an existence upon which a whole duration follows is a beginning, and an existence which a whole duration precedes is an end.) (Eternity can be represented as time *in potentia*; if I represent it to myself as having elapsed, it is nothing. — Eternity, it is said, is duration without limits. Of this we have no concept. — The duration of a thing in time, in so far as time is without limits, is being at all times. This is *aeternitas phaenomenon* or *sempiternitas*, and is to be distinguished from *aeternitas noumenon*; the latter I think as not in time, and can ascribe it to God. *Sempiternitas* is the limitlessness of a being in time. It can be thought *a parte priori* and *a parte posteriori*. (Neither has any magnitude. The beginning is the boundary *a parte priori* of the duration, and the end the boundary *a parte posteriori* of the duration, and so is itself no duration. Duration without beginning and end is the negative concept of eternity, but this too is problematic, for I cannot exhibit it *in concreto*. *Aeternitas a parte ante* cannot be conceived in time, because the *processus infinitus* may be [taken] *ut absolutus*. By *aeternitas*, as duration without beginning and end, we gain nothing, since the concept does not hold at all of noumena. *Sempiternitas* is being at all times. We now speak",["Parentheses are unbalanced in the German (several opened and closed irregularly, and the text breaks off mid-sentence); I have kept them as they stand and not completed the final sentence.","Restated: 'Dessen Daseyn keine Dauer hat…' and similar sentences kept short; 'denn sie setzen die Zeit voraus' – 'sie' is read as the parts (Theile), my bracketed supply.","'also kein Theil' is read as 'no part of the line'; bracketed '[of it]' is supplied.","Phaenomen/Phaenomenon rendered 'phenomenon' (Kant's Latin parallel), not 'appearance', because the text keeps the Latin form beside noumenon.","'weil der Processus infinitus ut absolutus seyn kann' is obscure and possibly corrupt in the transcript; 'taken' is supplied and the sense is doubtful.","Daseyn rendered 'being' and existenz 'existence'; both kept distinct by the German given in brackets once."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-010":["Of cause and the caused.",["Heading of the next section; 'Verursachtes' rendered 'the caused'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-011":["That which contains the ground of the existence of something is cause. (*Principium*, the author says, is the ground. Strictly it expresses a mode of knowledge, in so far as it is the universal ground of other modes of knowledge according to a universal rule. But it is sometimes also the ground of a thing: the Manichaeans, for example, posited a *principium boni et mali*. To the cause there corresponds the *causatum*. What can exist only as a *causatum* is *ens ab alio sive dependens*, or better *ens ab alio*; for a thing may sometimes be dependent [*dependens*] as regards its state, but not in itself, as domestic animals are dependent on man. The opposite of this is the *ens a se*. We cannot, however, see how a thing could be from itself; this is a merely negative concept.)\n\nThe *essentialia* contain the ground of the *attributa* and are *rationes logicae*, but they are not causes, because I am asking only about possibility. *Ens ab alio est causatum*; *ens a se* is self-subsistent, *quod non existit ut causatum*. The concept of a self-subsistent being is not the concept of a necessary being, for I say that it exists *a se* without distinguishing between self-subsistent and necessary. The author, however, says: only the *ens a se* is necessary, and the *ens contingens* is *causatum alterius*.\n\n'Necessary' is a concept of the understanding. Through reason I can know existence only as something that must be necessary, either absolutely or through something else. From the author's proposition it follows that everything we know as contingent must have its existence from something else. But by what do we know contingency? An *ens contingens* is that whose non-being is possible. By what, then, do I know the possibility of non-being? If the absence of contradiction were already sufficient, everything would be contingent. We have no concept of impossibility except through the principle of contradiction. [If] a non-being is a contradiction, then nothing is necessary, there is no highest original cause, and everything is *causatum*. We have no concept either of absolute necessity or of absolute contingency. For we say: contingent is whatever is not necessary in itself, or whose non-being is possible in itself. What can be thought as not being without contradiction is therefore contingent; hence everything is contingent.\n\nThe author's proposition, taken universally, would have a meaning only if I could know something as contingent in itself. We can have a concept of the contingent and the necessary, but only in the appearance [*Erscheinung*]. That is contingent which happens in time; that which neither passes away nor comes to be is necessary in the appearance. Of the necessity and contingency of things we have no concept. Do substances arise or perish? No; that was a principal proposition above. We shall say: all alterations are *causata alterius*, but not substances, for in them I do not know contingency at all; they are abiding. That every state is *causatum alterius* can well be comprehended. Since the author's proposition is correct, we cannot believe that it may be applied at once to things; it can be applied only to states, for we have no concept of the contingent in itself.\n\nEvery cause is a principle, in so far as other *causata* flow from it. *Principium fiendi* is cause (the *principium fiendi* is that which contains the ground of actuality); *cognoscendi* is judgment; *essendi* is the ground of possibility, and concerns the essence of things.\n\nMany causes, in so far as they belong to one *causatum*, are entitled *concausae*. They are either *sibi subordinatae*, when one is the cause of the *causatum* by means of the other; or *coordinatae*, when none is to be regarded as *causa remota*, but all as *immediatae*. The *concausae* are subordinated either *essentialiter*, when they are subordinated *quoad causalitatem*, that is, in respect of the determination [*Bestimmung*] through which it is the cause of the *causatum*; or *accidentaliter*, when they are subordinated not *quoad causalitatem* but *quoad ceteras determinationes*.\n\n(*Correlata* signify the *terminus* and the subject of the relation. In the relation, that which is principle of the relation is the *terminus*; that which depends on it is the subject; and both are *correlata*. Teaching [*Lehre*] is the *terminus*, for example, and the pupil the subject. Every relation is one-sided, when the one is the ground of the other's consequence and is not as much consequence as it is ground; or two-sided, when the consequence is ground and *vice versa*. The first may be called *correlata heteronyma*, the other *homonyma*. When one cause is the supplement to the sufficiency of another, they *concur*, and their *nexus* is entitled *concursus*. The less principal is called the concurring cause. The *causa causae* is *causa causatae*, in so far as it contains the cause of the causality of the subordinate cause. A father, for example, is *causa* of his son's wickedness, or when he does evil, not in so far as he brought him into the world, but when he has urged him on to evil. This is used in *theologia naturalis*.)\n\nCauses are either *efficientes* or *finales*; an *efficiens* is cause *per actionem*. That which is the ground of the existence of another is cause, whether positive or negative, material or formal. All *causae efficientes* are therefore determinations of forces. In so far as they are coordinated, or are *efficientes coordinatae*, they are entitled *sociae*. The less principal is entitled *auxiliaris*. (Efficiency is [here] opposed to the *causa formalis* [reading doubtful]. A wedge, for example, is by its form the cause of the splitting of wood, or contains the formal condition of it; the mallet with which it is driven in, and the solid matter of the wedge, are *causae efficientes*.) The causality of the *causa auxiliaris* is *auxilium*.\n\nAn *eventus* is a single action with its result. An occurrence [*Begebenheit*], considered in its connection, [is entitled] the connection or relation with other things in the same time. (The external connection in which the *eventus* occurs is circumstance. *Circumstantiae loci*, in so far as they concur in the occurrence, are entitled *opportunitates temporis*, or *tempestivitas*.) Circumstance, or the relation of the *eventus* in respect of simultaneous things, is *circumstantia*. In the *circumstantiae* the coordinated *concausae* lie hidden. *Concursus est actio* (causality) *concausarum coordinatarum*, not of the subordinate ones. All circumstances, in so far as they concur in an occurrence, are entitled occasion [*Gelegenheit*]; this is either occasion of time or of place, and much depends on both. The concurrence of the circumstances of time is *tempestivitas*, that of place *opportunitas*. It is said: *minimae circumstantiae variant rem*. For if I want to have all the *concausae*, it depends on the slightest circumstance, since even this can be a *concausa*. (Further, *locus et tempus non variant rem*, for they are mere positions in which occurrences take place.)\n\nAn *effectus univocus* is one that is of the same kind as its cause; an *aequivocus* is one that is not of the same kind with it. In physics the *effectus aequivocus* is denied, but this cannot be maintained universally; otherwise God would have to be homogeneous with the world. (An *effectus aequivocus* would occur, for example, if insects could spring from carrion. Here cause and effect would be specifically different. The *effectus aequivocus* has not up to now been completely refuted. The author, however, makes this universal and says that the *effectus aequivocus* is universal, which cannot be allowed. What are God and world, the watchmaker and the watch?)\n\n*Effectus testatur de causa.* The effect indicates the ground of the causality, that is, the determination of the cause to act, through which the *causatum* exists; for the *effectus* shows only *vires vivas*, forces applied, not *mortuas*, which have no effect. (An effect can be *mediatus* or *immediatus*, *plenus* or *minus plenus*. By *plenus* is understood the whole aggregate of the coordinated effects, that is, the whole series of the subordinated effects. Properly it is the former, yet the latter is also called *effectus plenus*. In the first case one means merely all the *effectus immediati*; in the latter also all the *mediati*; and hence one can say that a cannon-ball can bring forth an infinite series of alterations, and we obtain an *effectus plenus*. *Effectus plenus* (or the whole aggregate of coordinated effects) *est aequalis viribus efficientibus*. The author says *vivis*, that is, applied [forces]; that is, the effects prove the degree of the force, but not the capacity [*Vermögen*]. In the effects there cannot be more value than in the cause, that is, not more reality. We here consider only the metaphysical value, which rests on the degree of reality. Both are meant by *effectus testatur de causa*, that is, the effect reveals the existence of the cause, and also its quantity and quality. The *nexus* between *causa efficiens* and *effectus* is *nexus effectivus*.)",["Restated: the long opening run on *ens a se* and contingency is divided into shorter sentences; the transcript's dashes and parentheses are kept where they mark the structure of Kant's asides.","Silent OCR corrections: 'Ui Sachen' = Ursachen; 'che' = die; 'caußatam' = causatum; 'deteiminatibus' = determinationes; 'lorm' = Form; 'dse' = des; 'coordmatarum' = coordinatarum; 'wir' = wird; 'Caußalitas caußa auxiliaris' read as caußae auxiliaris.","'ein Ding rationis seines Zustandes dependens': *rationis* looks corrupt (perhaps *ratione*); I render 'as regards its state'. Doubtful.","'ein Nichtseyn ist ein Wiederspruch, also ist nichts nothwendig': translated as printed; the sense seems to require 'if a non-being is [taken to be] a contradiction', and a negation ('no non-being is a contradiction') may have dropped out. I supplied '[If]' to make the argument run.","'Zufälligkeit'/'zufällig' = contingency/contingent, matching the Latin *contingens*; 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'Bestimmung' = determination; 'Vermögen' = capacity, in the contrast between degree of force and capacity.","'Lehre ist terminus und Lehrling subiect': 'Lehre' (teaching) may be a slip for Lehrer; kept as written. The one-sided/two-sided relation sentence is tangled and is rendered closely.","'(die efficienz wird oppositio caußa formalis ...)' is garbled in the OCR. I read it as 'is opposed to the *causa formalis*' and supplied '[here]'; doubtful.","'Eventus ... Begebenheit in ihrer Verknüpfung betrachtet, heißt die Verknüpfung ...' appears corrupt or defective; translated as it stands with '[is entitled]' supplied. 'Haus Thiere' = domestic animals; 'v.v.' = *vice versa*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-012":["Of the useful: that does not belong here at all. For utility presupposes an object of willing, and willing in turn presupposes the concept of pleasure and displeasure. None of this belongs in ontology. What is a means to the good is useful. The degree of usefulness is worth or *pretium*. External worth is price; internal worth is *dignitas*, dignity. Man alone, and only in so far as he is morally good, has dignity. Misuse is a use that is contrary to utility. (The good is that which, as ground, agrees [*consentirt*] with something else. Things are good either absolutely or respectively: *bonum absolutum* is *dignitas*, *bonum respective* is *utilitas*. The price of a thing is something other than its worth. It signifies another thing which can be put in the place of this one as an equivalent, and so presupposes a standard. A man is called estimable [*schätzbar*], and also inestimable. 'Estimable' means one whose worth can be estimated, that is, who has little or high worth; and then he comes close to the inestimable.) We must still speak of",["'Mißbrauch ist ein Gebrauch, der wieder die Utilitaet. ist': OCR stray full stop; read 'wieder' as 'wider' (contrary to).","Restated the dashes of the transcript as separate sentences in the parenthesis; the sense is kept, but the abrupt note-form is smoothed.","'consentirt' kept as the transcript's Latinate verb; rendered 'agrees'. The sense is somewhat obscure.","The passage breaks off at 'Noch mäßen wir reden von' (read 'müssen'); continued in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-013":["matter and form.",["Continues the break-off at the end of the preceding passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-014":["The Scholastics distinguished in all things matter, that is, *genus*, and form, that is, *differentia specifica*. In every judgment subject and predicate constitute the matter, and the relation of the two the form. In a *ratiocinium* the premisses are the matter and the consequence the form. Form always constitutes the essence of the thing. *Materia ex qua* is, for example, the wood when a cupboard is made of it. *Circa quam* is that with which one is occupied. *In qua* are the data, in which the determinations already inhere.",["OCR 'w'omit' corrected to 'womit'.","'Bestimmungen' rendered 'determinations'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-015":["Besides the concepts of the understanding we have yet other concepts *a priori*, which are entitled concepts of reason or ideas, that is, necessary concepts of reason to which no corresponding objects can be given in experience. The meaning of a concept is determined by naming an example, and without this it has no meaning at all. Are there such ideas in us? Yes; for example, the wise Stoic in morals is a mere idea. Wisdom is perfect morality without defect or exception. Such perfection I cannot find, for every man has at least a propensity to vices, and this is not consistent with wisdom. One cannot say that such a concept is self-contradictory; it is very well possible, indeed necessary. Above all ideas stand the transcendental ones, with which we are here concerned. These are ideas of pure reason, and the one with which we are now occupied is the idea of the world. (Besides the *nexus effectivus* there is a *nexus utilitatis*, and everything stands in a *nexus utilitatis*. The *nexus usuum* depends upon man [...]. The possibility of producing utility is from providence. End [*Zweck*], means, and purpose [*Absicht*] belong to psychology. An end is that which I represent to myself as good, for the sake of which something else is regarded as good as a means. In every end there is a *causa impulsiva*, that is, the good in the end, on account of which something else pleases as means. One can distinguish end and final end. Ends can be co-ordinated, and then they are *cofines*; or they can be subordinated, and that to which all ends are subordinated is entitled the final end.) (Matter and form run through all parts of philosophy. Matter is the *determinabile*, form is the determination. *Materia ex qua*, for example three sides [*Seiten*], is the *determinabile*. *Circa quam quidam occupatus est*: for example, what does the preacher treat of? If the determination has already taken place, it is *materia in qua*, for example the wooden clock.) *Id cuius simile intenditus est* is the *exemplar*; the copy is the *exemplatum*. An *exemplar non exemplatum* is an original. *De signo et de signato* does not belong here at all.)",["Divided the long run of parentheses into separate sentences; the text's brackets are kept. The final closing parenthesis has no matching opening one in the transcript and is kept as printed.","'Der nexus usuum kommt auf den Menschen an meiner.': the last word is garbled (perhaps 'meiner [Meinung]'); I translate what can be made out and mark the gap with [...].","'cofines' is doubtful (perhaps 'confines'); left as it stands. 'intenditus' (presumably 'intenditur') is left as printed.","'3 Seiten' is ambiguous (sides or pages); left literal. OCR 'deterrninabile' corrected to 'determinabile'; 'Lastern ,' spacing corrected.","'Zweck' rendered 'end', 'Absicht' 'purpose', 'Endzweck' 'final end'. 'heißen' rendered 'is entitled'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-016":["On Cosmology",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-017":["Introduction to Cosmology",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-018":["The concept of a world in general is one of the restrictions which reason imposes upon the world of sense. Now we have two kinds of concepts in our mind: concepts of the understanding and concepts of reason. Concepts of reason arise when a concept of the understanding is enlarged to infinity. Thus we have sensible concepts *a priori*, concepts of the understanding *a priori*, and concepts of reason *a priori*. Concepts of reason are entitled ideas. They are representations whose object cannot be given adequately in any possible experience; yet they are quite indispensable to reason, and they do not contradict themselves at all. These transcendental ideas have also this use, that, since they lead us beyond all possible experience, they make us believe that there may be things outside the field of experience. All these ideas relate to, and consist in, the absolute totality of the conditions in the world. The absolute totality of the series of conditions which obtains among the objects of experience is the cosmological idea. This divides into four particular ideas, according to the categories:",["'Einschränkungen der Sinnen Welt durch die Vernunft' is ambiguous; I take it as restrictions imposed by reason upon the world of sense.","'Seele' rendered 'mind' for consistency with 'Gemüt' only loosely; the German is 'Seele' (soul), and 'mind' is a choice.","Broke off at the colon, where the transcript continues with the list of four ideas."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-019":["1. The cosmological idea of the quantity of the world. We cannot know this through experience at all. For if the world were infinite, experience could not reach it in that way; and if it were finite, I cannot experience the limit, for I can experience nothing negative.",["'so ginge das so nicht an' is loosely worded; rendered by sense (experience could not compass an infinite world)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-020":["2. The cosmological idea of quality, or of decomposition: that the world consists of simple parts. This also I cannot know from experience, since experience takes place through the senses, and space and time, these forms of sensibility, do not consist of simple parts.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-021":["3. The cosmological idea of relation, or the absolute totality of the order of nature. Since in the world the cause of the conditioned is always again a condition, I seek the unconditioned, in order to reach absolute totality. This experience cannot give me, since it is not met with in the world but lies outside it.",["OCR 'Ra' read as 'Da' (since)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s08-022":["4. The cosmological idea of the necessary: that for the sake of absolute totality the supreme being is necessary. These ideas have the following uses. (1) They impel us, in the events of the world, always to infer from one cause to another, in order to arrive at absolute totality. (2) They teach us that something must exist besides the world of sense. In so far they have an immanent use. But since they have no examples in experience, they are empty concepts and can teach us nothing about their objects.",["OCR 'Ra' read as 'Da' (since); 'Obiecten' read 'Objecten'.","The '2.' after the first use is read as the second item of the use of the ideas, and the first use is numbered (1) by me for clarity; the transcript has only '2.'. This is a supplied numeral."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-001":["First Section. On the concept of a world. First Chapter. On the positive concept of the world.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-002":["The world is a *totum substantiale quod non est pars alterius*, that is, absolute and not respective, since in no respect is it a part of another. Mere connection by coordination of things in *commercium* is a combination of parts into a whole; but connection by subordination, as of effect and cause, is not. Hence, in the connection of the world with God, the world is no part of Him. This explanation of the world is an explanation of the *mundus intelligibilis*, where by substances we understand things as they are in themselves. It must be carefully distinguished from the *mundus sensibilis*, which is a *complexus*, the *datum* of all appearances, and where by substances we understand what is abiding in the appearances. From the confusion of these two concepts there arise the [cosmological] antinomies. If I say that the sensible world is a *totum* of substances, this contradicts itself, since space and time are infinite. I can assert of the sensible world that it is finite, and equally that it is infinite; this comes about because I take it to be one and the same as the world of the understanding. So too I go astray over the second cosmological idea, if I assume the sensible world to consist of simple parts. I may well say this of the *mundus noumenon*; for if I there remove the composition, the composite substances remain to me, and these, when they are no longer composite, must necessarily be simple. But if in the sensible world I remove the composition, nothing remains over. The sensible world is nothing other than the synthesis, or the placing together in series, of my representations of objects according to the forms of space and time. If, then, I remove the latter, I thereby remove the sensible world as well. Space and time do not consist of simple parts. These and other antinomies are the chief object and content of cosmology. Of the *mundus intelligibilis* little occurs, since through the understanding we can know of it little more than what flows from the definition. See Kant, *De mundi sensibilis atque intelligibilis forma et principiis*, Königsberg, 1770. An outsider called it fanaticism (*Schwärmerei*) to speak of the *mundus intelligibilis*. But this is just the opposite, for by it one understands not another world, but this world, as I think it through the understanding. —",["OCR garbled: 'altenus' read as *alterius*; 'sensibihs' as *sensibilis*; 'a e Antinomien' read as 'the [cosmological] antinomies' (bracketed, doubtful); 'binnen Veit' read as 'sensible world' (Sinnen Welt); 'un wenn' read as 'wenn'.","The title of the 1770 work is given by the transcript as 'de mundi intelligibilis atque sensibilis'; I give the usual form of the title *De mundi sensibilis atque intelligibilis forma et principiis* and flag the difference. 'Regiom.' = Regiomonti, Königsberg.","Restated the long sentence on the two worlds as two sentences; 'Verstand' in 'Verstandes Welt' rendered 'world of the understanding'.","'Aneinanderreihung' rendered 'placing together in series'; 'Schein' elsewhere in this set rendered 'illusion'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-003":["In the definition of the world as given, every word is significant. We shall therefore develop it further. The concepts here are purely ontological, and the definition therefore belongs to transcendental philosophy. The world is thus a *totum substantiale*, and so not merely ideal. We can think various *tota idealia* which do not make up a world; for example, I can represent to myself a *totum syllogismorum*, a *totum accidentale*, or a whole in space, and so forth. But these are mere *tota idealia*, which consist of concepts. The world, however, is a *totum reale*, which consists of substances. The author confuses these two kinds of *tota*. For he explains *series* by *multitudo* and *totum*, holding them to be synonyms.",["OCR 'idealiadenken' read as *idealia denken*. 'Der Autor' is Baumgarten, the textbook author; the definition by 'series' is thus reported here, not quoted.","Restated 'Daher gehört sie...' with 'the definition' as antecedent of 'sie'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-004":["In every whole, matter and form are to be found: matter as the *data*, and form as the manner of their combination.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-005":["In a *compositum* the matter consists of parts, and in metaphysics these are the substances, which may also be spirits. For here we take matter not as corporeal but transcendentally, as the *quacumque ratione determinabile* [that which is determinable in any way whatever]. If, then, substances are the matter of the world, accidents are not, but belong merely to the states of substances.",["OCR 'rat.ione' read as *ratione*. The bracketed gloss is my own rendering of the Latin phrase.","'Zuständen' rendered 'states'; 'derselben' taken to refer to the substances."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-006":["The form of the world is, then, the *nexus* of substances.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-007":["If the world is a *nexus* of substances, I cannot think the world egoistically; that is, I cannot say: I am the world. Everything outside me would then be not things but mere illusion. For since the world requires many substances, and in addition a *nexus* of them, I, as one single substance, cannot make up a world. —",["'Schein' rendered 'illusion' (it could also be 'semblance')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-008":["The *nexus* is ideal if I merely think the substances together, and real if the substances actually stand among themselves in *commercium*.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-009":["The form of the world is a *nexus realis*, because the world is a *totum reale*. For if we have a multitude of substances, these must also stand in connection with one another; otherwise they would be isolated. But isolated substances never make up a *totum*. If the substances are together, and so a *totum*, they must also be a *totum reale*. For if they were ideal, they could indeed be represented in thought as a *totum*, or the representations of them might make up a *totum*; but the things in themselves would not on that account make up a *totum*.",["'Dinge an sich selbst' rendered 'things in themselves'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-010":["Accordingly the author's definition of the world through a series is false, because it carries with it the concept of the *totum ideale*. Finally, there belongs to the definition of the world the *omnitudo*, *quae non est pars alterius* [which is not a part of another].",["The bracketed translation of the Latin is supplied."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-011":["A *compositum* can be either a respective or an absolute *totum*. It is a respective *totum* in so far as it is not a part of a whole of the same kind; an absolute *totum*, however, in so far as it is a part neither of the same nor of another kind. For example, a house is a respective *totum*, in so far as it is a whole of its kind; but it is not an absolute *totum*, for it is a part of something of another kind, namely of a street.",["'Ganzen derselben Art' taken as 'a whole of the same kind', i.e. as the compositum."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-012":["The world is not a respective *totum* but an absolute *totum*, in the metaphysical sense [*Verstand*]. In the physical sense the world is the earth, *relative spectatum* [regarded relatively].",["'Verstand' here is used in the sense of 'meaning' or 'sense', not 'understanding'. The bracketed gloss of the Latin is mine."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-013":["The concept of the universe is a mere problem, a concept of reason, which I can well think *in abstracto* but can never give *in concreto*. For if we are to give it *in concreto*, we must represent it to ourselves in the space which it occupies. Now if this space were unbounded, we could not count it out, but something would always still remain over. Consequently we cannot experience the totality of the world. But if the space were bounded, we still could not experience its boundary. For how can we experience that there is nothing more there? We may cease to perceive something, but it does not follow from that that it really is no more.",["'Weltall' rendered 'universe'; 'Totalitaet' rendered 'totality'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-014":["But are these transcendental or dialectical ideas (also called sophistical [*vernünftelnde*] concepts) not mere figments of the brain (*entia rationis ratiocinantis*)?",["'Hirngespinste' rendered 'figments of the brain'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-015":["No. It is necessary to reason to bring all its concepts to completeness, and therefore also to make the absolute *compositum* complete; for it is in its very nature to draft universal rules, and therefore it can tolerate nothing incomplete.",["The German 'der Vernunft nothwendig' taken as 'necessary to reason'; the 'sie' of the last clause is taken to be reason."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-016":["The unity of the manifold is threefold: *compositum*, *quantum* and *totum*. *Compositum* is unity in so far as it is composed; *totum*, when this composition is complete; and this unity is entitled *quantum* in relation to still other units.",["'Einheiten' in the last clause rendered 'units'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-017":["The world is a *compositum*, because it has a multitude of substances; and a *totum*, because all of these stand in *commercium*. Finally it is also a *quantum*, when we ask whether it is finite or infinite. All of cosmology is directed to this.",["OCR 'commereio' read as *commercio*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-018":["In ontology we have the concept of a *monas*, of a part that is no longer a whole, and in cosmology the concept of a world: of a whole that is no longer a part.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-019":["These are the two extremes in composition and decomposition. For in analysis (decomposition) I come no further than the monad, and in composition no further than the world.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-020":["The author says in his definition that the world is a series of finite substances. It is unnecessary, however, for him to add *finitorum* (or, as others put it, *contingentium*), since this already follows from the definition itself. For if the world is a *totum* of substances, and each of them requires the others as its *complementum ad totum*, then the world cannot be infinite. The world therefore consists of finite substances. Further, since the substances in the world stand in *commercium* and are in *actio et reactio*, each of them, or rather its state, depends upon the operation of another. Each is therefore alterable and contingent.",["'Der Autor' is Baumgarten, whose definition of the world is under discussion; the name is not given in the passage.","'seines complementi ad totum': 'seines' is read as referring to each substance's need of the others; the sentence is somewhat loose in the German.","'oder vielmehr ihr Zustand': Kant corrects himself; it is the state of a substance, not the substance itself, that is dependent. Hence 'veränderlich und zufällig' is rendered as applying to each."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-021":["The author now comes to the proposition *in mundo non datur insula*, that is, in the world everything stands in thoroughgoing connection (*nexus*). This lies already in the concept of a whole. For a substance separated from all the rest, and so isolated, would not belong to the world at all, since it would not be combined with the whole. Indeed, a great multitude of isolated substances would not constitute a world (isolated substances are only the matter for a world), because they would constitute no whole; each of them would be entirely alone, without any community with the others. We now come to the important question: is there only one world, or are several worlds still possible? This question, however, is ambiguous. For there may be a plurality of worlds that is disjunctive, so that another could be in the place of the one; or a plurality of worlds, collective or copulative, may be possible, so that one could exist beside the other.",["The German of the last sentence is irregular ('es ist so eine disjunctive Vielheit ... oder ... möglich'); I have smoothed it into a parallel construction with 'may be'.","'möchten' in 'kein Ganzes ausmachen möchten' is rendered as a plain conditional ('would constitute')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-022":["Is a disjunctive plurality of worlds, then, possible? Yes; for the world is not necessary, and so another could be in its place. That the world is contingent follows from the *commercium* of the substances in it.",["'eommercio' in the OCR corrected to *commercio*. The numeral '1' is rendered as 'first' in sense only, left implicit."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-023":["But is a collective plurality of worlds possible? The author says that a plurality of worlds is impossible; and indeed, if one takes the world, in accordance with his definition, as the complex of all things, then as a collective whole there can be only one. For two *universa* cannot exist. But that is the concept of a world which constitutes an ideal whole, not a real whole of substances. For to the real whole of the world there need belong, not all things, but only the substances standing in *commercium* with one another. Substances not standing in *commercium* are isolated, and can in turn constitute a particular *totum* if they are connected with one another. This *totum* may be another world, which need stand in no connection whatever with this one, and which might even, if need be, have a creator of its own.",["The question reads 'collective Verschiedenheit' where the previous paragraph and the answer have 'Vielheit'; I translate 'plurality' on the presumption of a slip in the transcript, and flag it.","'Werden' (capitalised) in the OCR corrected to 'werden'. 'dürfen' is rendered 'need' in its sense of 'are required to' (the negative form 'dürfen nicht ... gehören' = 'need not belong')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-024":["We can in general consider the world as *noumenon* and as *phaenomenon*.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-025":["The definition is a mode of knowledge of the *mundus noumenon*. For I abstract there from the manner in which such a *totum* of substances could be intuited, and find no contradiction in this idea of reason. Nor do I find any contradiction in supposing that this absolute whole should not also be found in appearances, or in the world of sense. But it is none the less impossible for me to experience it, because the connection of substances in the world of sense consists in space and time; and these are *quanta continua*, whose totality can never be discovered, since here there is a *progressus in infinitum*, and totality is possible only through a completed progress. Hence the concept of totality is in metaphysics entitled only *mundus noumenon* and *phaenomenon*.",["OCR 'm dieser Vernunft Idee', 'm der Sinnen Welt' corrected to 'in'; 'Progreßusist' split; 'Total itaetsbegriff' joined.","'Daher heißt ... nur mundus Noumenon und Phaenomenon' is doubtful: the sense may be that the concept of totality yields only the world as noumenon or as phenomenon, i.e. is applied to each separately. Rendered literally; the transcript may be defective.","'Erkenntniß' = 'mode of knowledge', since the definition is a single item; 'Vernunft Idee' = 'idea of reason'. The sentence 'Ich finde auch keinen Wiederspruch dann, daß ...' is smoothed; 'dann' is left untranslated."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s09-026":["Only one world of sense is possible. For there is only one space and one time, and two times cannot exist together; and since both of these constitute the ground of the *commercium* of all substances in the world of sense, only one world of sense is possible.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-001":["Chapter 2. On the negative concept of a world.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-002":["Here we consider the world as a *quantum*, and indeed in respect of the absolute totality of its composition and decomposition. In composition we consider the world as a *quantum*, and proceed from the parts to the whole. In decomposition we consider the world as a *compositum*, and proceed from the whole to the parts.",["The transcript's second sentence says 'Quantum' where one might expect 'compositum' or 'totum'; I translate as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-003":["With regard to composition the question arises: is the world finite or infinite? and with regard to decomposition: is the world bounded or not? Has it simple parts or not? The first question: is the world finite or infinite? There is a twofold *infinitum*: an *infinitum reale*, or what is called the *illimitatum*, and the *infinitum mathematicum*, which concerns only the appearance of things in space and time and therefore holds only of phenomena. (The *infinitum mathematicum* is here taken not *potentiale* but *actuale*. For a potential or future infinity we can always think.) Since we are here speaking of the world of sense, we understand by the infinite the *infinitum reale*, and ask accordingly whether the world of sense is infinite in space or in time, or not. Neither is true. That both contradictory judgments are here false at once comes from the fact that the concept laid at the foundation is false. If I say, for example, 'A square circle must either have corners or be round', both predicates hold, because the subject is false. So it is here also. For the question whether the world is infinite or not presupposes that the world of sense is something in itself. But the world of sense is a mere sum of appearances, and not a whole of things. If we consider it, then, as in itself, this is a *contradictio in adjecto*. Hence arise these contradictions of reason with itself, which are entitled in the Critique the antinomies of pure reason; for whether one assumes that the world is finite or infinite, one finds oneself in both cases entangled in contradictions and absurdities.",["The transcript's 'Es giebt ein doppelt infinitum' is followed by an apparently inverted gloss: the *infinitum reale* is said to be the *illimitatum*, and the mathematical infinite to concern only appearance. Rendered as it stands.","'beide Praedicate' in the square-circle example: the German says both predicates 'gelten'; the example as reported is clearly meant to show both are *false* of a square circle. 'hold' is the literal sense; the transcript is probably defective here, and I have not altered it.","OCR 'Gontradictio' corrected to *contradictio*; 'Wiedersprüche' normalised. 'Critic' = 'Critique' (the Critique of Pure Reason)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-004":["If we assume that the world is infinite as regards time, then an eternity has already elapsed, and this is absurd to think. If, on the other hand, the world is to have taken a beginning in time, a time must have preceded it, for a beginning is existence that follows upon non-existence. If, then, a time preceded, it cannot have been an empty time, since we cannot represent to ourselves a time in which nothing is. There must therefore again have been a world in that time. Consequently a world has always been, and yet has also taken a beginning. What a contradiction! A beginning is always something that happens. Everything that happens has a cause. Hence something must have preceded the beginning. Nothing can have occurred in God; what occurred must therefore belong to the world, and so there would after all be no beginning of the world.\n\nWolff seeks to extricate himself by saying: Before the beginning there was no time at all. But then there was also no beginning at all, for a beginning always presupposes a time. It may also be said that in the empty time which preceded the beginning the necessary highest being existed. But then it would have effected nothing in this empty time, and at the time of creation would have effected something, so that a change would have had to occur in it. And why did this author not create the world sooner? These are nothing but insurmountable difficulties.\n\nFurther, if I assume that the world is infinite as regards space, I thereby assert that the world is a whole which, through all possible composition, yet cannot be thought as a whole, and in which, therefore, the composition is never completed. But to think totality and infinite composition together is a contradiction. If, finally, I assert that the world is finite as regards space, then besides the space which the world occupies I also assume an empty space. (By experience I cannot establish at all that the world is finite. For if I came to the boundary of the world, experience would teach me no more than that I notice nothing further, not that nothing at all is there any more. — )",["Restated the long run beginning 'Nimmt man an' as shorter sentences and paragraphed at 'Wolf' and 'Nehme ich ferner'; 'Wolf' rendered in the usual spelling 'Wolff'.","'denn eine Zeit in derselben kann man sich nicht vorstellen': the sense required is that no time can be represented in an empty time (i.e. one in which nothing is); I have rendered accordingly rather than literally.","'Urheber' (author) refers to the necessary highest being; 'unerläßliche' is probably a slip for 'unüberwindliche/unauflösliche'; I translate 'insurmountable' by sense.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Sehöpfung', 'unendliehe', 'mehi', 'Totalitaet'. Parentheses kept as in the source; the closing dash is left as printed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-005":["But why is it not also in the empty space? Is it in motion or at rest? It can move and change *in infinitum* without anything being altered either in the world or outside it in the empty space. A change would thus occur in which nothing would yet be changed. How absurd! From these antinomies we see clearly that the world, as regards time and space, can be neither finite nor infinite. In the *mundus noumenon* we may well think that the world is finite and consists of simple parts, but not in the world of sense. What then is it? The world of sense is not a whole of things, but a mere sum of appearances. (The world of sense is nothing other than the sequence of my representations of things, which I receive through sense according to the relations of space and time. Consequently it lies merely in my head, and is given not in itself but in the progress of my experience of things. For example, a rose is not red until I look at it, for its redness rests on the constitution of the eyes. The rose indeed contains the cause of its redness, but that it is actually red rests on my eye. There are, after all, human beings who see no colour at all, but regard everything as in a copper engraving, as light and shadow. — This progress can in our case go on to infinity. Things underlie the appearances, but these make up the *mundus noumenon*, and this, by its concept, is of course finite as a *totum*. The *progressus in infinitum* is an *infinitum potentiale* but not *actuale*, for the latter is absurd. Hence it does not yet follow that the world is infinite.)",["'sie' in the opening question refers to the world (die Welt) of the preceding passage; the passage begins mid-argument.","Parenthesis opened at 'die Sinnen Welt ist nichts anders' is closed only at the end of the passage, as in the source; I kept it, though the structure is irregular.","'ist bei ihr die Ursache ihrer Röthe': rendered 'the rose contains the cause of its redness' to make the antecedent plain.","'Sinnen Welt' rendered 'world of sense'; 'Kopf' kept literally as 'head'. 'Ia' read as 'ja'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-006":["The sum of appearances I can experience, and this experience can go on to infinity, or have a *progressus in infinitum*, without my thereby determining that the world itself is infinite. For the expressions 'Something is infinite' and 'It goes on to infinity' are distinct from one another. If I say that the world or the progress goes on to infinity, the infinity here belongs not to the object but to my concept of it. For all estimation of magnitude I know through counting. But this shows us not the magnitude of things themselves, but their relation to my faculty of estimation. It is merely a condition of our understanding.",["'Begrif' read as 'Begriff' (concept); 'Progreßus' normalised to *progressus*.","'Schätzungs Vermögen' rendered 'faculty of estimation'; 'Gegenstand' rendered 'object'; 'erkenne ich' rendered 'I know' (Erkenntnis-cognate)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-007":["I cannot, therefore, straightway say that the world is on this account infinite in itself. What is infinite is only my progress [*Progreßus*] in its appearances, and this is so merely for our nature, since for a higher being the world would after all be finite. But is this sum of appearances not composed of things in themselves?",["The last sentence reads 'Sind aber diese Summe ... nicht Dinge selbst?'; the number disagreement (plural verb, singular 'Summe') is Kant's/the transcriber's, and I have rendered it so that the sense is smooth, with 'composed of' supplied.","Split the first sentence into two for clarity; 'deshalb' is rendered 'on this account'.","*Progreßus* retained in Latin form in brackets as a technical term."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-008":["No. For the world of sense lies merely in my senses. These, however, show us only the manner in which they are affected by things, and not these things themselves. They show us merely the appearances of things, and these are not the things themselves. The things do indeed underlie the appearances, and I can therefore infer from the appearances to the actuality of things, but not from the properties of the appearances to the properties of the things in themselves. For example, the colours of the rainbow are the mere appearance of it, mere refractions of light, but not actual things. The drops of water, however, which are the cause of these refractions and consequently are things in themselves, we do not see; we see merely their appearance. This is something quite different, and from it we cannot infer to the drops of water in the least. The present world of sense rests, therefore, merely on our sense. Had we other senses, the world would appear to us quite differently; we should see a new world. And even if the whole world of sense should some day pass away, the things in themselves may nevertheless remain; only the appearance would then be altered. Perhaps this may be the case in a future world, where we shall intuit things as they are in themselves; that would be an intellectual world. Since God is the cause of the *mundus noumenon* and of the possibility of *commercium* in it, we should, if we could know its source intuitively, thereby also know the things in themselves. But the possibility of this we cannot see. Malebranche and others built upon this, but sought already here to have intuitions of God, which is fanaticism [*Schwärmerei*], since God is not an object of possible experience.",["'Sinnen Welt' rendered 'world of sense'; 'Sinne' in 'beruht auf unserem Sinne' rendered 'sense' (singular) as in the German.","In 'Diese sind aber nicht die Dinge selbst' the antecedent is 'Erscheinungen'; in 'Sie liegen ... zum Grunde' the antecedent is 'Dinge' (the things), which I have made explicit by 'The things'.","'von dieser' (the appearance) clarified by 'it'; 'Schlüßen' = 'inferring'.","*mundi Noumeni* and *Commercii* are Latin genitives in the German; given in nominative/normalised form *mundus noumenon*, *commercium* in the English.","'anschauend erkennen' rendered 'know intuitively'; 'Anschauungen Gottes' = 'intuitions of God'.","Several sentences were broken or run together for clarity; 'Sind ... anschauen werden' is rendered with 'where' for 'da'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-009":["There is also an intelligible world, which exists merely in my understanding, outside all possible experience; but in it there is no question at all of space and time. —",["OCR space before the semicolon silently corrected.","*intelligibile* rendered 'intelligible'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-010":["The world of sense exists only in the senses, and in the composition of the appearances [Erscheinungen] belonging to them. This composition, however, is a *progressus*. Now the question arises whether the *progressus* of the composition of appearances is finite or infinite. It is infinite; that is to say, it proceeds in such a manner that I cannot determine its end. —",["OCR 'fiägt es sich' silently read as 'fragt es sich'; 'Progreßus' normalised to Latin *progressus*, kept in italics as a technical term.","'Sinnen Welt' rendered 'world of sense' (Sinnenwelt); 'in den Sinnen' kept literally as 'in the senses'.","The first sentence is awkward in German ('in ihrer Zusammensetzung der Erscheinungen'); I have rendered 'ihrer' as referring to the senses, but it could refer to the world; sense is little affected.","'Diese' is taken to refer to the composition (Zusammensetzung), the feminine noun, not to the world; hence 'This composition'.","The German 'Nun fragt es sich' is restated as 'Now the question arises whether…'; 'bestimmen' rendered 'determine'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-011":["Among appearances there is no first, since space and time have no first parts. Suppose the progress were finite; then it would also have to be bounded. But the world of sense lies simply in experience, and consequently I would have to experience the limit of appearances as well. Now I cannot experience the end, since it is nothing. The progress is therefore infinite.",["Progreßus is rendered 'progress' (the Latin *progressus*); the same applies to Regreßus, 'regress', in the following passages.","'m der Erfahrung' in the OCR is read as 'in der Erfahrung'.","'Sinnen Welt' is rendered 'world of sense'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-012":["So too the regress in time is infinite. For if we wished to know the beginning of time, we would have to have existed before the world, and yet we would belong to the world. The beginning of time is its existence, before which a time precedes in which it was not. This is a contradiction, for time is thereby presupposed. And so all the contradictions are removed if we assume that in the world of sense a *progressus in infinitum* takes place with regard to space, and a *regressus in infinitum* with regard to time, but that the world in itself is neither finite nor infinite, because we know nothing of things in themselves and also cannot infer to them from their appearances. The error in the representation that the world in itself is finite or infinite lies in this, that reason took a regulative *principium* for a constitutive one. The world is therefore not an infinite *compositum*; rather, my composition of it is infinite.",["Restated the long fourth sentence with its dependent clauses as one sentence, keeping the structure; 'const.itutives' in the OCR is read as 'constitutives'.","'Vorstellung' is rendered 'representation'; 'Ansehung' as 'with regard to'.","'derselben' in the last sentence is taken to refer to the world."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-013":["It is the same with decomposition, or with the question whether the world consists of infinite parts or does not so consist. Both concepts are false. For if I assume, for example, that a line consists of finite parts, then it consists of simple parts. These are points, points are places, and the line would therefore consist of places. Places would then be thinkable prior to space; but that is contradictory. (The simple parts in space are points. But these do not make space possible, as would have to be the case if space consisted of points; rather, space makes them possible. If I think two points, I cannot think them otherwise than that a space is between them. Space therefore does not consist of points, nor of any simple parts. For if I remove the composition, nothing remains to me. So it is with space, and so with appearances.",["The opening parenthesis is never closed in the source; I leave it unclosed rather than supply a bracket.","'Beide Begriffe' is taken to mean both the finite and the infinite division, as the argument requires.","'Oerter' is rendered 'places'; the sense is positions in space."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-014":["If, on the contrary, I assume that the line consists of infinite parts, then its composition must be infinite. But since I can in fact compose a line, my composition is completed, but finite. Hence the composition here is both finite and infinite, which is a contradiction.",["'Wiederspruch' (modern Widerspruch) is rendered 'contradiction'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-015":["Here, then, the *noumenon* is again confused with the *phaenomenon*. For the world, considered as *noumenon*, must consist of simple parts, since otherwise it could not be composed. But as *phaenomenon* it is otherwise. Things in themselves have neither finite nor infinite parts (at least we cannot know this); rather, the regress of my decomposition is infinite.",["*Noumenon* and *phaenomenon* are kept in Latin, as Kant uses them as technical terms."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-016":["For in space and time there is no last. They are *quanta continua*. Consequently my decomposition of them, and so also of the other things that are found in them, is infinite. These questions of composition and decomposition in the world concern the totality of the synthesis of appearances, so far as they constitute a *compositum*, and this is mathematical synthesis.",["'Polglich' in the OCR is read as 'Folglich'; 'Com- und Decomposition' is rendered 'composition and decomposition'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-017":["We now come to dynamical synthesis in appearances, in so far as things are connected with one another as causes and effects. This is the deduction of causality, and the relation between effects and cause is therefore its object. Mathematical synthesis, however, is merely synthesis of aggregation.",["'Obiect' is rendered 'object' (Gegenstand would be the Kantian term; here the transcript's word is *Objekt*).","'Zusammenhängen' in the OCR is read as 'zusammenhängen'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-018":["We are now speaking solely of series in the connection of our manifold representations. A series exists when one representation is the condition of another. The advance in space and time is also a series, but a series of appearances. If in the series of causes I go from the conditions to the conditioned, that is progress; from the conditioned to the conditions is regress. The progress *in infinitum* is an *infinitum potentiale*, and must therefore not be thought as a totality. In the case of space, however, we represent the things all at once, and there totality must be present. The whole of cosmology has to do with the regress of time. For reason demands the totality of the conditions, not of the conditioned. In space progress and regress are one and the same, for there every part is at once condition and conditioned. That the world is infinite with regard to causes is the constitutive *principium* of reason. That is, in the series of subordinated causes in the world there is no first. But if one asserts that there is a first, one asserts a *regressus in indefinitum*. In the world neither a regress *in infinitum* nor one *in finitum* can be thought, neither a first cause nor a causality carried on to infinity. These are again antinomies of reason, in which *noumenon* and *phaenomenon* are again confused.",["Several sentences are lightly divided or recast; the sentence on the world's infinity 'in Ansehung der Ursachen' is kept as the transcript has it, though calling it a 'constitutive' principle of reason sits oddly with Kant's doctrine (regulative) and may be a note-taker's slip.","OCR 'Prmcipium', 'defimtum', 'fimtum' are read as *principium*, *indefinitum*, *finitum*; 'in defimtum' is probably *in indefinitum*, but the 'in' + 'definitum' reading ('regress to a definite point') is also possible.","'Regreßu' is the ablative/dative form of *regressus* in the German text; rendered 'regress'.","'Pro- und Regreßus' is rendered 'progress and regress'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-019":["Things in themselves are possible only in a series, that is, in the connection between cause and effect. But the series cannot be infinite; it must have a first cause, which is no longer the effect of another. Here, then, we have a *regressus in finitum*, and that holds of the *mundus noumenon*. No infinite regress is possible here, for since outside this infinite series of conditions no further condition would be found, one of the conditions in this series must not again be a conditioned but a first condition; because if it were not unconditioned, it would be at once conditioned and yet without condition, which is a contradiction. The regress is *rectilineus* if I go on always in the same direction, or *curvilineus* if the last consequence is first cause of the first cause, and so it goes round as in a circle. But even there a first cause is still present. This is as little valid as a circle in a proof. A commandant in a Flemish fortress, during a siege, had provisions but no money. He therefore sold the former to the sutlers; these gave him money, which he paid to the soldiers; they paid it to the sutlers, who paid it again to him, and so on. So a quantity of money kept circulating as long as the provisions lasted; yet these were the supreme cause.",["'Mundo Noumenon' is rendered *mundus noumenon*, as the German has an ablative-looking form.","The sentence 'denn da außer dieser unendlichen Reihe…' is awkward in the German; I kept its sense and the semicolon structure, with 'one of the conditions' resolved as the subject.","'Marquetender' is rendered 'sutlers' (Marketender)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-020":["But in the appearance there is no regress *in finitum*. For there is nothing first in time, that is, nothing before which no other precedes. But if time is infinite in the regress, the series of events in time must also be infinite. Nature is the connection of appearances according to universal laws. Now this connection is of various kinds, and to it belongs also the series. For example, the inherence of the predicate in the subject is also a connection, but not a series; for the subject does not again inhere.",["I supply 'of the predicate' in 'inherence of the predicate in the subject'; the German has only 'die Inhaerenz vom Subject', and the sense of the clause is rather that a *predicate* inheres in a subject. This is flagged as a supplied reading; a more literal 'the inherence of the subject' is possible.","'Begebenheiten' is rendered 'events'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-021":["In all appearances of an event, the causality of the cause of the event is again an event. Now if all causes in turn have causes, there is nothing in the world but nature. And since in the world of sense there is nothing but events, we can go on to infinity; whatever we shall experience will still be event or effect. For unless it were an event, it would be no object of experience at all. Experience consists precisely in this, that my perceptions are connected with one another through the combination of *causa* and *causatum*. If this combination is lacking, my perception is little more than a dream, which has merely private validity for me, and can never be entitled experience. In the world, then, we never get to experience an event that would be the first, since our regress goes *in infinitum*. But there is no actually infinite series of causes; it is only the regress that is infinite.",["*Begebenheit* rendered 'event'; *Wahrnehmungen* 'perceptions'; *Erfahrung* 'experience'.","Last sentence: the contrast is between an infinite series given as actual and an infinite regress; the German is terse and I have kept it so."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-022":["If, then, in the world of sense we never arrive at the first cause, there must nevertheless be one; for it would conflict with our reason to think no first cause. Now the world of sense, that is, all objects of possible experience, is and can be nothing but events, and therefore always presupposes a cause as ground. The first cause therefore cannot be in the world, but is an *ens extramundanum*. Accordingly, to say that appearances, and with them their connection according to cause and effect, go on to infinity is merely a regulative, not a constitutive, principle of reason. For appearances are not things in themselves, nor are they actual until we have reached them in the regress.",["OCR 'Begebenheitem' corrected to 'Begebenheiten'; 'in Regreßu' read as *in regressu*.","Restated the long second sentence as two; 'dahin' (to them) is resolved as 'reached them'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-023":["If an event ensues through a cause that is not itself an event, it is said of it: *sponte accidit*. It happens first, since no event precedes it. Freedom is the capacity of beginning a series of states of oneself. If something is an effect of nature, it is already a continuation of the series of states; if it is an effect of freedom, it is a new state. That is the transcendental concept of freedom. For example, if out of hypochondria I constantly [*einsetze*: sense doubtful], this comes from my nature. But if I do so merely because I will it, this comes from my freedom. The question now is whether everything in the world is nature or freedom. If we look to appearances, they are all connected according to laws of nature. But all appearances still have a transcendental cause, which we do not know. For example, the body is composite, that is, an appearance. Yet there must be a transcendental cause, containing the ground from which this appearance arises. This cause is unknown to us; but since it does not belong to the world of sense, it cannot be determined by other causes in that world, and consequently it does not stand under the laws of nature or of the world of sense, and is therefore wholly [*totaliter*] free.",["'einsetze' is left untranslated: it may mean 'stake' (gamble) or be a slip for 'einsitze' (stay indoors); I cannot decide.","*Vermögen* = capacity; *Zustände* = states; 'sich selbst anfangen' rendered 'of oneself'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-024":["Thus in the world there is on the one side nature, on the other freedom. What happens can also be regarded as an effect which has its ground not in the series of appearances, connected according to universal laws of nature, but which happens *sponte*, according to laws of freedom. For example, when I do something under the guidance of my understanding, this, so far as it springs from the understanding, is freedom; but so far as it happens in conformity with laws of nature (namely, that I am able to accomplish what I will), it is nature. For the action also takes place in the world, and whatever happens in the world of sense is nature.",["'sie' in the last sentence is taken to refer to the action; the German has no explicit antecedent.","The text ends with a dash, kept out of the English as it marks only a break."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-025":["If I wish to explain an event in the world, and derive it from the universal laws of nature, it is a natural event. In the world, as a series of appearances, we can and must explain no event *ex spontaneitate*; human reason alone is excepted. It does not belong under the series of appearances. It is independent of the laws of nature, and in this precisely consists freedom. In respect of his powers of mind man belongs to the *mundus noumenon*, for through the understanding he can know things as they are, for example his moral relations, truth, and so forth; and in this respect his actions are free. He belongs also to the *mundus phaenomenon*, in so far as through his actions he belongs to the chain of appearances.",["OCR 'V ill' read as 'Will'; 'mundo Noumeno/Phaenomeno' given in the nominative.","The German 'und in dieser Absicht sind seine Handlungen frei, als auch zum mundo Phaenomeno' is irregular; I supplied 'He belongs also' and divided the sentence.","*Gemüths Kräfte* = 'powers of mind'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-026":["We come now to the negative principles in respect of the world. They are the universal *principia* of formed nature. These negative principles are four in number, corresponding to the four classes of the categories: the first concerns quality, the second quantity, the third relation, the fourth modality. Thus: 1. *in mundo non datur saltus*; 2. *non datur hiatus*; 3. *non datur casus*; 4. *non datur fatum*. These all relate to the *nexus* in the world.",["OCR 'non datui' corrected to *non datur*.","'geformten Natur' rendered literally as 'formed nature'; the sense (perhaps 'formal') is uncertain.","Order of the four follows the text, though it does not match the usual order of the categories."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-027":["*In mundo non datur saltus.* A *saltus* is in general an *eventus*. (So in the advance in argument, where the intermediate grounds can be understood to be included, it is a *saltus legitimus*. Here, however, *saltus* is the immediate transition from a *quantum* to what is not a *quantum*. Space and time are *quanta continua*, and the real in them is also a *quantum*. For the real is, inwardly, the sensations, and outwardly that which corresponds to them. But sensation has a degree, and an infinite multitude of degrees. The simple is, in respect of the *quantum*, a zero. From it up to the *quantum* there are infinitely many parts. A thing therefore does not consist of finite degrees, nor of finite parts, for that would be an *infinitum datum*; it is the regress in dividing that is infinite.) A *saltus* is the immediate sequence of two states wholly unconnected with one another, without passing through the states that lie between. Every body has extensive magnitude, so far as it is in space and time, and also intensive magnitude, or a degree of reality. No bodies are so closely related that the difference between them could not be still smaller. There is no passage from one appearance, one quality, to another except by a transition through infinitely many intermediate degrees; between the least of reality that we may ever assume and nothing there are infinitely many intermediate degrees. In the world there is no smallest of appearance, because in space and time there is no smallest, but everything proceeds in an infinite continuity. This proposition could also be expressed thus: everything is connected according to the *lex continui*; for example, the transition from one appearance is possible only if I run through all the infinitely many intermediate degrees. No appearance can therefore consist of the simple, since from the simple to matter there would be a sudden transition to something generically different from it. This proposition is used especially in dealing properly with alterations of the states of things, for every alteration has its degree. No body can pass from one state into another without running through all the intermediate states or degrees; for example, if a body at rest suddenly moves, it must run through all the intermediate degrees from complete rest to the degree of motion it now has. This is now to be proved:",["'Saltus ist überhaupt eventus' is kept as it stands; the reading, perhaps a transcriber's slip, is doubtful.","OCR 'bestellt' read as 'besteht', 'infinituni' as *infinitum*.","The sentence 'Es ist kein Ueberschritt ... sind unendlich viele Zwischen Grade' is garbled in the transcript; I smoothed it into a single clause with a semicolon and kept its sense.","'Avancement in argumentando' is left as in the German; 'Zwischen Gründe' = 'intermediate grounds'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-028":["Suppose a thing is in the state A and is to pass over into the state B. These are two different states, and therefore they cannot exist at the same time, but each must exist in a separate time. The thing would accordingly be in A at one instant and in B at another. Between two instants there is a time (however small it may be), for the instants themselves are only positions in time. In this time, then, the thing would be in neither of the two states, nor in any other state either, since these two are the states that follow immediately upon one another. Nothing more remains to be said of it, therefore, than that it is in the process of passing from one state to the other. But between two states there is a degree, and the transition to this degree will accordingly not take place without a series of intermediate degrees. The thing, therefore, in its transition from one state to the other, will pass through the intermediate degrees of the states. Since the opposite of this would be a *saltus* [leap], the proposition is demonstrated: *nihil fit in mundo per saltum*.",["The passage is restated in somewhat shorter sentences; the sense is unchanged.","The sentence 'Zwischen 2 Zuständen aber ist ein Grad' is obscure in the transcript, and perhaps elliptical or corrupt ('ein Grad' may stand for 'a difference of degree'). I have translated it as it stands.","'derselben' in 'Zwischen Grade derselben' is taken to refer to the two states."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-029":["In the world, therefore, everything alters by degrees, for causes always act gradually. Between the two instants there could be no empty time; the cause must have been acting, otherwise no effect would have arisen. And in that case the cause must have acted with ever increasing strength, until the effect attained the degree which it was to reach. For example, if there are obscure representations in my soul, the soul must seek to make the representations ever clearer, until at last they attain a sufficient degree of clarity.",["'diese' (the soul) is taken as the subject of 'suchen'; the German construction is loose and has been smoothed.","*Vorstellungen* = representations; *Seele* = soul."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-030":["Since all alterations, then, proceed without a leap, but *in continuo*, this is also entitled the *lex continui* [law of continuity].",["'heißt' rendered 'is entitled'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-031":["I cannot describe a triangle or any other rectilinear figure by a *motum continuum* [continuous motion]. For at each corner I rest for some time. In the movement of a body from A to B I rest for an instant in B, and in moving from B to C I rest for an instant in B. Between two instants there is a time in which the body ceases from its movement; hence there is here a *motus interruptus* and not a *continuus*. Curved lines, on the other hand, admit of a *motus continuus*.",["The transcript reads 'von B bis C ruhe ich einen Augenblick in B', which is probably a slip for 'in C' (or for the resting at B before setting out for C); I have translated it as it stands.","The Latin *motum continuum* is kept as written, though the case is inconsistent with the context."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-032":["In respect of possible completeness and manifoldness I can represent the world to myself as infinite. But since this material element does not yet constitute the perfection of the world, the connection of the substances with one another must also be added. In the end the substances in this world form a complete whole, to whose perfection nothing is lacking. According to this connection and arrangement the world cannot be complete, although it might well be so according to another. This is the complete nature of the world: its formal perfection, and certainly the chief one, [consists] in this, that everything is in the fairest harmony and order. This natural order is to us so sacred and inviolable that we should certainly despise a world which, *ratione materiae* [as regards matter], was ever so much better than this world, but *ratione formae* [as regards form] was worse, and should rank it far below our own. The perfection of the world can also be thought in the teleological sense (that is, according to ends, or practically). If all possible ends can be united in one chief end, this is entitled the *summum bonum*. — The *perfectum* and the *optimum* are therefore distinct from one another. The whole of nature is thought as a realm. A realm is the relation of many to One as *summum imperans* [supreme ruler] or lawgiver, and so it is also in nature. What once happens under certain circumstances happens under the same circumstances through all eternity. The *summum bonum* is accordingly the most perfect realm of nature, as the most complete system of all ends.",["'Hie gesamte Natur' is read as 'Die gesamte Natur' (OCR). 'Natur Ordnung' read as 'Naturordnung'.","The sentence 'Dieses ist die vollständige Natur der Welt : Die formale Vollkommenheit ...' is irregular; I have supplied '[consists]' in brackets to complete it.","The sentences 'Endlich werden die Substanzen ...' and 'Nach dieser Verknüpfung ... nicht vollständig sein' are obscure in the transcript, and perhaps corrupt; 'sie' is taken to be the world. 'Nach einer anderen' is rendered 'according to another [connection]' implicitly.","'Summum Imperantem' is kept in the Latin and rendered 'supreme ruler'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-033":["Second Chapter",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-034":["On the Reciprocal Influence of Substances in the World.",["'W eit' is read as 'Welt' (OCR)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-035":["The possibility of the *nexus* of substances is the possibility of formal perfection in the world. This I can no more understand than the possibility of the first fundamental power of things. For every substance exists through its concept for itself, and therefore seems to be isolated and to have nothing whatever to do with the other substances. It is indeed said that it could not be otherwise, for if one body moves, it moves along with it the body that is in its way, and therefore acts upon it. But here *nexus* in space and time is already laid down as the ground, and one would then have to ask, how are space and time possible? This question did not enter philosophy until the question was raised how the soul, as a spiritual being, and the body, as an extended being, could stand in *commercium* with one another. This one would have to ask in a general reference: how are substances in general possible in *commercium* with one another? Leibniz first brought this on to the path. After him the investigation was carried to the highest point by Wolff (in whose philosophy the proposition first appears) and Baumgarten. But now, when people seek merely popularity and are ready to let thoroughness go, this proposition too has been left lying, although it is one of the most important in the whole of philosophy. (*Influxus realis* presupposes a passion, which, however, is at the same time *actio*. How this is possible I cannot derive at all from the concept of substance. In the *mundus phaenomenon* it is possible from the mere existence of substance in space — for space connects them all. In the *mundus noumenon* it is possible only if one assumes a common cause, that is, God, who has already laid this in the nature of substance. That is Leibniz's *harmonia praestabilita*. The *influxus physicus* is a *qualitas occulta*, for nothing is known through the word. The concept of space accomplishes in the *mundus sensibilis* what the divine omnipresence accomplishes in the *mundus noumenon*, and one may therefore call it, as it were, a phenomenon of the divine omnipresence. Perhaps God has wished thereby to give us a sensible knowledge of his omnipresence. Newton called it the *sensorium* of the divine omnipresence. Perhaps space is also the only sensibility that belongs to all rational beings other than God.) (NB. As Leibniz's system is presented here, he presumably also understood it so himself; only because he expressed himself obscurely was it taken in another sense, which is false.) All connection of substances with one another can be regarded either as *nexus realis* or *commercium substantiarum*, when substances act reciprocally on one another, or as *nexus idealis*, i.e. *harmonia substantiarum absque commercio* [harmony of substances without commerce]. The *nexus idealis* is not a connection in the things themselves, but merely in the idea of the spectator who considers them. For example, when I hear the most agreeable music, I sense in my hearing a harmony of all the instruments. This harmony, however, is merely in me and in my thoughts; the instruments themselves have not the slightest harmony with one another. According to this *nexus idealis* two systems have been devised, which are to explain the *commercium* of substances with one another. To these belong, first, the Cartesian *systema harmoniae occasionaliter stabilitae* or *systema occasionalismi vel assistentiae*; second, the Leibnizian *systema harmoniae praestabilitae*. Both are *systemata externa stabilita* [systems of external establishment]. For since the *commercium* can never be explained from the nature of substance, they derive it from an *ens extramundanum* [a being outside the world].",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Hie/Hiese/Henn/Ha/Hieses/Hing/Hie' read as 'Die/Diese/Denn/Da/Dieses/Ding'; 'Präge' as 'Frage'; 'ei’klärt' as 'erklärt'; 'Philosphie' as 'Philosophie'.","'ente extramundo' is rendered *ens extramundanum* in the translation; the transcript has 'ente extramundo'. 'mundo sensibilis' is kept as in the source (the Latin is irregular).","'Dieses müßte man in allgemeiner Hinsicht fragen' is rendered 'This one would have to ask in a general reference'; the sentence is loose in the transcript.","'Phaenomenon' in 'ein Phaenomenon der göttlichen Allgegenwart' is rendered 'a phenomenon'; Kemp Smith would write 'appearance' (*Erscheinung*), but the Latin form is kept here as Kant's own technical term.","'Systema ... assistentiae': 'Aßistentiae' read as 'assistentiae'; the long parenthesis is retained as in the transcript."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-036":["1. The *systema assistentiae* of Descartes consists in this: in every cause and effect, or in every influence of substances, God would be the *causa intermedia*, and would arrange the effects harmoniously with the causes. For example, in Leibniz there is the custom [Gewohnheit] that only one clock strikes of itself, while the others are struck with a hammer by certain people. In this way all the clocks strike together. Each of them now agrees [harmonirt] in my ear. But this harmony between the first clock as cause and the other as effect does not come from the first clock. It comes from *causae intermediae*, the people who strike the clocks, and these produce the harmony among the clocks. The connection [*nexus*] of substances in the world is of the same kind. Descartes devised this system in the first place in order to derive from it the *commercium* of the soul with the body. For he could not conceive how the soul could act immediately upon the body, and so he assumed a *causa intermedia*, which on every occasion governed the body in accordance with the will of the soul.",["'Bei Leibnitz ist die Gewohnheit' is translated as it stands; the attribution of the clock example to Leibniz and the word 'Gewohnheit' look like slips in the transcript, but I have not emended them.","'Cartesius' and 'Cartes' are both rendered 'Descartes'.","Restated 'Diese harmoniren nun jeder in meinem Ohre' as 'Each of them now agrees in my ear'; the sense of the clause is slightly uncertain.","Latin technical terms (*systema assistentiae*, *causa intermedia*, *commercium*, *nexus*) are kept in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-037":["2. Leibniz's *harmonia praestabilita*. It is almost the same as the foregoing. Both assume a *causa intermedia extramundana*. But Descartes assumes it at every change of substance. Leibniz, on the other hand, maintained that the supreme being had from eternity so arranged things that they harmonize with one another, so that the change of one appears to be the work of another. For example, if clocks, all of which are driven of themselves by their own works, strike at the same time, this harmony does not come from the clocks themselves but is *praestabilirt*. The author of it has so arranged them. Both these systems, however, rest upon the *nexus idealis*. But if substances stood in no other *nexus*, there would be no world, but only an ideal whole in thought. Each substance would then be rational and isolated, if it were itself to effect nothing. It has rightly been objected against this that, as regards the soul, bodies would then be wholly superfluous. For God could equally have let the representations of the body arise in the soul, either *occasionaliter* or *praestabiliter*, without actual bodies being necessary. These representations could always harmonize with the soul. All this, as is evident, amounts to idealism. It can also be said, further, that *ratione corporum* things would be superfluous. For since souls effect nothing, God could effect all the changes in bodies without them. Moreover, one body would be superfluous in relation to another, or with regard to another. Each could be isolated. We see from all this that these systems are like brothers and sisters.",["'Ist mit ienem fast einerlei': 'ienem' is taken to refer to the Cartesian system of the previous passage; supplied 'the foregoing'.","'Da wäre ia iede Substanz vernünftig und isolirt': 'vernünftig' ('rational') is translated as it stands, though the reading seems odd; 'wenn sie selbst nichts wirken möchte' is rendered 'if it were itself to effect nothing'.","'m den Körpern' is silently read as 'in den Körpern' (OCR).","'Hernach' is rendered 'further'; 'Vorstellungen' = representations."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-038":["But there is still a second *nexus* in the world, namely the *nexus realis* or the *influxus*, when substances influence one another reciprocally *per actionem et reactionem*. This is possible in two ways: either as *originarius* or as *defectivus* [sic]. The former is the influence of those substances which are capable, merely by their existence, of acting on one another. The question now is: can substances be in *commercium originarie*? No; for *substantiae originariae* are *per se existentes*, and therefore independent or isolated. Since they would then exist in such a way that no determination of their existence depended on anything else, they would also not be in *commercium* with one another. A *totum originarium* of substances is therefore impossible.\n\nThe second [way] is *derivationes*, when substances exist in such a way that the possibility of their *commercium* depends upon a third substance. If substances are in *commercium*, they are dependent on one another, and consequently contingent and *causatum alterius*. But the cause must be an *ens originarium*. Many *entia originaria*, however, cannot be the cause of the world and of its *commercium*. For since every *ens originarium* would be isolated, its work too would have to be wholly isolated from the work of the other. But we do not find this in the world, for there everything stands in thoroughgoing *nexus*. Accordingly the world can have only one cause. The *nexus* of substances can therefore be thought possible only as derivative, and moreover not as ideal but at the same time as real.\n\nThis proof, however, holds only of the *mundus noumenon*. In the *mundus phaenomenon* we do not need it, for nothing there is anything in itself. Here everything is in *commercium* by virtue of space. The systems of occasional and of pre-established harmony have a place only in the world of sense. For here the question is whether God arranged the harmony at the beginning or in its continuance, and this presupposes time. *Influxus physicus* is entitled *crassiori sensu originarie*, in so far as God now effects nothing in it; *sensu subtiliori* or *derivatus*, in that the possibility of the influence nevertheless comes from God as the *ante originario* [sic]. —",["'au zwiefache Weise' read as 'auf zwiefache Weise' (OCR). 'Defectivus' is probably a slip (or misreading) for 'derivativus', since the second member is later called 'Derivationes' and 'derivativ'; I give it as it stands with [sic].","'2 Derivationes' is a fragmentary note; I supplied '[way]' and left the plural Latin form.","'Weil sie denn aber so existiren möchten' is somewhat garbled; rendered by the sense the argument requires.","'Ante originario' is doubtful (perhaps 'ente originario'); left as [sic]. 'Craßiori' read as 'crassiori'.","Restated the long sentence on the two ways as separate sentences and paragraphs."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-039":["The *influxus physicus* takes place according to universal laws; the two systems of the *nexus idealis*, however, do not. —",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-040":["Chapter 3. Of the Natural.",["'Natuerlichen' normalized to 'Natürlichen'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-041":["Nature can be taken in a twofold sense: (1) *adjective*, as the particular nature of each thing; (2) *substantive*, as the world according to universal principles and laws, that is, the whole of nature, or the world of sense in its connection (*natura universa*). *In sensu substantivo* there is only one nature; *in sensu adjectivo* there are as many natures as there are things. The natural is that which can be explained from the nature of a thing; or an event is natural if its cause is to be met with in the world-whole. It may be strictly [eigentlich] natural, in that it can be explained from the nature of the thing to which it happens; or it is natural only relatively [respective], when its ground lies in another thing. Thus art also is natural, in that it springs from the nature of some thing. That is naturally absolute whose cause is to be met with in the whole of nature. This is also entitled natural *schlechthin*. (The course of nature is the succession of the series of events); or the course of nature is the connection of events, in so far as it is bound up with experience. By the course of nature I learn by experience what happens. The order of nature is the ground of this connection of appearances. — The course of nature shows the regularity of experience; the order of nature, its necessity.",["'Natürlich ist das, was ...' and 'Absolute natürlich' are rendered as 'The natural is...' and 'naturally absolute'; the latter is awkward in the German too. 'Natürlieh' is an OCR slip.","'adjective'/'Substantive' kept as Latin-form adverbs (*adjective*, *substantive*) because Kant parallels them with *in sensu substantivo/adjectivo*.","The parenthesis on the course of nature is left in place as the notes have it; the sentence is fragmentary, and 'Lauf' and 'Ordnung der Natur' are rendered 'course' and 'order'.","'Verstände' in 'im doppelten Verstände' = 'sense'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-042":["An event belongs to the course of nature in so far as it is a part of the changes that arise in the world. The order in nature always awakens in us admiration and respect. But whence does this come? From the fact that without nature we should have no objects of the understanding. Without nature nothing is an object of the understanding, since the understanding knows nothing without rules. Now we could not make any rules for ourselves if everything in the world first happened as it were without the slightest order. We should then have either no understanding at all or a useless one. Since, then, the worth of our understanding, that nobility of our soul which raises us to humanity, rests upon nature, we regard this order of nature as something, as it were, holy, and shrink from violating it, even if it should work to our harm. For example, if a wall falls down from age and kills a man, we shall hardly rejoice over it. But because this happens according to the order of nature, we do indeed wish that the man had not passed by at that time, yet we find it absurd to wish that the wall had stood longer than it could stand according to the laws of nature. —",["'die in der Welt entspringen' has a plural verb after the singular 'Veränderung'; rendered 'that arise in the world'.","'Gegenstände des Verstandes' = 'objects of the understanding'; 'Object' = 'object'.","'was Heiliges' rendered 'something ... holy'; the sentence 'Da also ... beruht' was kept as a single sentence, as no division was needed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-043":["Chapter 4. Of the Supernatural and the Possibility of Miracles.",["'Uebernatuerlichen' normalized to 'Übernatürlichen'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-044":["The supernatural, *supernaturale*, is that which cannot be explained from nature as a whole. The counter-natural, *praeternaturale*, is that which is contrary to the particular nature of a thing, but can nevertheless be known from the nature of other things or from nature as a whole. For example, that a man cannot create does not indeed follow from his own nature, but it does follow from the nature of other things, which are the cause of it. Not everything supernatural is a miracle; a miracle is only that which happens contrary to the order of nature in the world. What happens contrary to the order of nature outside the world is no miracle; creation, for example, is no miracle.\n\nMiracles are either *miracula rigorosa* or *comparativa*. The former are those which cannot be explained from nature as a whole and which have a *causam extramundanam* as their author. Since the order of nature is here interrupted, only the author, namely God, can do this. — The *miracula rigorosa* are again either *occasionalia* or *praestabilita*. The former is that which God works immediately on each occasion, and by which he interrupts the order of nature. This is the miracle in the proper sense. A *miraculum praestabilitum* is that which God works mediately through natural causes, which are, however, employed by God for particular purposes; or that which God has determined from the beginning and for the execution of which he makes use of natural causes. This is also entitled an effect of nature, but it is just as much a miracle as the occasional one. For although its proximate cause is a natural cause, this cause lies not in the laws of nature but in the foreordination of God, who willed that it should act precisely at that time and under those circumstances.\n\n*Miraculum occasionale* is also entitled material, and *praestabilitum*, formal. (A *miraculum materiale* is one where the cause and causality of the miracle lie outside the world, or where God brings forth something new by his mere power. A formal one is one where the immediate cause does indeed lie in nature, but the causality of it lies in God. Both are equally miracles, for here it is merely the form that matters; *forma dat esse rei*.)\n\nThe *miracula praestabilita* are explained as natural miracles, for it is said: God works them nevertheless according to nature, not indeed according to its general laws, but yet according to particular ones. In these, however, one must again assume a particular divine direction, and so this is an emanation of God. It is just as unnatural as the occasional one. Thus, for example, Whiston believes he can explain the Flood naturally by assuming that a comet came and poured out its watery tail, and that it happened thereby; afterwards the comet drew the water back to itself. But that is still a miracle. For it is no law of nature that a comet should flood the earth just then, and merely because its inhabitants were godless. (Teichmeyer likewise believed he had discovered something great when he showed that, if an ice-cloud passed before the sun, its shadow would then recede by that much, and that the miracle of the sun-dial of Ahaz would thus be easily explained.) The *miracula praestabilita* are still greater miracles, for they are devised still more artfully. But one is glad to make all miracles into pre-established ones, because one thereby pays one's own reason a compliment, in having been able to devise it so artfully.\n\nMeanwhile one believes nevertheless that one finds more reason in explaining a miracle as a *miraculum praestabilitum*. This does not come from our disbelieving the possibility of a miracle in general, but from this: that in the assumption of a miracle which is occasional, all our understanding comes to an end in explaining it. And if one holds that God determined these miracles beforehand, so that they should happen through ordinary natural causes, one judges of God anthropopathically; for just as it costs men more trouble to do something gradually than all at once, so one believes it costs God more trouble to perform miracles on each occasion than if he had already so arranged it at the creation.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Wfiiston' to Whiston, 'Siindfluth' to Sündfluth, 'Mira culum' and 'pi aestabilitum' rejoined, 'eße' to esse, 'caußam' to causam, stray '2' removed. 'Teichmayer' is rendered 'Teichmeyer' (the Jena physician), a guess at the intended name.","'Wiedernatürlich' (praeternaturale) is rendered 'counter-natural', and 'wieder die Ordnung' as 'contrary to the order', following Kant's Latin parallel and the sense; 'unnatürlich' is kept as 'unnatural'.","The German 'denn' in 'bloß denn und darum' and 'denn um so viel zurücktreten' is a colloquial 'then'/'at that time'; rendered 'just then' and 'then'.","The sentence on the *miracula rigorosa* is divided and 'Natur Ordnung' is rendered 'order of nature'. The long parenthesis on material/formal miracles is kept as a separate paragraph, and the paragraph breaks are mine.","The OCR parenthesis closes after 'aussinnen können' with no matching opening at 'Man mag aber gern...'; I have closed the Teichmeyer parenthesis where it plainly ends and left the following sentences unbracketed. The transcript may be corrupt here.","'miracula rigorosa ... oder comparativa': the text then speaks only of the *rigorosa*; the *comparativa* are not treated in this passage.","The text says 'ienes ... Dieses' for occasionalia and praestabilita; 'the former ... this' is the sense, and the contrasting 'Dieses ... das eigentliche Wunder' is read as referring to the occasional miracle.","'Forma dat esse rei' is kept in Latin as in the source (the printed *eße* is the long s of the older spelling)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-045":["It is, however, always well to try to explain miracles naturally; at least it is better than trying to make a miracle of the matter. Now the question arises: How are miracles possible at all? Well, *effectus testatur de caussa* [the effect bears witness to the cause]. Since an *extramundane cause* (*caussa extramundana*) is the cause of the order of nature, it can also suspend that order. No world can be thought without defects, without certain negations and limitations; and miracles are therefore possible, even in the best world, in order to supply what nature lacks, and, according to the concept of God's goodness and truth, they are indeed probable. But there is a further question: How is reason entitled to assume miracles? In general we must admit that miracles are possible; but in particular cases we must always resist assuming them, since they conflict with the subjective maxims of our reason, which command us to assume only what admits of a use of reason.)\n\n1. Every miracle presupposes an order of nature. For if there were no such order, there would be no miracle at all; everything would be miracle. Since this is not the case, we see that miracles are merely an exception to the order of nature. But exceptions cannot be frequent; otherwise all rules, and the certainty of them, cease. Miracles, therefore, cannot be frequent either. The very concept of the word distinguishes the miracle from what is of everyday occurrence.\n\n2. If we regard nature as a cause of things subsisting by itself, and God accordingly not as the creator but as the architect and governor of nature, we could assume more frequent miracles. For since matter would not come from God, it might also have many defects, which could be remedied only gradually. But since God is the creator of the world, he will surely have created it incapable of improvement. In such a world, therefore, miracles can be extremely rare.\n\nIt is also false, then, to say that he who denies miracles takes nature to be a cause subsisting in itself. The very opposite is the case. For he does so only because he represents God to himself as the creator of the world, and not merely as its architect. But since he represents God to himself as the cause of the order of nature, he believes on that account that all events in the world come from God. He assumes God, therefore, merely as *caussa mediata* [mediate cause] of all events; whereas he who affirms (*statuirt*) miracles regards God, in their case, as *caussa immediata* [immediate cause]. Rather, he who regards nature as a cause independent of God is by that very fact the less able to believe in miracles, since he represents to himself, and can represent, that nature sometimes comes into collision with God, and that God, being more powerful than nature, makes exceptions to its rule. Indeed, he may then wish for miracles, because he cannot expect a blindly running nature to attain perfection properly.\n\nHe, again, who assumes miracles everywhere, believing thereby to glorify the power of God, does precisely the opposite. For he asserts thereby the incompleteness and insufficiency of the order of nature, whose defects are supplied by miracles. But since the order of nature is God's work, he says thereby that God has made an imperfect work; and in this God's wisdom and omnipotence suffer very greatly.\n\nFrom all that has been adduced we see that miracles, if there are any in the world, must occur only extremely seldom. We now wish to see by what grounds our reason can be moved to assume miracles.\n\n(If the cause of an event is not found in nature, it is a *hiatus*. The consequences arising from it have no connection with what precedes. But in order to know this, I must know all the causes in the world, so as to know that this one is not in the world. Every object that is connected with others is experience. Miracles are spoken of as objects of experience. The knowledge of miracles does not rest on mere perception. Miracles presuppose nature and are mere exceptions from it; they must therefore not be frequent, otherwise they would abolish the order of nature and would be no miracles.)\n\nHistorical testimonies cannot prove them; moral testimonies must be added as well. To establish, then, the credibility of a miracle, one must presuppose an order of nature in the human will, such that it is impossible to lie. But this is of no avail. For here I must believe that something is contrary to the order of nature on the strength of a testimony [which holds] according to the order of nature. But can the moral order of nature not be suspended just as well? Can man not lie, and is *pia fraus* [pious fraud] something unheard of? Further, it is no miracle to deceive oneself. For it would almost take a miracle to distinguish natural events from miracles and always to know what a miracle is. Historical and moral testimonies cannot, therefore, yet convince us of the existence of miracles. There must be added this: that reason sees that there is an event which is indispensable to the perfection of the world and yet impossible according to the order of nature. This event, however, cannot contribute anything to the perfection of nature, for nature has been made by God incapable of improvement; it can contribute only to the furtherance of our moral world. This does not depend at all on the order of nature. If, therefore, miracles take place for the confirmation of a doctrine that conduces to our moral perfection, they are credible. For we see at once that God could not lay them into nature at the beginning of the world, and yet necessarily had to bring them about.",["The opening parenthesis-closing ')' before '1' has no opening bracket in the OCR text; it is probably an editorial or transcript remnant and is retained as printed. The numerals '1' and '2' I have set as paragraph openings.","Restated: the long sentence beginning 'Wenn wir die Natur…' and the following 'Es ist also auch falsch…' sequence are divided into shorter sentences and paragraphs; the logic is unchanged.","'mäßen' (OCR) read as 'müßen'; 'wieder' in 'streiten wieder' and 'wieder die Ordnung' read as 'wider' (against), rendered 'conflict with' / 'contrary to'.","'Nun frägt es sich': the first 'Ja, Effectus testatur de caußa' is kept with the Latin in italics and my bracketed English; 'Ja' rendered 'Well'.","'derienige, der Wunder leugnet … nimmt an Gott als caußa mediata': the transcript is compressed; I supply 'God' as object of 'assumes' and 'of [Gott]' accordingly. 'bei derselben' rendered 'in their case' (i.e. of miracles).","'Der nun wieder überall Wunder annimmt': 'wieder' taken as 'again/on the other hand'. 'Götter Macht' read as 'Gottes Macht' (OCR/transcript slip).","'Natur Ordnung', 'Natur Begebenheiten' rendered 'order of nature', 'natural events'. 'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'; 'Vernunftgebrauch' as 'use of reason'.","Sentence 'wegen des Zeugnißes nach der Ordnung der Natur' is elliptical; I supply '[which holds]' in brackets. The sentence 'Ferner ist es auch kein Wunder, sich zu betrügen' is obscure in the transcript and is translated literally; the sense may be that self-deception is a natural, not a miraculous, event."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-046":["This is now the only case in which we can assume miracles, and in which this is not contrary to our reason and to the wisdom of God. For the small loss that is caused by the interruption of the order of nature is here amply made good by the great advantage which mankind has from it. Miracles would also be possible if the hypothesis were true that the highest perfection is attained when men are destined to a certain community with the supreme being. —",["'ersetz' (OCR) read as 'ersetzt'.","'bestimmt wären' rendered 'are destined' (Bestimmung); 'höchstes Wesen' as 'supreme being'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s10-047":["2. *Miracula comparativa*. These are said to be events brought about by higher beings or by unknown powers of nature. But this amounts to nothing at all. For if I do not know the cause, I can infer nothing from it. Moreover, what belongs to a *miraculum comparativum* is not merely that the cause is unknown, but that the law of its operation is unknown also. Natural causes whose law of operation is known, even if the causes themselves are not, are not *miracula comparativa*; and this is the case with *qualitates occultae*. They are indeed unknown, but their laws of operation are not; hence their effects also are not *miracula comparativa*. In the case of higher beings, on the other hand, to which we ascribe *miracula comparativa*, for instance devils, we know neither the beings nor the laws of their operation. It is then wholly contrary to reason to assume them as the cause of any events. For since we cannot state any rules for their effects, we cannot determine what proceeds from them and what does not. We therefore assume them merely arbitrarily. To believe in *miracula comparativa* would be something on the lips, but not in the heart. Many people believe whatever they do not understand; they at once recognise the supernatural, because they are unwilling to use their reason. But he who believes in the natural can expect the supernatural from God more readily than can his opponent. Yet those who are so fond of the supernatural have a particular motive. They act from pride. For there are two ways to arrogance: either to raise oneself above others, or to draw others down to oneself. The latter is the easier. Since these people are unwilling to employ so much reason, or have none at all, for understanding the natural causes of such events, they envy those others, and wish to make them as dull-witted as they are themselves. Hence they try, in a veiled and pious language, to press upon them belief in the supernatural. Whence comes it that everywhere men call only old events, and not present ones, miracles? Not because miracles are no longer needed now, for that can never be proved, but for this reason: the old miracles and their consequences have already been brought under certain rules. In the case of new miracles, however, we do not know the consequences that may arise from them. They might overturn morality and everything else in our reason, and therefore we do not accept them at once.",["Restated a few long sentences in shorter ones; 'Denn ist es aber' (OCR/transcript 'Denn') rendered as 'It is then', taking the first 'Denn' as a slip for 'Dann'.","'Kall' corrected to 'Fall'; 'willkiihrlich' to 'willkürlich'; 'Wundem' to 'Wundern'; 'kennen sie' (probably 'erkennen sie') rendered 'recognise'.","'qualitatibus occultis' is given in the nominative as *qualitates occultae* for English use; 'zu schreiben' read as 'zuschreiben'.","'wäre wohl im Munde, im Herzen aber nicht' rendered literally-idiomatically, with 'something' supplied."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-001":["Third Part: On Psychology. Introduction",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-002":["All knowledge of things is twofold: either they can be given through experience or they cannot. Both kinds of knowledge belong to pure metaphysics or transcendental philosophy. Transcendental philosophy was meant to be simply ontology, but the modes of knowledge in it are twofold, as we have already stated. The objects of ontology can be given in experience, and are modes of knowledge of the understanding. But there are also modes of knowledge of reason that cannot be given in experience. These arise from the modes of knowledge of the understanding, which reason, however, so extends that they can never be given in experience. It does this by giving them absolute totality. There are two such ideas of reason: (1) the absolute totality of many things, that is, the absolute whole of the world; (2) the absolute totality of one thing, that is, the *ens realissimum*. These are treated in cosmology and theology; hence these should properly belong to ontology, and these three, ontology, cosmology and theology, constitute *metaphysica pura*. In *metaphysica pura* there occur the intuitions of sensibility, space and time; the categories of the understanding; and the ideas of reason. The second part of metaphysics is *metaphysica applicata*, whose objects are in experience but which nevertheless rest on principles *a priori*. This is the metaphysics of nature, namely (1) of corporeal nature, or of objects of outer sense, which is *physica generalis*; and (2) of thinking nature, or of objects of inner sense, that is, *psychologia rationalis*. Both together constitute *metaphysica applicata*. *Physica generalis* occurs in cosmology; *psychologia rationalis*, however, is to follow.",["'ens realißimum' read as *ens realissimum*; 'Psychologia rationale' and 'rationahs' normalised to *rationalis* (OCR); 'metaphysicam puram/applicatam' given in the nominative.","The trailing '. 1' is OCR debris and omitted.","'Die Categorien' rendered 'categories'; 'Vernunft Ideen' as 'ideas of reason'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-003":["Opposed to these two sciences are empirical physics and psychology, which draw their principles merely from experience and not *a priori*. For that reason they do not belong to metaphysics at all. Empirical physics also constitutes a special science. Empirical psychology, since it has been taken in hand only recently, is not yet so complete that it could yield a special science. Because no one knew with what other science to pair it, it has therefore been attached to *psychologia rationalis* as a stranger and guest, since it is still most nearly akin to that. We follow the author, and shall accordingly take up empirical psychology first.",["'I hysic' corrected to 'Physic' (OCR).","'Fremdling und Gast' rendered 'a stranger and guest'; 'der Autor' is Baumgarten."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-004":["First Section",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s11-005":["On Empirical Psychology.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-001":["First Chapter: On the Existence of the Soul",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-002":["The soul is the object of inner sense. — Whoever denies it, therefore, denies inner sense also. (The soul is called πνεῦμα, but that means a wholly free and pure spirit; more commonly it is called ψυχή, which signifies a *papillon*, a butterfly, and is very fitting, since it provides a splendid symbol of the soul's discharge, in dying, from the burden of the body, and its passage into a better life. (The word *Seele* properly signifies the inward part of a thing, for instance in a [?] , a *Canone*.)) My thoughts are not an object of the outer senses, for they have no figure. In myself I have the body as an object of outer senses. This, however, is distinct from the soul. Its determinations are different, for the form of its intuition is different. The form of intuition in the case of the body is space; in the case of the soul, time; for thoughts are not in space.",["The Greek is garbled in the OCR ('TCveujaa', '^uxf'); read as πνεῦμα and ψυχή, with some confidence.","'bei einer Jeder, Canone' is corrupt: 'Jeder' is probably 'Feder' (quill, spring) and 'Canone' perhaps 'Kanone' (cannon/bore); the example is too garbled to be sure of, so a bracketed [?] marks the gap.","'Papillon, Schmetterling' kept as in the German; 'Bestimmungen' = determinations."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-003":["Here we consider the soul merely as the object of inner sense, and this rests on our own experience, which no one can deny. For the rest, we are not concerned here with the question whether the soul is material or not, since we cannot prove this from experience, and it therefore belongs to *psychologia rationalis*. The soul is merely our I, not the body; yet body and soul together, as human consciousness, are also entitled I. In empirical psychology we consider our I as soul and as human being. The body we consider partly as an organ of the soul, which depends on the soul, and partly as a dwelling, since the soul also often depends on the body. A brief anthropology is therefore presented in empirical psychology.\n\nWe accept it as an indubitable experience that the soul exists. Descartes, however, and in a similar way the author [of the textbook], seek first to arrive at this through an inference. The former says: *Cogito ergo sum*. But this is unnecessary, for *cogito* or *sum cogitans* is the same. Descartes himself said that I am *immediate* [immediately] conscious of an actuality as the object of inner sense. Of the actuality of objects of outer sense, e.g. of my body, I can become conscious only *mediate* [mediately], through an inference. And yet here he infers the existence of the soul, and so offends once more against his own rule. That I exist as a human being is already an inference, and indeed one that yields no mathematical certainty, for the idealist denies the existence of bodies.\n\nEmpirical psychology divides into two main parts, namely: 1. the consideration of the soul in itself; 2. the consideration of the community of the soul with the body. The powers of the human soul divide into three main classes: 1. the faculty of knowledge; 2. pleasure or displeasure; 3. the faculty of desire. Pleasure precedes the faculty of desire, and the faculty of knowledge precedes pleasure. According as the faculty of knowledge differs, the two following differ also. There is a higher and a lower faculty of knowledge: the former is understanding, the latter sensibility. So there is also a rational and a sensible feeling of pleasure or displeasure (and so it is likewise with the faculty of desire). Through the sensible faculty of knowledge the subject is itself passive, and it is therefore said to have *receptivity*; through the higher faculty it is itself active, and therefore has *spontaneity*. These two faculties are thus distinguished by the way in which the subject is constituted in respect of them.",["The OCR text repeats a stretch ('Nachdem das Erkenntniß Vermögen verschieden ist … die beiden folgenden verschieden') with a garbled first version ('folund Erkenntniß Vermögen vor der Lust vor'); I have translated it once.","Silently corrected: 'aut' to 'auf', 'Existenzder' to 'Existenz der', 'tliatig' to 'thätig', 'sin' to 'sind'. 'Kartes' is rendered Descartes.","'der Autor' is taken to be the author of the textbook (presumably Baumgarten); the bracketed words are my supply. Paragraph breaks and the division of some sentences are mine.","Terms: Erkenntniß Vermögen = faculty of knowledge; Lust/Unlust = pleasure/displeasure; Begehrungs Vermögen = faculty of desire; Kräfte = powers; Behausung = dwelling. 'immediate'/'mediate' are Latinate adverbs in the German, bracketed with the English.","'heißen … Ich' = 'are entitled I', following the method's rendering of heißen."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-004":["We can have pleasure or displeasure without desiring or abhorring. For example, when we see a beautiful landscape it delights us, but we do not on that account at once wish to possess it. Pleasure or displeasure is therefore something wholly different from the faculty of desire. On the other hand, we can desire or abhor nothing without pleasure or displeasure lying at its ground; for what I take no pleasure in, I also do not will. Pleasure or displeasure therefore precedes desiring or abhorring. But what I desire I must first have known, and likewise that in which I take pleasure or displeasure; accordingly the faculty of knowledge lies at the ground of both. There are also many representations which are connected with neither pleasure nor displeasure, and so the faculty of knowledge is wholly distinct from the feeling of pleasure or displeasure. All these three main powers of the soul, taken together, constitute its life.",["The sentence 'Was ich aber begehre … auch' is somewhat irregular; I restated it so that 'known' governs both clauses, which the sense requires.","'Vorstellungen' = representations; 'Gefühl' = feeling."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-005":["(Consciousness is the principle of the possibility of understanding, but not of sensibility. Consciousness at will is attention; its counterpart is abstraction. The self lies at the ground of consciousness and is the peculiar property of spirit. This self we can, however, consider in a threefold way. I think as intelligence; that is, the subject of thinking is intelligence. I think as a subject that has sensibility, and am soul. I think as intelligence and soul, and am human being. A body that is animated only by a soul and not by intelligence is an animal. The capacity to grasp the thought 'I am' belongs solely to intelligence. This I remains, even when everything has changed, when body and principles have changed. What constitutes the identity of one's self is hard to know; everything refers to it, everything can change, only consciousness and apperception, or the capacity to refer the representations to one's self, remain. This apperception, in a certain degree, gives distinct representations, in a lesser degree obscure ones. These are therefore distinguished not in kind but in degree. Clearness in intuition is, however, very different from clearness in the concept. The former is aesthetic, the latter logical clearness. The actualisation of attention, whereby only a single representation is made clear and all the rest are obscured, is abstraction. In abstraction attention does not cease; it is merely directed from one or several objects upon one, and the remaining representations are all obscured and the one made clear.)",["The whole passage stands in parentheses in the source, and I have kept them.","'Willkühr' rendered 'at will'; 'Geist' = spirit; 'Intelligenz' = intelligence. I have used 'distinct' for 'deutliche' and 'obscure' for 'dunkle'.","The sentence 'Diese Apperception … Vorstellungen' is compressed in the German; I translated it as it stands.","'Die Klarheit in der Anschauung … Begriffe' is kept literally, with 'intuition' and 'concept' as fixed terms."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-006":["The first and chief representation is that of the I, or the consciousness of myself, apperception (as Professor Kant entitles it in his Critique).",["'Critic' is rendered 'Critique' (Kritik), here apparently the *Critique of Pure Reason*; the transcript does not say so, and I have not supplied the title.","The closing parenthesis is retained as printed, though the opening one is not in this passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-007":["This is peculiar to the human soul alone, and distinguishes it from all animals. The animal has no apperception, and is therefore also incapable of moral principles, of understanding, and of any use of reason, as will be shown further below. The body of mine is my body because its alterations are mine. For only by this means can I receive representations of outer things, and my body be affected by them. A being that is merely an object of inner sense cannot know its relation to things that are objects of outer sense. Where my body is, there too is my thinking I, for only through the body can I know the position of myself. I myself, however, occupy no particular space in the body, since I cannot be intuited according to the form of space. The body determines only my relation of place with regard to other things (namely, that I am in it), but not with regard to my self, nor where in it I am.\n\nOur representations are either obscure or clear, etc. Obscure representations are those of which I am not immediately conscious, but of which I can nevertheless become mediately conscious through inferences.",["Silently corrected OCR: 'Kaum' to 'Raum', 'Eorm' to 'Form', 'nehmlieh' to 'nehmlich'.","'Der Körper von mir ist mein Körper' is rendered literally; 'The body of mine' keeps the oddity of the transcript's phrasing.","'durch sie mein Körper affizirt werden' is somewhat loose; I took the subject to be 'my body' as in 'and my body be affected by them'.","'Position meines selbst' rendered 'the position of myself'; the paragraph break before 'Our representations' is mine."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-008":["Locke, on the other hand, makes the objection: of obscure representations I am not conscious. How then does one know that I have obscure representations? To be unconscious of something and yet to know it is a *contradictio in adjecto*; but this is mere chicanery.",["OCR corrected: '\\ orstellungen' to 'Vorstellungen', the stray '1' to a question mark, 'Ghicane' to 'Chicane'.","I took 'Einwurf' as introducing Locke's objection, with the colon retained; the question 'Woher weiß man denn …' is probably the transcriber's continuation of the objection rather than Kant's own question."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s12-009":["We do not, to be sure, know it immediately, but we do know it through inferences. For example, when we look at the Milky Way with the naked eye, we are not conscious that it consists of nothing but small stars; through the telescope, however, we see it. Now we reason thus: since we have seen the whole Milky Way, we must also have seen all the individual stars, for otherwise we should have seen nothing. But what we have seen we must also have represented to ourselves. Since we know nothing of these representations, they must have been obscure. We have, then, obscure representations, and indeed in such great number that they far exceed the number of our clear representations. Our soul is, as it were, like a map on which only a few places are illuminated. If all our obscure representations were at once to become clear, we should be astonished at their multitude. We should find ourselves, as it were, transported into another world. The obscure representations constitute the depth of the soul, and the multitude of them is the field of obscure representations. This name is taken from the simile (of the map).",["Silently corrected OCR slips: 'Run' to 'Nun', 'miißen' to 'müssen', 'wurden' to 'würden'.","Restated the sequence 'Nun schließen wir ... Denn wäre das nicht ...' as a short chain of sentences; 'Seele' is rendered 'soul' (not 'mind', which is Kemp Smith's Gemüt)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-001":["Chapter Two. Of the Lower Faculty of Knowledge",["'Vermögen' is rendered 'faculty' here, since the sense is that of a standing faculty of the mind."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-002":["All our modes of knowledge, our pleasure etc., and our desire are either sensible or intellectual. So there are also sensible and intellectual representations.",["'sensuell' is rendered 'sensible', the same as 'sinnlich'; the transcript uses both words with no apparent difference.","'Erkenntniße' is rendered 'modes of knowledge' because the word is plural."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-003":["According to the author [Baumgarten] they are distinguished merely by their logical form; but this is false. In this the author, along with Wolff and Leibniz, differs from all other philosophers, and they create great confusion. They distinguish the two merely in respect of distinctness and indistinctness. But that is no essential difference. For intellectual modes of knowledge may also be obscure or indistinct, and sensible modes of knowledge may, on the other hand, be ever so distinct; the former nevertheless remain intellectual on that account, and the latter sensible. For example, the concept of right and wrong is obscure and yet intellectual, whereas the concept of tones is very distinct and yet sensible. (Distinct knowledge is not a knowledge differing in species [*in specie*]; it rests merely on the relation of consciousness to the knowledge.) The cause of their difference must therefore lie in their different origin. Sensible representations are representations according to the manner in which I am affected by things; intellectual representations are those which are independent of this. Knowledge that rests on receptivity is sensible; knowledge that rests on spontaneity is intellectual. Through sensibility I know things as they are for us; through the understanding, however, as they are in themselves. For all objects of our senses are mere appearances. In all our knowledge sensibility is the material [element]. (It is the faculty of intuition, and the science of it is aesthetic.) Everything that we represent to ourselves comes from the senses, for only through them can we intuit things. Had we intellectual intuitions, our understanding would have to be creative and itself produce the things. Since this is not so, things must produce the representations in us, and that through sensible intuition. The understanding then adds nothing to experience but the form. In knowledge sensibility yields sensation; in pleasure or displeasure, gratification [*Vergnügen*]; in the faculty of desire, the incentives [*Triebfedern*].",["'Nach dem Autor': 'Baumgarten' supplied in brackets, as the author of the textbook (the Metaphysica) used in the lectures.","'sie' in 'machen große Confusion' is left as 'they', referring to the three named philosophers; Kant's grammar is loose here and I have kept the sense.","'aus d< n Sinnen' is read as 'aus den Sinnen' and 'Schaft' as 'schafft'; 'denn' in 'Der Verstand thut denn' is read as 'dann' (then). 'das materiale' is rendered 'the material [element]', the noun being supplied.","Several sentences were kept short as in the German. The parentheses are preserved as in the transcript.","'Wissenschaft davon Aesthetic': 'aesthetic' in the transcendental sense, the science of sensibility."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-004":["The power of producing sensible representations is the sensible faculty of knowledge, and the power of producing intellectual representations is the intellectual faculty of knowledge. The former is entitled the lower, the latter the higher, faculty of knowledge.",["'Kraft' rendered 'power', as it is distinguished from 'Vermögen' (faculty) in the same sentence; 'obere' is rendered 'higher'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-005":["The sensible faculty of knowledge can be divided, A, in respect of time. Here it is (a) the faculty of intuition of the present, or the senses, *sensatio*; (B) of the past, or reproduction; and (C) of the future, or *praevisio* [Praevision] (reproduction, or reproductive imagination, combined with consciousness is *memoria*). Time and space are conditions of sensible representations, all of which rest on the faculty of intuition. The faculty of intuition, so far as it begins from the presence of the object, is sense; so far as it is without an object, but still with regard to time, it is imagination; and without any relation of the object to time it is the faculty of poetic invention [Dichtungsvermögen]. These three faculties constitute the intuition of objects.",["The lettering 'A ... a ... B ... C' is kept exactly as the transcript has it, though the sequence of capitals and small letters looks irregular.","'Praevision' is given with the Latin form *praevisio*, as the sense suggests.","'Dichtungs Vermögen' is rendered 'faculty of poetic invention'; it could also be 'faculty of fiction'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-006":["B. In respect of the generation of representations the faculty of knowledge is (a) *facultas comparandi*. To this belong wit [Witz], as the faculty of finding resemblance, and the power of judgment [Urtheilskraft], as the faculty of finding difference among things. (b) *Facultas fingendi*, or the faculty of producing new representations, i.e. productive imagination. (c) *Facultas signandi*, the faculty of connecting representations with a subject [?], *vel facultas characteristica*, or the faculty of producing other representations by means of certain representations as instruments, or the faculty of producing *repraesentationes vicarias* or *subsidiarias*, which have no validity in themselves but still serve to produce other representations in us. For example, the word Rome produces the representation of a city in Italy. Language also rests on this. The sensible faculty of knowledge rests on the manner in which we are affected by things; hence it is receptivity, or a passive property of being determined by other things. The intellectual faculty of knowledge rests on spontaneity, or the power of determining itself, for it is independent of sensation. The understanding belongs to spontaneity, and so to the higher faculty of knowledge. The feeling of pleasure or displeasure, however, is partly spontaneity and partly receptivity.",["'V itz' is read as 'Witz' and 'Erkenntnil' as 'Erkenntniß'; the stray '30' before 'Z.E.' is a line number and is omitted.","'mit einem Subject' in (c) looks doubtful or corrupt (perhaps a sign is meant); I translate it literally and mark it with [?].","The sentence on *facultas signandi* is long and was kept as one, with the Latin terms retained in italics; the dash-parenthesis on receptivity was resolved into a following clause."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-007":["Chapter 3. Of Sense",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-008":["A. Sense (*sensatio*) is twofold: 1. inner sense and 2. outer sense. The capacity for inner intuition is inner sense. Inner sense is the consciousness of our representations themselves. (Apperception lies at the foundation of inner sense.) Its object is the soul. If the soul is conscious of itself without being conscious of its state, this is apperception. If it is also conscious of its state, this is sensation or perception.\n\nOuter sense is divided 1. into organ senses, which are confined to certain parts [of the body] and of which there are, as is well known, five; and 2. into the vital sense, that is, the sense of feeling something without any special organ. This sense is found wherever there is life; and since life resides in the nervous system, it is found especially in the outer ends of the nerves. It shows itself in a certain gentle sense of well-being, in shuddering, horror, and the like. It is the sixth sense of the French, namely the sensation of voluptuousness in sexual intercourse. The vital sense may also be entitled *sensus vagus*, and the others *sensus fixus*.\n\nAll the organ senses have something subjective and something objective, but some are more subjective than objective, and others the reverse. That is to say, in the case of some we represent to ourselves more alterations of the subject than of the object, and *vice versa*. (Other creatures may have more or fewer organ senses, and this is known of some animals. Thus the turtles which at a certain season swim 300 miles from the island of Yucatan to the Cayman Island must possess a special sense for the purpose. We too may possess still more senses, which we however reckon as belonging to feeling.)\n\nSmell and taste are more subjective. For if one merely smells or tastes, one cannot thereby distinguish the thing from another; I cannot know colour, shape, and so on [through them]. For this reason these two senses also have the strongest effect upon us. Strong smells can make us faint, and a bad taste produces nausea, and thereby convulsions throughout the whole body.\n\nTouch, sight and hearing are more objective than subjective. In the case of touch one might not [be inclined to] doubt this; but then one combines it with inner sensation, and that is the vital sense, which certainly relates more to the subjective than to the objective. Here, however, we are speaking of outer touch, that is, of the sensation of things by handling them. This sense resides chiefly in the fingers. By it I can determine the figure, the hardness and the softness of a thing. It lies at the foundation of all the other senses. A man born blind can by touch even distinguish colours; and when he comes to see, his seeing is at first of no use to him. Sight has still more objectivity in it. When I look about me, I do not feel what is going on in my eye, for it is so slight that one does not always notice it.\n\nHearing is more objective than subjective. For when I hear speech, I feel no blows or impacts of the air upon my ear-drum, unless someone screams very loudly; then indeed the ears hurt.\n\nThe question now is whether there can be still more senses. (Yes; for how many fine matters there may be which we do not perceive because we lack the organs for them, for example the magnetic matter. It is believed that animals have certain senses of which we have no concept, and this is quite possible. For example, when wild geese migrate in summer to cold countries, they fly in the darkest night in the straightest line towards the north.)\n\nSome senses have a great sphere within which they can perceive things. Touch and taste have the narrowest sphere; smell a wider one (the eagle, for example, smells carrion at a distance of a mile); hearing a still wider one; and sight the widest. It is admirable that we can still see stars the nearest of which is 200,000 times farther away than the sun. This is due in part to the fact that the air is only a fine matter, through which light, as a still finer one, easily penetrates.\n\nWe now come to the fourth chapter, or the",["The opening 'A' is the transcript's section marker and is kept as it stands; the passage breaks off mid-sentence ('oder der'), left uncompleted.","I divided the very long paragraph into shorter ones and sentences for clarity, and supplied 'the sensation' etc. in no place beyond the bracketed words '[of the body]', '[through them]', '[be inclined to]'.","Gefühl is rendered 'touch' where the text itself says it means outer feeling by handling ('Anfassen'), and 'feeling' in the parenthesis about further senses; 'Dieser' in 'Dieser besteht vornehmlich in den Fingern' is taken to refer to touch.","'Iucatan' is rendered 'Yucatan' and 'Caiman Insel' 'Cayman Island'; 'auf einer Meile' is taken as 'at a distance of a mile'.","'Beim Gefühl könnte man nicht zweifeln' is elliptical; I supply that the doubt concerns its objectivity.","Vermögen = capacity; Empfindung = sensation; Wahrnehmung = perception."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-009":["B. Reproductive power of the soul, or imagination",["Heading; 'B' is the transcript's section marker. 'Reproductions Kraft' rendered 'reproductive power'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-010":["This is the capacity to represent to ourselves past states. We become conscious of our past representations, and that in accordance with the laws of association, or *Aßociation*: *Ideae sociae sese invicem reproducunt* [associated ideas reproduce one another].",["The bracketed English gloss of the Latin is supplied by me.","'Vergesellschaftung' rendered 'association'; Kant's *Aßociation* kept."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-011":["The *facultas imaginationis* is *reproductiva* in respect of past time, *praevidendi* in respect of future time, and *productiva* in respect of no time. The first has the *lex aßociationis idearum sociarum*, through which ideas that have been connected with one another follow one another again; the second, the *lex expectationis casuum similium*; the third, the *lex sociabilium*, [namely] that one must rhyme what is grasped together.",["'daß man reimen müßte, was sich zusammen faßt' is obscure (reimen = 'rhyme', perhaps 'bring into accord'); translated literally and the 'namely' is supplied. Possible transcription fault.","Latin technical terms kept in italics."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-012":["The *facultas imaginandi* may be divided a. into the *facultas praevidendi* or *fingendi*, when my soul brings forth new representations through the imagination, and b. into the *facultas reproducendi*, when it merely renews those it has had. The reproductive imagination relates either to inner or to outer sense. Connected with it is:",["Passage ends with a colon, continued in the next passage.","'praevidendi oder fingendi' as given in the transcript; the pairing seems odd, since *fingendi* elsewhere belongs to the productive faculty, but it is translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-013":["C. The *facultas praevisionis*, when I infer to my future state. (The *facultas praevisionis*, or anticipating imagination, rests upon the reproductive. For example, the bell is rung, and it occurs to me that it is then mealtime; this is reproductive imagination. But I now at the same time infer from this that when the bell is rung a meal follows. The anticipating imagination with consciousness is *Praesagition*; without this it is *praesensio*.) And I infer to the future when a similar case has already occurred upon which something followed, and I therefore believe that this case also will have a like consequence. It is thus merely an application of the law of the reproductive imagination to future time.\n\nAll these three faculties of knowledge [*Erkenntniß Vermögen*] may be accompanied by apperception or not. If they are, they belong to man alone; if not, animals have them too. We should therefore have two different names for each of them, but we have these only for one, namely the reproductive imagination; for this, when accompanied by apperception, is entitled memory. We now come to the second distinction among the sensible powers of knowledge, namely",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'hall' to Fall (case), 'b* amen' to Namen (names).","'Praesagition' is kept as the transcript has it (cf. *praesagitio*); the sentence on it is placed in the parenthesis as in the original.","'sind sie es' is read as 'if they are [accompanied by apperception]'. The passage breaks off at 'nehmlich', left uncompleted."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s13-014":["II. With regard to their production, and first, A, to the *facultas comparandi*. This is quite distinct from the *facultas coniungendi seu componendi*. For in comparison I do not put concepts together, but only hold them against one another in order to bring out new representations. Here we look to *identitatem* and *diversitatem*. The *facultas ad cognoscendum identitatem* is *ingenium*; *acumen*, however, is [the faculty] *ad cognoscendum diversitatem*. The utility of *ingenium* is positive, that of *acumen* negative. The latter preserves us from errors, for it shows us not to accept as one and the same things which are not so. But it lacks the agreeableness and entertaining quality of *ingenium*.",["OCR slip corrected: 'I acultas' to *Facultas*. The Latin accusatives *identitatem*, *diversitatem* are left as printed.","'A zu der Facultas comparandi' is elliptical; 'first' and 'to' are my reading, and the bracketed '[the faculty]' is supplied.","*ingenium* (wit) and *acumen* (acuteness) kept in Latin, following Kant's own terms."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-001":["Chap. 7. On the faculty of fiction [*Dichtungs Vermögen*]",["OCR 'C ap.' read as 'Cap.' (Chapter). *Dichtungsvermögen* rendered 'faculty of fiction'; 'poetic invention' would also be defensible."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-002":["The *facultas fingendi* is the faculty of producing representations of things which we have never seen. It is either imagination [*Imagination*] or fantasy [*Phantasie*]. It is imagination when we play with the power of imagination [*Einbildungskraft*] and invent something for certain aims and purposes. It is fantasy when the power of imagination plays with us. The former is voluntary, for we can raise it up and direct it as we please; the latter is involuntary. Every invention must proceed in accordance with the analogy of experience; otherwise it is *effrenis*, unruly fantasy. Hence we can invent nothing materially, but only formally. If the invention accords with the analogy of experience, it is *phantasia subacta*. If it is involuntary, it is entitled, in particular, *phantasia effrenis*. Hypochondriacal people commonly have this; one who has it is entitled a visionary [*Phantast*].\n\n(The feeble power of imagination is like the donkeys in England, with which people make sport at the end of a race. When the rider is quite near the goal, he spurs the donkey, and since the animal cannot bear this, it stands still at once. The same happens with a power of imagination that is spurred too hard. If lawlessness and unbridledness [*Regel- und Zügellosigkeit*] are united in the power of imagination, there arises what is called frenzy [*Koller*]. This is sometimes ascribed to too much study, but it does not come from that, unless the person already had it beforehand, or at least came to study with a predisposition to it. Delusion [*Wahnsinn*] consists in our taking the images of the power of imagination for real; folly [*Wahnwitz*] consists in passing quite perverted judgments. The criterion of a deranged person is that he is unable to compare his judgment with those of others, and does not ask about them at all. Another phenomenon of the power of imagination is the dream. It arises quite naturally. For since the power of imagination is constantly active, and the operations of the understanding cease in sleep, the former is left over and thereby gets free play, and presents us with representations of things in place of the understanding. Hence it comes about that these seem necessary to us. It is the same as with the moon.)\n\nThis productive power of imagination manifests itself chiefly in dreams. A dream is a series of inventions, which however are involuntary. When awake we live in a common world; in dreams, however, in our own. The dream is very similar to the power of invention in waking life, with this difference: in dreams the productive imagination is involuntary, without order and design. When awake, on the other hand, I can for the most part give my fantasy all sorts of directions and order, and can always call myself back from it when I please. In waking life fantasy is indeed sometimes involuntary also, but the ideas are not yet so clear as in dreams, because here the impressions of the senses hinder us, whereas there all the senses are at rest and only the field of the productive imagination is active. For the dream removes all consciousness of our state. Hence also comes the curious fact that we represent past time to ourselves without knowing that it is past. Here, so to speak, a compartment of the reproductive imagination is simply opened to us, in which we roam about without being conscious of our present state.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'me' → 'nie' (never), 'aut' → 'entweder/oder' read as 'either … or' (doubtful: 'aut' is probably Latin 'or'; 'Die ist aut Imagination oder Phantasie'), 'nehmlieh', 'Regelund', 'metaphysicsh' etc.","'Die ist aut Imagination oder Phantasie' is slightly garbled; I read it as 'either imagination or fantasy'.","*Einbildungskraft* rendered 'power of imagination' and *Imagination* kept distinct with the German in brackets; *Phantast* = 'visionary' is a choice.","'heißt' rendered 'is entitled'. The parenthesis is divided into several sentences; the Latin technical terms stay in italics.","'Es ist so wie mit dem Monde' is left as it stands; the allusion is obscure in the transcript."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-003":["Yet the dream, like everything in the world, has its use. For if the mind were not always in consciousness, the body would seem not to be sufficiently stirred. Now if our body were not affected inwardly, and, since it is asleep, also not outwardly, this would greatly harm it. In dreams the mind, and through it the body also, is set in motion, especially by the affects. Through this the soul acts upon the sources of life, and that is very salutary and necessary to us, because by no bodily consciousness can we bring the sources of our life into consciousness and activity, but can only promote the circulation of the blood. The soul acts especially upon the respiratory system, and without this we cannot subsist. For example, if someone has taken good exercise and then goes to sleep, he commonly sleeps quite soundly. Since he then also dreams nothing, his breathing fails, and he then commonly has anxious dreams, from which he awakes again. After dreaming one is also much more alert in the morning than otherwise, since life has thereby been, as it were, strengthened. What then is sleep? It is a phenomenon belonging to physiology, or the science of the human body, and therefore cannot be determined metaphysically. One who is healthy also sleeps well.",["'Bewußtsein' in 'in Bewußtsein und Thätigkeit versetzen' kept as 'consciousness'; the transcript's 'kein körperliches Bewußtsein' is obscure and rendered literally.","'Affecten' rendered 'affects'. 'metaphysicsh' silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-004":["If the productive power of imagination is compared with the *vis signatrix* [power of signifying; the transcript has *vi signatrici*], there arises the art of interpreting dreams. Dreams are held to be signs of future events, partly because they often agree little with experience [sic, as transcribed], and because they represent events so vividly that one believes these to have really happened. These *signa prognostica* can be 1. natural and 2. arbitrary. *Signa naturalia* are the representations of their effects. These do indeed occur, and in this respect dreams can always be *signa prognostica*; for example, when in a person the bile is discharged in the night, he has dreadful and vexing dreams. These are the cause of his rising in the morning full of fancies, and, because of his peevish and sullen disposition, finding vexation, annoyance and quarrels everywhere. Thus the bile is the proper cause, but the dreams only the mediate cause, of the vexation of the following day.",["'weil sie oft mit der Erfahrung wenig übereinstimmen' is translated as it stands; the sense (one would expect 'agree with experience' as a reason for belief) may reflect a transcript fault.","'Signa naturalia sind die Vorstellungen von ihren Wirkungen' is left literal; the sense is probably 'representations of their effects' as signs of causes. Possibly corrupt.","'Gemüthsart' rendered 'disposition'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s14-005":["2. *Signa arbitraria.* This is mere superstition, for here I find not the slightest connection. Indeed I cannot know at all what they signify. The use of the lower powers of knowledge [*Erkenntnißkräfte*] is governed by the higher, and indeed the higher have, by means of imagination, dominion over the lower. It is admirable when the lower powers of knowledge let themselves be ruled by the higher. In this condition is one who cannot abstract and cannot attend to anything. The imagination must rest wholly on our choice [*Willkühr*], and it is with it as with the function of the lungs in breathing, which is in our power, yet not so that we could dispense with it altogether. We must therefore indeed let our imagination run at times, yet so that we can always govern it and always check it. If, however, it [*respect-irt*] the guidance of the understanding, it is *effrenis*.",["'Wenn sie aber die Lenkung des Verstandes respect-irt' is as transcribed; the sense requires 'does not respect', so a 'nicht' has probably dropped out. I translate the text as it stands and leave 'respect-irt' untranslated in brackets.","'In diesem Zustand ist derienige, der nicht abstrahiren … kann' is as transcribed; the sentence seems to have lost its connection to the preceding one.","*Erkenntnißkräfte* rendered 'powers of knowledge'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s15-001":["On the Faculty of Designation [*Bezeichnungs Vermögen*]",["Heading; *Bezeichnungsvermögen* rendered 'faculty of designation'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s15-002":["The *facultas characteristica* is the faculty of attaching certain signs to one's understanding, or of so associating representations that the one becomes the means of reproducing the other; it is also entitled *facultas signandi*. It is mechanical, that is, without exertion of power and also involuntary. For example, when I say 'Rome', the representation of that city arises at once, whether I will or not. This comes from habit. I invent signs in order to express by them what I think. These are words, and are either a. *demonstrativa* or *repraesentativa*, which concern what is simultaneous, for example, the pulse is a sign of health or sickness; b. *rememorativa*, which relate to the past, for example, Cicero; c. *prognostica*, which relate to the future, as, for example, the low flight of swallows is a sign of impending rain.",["'seinen Verstand mit gewissen Zeichen zu belegen' is rendered literally; the sense may be 'to furnish its thought with signs'.","'a. demonstrativa oder repraesentativa' kept as transcribed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s16-001":["On Understanding, Power of Judgment and Reason",["Heading; *Urtheilskraft* rendered 'power of judgment'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s16-002":["The higher faculty has spontaneity in its representations. We therefore regard ourselves as the compelling cause of them. Thus the will also belongs to the higher faculty of knowledge, inasmuch as it is its own master; and the inclination to the receptivity of the higher faculty of knowledge is entitled understanding in general. The intellectual faculty of knowledge is the faculty of thinking, or of making concepts for oneself. It represents the object only in general, without regard to the manner in which it appears [*seiner Erscheinung*]. The latter [intellectual knowledge] is discursive, the former [sensible knowledge] intuitive. An *intuitus* is an immediate representation; a concept, *conceptus*, is a mediate representation, through a mark.\n\n(The faculty of being conscious of the manifold through concepts is, after all, an imperfect faculty; and the greater and more perfect the faculty, the more nearly it approaches intuition. The most perfect understanding would accordingly be that which knows solely through intuition, though not, of course, through sensible intuition. This is how we represent the divine understanding to ourselves. The question here arises whether we can arrive at general representations only through comparison. The truth is rather the reverse: it is in the course of comparison that general concepts arise for us. Thus we do not arrive at the concept of a triangle, for example, through comparison. When we see it for the first time, we at once become aware that we are not restricted by its size in applying the name 'triangle' to all three-cornered figures that we shall see hereafter.)\n\nA concept is the consciousness that one representation contains the same [mark] as another, or that the same marks are contained in many representations. A concept therefore presupposes consciousness, or apperception. Animals do indeed compare representations with one another, but they are not conscious of that in which the representations agree or disagree. Hence they have no concepts; and since the higher faculty of knowledge consists of these, they have no higher faculty of knowledge. The higher faculty is thus distinguished from the lower by apperception. The lower we share with the animals, since we are animals ourselves; the higher raises us, as thinking beings, above them. Whoever can make concepts for himself thinks.\n\nAll thinking is threefold: (1) through concepts, and the faculty for this is entitled understanding; (2) through the combination of two concepts, that is, through judgments, that is, the power of judgment [*Urtheilskraft*]; (3) through the derivation of one concept from another by inferences, that is, reason [*Vernunft*]. The understanding is the faculty of bringing various representations under a rule. It rests on apperception.\n\n(It is the faculty of determining the particular through the universal. In the higher power of knowledge we consider the faculty of knowledge not in relation to intuition but in relation to the unity of consciousness. This is the representation of one's representations, and is for that reason also entitled apperception. Without consciousness of the identity of a representation in many representations no general rule would be possible. For a rule is the necessary unity of the consciousness of a manifold of representations, the relation of the manifold of representations to one consciousness.)\n\nBut how are concepts possible through apperception? Through my representing to myself the identity of my apperception in many representations. A concept is a *perceptio communis*, for example the concept of body. This holds of metal, gold, stone, and so on. In it I represent to myself one [thing] in the manifold. Its logical function consists in universality. This is the analytic unity of apperception; the many in one is its synthetic unity. The analytic unity of apperception presents nothing new to us, but is only conscious of the manifold in a single representation. The synthetic unity goes to the many, so far as it is contained in one. In so far as the understanding judges in accordance with this, it is a pure understanding. The understanding makes rules. From manifold representations it draws out the universal, what is met with in all of them. It is therefore also entitled the faculty of rules. Experience presupposes understanding, because it is a connection of perceptions according to rules. The understanding has rules *a posteriori* and *a priori*.",["Sentence 'So gehört der Wille … heißt überhaupt Verstand' is obscure and possibly corrupt in the transcript ('die Neigung zur Receptivitaet'); rendered literally, not emended.","'Dieses ist discursiv, ienes intuitiv': the antecedents are unclear; I supplied bracketed identifications (intellectual = discursive, sensible = intuitive) as the argument seems to require, though the German could be read otherwise.","The opening of the parenthesis after 'durch ein Merkmal' is closed only at 'belegen)' in the German; I broke the text into paragraphs at that point and closed the parenthesis after the first clause, a restructuring only of layout.","Supplied '[mark]' in 'contains the same' (German has only 'dasselbe'), following the clause that follows ('einerlei Merkmale').","'Das letztere haben wir als Thiere mit den letztem gemein' has 'letztem' for the animals; the first 'letztere' is rendered 'lower' because the sense requires it. Likely a slip in the transcript.","'Erkenntniß Kraft/Vermögen' both rendered 'faculty/power of knowledge'; 'Erkenntniß' = knowledge.","'Urtheilskraft' rendered 'power of judgment'; 'a posterioii' silently corrected to *a posteriori*; 'w'ir' corrected to 'wir'.","'dreiekigte' and 'Triangel' are rendered 'three-cornered' and 'triangle'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s16-003":["(Without the power of judgment [*Urtheilskraft*] we should not know the *differentia specifica*, and without perception we should not know the *genera*.) (Every station in life requires the power of judgment, though one more than another; the physician, for example, more than the jurist. In the latter case there are many instances of appeal: if one does not decide rightly, another will; but in the former it is not so.)\n\nThe power of judgment brings objects under rules. Reason applies the rules to them, and is therefore the faculty of acting according to principles. Reason determines the particular from a general rule. Principles are the determinations of the particular from the universal. The power of judgment sees under what rule each thing stands, and whether this thing is contained under this rule or not.\n\nThere are persons who think much *in abstracto* and little *in concreto*; that is, they have understanding and no power of judgment. For the power of judgment must be innate and cannot be learnt. If it could, we should have to give new rules showing how the rules of the understanding are to be applied; but we should then have to teach how these new rules are to be applied, so that there would again have to be new rules, and so on without end. Prudence consists in the power of judgment. Hence many a man is very learned and yet not prudent.\n\nReason infers that what the rule says universally holds also of this or that thing in particular. The understanding draws the universal from the particular, that is, from the various things in experience; reason draws the particular from the universal. These three powers of knowledge are found in the syllogism. The understanding gives the general rule, the *propositio major*. The power of judgment is the subsumption of one concept under another. Reason finally makes the conclusion, for it infers that what holds universally holds also of the thing subsumed.\n\nWhat reason knows is necessary, but it is very limited, because it fetches nothing from experience. The power of judgment must be cultivated and improved through practice [*Praxin*].\n\nWe now come to the second main part of this psychology, namely the feeling of pleasure and displeasure.",["'Was die Vernunft Erkenntniß erkennt': the word 'Erkenntniß' seems a stray or misplaced insertion; rendered 'What reason knows'.","The sentence 'Der Verstand zieht das allgemeine aus dem besonderen. Besonderen, nehmlich …' repeats 'Besonderen' (an apparent transcript slip); the repeated word is read as the start of the gloss 'that is, from the various things in experience'.","'nothwendie' read as 'nothwendig'.","'Erkenntniß Kräfte' rendered 'powers of knowledge'; 'Praxin' is the Latinised accusative of *praxis*, rendered 'practice'.","I divided the text into paragraphs for clarity; the German runs continuously."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s17-001":["Pleasure or displeasure",["Heading only; 'Lust oder Unlust' rendered 'Pleasure or displeasure'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s17-002":["We cannot define pleasure or displeasure (*Lust* or *Unlust*) unless we presuppose the faculty of desire. Through the feeling of pleasure or displeasure the faculty of knowledge is connected with the faculty of desire. The author [Baumgarten] entitles it *voluptas* and *taedium*. That is wrong, for this holds only of sensible satisfaction. For the understanding can often take displeasure in what pleases the senses best. We must entitle it *facultas complacentiae* and *displicentiae*. One might properly call this an *sensus internus*, since it concerns our own state; yet nobody has so far developed the concept of it correctly.\n\nThe feeling of pleasure is the capacity of my power of representation to be determined, through a given representation, to the preservation and furtherance of that representation, or [in the case of displeasure] to its avoidance. In displeasure we summon all our power to prevent the representation from penetrating further into the mind. Whatever arouses the feeling of a furtherance of life awakens pleasure. (The faculty of a subject, in so far as it is determined through pleasure in an object to bring this object into existence, is entitled the faculty of desire.) It is remarkable that our representations can become the cause of the actuality of objects; for example, the musician is the cause of the tones which he produces on his instrument. Pleasure is therefore the consciousness of the agreement of an object with the productive imagination of our soul. This definition will be made clearer when we come to the cognizability of the will.\n\nOur satisfaction or dissatisfaction is either mediate or immediate; the former is intellectual, the latter sensible. That pleases me mediately which pleases me not in itself but merely as a means to an immediate satisfaction. It is not a special object of pleasure but an object of reason, which knows that through it I shall come to share in a pleasure. For example, I take a mediate satisfaction in money. In itself it does not please me, but merely because by means of it I can procure a thousand kinds of enjoyment. Since I need reason to know whether something is good as a means to its end, this too is an object of reason. We must therefore have a means to satisfaction solely for the sake of the end; and if we do not, we act foolishly. For example, he who takes satisfaction in money in itself, without regarding it as a means to an end, and therefore keeps it always locked in his chests, is a miser.\n\nThat in which I take only a mediate satisfaction is also entitled useful or beneficial. This is not universal but depends on each person's purpose: what is useful to one is harmful to another. But of this we cannot speak here.\n\nThe other kind of satisfaction is the immediate, which pleases us in itself. This is of two kinds, subjective or objective. It is subjective when it rests on the alteration of our state and so lies merely in the subject. This is entitled the agreeable; it is also entitled private satisfaction, because it occurs only in relation to the senses of this or that subject and is therefore not universal. Warmth is an example: one person may find a certain degree of warmth quite agreeable which is intolerable to another, and this springs from his constitution of life and in general from his state.\n\nThe satisfaction is objective which rests not at all on the subject but merely on the object; hence it is a universal satisfaction, because it must please all who sense the object. This pleases universally either according to laws of sensibility, and then it is beautiful (the beautiful pleases in the imagination); or according to laws of the understanding and of reason, and then it is good. The good is mediate and immediate. Mediately good is the useful; for example, the punishment of the wicked does not please in itself but as a means to attaining a good end. Hence it is useful and good for us. Much is mediately good, but only morality is immediately good. Man is not immediately good—[witness] the cannibals and slave-traders, by their custom; he is so [immediately good] when he is moral. For then he is himself an end.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Lhilust' to *Unlust*; 'aul' to 'auf'; 'Gas' to 'Das'; 'ihiem' left as garbled (see below); 'ienes/iedes' normalised.","'woran' in 'woran ein Mißfallen haben' read as 'wohl'/'woran'; I translate 'can often take displeasure in'. Reading uncertain.","'facultas complacentia' emended to *complacentiae* (and *displicentiae*) in the translation; the OCR gives the nominative/uncertain form.","'Der Autor' = Baumgarten; supplied in square brackets. 'oder zur Vermeidung derselben': I supplied '[in the case of displeasure]' to resolve the ambiguous reference.","'Erkennbarkeit des Willens' is odd (perhaps a mis-hearing of some other term); rendered literally as 'cognizability of the will'.","Long sentences were divided and paragraphs introduced for clarity; Kant's 'Vermögen' rendered 'faculty' or 'power' as the sense required.","'ihiem Brauch nach' is garbled: I render 'by their custom' and supply 'witness'; the clause 'die Menschenfresser…' is elliptical in the German.","'Davon kann aber nicht geredt werden' translated 'we cannot speak here' (i.e. not in this place); 'Wohlgefallen' = satisfaction, 'Lust' = pleasure."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s17-003":["The beautiful pleases universally; it therefore has universal rules. For example, a person is beautiful if she pleases everyone. But if she has charm merely for this or that subject, she is merely agreeable. Hence it comes about that we dispute only about the beautiful and not about the agreeable, because the former rests on universal rules; and it might therefore better be said: *de gusto est disputandum*. The faculty of judging the beautiful is taste. The faculty of judging the agreeable has no name and needs none. Taste judges according to universal rules; the agreeable one judges according to its source, sensation. The power of judgment is therefore an objective faculty.\n\nThe universal rules of taste belong only to the sensibility of man and to beings which have a like sensibility with him. The universal rules of the good extend to all rational beings, even to God, for they concern knowledge. In taste, sensibility and understanding are combined in judging. For since it rests on universal rules, the understanding must supply these; but through the senses it must sense the object.\n\nWhat pleases in sensation, or in the agreeable, gratifies; what pleases the sensible power of judgment, namely the beautiful, pleases in the stricter sense. Something pleases me when its existence is indifferent to me. For example, a beautiful plan may please me although the thing itself does not yet exist; but the existence of the agreeable is not indifferent to me. If a beautiful landscape pleases me, it does so because it would please me in the same way even if it were not there, and I merely represented it to myself in thought. What gratifies me I either possess, or its existence is not indifferent to me. Every mediate gratification presupposes an immediate one.\n\nBeauty is the universality of sensible satisfaction, or the judging of the object according to universal satisfaction. Feeling concerns the matter; taste, however, the form of the composition of the manifold in sensation. Taste requires talent; sensation does not. The agreeable has more degrees, or intensive magnitude, than the beautiful, and the latter again more extensive magnitude than the former. The good has still more extensive magnitude. Because in taste some exceptions still always occur, some fall into the extreme of denying its universality.\n\nBeauty is the object of satisfaction in sensible intuition; or, according to the author, *perfectio phaenomenon*, if by *perfectio* one understands the object of sensible satisfaction. Goodness, or the good constitution of a thing, is the object of satisfaction according to concepts of the understanding.\n\nThe agreeable gratifies, the disagreeable pains, the beautiful pleases, the bad displeases; the good is approved, the evil disapproved. Sensation belongs to the agreeable (the agreeable pleases on account of the matter); taste to the beautiful (the beautiful pleases on account of the form); and a [certain] way of thinking (*Denkungsart*; sentiment) to the good.\n\nVirtue is a true inner good, but nothing agreeable. In it the judgments are intellectual and not sensible. Yet it has something which comes near to the beautiful. For if one represents in thought a whole realm of virtuous persons, the order and regularity of their conduct, which would necessarily spring from it, awakens in us a sensible satisfaction.",["OCR slips corrected: 'nehmlicli' to 'nehmlich'; 'V erstände' to 'Verstande'; stray spacing before colons removed.","'Beurtheilungs Kraft' rendered 'power of judgment'; 'Geschmak' = taste; 'Wohlgefallen' = satisfaction; 'vergnügt' = gratifies.","'im strictern Verstande' means 'in the stricter sense' (Verstand in its older sense), not 'understanding'.","'Es gefällt mir etwas, wenn mir die Existenz desselben gleichgültig ist' is elliptical: the sense is that the beautiful pleases only if its existence is indifferent; I kept the wording close but the claim is not general for 'gefallen'.","'und dieses wieder mehr extensive Größe als das vorhergehende' is ambiguous; I take 'dieses' = the beautiful, 'das vorhergehende' = the agreeable.","'Ein [certain] way of thinking': 'certain' supplied; 'Sentiment' kept in parentheses.","*perfectio phaenomenon* is Baumgarten's phrase, left in Latin as in the text (the form 'phaenomenon' is as printed)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-001":["On the faculty of desire",["Heading (*Vom Begehrungsvermögen*); *Begehrungsvermögen* rendered 'faculty of desire'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-002":["Causality is the determination of a cause whereby it becomes a cause, or the determination of the relation of a thing, as cause, to a determinate effect. The cause is therefore always to be distinguished from the causality. For example, a man is the cause of the letters, but not by means of his head or his feet, etc., but by means of his fingers. — The faculty of producing objects through its representations is the faculty of desire [*Begehrungsvermögen*]. The faculty of desire rests on the principle: *Nihil appeto nisi quod placet, nihil averto nisi quod displicet* [I desire nothing except what pleases, I turn away from nothing except what displeases]. But representations cannot become the cause of an object unless we have pleasure or displeasure in it. This, then, is the subjective condition under which alone the representation can become the cause of the object. Hence desire [*Begierde*] is also a *complexus* of the actuality of the object and of the faculty of desire. [It is] the faculty of determining oneself to act in conformity with the object of liking [*Wohlgefallen*]. The objective side of desires is the physical power to satisfy them. This, however, belongs to the mechanism of nature. We presuppose it, since otherwise our desires would be empty. Here, however, we consider desires in general. (Besides the things which are moved by external causes, there are living things, which are moved by internal causes. A being is living if the power of its representations can be the ground of the actuality of their objects. The causality of representation in respect of the actuality of its objects is therefore life. This causality of representations is, in respect of the subject, the feeling of pleasure or displeasure; in respect of the object, however, it is the faculty of desire.)",["Restated slightly: the elliptical sentence 'Das Vermögen, sich ... zu bestimmen' is kept as a fragment with '[It is]' supplied, since its grammatical subject is unclear.","'durci' in the OCR read as 'durch'. 'Begehrungsvermögen' rendered 'faculty of desire'; 'Begierde' 'desire'; 'Wohlgefallen' 'liking' (a choice).","'Vorstellungs Kraft' rendered 'power of its representations'; Kant's parenthesis is retained as in the source.","'Das obiective der Begierden' interpreted as the objective side, i.e. the physical power to realise them, as the following sentence indicates."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-003":["Pleasure is the representation of the agreement of an object with the productive power of the soul, and displeasure the opposite. The faculty of desire is the causality of the object that is produced. Accordingly pleasure is agreement, and displeasure conflict, with our faculty of desire. But pleasure is also the feeling of the furtherance of life; and whence does this come? — From this. A thing lives if it has a faculty of moving itself according to choice [*Willkühr*]. Life is therefore the faculty of acting according to choice or according to its desires. This, however, is the faculty of desire taken practically. Since pleasure, then, is agreement with the faculty of desire, it is also agreement with life, and displeasure is conflict with life. Pleasure and displeasure, however, presuppose sensation [*Empfindung*]. Accordingly I can also say: pleasure and displeasure is a feeling of agreement and conflict, or, what is the same, of the furtherance or the hindrance of life. All desires always relate to something future. All desires, like pleasure and displeasure, are intellectual or sensitive. —",["'Willkühr' rendered 'choice'; 'Seele' as 'soul' (the text does not say Gemüt).","'Woher das? — Daher.' rendered literally as a lecture-style question and answer.","'Caußalitaet des Gegenstandes' kept as 'causality of the object'; the German is compressed."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-004":["That which is the cause of desire is the *causa impulsiva* or *elater animi* [spring of the mind]. If they have arisen from sensibility, they are entitled *stimuli*, and their effect *appetitio per stimulos* or sensible desire. But if they spring from the understanding, they are entitled *motiva*, and their effect *appetitio per motiva* or intellectual desires.",["'sie' (plural) does not agree with the singular 'Ursache'; it is taken to refer to the impelling causes generally."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-005":["From the agreeable arise the *stimuli*, because the agreeable is for the senses and is merely a private judgment. The beautiful and the good are intellectual, because the judgment upon them is logical; hence they are also the source of the *motiva* or moving grounds. Desires, further, are idle (*otiosae*) when the objects are not in our power. They are also entitled fantastic and contemplative desires. For example, when we wish to see a comet this year, we have idle desires, as do readers of romances. (In affect one feeling is greater than all the others, and so too in passion one inclination. The consciousness of the agreement of all our inclinations is contentment. Affects destroy agreeableness; passions, ends.) We often desire something before we have yet seen whether it is possible for us to attain it. But why has nature given us this? To rouse us the more to activity. For thereby we are impelled to inquire carefully whether they are not in our power; but if we find that they are not in our power and yet still cherish the desires, we act unnaturally and absurdly. We also have a contemplative liking, for many things happen in which we take pleasure without their being in our power. This always occurs, but idle desires do not. Animals have no idle desires, for even if they apply their powers in vain, they do not know that the thing is not in their power. Opposed to idle desires are practical ones. These are desires for such things as are in our power. If *stimuli* have become habitual, they are inclinations, and their source is instinct or habit; habitual sensible desires are passions. As desires and affects, they concern sensation. In the one case [inclination/passion] the subject is not capable of comparing the inclination to one object with the sum of all inclinations; in the other [affect], not capable of comparing one sensation with the sum of all. The author explains affect indeterminately as a higher degree of pleasure or displeasure. The harmony of the lower and the higher faculty of desire must be negative, namely in such a way that the lower puts no hindrance in the way of the higher. Hence the *causae impulsivae* to virtue must not be taken from sensible advantages.",["The sentence on instinct and habit ('Instinct oder Gewohnheit habituelle ...') lacks punctuation in the source; I have placed a semicolon before 'habitual sensible desires'.","The 'Dort ... hier' sentence is obscure; the bracketed attributions (inclination/passion versus affect) are my judgment of the sense, supplied in square brackets.","'Emphndung' and 'Llnlust' are OCR slips, silently corrected; 'Harum' read as 'Darum' (hence).","'Der Autor' is presumably Baumgarten, the textbook author; the text does not say so.","'Annehmlichkeit' rendered 'agreeableness'; 'Zufriedenheit' 'contentment'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-006":["On Choice [*Willkühr*]",["Heading; 'Willkühr' rendered 'choice', a choice among possibilities such as 'will' or 'power of choice'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s18-007":["The faculty of desiring practically, or *facultas appetitionum practicarum*, is *arbitrium*. The *arbitrium*, or choice, is either *sensitivum*, which presents to us things that are agreeable to the senses, or *intellectuale*, [which presents] things which the understanding approves. It is better, however, to divide *arbitrium* into *brutum* and *liberum*. The *brutum* is that which is determined or necessitated by *stimuli*, the *liberum* that which is determined by *motiva*. The former animals have, the latter men; hence it is also entitled *humanum*. Man can indeed be affected by *stimuli*, but cannot be necessitated by them, for he is independent of the *stimuli*.",["'Has Vermögen, prac tisch' is an OCR break, corrected to 'Das Vermögen, practisch'.","'which presents' before 'things which the understanding approves' is supplied from the parallel clause, in square brackets."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-001":["On Freedom",["Heading only."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-002":["The *arbitrium liberum* is determined *per motiva*. Since these [motives] come solely from the understanding, they are *causae impulsivae intellectuales*. They are the concepts of the good. The *arbitrium liberum* is *voluntas*. Freedom is therefore a faculty of acting in accordance with concepts of good and evil. (In a pure intelligence objective necessity is at the same time subjective necessity. So it is with God.) That is the positive concept of freedom, taken in the practical sense of the understanding. Negatively, it is the power of choosing independently of sensible incitements, likewise in the practical sense. We are therefore free from *stimuli*, but not from *motiva*. The faculty so determined may be entitled the higher faculty of desire, because its rules (the concepts of the good) are universal. (What is determined by motives is an end; what precedes the resolution is deliberation. *Nihil appeto nisi sub ratione boni*, and *nihil [aversor] nisi sub ratione mali*, is indeed good as a principle, but false as a proposition of experience; for under the representation of the agreeable I often pursue what is evil, and under the representation of the disagreeable I reject what is good.)\n\nHow do we know that the will is free? Freedom is not a property which we learn from experience, for we can experience nothing negative. We perform, it is true, many actions in which we seem to act against all *stimuli*; but we cannot yet conclude from this that we do so. A secret *stimulus* may nevertheless have impelled us. For example, when a criminal confesses nothing despite torture and every torment, he acts indeed against all *stimuli*, yet in favour of one *stimulus*, namely the fear of death. The inference, 'Since I notice no *stimuli*, there are none', may therefore be very uncertain.",["Sentence 'Diese kann das obere Begehrungs Vermögen heißen' is obscure: 'Diese' has no clear antecedent; I have resolved it as the faculty determined by motives and said so in the English ('The faculty so determined').","'im practischen Verstände' (also 'in eben dem Verstände') rendered 'in the practical sense'; Verstände here means 'sense, respect' (in praktischer Hinsicht), not the faculty 'understanding'. The first occurrence I have glossed as 'of the understanding' no further; read 'practical sense'.","The text reads 'et nihil nisi sub ratione mali', with the verb omitted; I supplied [aversor] in brackets as the usual completion of the formula (the Latin verb is not in the passage).","'Motiven' and 'motivis' are kept as *motiva* / motives; 'stimulos/stimulis' kept in Latin as *stimuli*, with 'Anreitzungen' rendered 'incitements'.","'wenn ... wieder alle stimulos zu handlen scheinen' is slightly garbled; read as 'against all stimuli' (wider), following the example that follows. 'Wir können...nicht schließen' supplemented with 'that we do so' for sense.","Punctuated the last sentence's inference in quotation marks to make plain what is the faulty inference; the German has none."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-003":["We are not even immediately conscious of freedom. We are conscious only of the incentives or *stimuli*, which are clear representations. But we may also have obscure representations, and *stimuli* to something of which we are therefore not conscious. (All our actions are *actiones voluntariae*; but these are again either *libentes* (actions done willingly), when we are conscious of no desire to the contrary, or *invitae*, in so far as we are conscious in them of one or more grounds to the contrary. These [latter] are composed of *appetitio* and *aversatio*, only that the former is the stronger. Constraint, *coactio*, is the *necessitatio actionis invitae*. It is either *in se ipsum* or *ab alio*. It may be physical or moral. Physical constraint is either through *illecebras* or through *minas*. Through *illecebras* there is strictly no constraint, but [only] compulsion (*Nöthigung*); for example, hunger is the thief's *illecebra* to steal. Physical constraint without *illecebras* and *minas* is *extorsio*. The constraint is not a *necessitatio arbitrii sensitivi*, but [a *necessitatio arbitrii*] *intellectualis*; for were it a *necessitatio per stimulos*, man would be an animal. As it is, man can by reason always give himself a preponderance over the sensitive representations, even under the strongest physical compulsion. That is to say: *voluntas non potest cogi physice, sed semet ipsam cogere potest volendi*. So we can constrain others by presenting them with *motiva* which move them to constrain themselves. The *necessitatio actionis* [*in*]*vicem moraliter bonae* [reading doubtful] is *obligatio*; that is practical constraint. If practical, or objective, constraint were in man at the same time always subjective, he would be perfectly free. Moral obligation can be thought only in relation to a sensible power of choice (*Willkühr*) which is affected by *stimuli*. In animals, therefore, there is no moral obligation, but much necessity. Every created being, however perfect, has moral obligation; for all have needs, which give certain *stimuli* with which they have to contend. Man therefore has not holiness, but virtue. We may indeed take satisfaction in the object, only we lack an *elater animi*; these [satisfactions] are therefore not the causes of pleasure or displeasure. If the satisfaction is connected with my state, then the existence of the object will not be a matter of indifference to me, that is, I shall have an interest in it. In the case of virtue, therefore, we must also have an interest. The moralists, however, are not agreed about its incentives.)",["'neceßiatatio' corrected to *necessitatio*; 'appetitio' kept as in the text (strictly *appetitio*/*aversatio* pair).","'Diese sind aus appetitio und aversatio zusammengesetzt': 'Diese' resolved as the *actiones invitae*, since the context requires them; supplied [latter].","'neceßitatio arbitrii sensitivi, sondern intellectualis': I supplied [a *necessitatio arbitrii*] to complete the elliptical Latin.","'mvicem moraliter bonae' is an OCR corruption; I have not tried to restore it. [*in*]*vicem* is only a conjecture and should not be trusted; the sense ('of an action morally good') is clear from context.","'Nöthigung' rendered 'compulsion' and 'Zwang' 'constraint' to keep the distinction Kant draws; German kept in parentheses.","'elater animi' (spring of the mind) left in Latin. The final sentence of the passage closes a parenthesis begun earlier; I keep the parenthesis as the text has it, though it runs very long and the passage is evidently loosely assembled from notes.","'Wir können wohl Wohlgefallen am Gegenstand haben ... diese sind also nicht die Ursachen' is loose; 'these' is taken to refer to the satisfactions, by judgment."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-004":["Freedom is a mere idea, and to act in accordance with this idea is entitled being free in the practical sense. Practical necessity is objective and not subjective. Here I must do something; but natural necessity through *stimuli* says only: it will happen. That is practically necessary without which our actions would not take place, namely, without the laws of understanding and of reason. Freedom is therefore practically necessary. Man must therefore act in accordance with an idea of freedom, and he cannot do otherwise. But this does not yet prove freedom in the theoretical sense. Thereby all the difficulties and contradictions which the concept of freedom has occasioned fall away. One may prove or refute freedom in the theoretical sense as one likes; it is enough that one will still always act in accordance with ideas of freedom. Many people do not concede certain propositions in speculation, and yet they act in accordance with them.",["'im practischen Verstände' / 'im Theoretischen Verstand' rendered 'in the practical / theoretical sense' (Verstand = sense, respect), not the faculty 'understanding'.","'Hier muß ich etwas thun' left as a direct statement; 'es wird geschehen' rendered as a quotation-like clause after a colon.","'Gesetze des Verstandes und der Vernunft': here Verstand is the faculty, so 'understanding'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s19-005":["Freedom is merely the capacity to act in accordance with the *arbitrium intellectuale*; it is not the *arbitrium* itself. The *causae impulsivae intellectuales* are either *secundum quid* or *simpliciter tales*. A *causa impulsiva secundum quid* is present when I merely choose a good means for attaining my end, the end itself arising from a stimulus. For example, when someone writes a book he commonly gives out that he does so from love of truth, although he often does so in order to earn money. The means he employs, namely writing a good book, is good. He has to overcome many stimuli, such as love of idleness, and he may also benefit the world; but his purpose is none the less not intellectual but sensuous. His end was a comfortable life in the future; this was therefore a *causa impulsiva secundum quid*. But if someone writes a book from mere love of truth and has it made public only after his death, when he can hope for no further advantage from the world, then his end is good, and the *causa impulsiva* is intellectual. This depends on no other stimulus. Morality has such laws, intellectual *simpliciter*, and in morality freedom must nevertheless be presupposed.\n\nAll men choose good means, whatever the purpose may be. They proceed prudently, since their desires always go to something good, but only as a means to a sensuous end, whatever the purpose may be. The end he regards as agreeable; the means he recognises as good through understanding and reason. Here he always acts *secundum quid*; the *arbitrium* is merely intellectual only if it is such *simpliciter*. It is not the means but the ends that determine the desires. If reason settles what is really good, or prescribes the ends, it looks after its own interest and is mistress [*Domina*]. If it merely discovers a good means to the best attainment of an end that springs from inclination, it looks after only the interest of inclination and is the servant [*Serva*]. In that case it is not reason but inclination that is the incentive.\n\nThe *arbitrium simul intellectuale* is also *purum*, for here reason alone has place. But where reason and sensibility both govern, and sensibility is the ruler, there the *arbitrium* is *sensitivum* or *impurum*, since the *causa impulsiva* lies in sensibility. And since the end counts for more than the means, the denomination is made *a potiori*.\n\nHappiness has two meanings: (1) the sum of all agreeable sensations, which we cannot determine; (2) the satisfaction of all present inclinations. If the purpose of our actions is merely happiness and the satisfaction of all our sensuous inclinations, our *arbitrium* is merely *sensitivum*. It is therefore not virtue when we perform good actions for the sake of certain advantages, as when a citizen does his duty because he fears punishment. It is virtue when we do the good because we see that it is good and our duty. In the latter case we act intellectually, in the former sensuously. [This is] *arbitrium intellectuale*, or [the *arbitrium*] so called in the stricter sense, or *arbitrium purum*.\n\nFreedom, then, is the capacity to choose what is good in itself and not merely as a means. We are therefore free when we order our actions wholly in accordance with the laws of understanding and reason, and the more we do so, the freer we are. For even if the will is free from stimuli, it still cannot be altogether free. Since we desire only what pleases us, the pleasure is the cause of our desiring. The cause of the pleasure, however, is either sensibility or understanding; no third is possible. If, then, I do not desire something from sensibility, I must desire it because it pleases my understanding. But if I desired neither in accordance with understanding nor with sensibility, I should will what displeases me; I should act without incentive and cause, and that is impossible.\n\nUnderstanding and reason give laws to the will, to which it must conform if it is to be free. But we cannot be determined by mere representations of reason; reason must also supply us with incentives, and this it does, since our conscience approves or disapproves. For if we follow reason we can attain a quiet conscience and all spiritual happiness. Here reason exercises not only the legislative power [*vim legislatoriam*] but also the executive [*executivam*]. According to it, therefore, [the law] is not only objectively necessary but also subjectively possible. Man is nevertheless free on this account, for he can choose between sensibility and understanding; but if he acted according to sensibility, he would become like the animals. Reason, however, raises him above the animals, and the more he acts according to it, the more moral, and at the same time the freer, he becomes. For morality is the science of what is good; otherwise the concept of freedom would be superfluous if there were no morality, and then reason too would be superfluous, since it exists solely for the sake of the morality of laws.\n\nThe field of morality is either affected by inclinations or not. Both are *arbitrium liberum*, for even in the former we have the freedom to act according to the understanding. The *arbitrium intellectuale* is present when we act independently of inclinations, although we have them. And then we can consider whether we ought to follow them or not. We are free only in order to follow the laws of morality; otherwise we should not be permitted to have reason.\n\nThus far we have spoken of freedom in the practical sense. Freedom in the transcendental sense, however, is a capacity of determining oneself independently of all remote or natural causes. Here again there is an autonomy of reason; for whether I assume this or its opposite, I lose myself in contradictions. Sensibility is determined by objects, these by laws of nature, and these by God. God, therefore, determines our sensibility, and we are not transcendentally free. But if we assume that everything happens merely in accordance with necessity, we never arrive at a first [cause], and then we may also ask by what God is determined. None of these speculative considerations of the contradictions, however, harms the practical concept of freedom. For the practical concept looks not to how something happens but to the fact that it ought to happen, and 'ought' presupposes freedom.\n\nIf in the use of our will we have regard merely to empirical happiness, then reason determines not through the concept of the highest good but through the concept of the highest agreeableness. In that case inclination rules and fixes the end, and reason is the slave that must procure the means. In morality, however, the rule is that the senses are not to argue; reason alone must be master. For I cannot satisfy the desires completely; new ones constantly arise. But the highest good I can attain. Hence the concept of an *arbitrium intellectuale* must necessarily lie at the foundation, and it [or: she] must be of importance for every man.",["Silent OCR corrections: iemancl→iemand, Glrikseeligkeit→Glückseeligkeit, sirnul→simul, le→je, wrir→wir, kieiheit→Freiheit, Serua→Serva.","Long German sentences are divided into several English sentences and paragraph breaks have been introduced; the transcript has none. Repetitions (e.g. 'die Absicht mag sein, welche sie will') are kept, although the second clause is awkward in the German and may be corrupt.","'Triebfeder' = 'incentive'; 'Zweck' = 'end', 'Absicht' = 'purpose'; 'Neigung' = 'inclination'; 'Begierde' = 'desire'. 'Glückseeligkeit' (sic) = 'happiness'.","'im practischen Verstande' / 'im transcendentalen Verstande' rendered 'in the practical / transcendental sense' (Verstande = sense, not the faculty 'understanding').","'Domina' and 'Serva' are Latin in the text and are retained in square brackets after the English.","'Ihr zu Folge' is taken to refer to reason (Vernunft), and the subject of 'nothwendig ... möglich' is supplied as '[the law]', which is my supplement.","The final 'sie selbst' is ambiguous (the concept? morality? reason?); I leave it as 'it [or: she]'. The antecedent is doubtful.","'Arbitrium intellectuale oder strictius sic dictum ...' is a fragmentary list, and '[This is]' and '[the *arbitrium*]' are supplied. 'a potiori denominatio' = the denomination is taken from the predominant part."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s20-001":["On indifference and equilibrium.",["'Gleichgültigkeit' = indifference; 'Gleichgewicht' = equilibrium (literally, equal weight)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s20-002":["That is indifferent [*gleich gültig*] to us in which we have neither agreeable nor disagreeable sensations; it is therefore the same as insensibility. We are also indifferent when we neither desire nor abhor something. Equal [*gleich*], however, is that which we desire and abhor equally, or for the performance of which we have equal grounds and counter-grounds.",["The transcript plays on the separated 'Gleich gültig' and 'gleich'; the German is shown in brackets to preserve this.","'Empfindungen' = sensations; 'Unempfindlichkeit' = insensibility. 'Gründe und Gegengründe' = grounds and counter-grounds."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s20-003":["It is sought to prove, from the capacity to choose between indifferent things, that freedom is a lawlessness with respect to all incentives [*Triebfedern*]. But *libertas indifferentiae* is an absurdity. For what does not please me I cannot will either, and I therefore remain in the same state. Equilibrium, or equality, obtains when we desire and abhor something equally; but this seldom occurs in man. We do not find a complete *aequilibrium* even in the scales of a balance. The *aequilibrium* may be either of two kinds. The first is *aequilibrium stimulorum*, where the incentives of sensibility are equal on both sides. Here, it is said, man will nevertheless be able to do and to choose one of the two, and so to act freely. But no: he will then rather do neither of the two, and will refrain from the action. This refraining is itself a state of equilibrium; or else it still remains open to him to alter and determine himself through reason. For example, suppose someone is left free choice, for the sake of something specific that he is to do, to take either 5 thalers or a Friedrich d'or. In respect of what he is to take he is then *in statu aequilibri*. But he is not permitted to take either of them, if he wishes to act honestly.",["The long sentence on the first kind of equilibrium is divided into several shorter sentences; \"Hier, sagt man\" etc. is kept as a report of an opposing view.","\"Dieses Unterlaßen ist auch Gleichgewicht, oder es bleibt ihm noch immer übrig…\" is syntactically loose in the transcript; rendered as it stands with \"or else\".","\"5 rthl.\" = 5 Reichsthaler, given as \"thalers\"; \"Friednchsd'or\" is an OCR slip for Friedrichsd'or. The sense of \"für etwas Bestimmtes zu thun\" is obscure; rendered literally.","\"findt\" and \"handlen\" are the transcript's spellings; \"Triebfedern\" rendered \"incentives\", \"stimuli\" kept in Latin where Kant's Latin term occurs."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s20-004":["The second kind is *aequilibrium motivorum*. But here complete equality can never obtain. Were there complete equality, he could not choose; for where there is no ground there is also no choice, or else the action may be omitted. Nor is there complete equality; for even if the objective grounds on both sides are equally strong, particular subjective grounds may still be added on one side, giving it the preponderance. For example, if I am given the liberty of choosing one of two ducats perfectly alike, the objective incentives on both sides would be equally strong. I should then deliberate a while which to take, until at last a subjective incentive, avarice or else impatience at delaying so long, would so move me that I should take the first that happened to catch my eye; or I might from sheer convenience take the one nearest to me. Thus we see that a subjective cause, of which, according to the constitution of our mind at any given time, there may be countless kinds, often remaining obscure to us and working in secret, must give the object its deciding weight. Otherwise, in complete equilibrium, man will be able to choose nothing. *Libertas aequilibrii* is therefore nothing; for even here we always act according to the greater number of incentives, and so not by our own caprice.\n\nFinally, the concept of true freedom has been placed in this, that one can commonly choose that to which *causae impulsivae* drive us. This is entitled *libertas contrarietatis*. But that comes from having conceived the concept of freedom merely negatively. We must, however, have a positive concept of it, otherwise we have no concept at all. The positive concept is the capacity [*Vermögen*] to act according to concepts of good and evil.\n\nBecause freedom is not subject to physical laws, or to the laws of sensibility, it is believed to be subject to no law. But that is an over-hasty inference. That we are free from the laws of sensibility is our prerogative. Animals are not so, and are therefore merely passive. We are not passive when we act according to laws of the understanding, since these come from ourselves. We are thus determined by no one, but determine ourselves, and so have spontaneity. Since here our own reason is the incentive to the laws, we are free, inasmuch as we govern ourselves. Freedom is therefore in no way a capacity to choose evil, but to choose the good, since our reason commands us only the good. If we are free from the *stimuli*, we must nevertheless have a ground of motion [*Bewegungsgrund*] by which we can direct ourselves. For to act without a motive would be for us no prerogative at all, but a disgrace. What should one think of the man who, to show his freedom, wallowed in the gutter in fine clothes? The *libertas contrarietatis* is a merely imaginary dream; for in all our actions we shall find that they happen from causes, and it would also be contradictory not to will what pleases me in the highest degree, and to will the opposite. The good is always what every man wills, and he would always do it, if only it were not difficult for him to carry it out; and if our nature were so constituted that we always acted according to the concept of the good, we should be truly free. But since reason always proceeds from other causes, man is free only *secundum quid*.\n\nAn objection is also raised, when it is said: A being so created, which by its nature can do nothing but good, is not free. For it could then not lie, and would have to speak the truth, and would accordingly be constrained and not free. To this one may answer: it lies in its power, or is physically possible, that it could lie, but it will never lie. Yet a spirit so created is limited by the supreme cause and acts like a clock. All these disputes over the transcendental concept of freedom have no influence upon the practical; for there I look not to the supreme cause but to the final end.",["Very long passage divided into paragraphs and shorter sentences; the transcript's dashes and run-on connections are smoothed.","\"Liberias aequilibrium\" is an OCR slip; read as *libertas aequilibrii* (cf. *libertas indifferentiae*). \"I reiheit\" read as \"Freiheit\"; \"contrarietalis\" and \"caußae\" corrected; \"praerogativ\" rendered \"prerogative\".","\"Dünkel\" rendered \"caprice\"; \"Triebfeder\" \"incentive\"; \"Bewegungs Grund\" \"ground of motion\" / \"motive\".","\"Weil aber die Vernunft von immer andern Ursachen herkommt\" is obscure (reason, in us, derives from other causes); rendered literally, sense doubtful.","\"Denn sind wir nicht paßiv…\" read as \"Denn wir sind nicht paßiv…\" (the \"sind\" order looks like transcript slip); \"Uhr\" = clock or watch. \"Es stehe in seiner Kraft\" is reported speech, rendered in indirect form."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s21-001":["Second Section. Rational Psychology. Division",["Heading as it stands; \"Einteilung\" rendered \"Division\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s21-002":["This [science] is to be derived wholly independently of empirical principles, purely *a priori*. Here we have three kinds of thing to observe: (1) the transcendental predicates of the soul, whether it is substance and abiding, finite or infinite, etc.; (2) the soul in comparison with other beings, namely (a) in comparison with matter, (b) in comparison with other thinking beings; (3) the connection of the soul with other beings, (a) with the body, (b) with the world by means of the body. This connection is entitled the *commercium animae et corporis* and constitutes life. Thus: (a) the possibility of life, (b) its beginning, (c) its end or death, (d) accordingly the state after death, immortality.",["\"Diese\" is taken to refer to rational psychology (the heading); supplied in brackets.","\"beharrlieh\" is an OCR slip for \"beharrlich\"; rendered \"abiding\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-001":["On the Nature of the Human Soul",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-002":["Here we treat of the transcendental predicates of the human soul. The first question is: Is it material or immaterial? The 'I' is already a mark of the immateriality of the human soul. The 'I' is throughout an object of inner sense, and everything of which I am conscious in myself is solely an object of inner sense. All efforts are therefore in vain that seek to make the faculties [*Vermögen*] of the soul distinct by means of corporeal intuition. We do not find the slightest analogy between thinking and matter.",["\"selbet\" is an OCR slip for \"selbst\"; \"frägts\" = \"fragt es sich\".","\"deutlich machen\" rendered \"make distinct\" (Kant's technical sense of clear and distinct representation); \"Bemühungen\" \"efforts\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-003":["2. The soul is substance; and not only so, but I am also conscious of the substantial in the soul. For of matter only the accidents are known to me, not the substantial. I am the ultimate subject, and I know myself without accidents. Of the substantial, however, both in body and in myself, I properly have no concept; I know nothing of it save that it is a something. It now depends only on deriving the properties of the soul from this sterile concept of something. Upon it the whole of rational theology is supposed to be built. We readily see that this is impossible, for from the concept of something nothing can be comprehended.\n\nThat the soul is a substance we cannot prove from its permanence. For how do we know this permanence? That is substance which nevertheless remains through all alterations. Of the soul we have experience only of itself, and experience in advance only up to death. Whether it will still remain after death I do not know. If, then, we entitle the soul a substance, we can say no more than that substance here is a logical function, namely, the subject and not the predicate. If I consider this subject alone, apart from all predicates, I consider the substantial, or the something. To pick out all properties from this is not possible. Yet if we cannot discover new properties, we can at least avoid false inferences and errors. Thus, for example, from the narrow concept of a subject, from the I, I can establish that the soul is not a *compositum*. The I is the ultimate subject; but the ultimate subject is never composite.\n\nFurther, we know the soul through predicates of which not a single one can be known through sensible intuition. They are mere objects of inner intuition. Matter, however, as object of outer intuition, we know by its extension and its figure. Thought produces no extension, no figure. The soul must therefore also be something other than matter. If this inquiry has no positive use, it has at least a negative one, which consists in this, that we do not fall into the errors of the materialists and explain the actions of the soul physically and mechanically. The materialist here really commits a *metabasis eis allo genos* [μετάβασις εἰς ἄλλο γένος]. That the soul is not matter can be clearly established; but it may still be that the *substratum* of matter is the same as the *substratum* of the soul. The phenomena, however, are different.\n\nThe simplicity of the soul, and hence its immateriality, is proved in the following manner. Were the soul composite, its representations would have to be so divided that in each part of the soul there would be one representation, which taken together would make up the whole representation. (In every thought there is a unity of consciousness, always the same I, which therefore presupposes also unity of the subject.) That would be as if, in a company, a single word of a thought of mine were spoken by each member of the company. The whole thought could not possibly arise in this way, nor could I be conscious of it; there must again be a subject which gathers together all the parts of the thought and so forms the whole thought for itself.\n\nBut can the permanence of the human soul not be inferred from its simplicity, as Mendelssohn holds? If we could conceive of no other ceasing to be than through division into parts, this proof of the permanence and immortality of the soul would be irrefutable. But another perishing of the soul is possible, namely through *evanescence*. Its reality can grow ever smaller until it vanishes.\n\nThe soul is not matter, yet it may for all that be material; that is, the *substratum* of the soul, the I, the ultimate subject, may be of the same content as the *substratum* of matter, and might have become the substratum of matter. So Leibniz thought, and he was led to this thought by the Monadology. Since he believed that all monads have *vires repraesentativas*, and that the soul also has a *vis repraesentativa* and is a monad, he held the two to be one and the same. We have, however, not the slightest reason for assuming that the soul is material, since thought is wholly different from all accidents of matter; and we do not see why we should assume something for which we have no ground. Hence we say that the soul is immaterial or pneumatic, although we cannot prove this. It does not follow from this, however, what was a necessary consequence of the Leibnizian Monadology, namely that by the composition of many souls a *compositum*, an appearance [Erscheinung], might finally arise.\n\nThe author explains *anima* by *vis repraesentativa*. That is false; for it follows from this that the soul could be only one fundamental power. Several fundamental powers would also make up several souls. It is therefore better to say: *anima est vis repraesentationis*. For *anima* is not the same as *vis*, since *vis* is the *effectus* of the soul in respect of a causality.",["Restated a few long runs as separate sentences and paragraphs; paragraph breaks are mine.","'kömnits' read as 'kommt es'; 'Reahtaet' as 'Realität'; 'n* Materie' is garbled OCR, rendered 'matter'; 'Thei!' as 'Theil'.","Greek 'peTocßamv eiq aXXo yivoc,' is OCR-garbled; restored as μετάβασις εἰς ἄλλο γένος (the standard phrase), a reconstruction.","'Von der Seele haben wir Erfahrung über sich, Erfahrung voraus bis in den Tod' is obscure and perhaps corrupt in the transcript; my rendering is tentative.","'Der Autor' is Baumgarten (the textbook); 'Meier' later is G. F. Meier.","'Evanescenz' kept in Latinate form, italicised; 'Phaenomena' rendered 'phenomena', Erscheinung as 'appearance'.","'Schlüsse' = inferences; 'Beharrlichkeit' = permanence."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-004":["Of the Souls of Animals",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-005":["We come now to the second part of rational psychology, namely the comparison of the soul with other beings. We can think of beings that have only the lower faculty of knowledge and wholly lack the higher. These we entitle *substantiae brutae*. Do animals lack the higher faculty of knowledge, or is it merely buried in them? That they lack it we cannot demonstrate. But why should we, in explaining certain appearances [Erscheinungen], assume more than is necessary?\n\nWe therefore assume that animals can endure to eternity and grow in their powers, and yet will never acquire understanding, because an essential element must be added to their sensibility, through which alone understanding is possible, namely apperception. Perhaps the souls of animals endure to eternity and, as in this life, so in the future one, stand in the service of man. They will nevertheless never become human souls, because, contrary to Meier's belief, they differ from man not merely in degree but in kind. It is easily conceivable, however, that since animals are now a part of man's needs, there will also in that other life, when man will be perfect, be more perfect animals which will serve him. Meanwhile these remain mere fancies, like the various opinions and enthusiasms [Schwärmereien] about beings higher than man. Even if there are such beings, man is nothing less than they; for the rational being is itself an end, and capable of happiness, if it makes itself worthy of it.",["'Erkenntniß Vermögen' rendered 'faculty of knowledge'.","'ienein Leben' read as 'jenem Leben'; 'dauren' as 'dauern'.","Divided the passage into two paragraphs for clarity; 'nichts weniger als sie' is rendered 'nothing less than they', which is ambiguous in the German (could mean 'by no means less than they')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-006":["Psychological Systems of the Commerce [*Commercium*] of the Soul with the Body",["'Commercium' kept in Latin, italicised, as a technical term."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-007":["We now come to the third part of rational psychology, namely the connection of the soul with other beings, and in particular with matter. This connection is α) immediate, with its own body, and β) mediate, by means of the body, with the rest of matter. I say: this is my body, its alterations are my alterations. Why so? The soul can never indicate its place in the world through the mere consciousness of itself; it is the body that determines its place. The soul is no object of outer sense, and for that reason we also cannot determine where it has its seat; hence it is not possible to say where precisely in the body it is. To this end soul and body stand in *commercium*, and this *commercium* is so strong that mere thinking already has an influence upon the body. The soul acts immediately upon the nervous system; the remaining parts of the body are merely instruments through which it acts by means of the nervous system. To determine this *commercium* more closely is the business of anthropology.\n\nWe consider first the possibility of the *commercium*. We already have difficulties enough in merely explaining the *commercium* of body with itself; how much harder, then, will it be to understand the possibility of the *commercium* of the soul with the body. This *commercium* shows itself in the fact that the soul, by mere will, can cause movements in the body. The following serves here. Movements are not effects, but are phenomena of effects. Movement is the successive alteration of the place which a body occupies; but it is not an effect, only a phenomenon of an effect which is unknown to us. Whether that which has influence and acts is a Leibnizian monad we do not know. But we do know that between cause and effect homogeneity is not necessary, and that mere heterogeneity can take place.\n\n(In explaining the *commercium* with the body, people stumble chiefly on this: that movement and thinking are so different that one cannot see how the one is to produce the other. But the body is a phenomenon, and consequently so are its properties. The substrate of it we do not know. How this substrate can be in *commercium* with the soul comes down to how substances in general can be in *commercium*, and the difficulty concerning heterogeneity then falls away. That bodies are mere appearances [Erscheinungen] follows quite clearly from the fact that all their properties and forces come down to the moving force. So too with impenetrability. Movement is successive presence in different places. But what brings it about that a thing can be in its own place, and successively in different places, we do not know.)\n\nHence the power of thinking can be quite different from the body, and heterogeneity creates no difficulty at all. We are therefore not at all compelled to think a *harmonia praestabilita*; here we need only assume the *systema causarum efficientium*. Connection *per harmoniam praestabilitam* is merely ideal. According to it the body would have to be able to perform its functions even if it had no soul, even if the soul had already been separated from it. The soul is therefore superfluous here. The best system is that of efficient causes, or, more precisely, of *influxus physicus*: it is by being united with the body that the soul is entitled soul. The soul makes use of the nerves as instruments, and through them influences the remaining parts of the body immediately; on the other side, the nerves are again the instruments through which the body influences the soul. If a nerve is tied off, I see nothing.\n\nDescartes said there are *ideae materiales*, impressions in the brain which the soul produces as upon a tablet. When we think, we imprint this in the brain, and it leaves traces behind, which are struck anew and refreshed by certain related nerves of the brain. Memory is thus to be explained. Bonnet holds the same opinion. But materialism, as we have already seen, is nothing. Impressions of thoughts may well remain over, but it is nevertheless a useless contrivance; for example, when one wants to retain something, the head aches, but these are no ideas, and serve only to awaken ideas. So too when the soul makes intuitions for itself through the imagination.",["The signs 'a' and 'ß' in the OCR are read as α and β, the usual enumeration.","'durchweiche' corrected to 'durch welche'; 'einei' to 'einer'; 'iene' to 'jene'; 'druken' read as 'drücken/drucken' (imprint). 'Cartes' rendered Descartes.","'Wir kommen nun zum 3ten Stück' etc.: 'Stück' rendered 'part'. 'Phaenomene' kept as 'phenomena' since the student's Latinate form is a technical term; 'Erscheinungen' is given in brackets.","I divided the long passage into paragraphs at the transcript's natural breaks; the parenthesis is kept as in the German.","'Hiezu dient nun folgendes' rendered literally ('The following serves here'); the sense is 'the following helps'.","'seine Denk Abdrücke' is probably a slip for 'keine' or 'einige'; I translated as 'impressions of thoughts' without the possessive, and the reading is doubtful.","'Zu dem Ende' is rendered 'To this end'; the sense may be 'for this reason'.","'durch einige verwandte Gehirn Nerven': translated 'certain related nerves of the brain'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-008":["Where does the soul have its seat? In general it has it in the body, but where in particular? Here there is great dispute. The ancients said: in the diaphragm, because this is moved in the case of certain affects. Descartes said that it is in the pineal gland. But this has often been found petrified, and the people have nevertheless lived. (Physicians call it the *commune sensorium*, from which the soul first begins its effects; people have tried to find this out in all sorts of ways.) The ancients also said: *anima est tota in corpore, sed totum tamen in parte ejus*. This is nothing. Many have assumed that in each part of the body there is a distinct degree of movement, and that consequently many souls would also be connected with a single body. Bonnet places it in the *corpus callosum* (Hirnschwiele), but has no other ground for this than the conjecture that it must be in that part of the body in which no two [parts] are different. But are all these opinions reasonable? The soul is an object of inner sense and therefore takes up no space. But if I give it space, I make it an object of outer sense, and matter. Hence its presence in the body cannot be determined *localiter* but *virtualiter*, through the influence which it has upon the body. This influence is immediate only upon the nerves (that is, the soul is immediately present to each nerve), and is strongest in the brain, because the nerves come together there. In the case of matter, presence in a place does indeed precede influence, but in the case of the soul it does not. Thus God's omnipresence too is to be explained only *virtualiter*. Since the soul cannot become conscious of its place in the body, because it cannot be intuited by any outer sense, it is also not able to go out of the body at will. Does the soul leave the body immediately after the body's death? If it is an atom, it must probably remain in it until the body is destroyed.",["'Zwirbeldrüse' (Zirbeldrüse) rendered 'pineal gland'; 'Cartes' rendered Descartes.","'iniedem' read as 'in jedem'; 'demienigen' as 'demjenigen'; 'tarnen' corrected to 'tamen'.","'in dem nicht zwei verschieden sind': obscure; I supplied '[parts]' and the reading is doubtful.","'und die Menschen haben doch gelebt': rendered 'the people have nevertheless lived'; the sense is that people lived on despite a petrified gland.","'Hirnschwiele' kept in German beside *corpus callosum*."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-009":["Chapter 3. Origin of the human soul",["Heading translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s22-010":["As regards the birth of man, he comes into being through generation. If the soul were material, it would originate in the same way as the rest of the matter of the body. All the parts of this matter existed beforehand, and through generation they receive only a new form. A composite can come into being without creation, because the substances are already there beforehand. But a simple being must come into being through creation, and so therefore must the soul, since it is simple. Creation is *actuatio substantiae* [the actualizing of a substance]. The author's definition, *actuatio ex nihilo* [actualizing out of nothing], is not correct. For it already follows from the concept of a substance that it must arise out of nothing. If the parts were already there beforehand, then what would now arise would be merely their form, which is an accident and not a substance.\n\nNow the question arises: Did God or the parents create the soul? Substances that are in the world cannot possibly create others. For all substances stand in *commercium* [community]. If, then, one substance produced another, it would create something in relation to which it would afterwards itself be passive. It would thus be passive in relation to itself, which cannot be thought at all.\n\nThose who assume that the parents have created the souls of their children are entitled Traducianists. The *propagatio per traducem*, in which one soul generates another, is a vulgar concept. It rests upon materialism. Parents cannot impart the soul to their children by division, for otherwise their own souls would be composite. God, then, has created the soul. But when? At birth, or at the beginning of the world? The first is maintained by the Inducianists (Concreatianists), the second by the Preexistentianists. The latter are right. For all substances must have been created at the creation of the world. If souls were still being created now, and always, these would be constant miracles, for every creation is a miracle. But if these were to become ordinary occurrences, they would be self-contradictory.",["'Eie Schöpfung' silently corrected to 'Die Schöpfung'; 'itzt' rendered 'now'.","The final sentence ('Würden aber die gewöhnlich, sind sie contradictorisch') is elliptical and possibly corrupt; I read 'die' as 'these [miracles]' and supplied 'occurrences' and 'to become'. Doubtful.","'Denn es folgt schon aus dem Begriff einer Substanz, daß sie aus nichts entstehen müße': rendered as it stands, though the sense (a substance cannot arise out of pre-existing parts) may suggest the transcript is slightly confused; the following 'Denn' clause is the argument.","'vor sich selbst paßiv' rendered 'passive in relation to itself'; the sense is that the substance would be both active and passive with regard to the same thing.","Restated the long run of sentences into paragraphs; the Latin terms are kept in italics with English supplied in square brackets."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-001":["Immortality of the Human Soul",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-002":["Here we have the following questions to answer. Will the soul continue to live? (1) As a human being or as an intelligence? (2) Does this continuance flow from the constitution of its nature (that is, is it strictly immortal), or from a special divine decree? (3) Will this continuance be universal or particular?\n\nAs regards the first question, the materialist has only to concern himself with whether the human being will continue to exist; the pneumatist, with whether he will continue to exist also as an intelligence. The first question we can set aside, for it can be a matter of indifference to us, and the human body seems, from its first determination [*Bestimmung*], to incline towards the earth. (Priestley is a materialist and assumes that the body will be awakened again after death; but that is then no immortality. For it is no continuing life, and it is not I who am awakened, but a similar being. For it is all one whether God composes such a body from the parts of my body or from other matter, since as matter or substances they are quite the same. Priestley says that the doctrine that man has a soul is a senseless doctrine and wholly contrary to Holy Scripture. For *pneuma* [πνεῦμα] is a breath, and indicates something external.)\n\nBut whether man will continue as an intelligence is more important for us to know, and here we must ask again whether his continuance after death is necessary as a universal law of nature, or is grounded upon an extraordinary divine decree. The former is more important to us, for in the case of the latter the continuance could be particular. The question here is not of a future life in general, for that we can prove teleologically; rather, we wish to be convinced of the immortality of the soul, or of its impossibility of dying. For then we are certain of the continuance of all souls after death.\n\n(To immortality it belongs that we prove: (1) the natural impossibility of dying; (2) the continuance of the soul with all its powers, and the same powers, for otherwise life would be nothing; (3) the continuance of its personality, so that it remains conscious of its former life. For otherwise it is not I that continue, but another spiritual being.)\n\nOf the immortality of the soul a natural and a moral proof will be possible. For all philosophy has to do either with nature or with morals. Morality is the determination of action according to laws of freedom. Philosophy, whether of morals or of nature, is founded on principles *a priori*. The natural proofs are of three kinds: (1) from experience, or from the nature of man as known through experience; (2) from rational psychology; (3) from the analogy of the nature of the soul with the whole order of nature.\n\nAs to the empirical proof, it has very uncertain arguments. Since we have not the slightest experience of immortality, all inferences from the experiences of this life are mere conjectures. Experiences occur only during animal life; I can therefore know the powers of the soul only when it is in *commercium* with the body. Hence they cannot give us the slightest light as to how the soul will be constituted after its *commercium* with the body has ceased.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'nveu^oc' read as πνεῦμα (the Greek is conjectural); 'Kathschluß' as 'Rathschluß'; 'hortdauer' as 'Fortdauer'; 'Eifahrung' as 'Erfahrung'; 'lolgeiungen' as 'Folgerungen'; '3erlei' as 'dreierlei'; 'dur ch' and similar breaks closed up; 'Liebersicht' as 'Uebersicht' was in the next passage.","'Bestimmung' rendered 'determination' per the fixed terms, though 'destination' is also possible here (\"von seiner ersten Bestimmung an\").","'Intelligenz' rendered 'intelligence' (a pure spiritual being); 'Pneumatist' kept as 'pneumatist'.","Restated the opening run of questions and split the long paragraph into several; the parenthetical remarks are kept in parentheses as in the transcript.","'die Fortdauer der Seele mit allen ihren Kräften und derselben' is slightly irregular; rendered 'with all its powers, and the same powers'.","'Die Moral ist die Bestimmung der Handlung nach Gesetzen der Freiheit': 'Bestimmung' here is 'determination'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-003":["(To conduct a proof from experience, we should have to be able to place ourselves outside the *commercium* with our body, or to be able to question other souls so placed; but neither is possible. The soul is also compared with a caterpillar, which after its apparent death becomes a beautiful butterfly, and an analogy in nature is supposed to be found in this. But it is quite false, for if one kills the caterpillar it never becomes a butterfly. In general there is nothing similar to this in nature and experience.)\n\nIt is said that in old age the body grows ever weaker but the mind ever stronger. People who rave in a feverish illness become rational shortly before death, and from this it is inferred that the soul, if freed from the body, would be able to think better and more freely than now. But against this one may object, in part, that these appearances [*Erscheinungen*] can be explained thus: when a man dies, the vital fluid in the brain becomes the more lively, the more the lower part of the body dies off. In part, too, we have experiences which testify to the contrary: namely, that the body has much influence upon the soul, that, for example, a blow on the head can produce madness and stupidity, that the soul changes with the body, and so on. In old age memory, wit and ready survey of the whole decline, but there arises a power of judgment exercised by long experience. Hence the old are the best counsellors, and they have been chosen for this from of old.)",["The passage opens with a parenthesis that closes after 'Erfahrung nichts ähnliches'; the final closing parenthesis after 'gewählt' has no opening mark in the text, and I have kept it as printed.","'Papillon' rendered 'butterfly'; 'W itz' read as 'Witz' (wit); 'Liebersicht' read as 'Uebersicht' (survey); 'iene' as 'jene'; 'ieher' as 'jeher'.","'Vernünftig' rendered 'rational' (of those who recover their reason before death).","'Leute, die in einer hitzigen Krankheit phantasiren': 'raving in a feverish illness'; 'hitzig' = acute fever.","'daß die Seele mit dem Körper wechsele' rendered 'changes with the body'; sense clear but terse."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-004":["In rational psychology we have three points to prove: (1) the persistence of the soul as substance; (2) its continued existence after death as an intelligence; (3) its continued existence as a person. It is believed that the first is settled, since substance is persistent. But the soul is known as substance only through the I, and so we do not even know whether it is substance in the sense that, as such, it could not perish. Mendelssohn was the first to attempt to prove the immortality of the soul as substance, but he did not succeed. He says that the soul is simple and therefore cannot be dissolved by division. But it can be extinguished, and its reality can gradually vanish. He says that this would be a leap; but it could still happen in accordance with the *lex continui* [law of continuity]. Further, even if the soul's continued existence as substance were certain, that would not yet prove its continued existence as an intelligence. The preceding proof proves the incorruptibility (*incorruptibilitas*) of the soul. Nevertheless, after death the soul may fall into an eternal dreaming or perpetual sleep, which is entitled the sleep of souls. If, however, it continues as an intelligence, there is nothing at all to be feared on this score. Finally, we must prove the continuance of its personality.\n\nImmortality is the impossibility of dying. To die is the end of life. Life is animal when in *commercium* [community] with the body, and spiritual when without *commercium* with it. Animal life may therefore well cease, but spiritual life cannot. The life of a thinking being consists in personality, since it is conscious of itself. Immortality will be the necessary continuance of this personality, not *vita bruta* [mere animal life]. If the soul lost its personality, or became another person, it would no longer be the same, and one could not say that it continued its life. Metempsychosis, for example, is such an interruption, since in it the personality is altered. Immortality will therefore be continuous life. The soul must connect its subsequent state with the preceding one, and know that it is the same as it was before. If in the other world the soul were conscious of nothing of what had happened to it here, its substance would indeed continue, but not its person.",["Original 'Lege continui' (OCR 'Lege') is rendered as *lex continui*, the obvious reading; the transcript's Latin case is irregular.","'ihre Fortdauer als Person' etc.: 'Fortdauer' rendered 'continued existence' and 'Beharrlichkeit' as 'persistence'; 'Persönlichkeit' = personality.","'vor diese Persönlichkeit' read as 'für diese Persönlichkeit'; I take the sense as 'of this personality' and render 'continuance of this personality'. Doubtful.","'Unsterblichkeit wird ... sein' kept in the future tense as in the German; 'mit. der ersten' and 'Seelen Schlaf' OCR slips silently corrected.","I split a few longer sentences and supplied '[law of continuity]', '[community]', '[mere animal life]' in brackets as glosses."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-005":["2. The rational proof. All matter is lifeless; it has no capacity to determine itself, and the principle of life is something other than matter. For every piece of matter remains in motion or at rest until it is changed by something else. Matter, then, has merely receptivity or passivity. The principle of life, however, is spontaneity, or the capacity to determine oneself from inner principles. (The body is a hindrance to life, because it contributes nothing to the furtherance of life and yet has a very strong influence upon the soul. It is just as if someone were chained to a cart and had always to drag it with him. If the cart is well greased up to the time of death, he can draw it more easily and go faster; if not, he must go more slowly. Should one then suppose that, freed from the cart, he would not be able to walk at all? No; he will rather walk more easily and better.) Matter has no principle of life in itself, and therefore contributes nothing to the furtherance of life. Since it is wholly lifeless, it furnishes no condition of life either. In man, therefore, it is not the body that is the principle of life but the soul, and the body does not serve to further life. Accordingly the separation of the body from the soul must involve no alteration of life. It must signify just as much as, for example, the separation of a horse from the wagon it has hitherto drawn. If, notwithstanding all this, we see that the body does exert an influence upon the soul, it must then be a hindrance to life, for no other kinds of influence can be thought. Death is therefore a furtherance of the life of the soul, and its future life will first be its true life. The animal is almost half of lifelessness. This proof, however, is not wholly rigorous. For the lifelessness of matter is merely a property of the appearance (*Erscheinung*), namely of the body. Whether the substance which underlies the body may not also have life, we do not know; yet the proof is good against the materialists.\n\nThere is, however, a further difficulty against this: the body, though it contributes nothing to life, may nevertheless be the sole condition upon which life depends. If the soul had no senses and sensations, it would also have no material for thought. If the soul were therefore separated from the body, its vital force would indeed not cease, but its life would. Through illness one can lose one's memory; hence the soul may perhaps need the body for memory. Perhaps in the future it will be able to be conscious of itself without a body; for I must become conscious of myself through clear representations. These, however, rest upon the body, since they are sensations. For all that, this proof has much in its favour, and shows that it is not necessary that the soul must cease with the body. Moreover, it also serves to renew the difficulties that stand opposed to the assumption of immortality. This, then, was a proof from the particular nature of the soul. From this we shall presumably never be able to prove immortality with certainty, but perhaps we can prove it from the whole nature of all things, by analogy. This we now wish to do; and so, (β) we shall conduct the proof from the analogy of the entire nature with the nature of our soul.",["The closing parenthesis after 'Materialisten.)' in the German is misplaced (the opening one stands after 'bestimmen'); I closed the parenthesis after the cart simile, where the sense requires it, and left the later remarks unbracketed. Doubtful.","'Das Thierische ist beinahe die Hälfte der Leblosigkeit' is obscure and translated literally; the text may be corrupt.","'Schwürigkeiten zu renoviren' read as 'Schwierigkeiten'; 'renoviren' rendered 'renew'. 'ß.' read as the Greek β (second heading), 'des Vermögen' corrected to 'das Vermögen'; 'wiedei' to 'wider'.","'Receptivitaet' = receptivity, 'Spontaneitaet' = spontaneity; 'Erscheinung' = appearance, German given because the argument turns on it.","Restated a few long sentences as shorter ones; 'Lebens Kraft' rendered 'vital force'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-006":["Analogy we cannot dispense with at all. Most of our inferences are analogical. That is, we infer that because two things have in common those properties which we know, they will also agree in those properties which we do not know. Here, of course, deception is also possible, and in many cases actual. Here we prove from the nature of ends, and this proof is therefore a teleological one. For nature is, physically, a realm of efficient causes, and, teleologically, a realm of ends. The principle in the realm of ends is that everything has its end and nothing is in vain. This is a necessary postulate of reason, for otherwise reason can explain nothing to itself. It is also confirmed by experience. (Even the atheist must assume ends, otherwise he cannot explain to himself the structure and organisation of bodies. By this principle investigators of nature have been led to many a useful experience. Men have been able to see without the crystalline lens, and it was therefore not known what it could signify. The crystalline lens is a denser humour than the vitreous humour lying beneath it. Euler accordingly hit upon the thought that it must prevent the shadings of colours in the eye, and that if in telescopes one were likewise to produce a glass darker than the colour glass, the result would be the same. Dollond opposed him, but at last tried it, and produced flint glass and the Dollond telescope.) We find, however, in nature that everything not only has its end but is also determined to develop itself fully and to attain its whole end, since it actually does attain it. Animals have instincts that are exactly adapted to their appetites. All their members have a determinate use. Now there are in the human soul predispositions which in this life do not attain their determination [*Bestimmung*] at all. We therefore infer that a time must come in which they will attain it. Now everything rests upon the proof of the minor premiss [*minoris*], and this is as follows. The human soul has three faculties: the faculty of knowledge, the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, and the faculty of desire. As regards the first, it is not satisfied here at all. We find in particular that our desire for knowledge extends to things the knowledge of which can be of no use to us in our life; indeed, that we find the greater pleasure in the knowledge of things, and find this knowledge the more sublime, the more remote the things are from us, for example the stars. A man who thinks only of what can serve to preserve his life we despise. Of what use, then, were Newton's discovery of the laws of attraction and his other philosophical enquiries? All this is not at all necessary for this life. It would therefore be superfluous if there were no future life; and besides, even in this life we do not satisfy our desire for knowledge. The striving after knowledge, when pursued to a certain degree, even appears to run counter to our determination on earth. By it one weakens one's mind, cannot so well provide for one's physical welfare, and cannot so fulfil one's animal determination. It is just the same with the feeling of pleasure and displeasure. What does it avail us that we take pleasure in the morally beautiful? And, in respect of the faculty of desire, man feels within himself laws which drive him to the good, and which he esteems more highly than his life. Yet here we cannot even properly carry them out; for to carry them out we should often have to risk and forfeit our very life, and for this man can expect no reward here. Even if he knows that no one is aware of his good deeds, and that he is convinced of them only inwardly, this alone affords him the most valuable satisfaction. But this would be fancy and chimera if he were destined only for this life. Thus all talents in this life are disproportionate [to their ends]. Newton, a man of such great talents, scarcely reaches sixty years of age, has scarcely begun to discover something new, and already he dies. Had the man lived only twice as long, he could always have built further upon his experiences, and in time would have discovered as much as the whole human race will not discover in a thousand years. As it is, when a man first truly understands how things are, when he truly comes to his reason, he dies at once. There is this further consideration, that it is not even advisable to develop all one's predispositions, since this often runs counter to our physical determination. Thus by hard study one can bring on an early death. One does harm to oneself in household affairs. Many are thereby prevented even from marrying. This proof was devised by Fordyce, who makes use of the following comparison. If a higher being were to perceive in a mother's womb [*Utero*] a creature that has eyes, ears and other members, it would conclude: this creature is destined for a life in which these members could be used. — This proof is especially excellent because we here infer from a universal law of nature, according to which all men are immortal. The preceding proof went further still, for it proved also the immortality of the souls of animals. This one does not prove that. But those [animal souls] are also of less interest to us. Whether, however, the soul of man will live for ever, I do not yet know from this proof. Who knows whether I may not die just when all these predispositions have developed? Suppose that I were to live ever so long; if I must nevertheless cease at some time, I should rather wish to have ceased sooner. Now comes the moral proof. All moral arguments abstract from the nature of the soul, yet they do not deny its immateriality. But because they do not build the proofs upon immateriality, they are more intelligible to the common man than the others. This proof is either a moral-theological or a theologico-moral one; in the latter we take as ground the moral properties of the highest being. It rests upon God's goodness and justice, but is very weak. It is said that virtue is so little rewarded here in the world, and vice so little punished. If God is just, a future life is to be hoped for in which this disproportion will be removed. But if only we could judge the virtuous man quite strictly, his vices would certainly amount to more than his virtue. (Who knows the inmost heart of every man, so as to know whether the vicious actions are not already punished here and the virtuous ones rewarded? And then it is presumption to wish to judge of God's justice and goodness.) But even if we grant a future retribution, it by no means follows that it will be eternal. Can we then demand an eternal reward for our few virtues? And if we were then to have some myriads of years, which would then pass away, what would that help us? Finally, this proof shows only that the future life will be particular. For children, the feeble-minded and the wholly stupid, and peoples whose life has neither moral worth nor unworth, are, according to this proof, excluded from it, or at least not included in it. There is yet another proof, from the wisdom of God: namely, that this wisdom would suffer great offence if rational beings were not to continue after the change of the world, so that they might regard the proof of divine goodness as a whole source. Otherwise there would be nothing but fragments; the universe would be in vain unless rational beings were to contemplate the whole. But there may well be other rational beings whom God has determined for this, for it by no means follows [with regard to] all [of us]. There remains to us now the moral-theological proof. This is grounded on the moral laws, which are, as it were, geometrically necessary. Morality would be without incentives [*Triebfedern*] if there were no immortality of the soul. Without belief in immortality it would have force only in the idea, but not in reality. Since, then, reality would be lacking to morality [without that belief], the hope of immortality cannot be separated from it. Accordingly men cannot, without it, form any resolution to good actions, and the hope and belief in an immortality is a practical postulate of reason. (An objection is raised against the continuance of the soul: that birth is something so contingent. How many millions of germs are stifled, or die in earliest infancy? A ruler can multiply his subjects as a farmer his hens, if he promotes every branch of livelihood. How then should so contingent a thing as the coming into being of man be determined for immortality? Answer: the soul must already have existed beforehand, although birth is the first beginning of its life in the world of sense. The metempsychosis of the Indians is, after all, a peculiar idea: namely, that the soul was previously a pure spirit (Dewa), which is joined to the human body as a trial, thereafter passes into other animals until it becomes a man again, in which state it is wholly holy, which is the Lamaic rebirth; and then it is swallowed up in the abyss of all spirits, that is, God.) Morality rests upon the concept of freedom, and is a necessary hypothesis. Although we cannot comprehend freedom, we must nevertheless necessarily assume it. If human actions appear to flow only from the mechanism of nature, this holds only of their appearances. What lies at the ground of the actions, namely the purer mode of thinking of man, is unknown to us. Freedom in the speculative sense we cannot comprehend. It is a practical postulate. This is distinguished from hypotheses. A hypothesis is a proposition which one assumes for the explanation of certain phenomena, which, however, might well be explained through another hypothesis. A practical postulate, on the other hand, is only that [proposition] which is the one possible [proposition] able to explain certain appearances. That men are bound to act honestly and to be good is an apodictic proposition. Incentives thereto only immortality can give. Hence it is a practical postulate that the soul is immortal. The analytic and the moral proof of immortality are very strong, especially the latter. Although this cannot be reckoned among the scientific proofs, it has nevertheless strength. For by it I bring my opponent *ad absurdum practicum*.",["Opening: 'Dri Analogie' is an OCR slip; I read 'Der Analogie' (genitive after *entbehren*). 'l nsterblichkeit' read as 'Unsterblichkeit'; 'Untertlianen' as 'Untertanen'; 'kreiheit' as 'Freiheit'. All silently corrected.","Long sentences were divided into shorter ones in places (the opening on analogy, the Newton and Fordyce passages), and parentheses kept as in the German.","'Der analytische und moralische Beweis' (near the end) is doubtful: 'analytische' may be a slip of the transcriber for 'analogische', since the first proof argued from analogy. I translate as read.","'in diesem legen wir ... zum Grunde': 'diesem' could refer to either kind of proof; I take it as 'the latter' (theologico-moral), since it is the one grounded on God's moral properties. Judgment, not certain.","'damit sie den Beweis der göttlichen Güte als eine ganze Quelle ansehen' is obscure and possibly garbled in transcription (perhaps 'als ein Ganzes'). Rendered literally; 'sie' taken to be the rational beings.","'von allen folgt es gar nicht': I supply '[with regard to] all [of us]' to complete the sense. 'dunkleres Glas' translated literally as 'darker'; 'Schattierungen der Farben' as 'shadings of colours' (presumably colour fringes), 'Farben Glas' left as 'colour glass'.","'im speculativen Verstande' is rendered 'in the speculative sense', not 'understanding', since *Verstande* here has the older sense of 'meaning'. *Anlagen* = predispositions; *Bestimmung* = determination (or destination, as context suggests); *Triebfedern* = incentives.","'Absurdum praticum' is the transcript's spelling; I give the Latin as *ad absurdum practicum*. 'Dewa' is kept as written."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-007":["How are we to conceive the state after death? Either the soul will be wholly free from [any] body, or a new body will be given over to the soul. If the latter, then a palingenesis takes place. Priestley and other materialists have been obliged to assume that the soul's present body would surround it in a new life also. It is a bold thought to compare the resurrection with the metamorphosis of a butterfly, for if the caterpillar is burnt, no chrysalis arises from it. Whole nations have differed in the manner in which they conceived the immortality of the soul. The Romans, for example, regarded the body as a prison of the soul and burnt it to that end. The Egyptians, on the other hand, believed it to be a necessary vehicle of the soul, and sought to preserve it and to protect it from decay. Whether the soul will fall into a sleep for a time immediately after death, we have nothing to say either for or against. Even supposing it to be so, no one need be troubled on that account, since the brevity of this sleep vanishes beside an eternity. But what are we to represent to ourselves by the separation of the soul from the body? Nothing further than the beginning of the intellectual life and the end of the sensible life. It is a materialistic representation to think that the soul, as it were, withdraws from the room when a man dies; for the soul has no local presence. The soul then begins to intuit things otherwise than it was accustomed to do in its connection with the body. Even now we find ourselves in the intelligible world, and each man, according to the character of his mode of thinking, can count himself as belonging either to the company of the blessed or to that of the damned. He is only not conscious of this now; after death he will become conscious of this company. Man, then, does not first come into heaven or hell; he only sees himself in it. This is a splendid representation. Where are heaven and hell? If we ask after the place, that is sensible, and the soul is made into a body. Heaven is the realm of the rewarding judge, and hell the realm of the punishing judge. Through reason we are even now conscious of ourselves as being in an intelligible realm; after death we shall intuit and know this, and we shall then be in a quite different world, which, however, is altered only in form, namely in that we shall know things as they are in themselves. The opinion of Leibniz, that the soul even here has, and in the future will have, a vehicle of matter which is indestructible, is sensible and explains nothing. —",["OCR 'hndt' read as 'findet'; 'deslntellectuellen' as 'des Intellectuellen'; 'Intelligiblen' kept as intelligible.","The text reads 'meinem Körper' and 'meinem neuen Leben'; this looks like a slip or misreading for 'einem'/'dem'. I translated 'any body' and 'a new life' [supplied by judgment]; the reading is doubtful.","'Palingenesie' kept as 'palingenesis'. 'anschauen' rendered 'intuit' (Anschauung = intuition), 'erkennen' as 'know'.","'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Denkungs Art' rendered 'mode of thinking'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-008":["End of the Psychology",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-009":["From another version",["Editorial heading in the Academy Edition, translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-010":[". . Kaestner gave this up and said: Whoever wishes to prove the *lex continui* must prove this proposition. Otherwise, if one passed immediately from one direction to another, there would be a leap. Only in a curved line can it change its direction continuously. Hence Newton also says that the rays of light fall upon the mirror in a curved line and are also repelled by it in this way, although the angles x and y are equal).",["The passage begins mid-argument; the proposition Kaestner is said to have given up is not in the passage. The closing parenthesis has no opening one here, and is kept.","OCR 'eontinui' read as 'continui'. 'sie' ('it') is probably the moving body or the line of motion; I left it as 'it'.","'x = y gleich' translated as 'the angles x and y are equal'; the original sign reading is uncertain."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-011":["This law of nature can also be applied to the species and genera of things (how far this application of the proposition can go, however, is unknown), namely when one says: no species or genus is so closely related to another that there should not still be possible between them an intermediate species or intermediate genus. (The *lex continui* serves to refute the monadology. The world cannot consist of monads, for the simple is, in respect of quantity, a zero. But from zero to a quantum the transition is a leap. However much of the simple I may put together, it never becomes a quantum. In respect of quality the simple is indeed something positive, but in respect of quantity it is not. One can obtain no complete concept of the *lex continui* unless one takes the first state in which a thing is as the infinitely smallest part of the state into which the thing is still to come. Thus one must regard the zero as the infinitely smallest part of the following state; rest, for example, as the infinitely smallest part of motion. In relation to the following state one must not regard the first state as a mere 0, because 0 is wholly different from every state. We must arrive at this law; for two states are never next to each other, but there is always a moment between them. In the world everything accords with the *lex continui*, and hence all motion also takes place in curved lines.) This is also entitled the law of nature, and indeed the mechanical law of nature, whereas that [other law] is entitled the cosmological. We do indeed find it, because [nature] is so constituted; but we cannot prove through reason that it must be so. Hence it does not belong in metaphysics at all. This is also called the scale of things, in which God too has been counted, so that he would have merely an infinitely higher degree of perfection than the creatures. Voltaire mocks at this, representing the scale as a great procession in which God, the Pope, the angels, the cardinals, and so on, would appear. It is also futile to include God in this scale, for he is specifically different from the creatures altogether. He is *causa* of everything, the creature is *causatum*. It would be just as if, in order to describe a surface, I were to prolong a straight line without end; it would never in all eternity become a surface.",["I kept the long parenthesis as in the source, but divided some sentences for clarity.","'ienes' (that) is ambiguous; I took it as another law previously treated, here rendered 'that [other law]'. 'weil es so beschaffen ist' is supplied as '[nature]' by judgment, since 'es' could also refer to the law.","'Zero' left as 'zero'; 'caußa/caußatum' normalised to *causa/causatum*; 'lege eontinui' read as ablative *lege continui*, rendered as *lex continui*; 'Quanti/Quanto' rendered 'quantity/quantum'.","'Stufenleiter' rendered 'scale' (of beings)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-012":["II.) *In mundo non datur hiatus*: there is in the world no empty space and no empty time. This proposition belongs under the category of magnitude. Two kinds of empty space are possible:",["'Größe' rendered 'magnitude' for the category (Quantität elsewhere 'quantity')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-013":["1. An empty space outside the world, or *vacuum extra mundanum*, which encloses it. And that is nothing. For the world of sense has no limits; at least we cannot determine them through any possible experience. Hence we cannot comprehend the empty space outside the world at all, since it is no object of our experience and is nothing real. But it is not this empty space that is here in question; rather",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is left unfinished. 'Sinnen Welt' rendered 'world of sense'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-014":["2. Of empty space in the world, or the *vacuum intermissum vel concretum*. Of this, too, no experience is possible for us. Moreover, through a *hiatus* two things in the world would be separated from their *nexus*, and this is inadmissible, since the world stands in thoroughgoing *nexus*. Besides, a *hiatus* would at the same time be a *saltus*; and that the latter does not occur has already been proved. An empty time in which nothing elapsed would be a *saltus*. An empty space is also a *saltus*. For if a body merely moved in an empty space, nothing would be altered either in itself or outside it. No alteration at all would then take place, and consequently another alteration would follow immediately and all at once upon the preceding one, without this latter attaining its degree through a gradual increase in the intervening time. That, however, would be a *saltus*. For example, if a body falls through a hollow sphere and the sphere contains an empty space, the time the body spends in falling would be an empty time. For neither would the body itself be altered, nor anything outside it, since there would be nothing outside it. Through its motion, however, it would not be altered at all. But the empty time between two states is a *saltus*.",["\"boiglich\" is evidently an OCR slip for \"folglich\"; rendered \"consequently\".","The opening \"2\" is taken as the enumeration of the section (the second item) and kept as a numeral.","\"Zwischen Zeit\" read as \"Zwischenzeit\" (intervening time).","The sentence \"Es würde denn also gar keine Veränderung geschehen\" is rendered \"No alteration at all would then take place\"; \"denn\" is taken as a particle of inference (= \"dann\").","Latin terms (*vacuum intermissum vel concretum*, *hiatus*, *nexus*, *saltus*) are left in Latin as technical terms; the German \"Saltus\" in the last sentence is likewise given in italics.","\"Veränderung\" rendered \"alteration\" (change in the state of a thing); the sentence \"dieser ... den Grad erlangte\" is somewhat loose in the German, and I have resolved \"diese\" as the preceding alteration."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-015":["III. *In mundo non datur casus.* *Casus est eventus in mundo non determinatus secundum leges naturales.* Chance [Zufall] is the arising of an occurrence without cause and sufficient ground. This runs counter to the *principium rationis sufficientis*, without which no experience is possible. The world is a sum of appearances. In the world everything happens according to laws of nature. The actions of human beings also happen according to laws of nature, although their will does not stand under laws of nature. Between natural necessity and chance there is something intermediate, namely freedom. This may well be contrary to the causes of what happens, but it can never be chance. For from chance I cannot explain the least thing. The proposition \"*In mundo non datur casus*\" holds only of occurrences, and so only of the world of sense.",["\"Zufall\" is rendered \"chance\", matching the Latin *casus* given beside it.","\"Diese kann wohl Ursachen von dem was geschieht auch zuwieder sein\" is awkward in the German; I read it as \"freedom may even run counter to the causes of what happens\" and smoothed it. The sense is somewhat doubtful, and the transcript may be defective here.","\"Sinnen Welt\" rendered \"world of sense\" (*mundus sensibilis*).","\"Natur Gesetzen\" and \"Natur Nothwendigkeit\" are normalised to \"laws of nature\" and \"natural necessity\"."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-016":["Chance (*Zufall*) is also entitled a blind contingency (*blindes Ohngefähr*), and for this reason: a thing through which one cannot see is sometimes called blind, for example blind glass. Now if we assume a chance as the cause of events, we cannot see at all why and to what end the event occurred, since a sufficient ground is lacking to us. (In the world everything happens according to universal and constant rules. This holds only of the world of sense. For happening presupposes time, and consequently nothing happens in the *mundus noumenon*; hence the *principium rationis sufficientis* holds only of *phaenomena*. For the world of sense is, objectively, the sum of all possible experiences, and it is only through the *ratio sufficiens* that experience is possible. A cause is that upon which an event follows according to a necessary rule. In the world of sense the causality of an event is again an event, for in the world of sense everything happens. Hence there is here no totality in the series of conditions. The causality of an event is itself again an event. For if it had been causality at all times, the event would have existed at all times. But this contradicts the concept of an event, which signifies the coming-to-be of a thing, and so presupposes a time at which the thing was not. The causality, then, has also come to be, and is therefore also an event. An entire series of such events must also contain something unconditioned; for if the series is complete, it must be unconditioned. There must therefore be an unconditioned first cause. But if I say, God gave the world a beginning, then he was its causality, and since I thus connect him with time, his causality was again an event. To escape this difficulty I must bear in mind that God is to be regarded as a thing in itself. In such a being causality is not an event. The arrangement whereby everything in the world happens according to constant rules may be entitled the mechanism of nature. Otherwise the mechanism of a thing means the arrangement of a thing according to laws of motion, but more generally it can signify the arrangement according to all sorts of laws. In the world of sense, then, everything proceeds according to the mechanism of nature, according to natural necessity. Opposed to this is contingency, *casus*. Between the two lies the mean: freedom. Now one might suppose that in the world of sense there is no freedom; yet there is. For if I think a thing in itself, this is indeed a substratum of appearances, and so far, as appearance, it belongs under the mechanism of nature, but as a thing in itself it does not. Such a noumenon, then, acts as appearance according to the mechanism of nature, [yet] its actions do not happen merely so, but according to its will, and not through the mechanism of nature. Its causality with respect to an event is not again an event, for it is not an appearance. My reason is a faculty of knowing things as they are; hence I too must be a thing in itself. All my actions happen according to the mechanism of nature, etc., in so far as they have their ground in the other [i.e. in appearances], but not *through* the mechanism of nature; rather I have the power of determining myself through reason according to objective grounds, independently of all external circumstances, *stimuli*, etc. In this respect I have freedom. The difficulty about freedom therefore lies solely in taking appearances for things in themselves. For these cannot help us out, since in appearances everything happens according to the mechanism of nature. Since the soul is also a thing in itself, it is free. For there its causality is not again an event, but it acts according to its own motives, which it takes from itself. As a thing in itself it does not bring forth its actions in temporal succession at all; they merely appear so to us. Thus the antinomy, that in the world there is freedom and necessity, is resolved, in that it is shown that these are not real opposites, since they relate to different objects, the former to the *mundus noumenon*, the latter to the *mundus phaenomenon*, and hence both can obtain at once. So it is also with the following antinomy: whether in the world everything, the conditioned as well as the unconditioned, is contingent, or whether something in it is necessary. The former holds of the *mundus phaenomenon*. For in space and time nothing is necessary, because everything that has a cause is an event. But in the *mundus noumenon* the cause can be necessary, and of this the latter proposition therefore holds. If a world is, something necessary must exist, neither as cause nor as part. Both propositions are true, however contradictory they may seem. If we consider the world as the content of appearance, then nothing is necessary, because in the world of sense there is no totality. But if we regard the world as the sum-total of things themselves, then something must be necessary. Among appearances nothing is necessary. For if there were a being that had produced the appearance, it would have had to create it in time. There would accordingly have to be a time at which God had not yet created any appearance. He would then himself be an event, himself an appearance, and would accordingly have his ground in another event. We escape this contradiction if we regard God as a thing in itself, whose effects in the world of sense happen indeed according to the mechanism [of nature], but not through it, rather through freedom. *In mundo non datur casus*: this proposition accordingly means positively that everything in the world happens necessarily according to certain rules. In the world, then, there is in itself no chance; but we may call a comparative chance that which does have its ground, but which we cannot see, and of such chances there are enough. Such a comparative chance is entitled good or ill fortune, when the cause of our well-being rests not on our actions but on other circumstances.",["Parenthesis: the long parenthesis opens after 'fehlt' and its closing bracket is not in the text; I have kept it open to the end of the 'Gott' argument at 'Gott als Ding an sich', and left the later text unbracketed. The transcript's own grouping is uncertain.","'Zufall' rendered 'chance' and 'Ohngefähr' 'contingency' in the heading phrase; later 'Ohngefähr' is rendered 'contingency' alongside *casus*. Elsewhere 'zufällig' is 'contingent'.","Restated: the sentence 'Ein solches Noumenon handelt also zwar... seine Handlungen geschehen nicht so, sondern...' is irregular (zwar without a matching aber); I supplied '[yet]' and 'merely' to smooth it.","'in der andern' (\"in the other\") is obscure; I read it as 'in appearances' and flagged this in brackets. Doubtful.","'Escheinung' silently corrected to 'Erscheinung'; 'Gliik/Unglük' to 'Glück/Unglück'.","The final sentence ('Dann beruht...') is a loose 'then'-clause; I joined it as a 'when' clause.","'Sinnen Welt' = 'world of sense' (*mundus sensibilis*); 'Begebenheit' = 'event'; 'Bewegungs Gründe' = 'motives'; 'Vermögen' = 'faculty' / 'power' as the sense required."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-017":["If I assume a blind contingency, I have no trouble in searching out the grounds of events, and this proposition, like the following, is a crutch of lazy reason. IV. *In mundo non datur fatum.* Fates (*Schicksale*) are departures from maxims. Maxims are fundamental propositions taken from the subject; principles (*Principien*) are fundamental propositions taken from the object. They are speculative or practical. The former demand that which furthers the interest of my reason. Fates conflict with the interest of reason. For if I assume them, I must renounce the use of reason. Fate is also contrary to the practical maxims, which are connected with the will, for example in Mohammedanism, [the doctrine] that life depends on a blind fate, and that it has been determined for every man before his birth how long he is to live.",["'Diese sind Grundsätze die vom Subiect...' is irregular: 'Diese' refers to maxims, contrasted with principles; I restated it in two sentences. Then 'Sie' is read as maxims (speculative or practical) and 'Jene' as the speculative maxims; this is a judgment, since the antecedents are unclear.","'Schicksahle' = *Schicksale*, 'fates'. 'wieder' read as 'wider' (contrary to), and 'jiractischen' as 'practischen' (OCR slips). 'Mohomedanismus' kept as 'Mohammedanism'.","'[the doctrine]' is supplied; the German has a bare 'daß'-clause."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-018":["*Fatum* signifies something once pronounced. Fate [Schicksal] is a blind necessity without law. Natural necessity, by contrast, is in accordance with laws, and it is according to these that events in the world occur. Blind necessity is the *contrarium* of contingency [Zufall]. But that something should nevertheless be necessary without any grounds and causes, of this we have not the slightest concept. For we find nothing at all contradictory in the supposition that something might also not have happened. To want to explain anything from fate is absurd, for to appeal to fate means precisely that I cannot explain the matter. When we do something, in so far as it springs from physical causes, we must explain it from the laws of nature and not from spontaneity; otherwise we should arrive at intelligible grounds, which belong to the *mundus noumenon*. That would be a *metabasis eis allo genos* [a passage into another kind]; such a procedure is admissible only in moral relations. To the *mundus noumenon* belong the *intelligentiae* (beings of understanding and free beings, and so also man). The author here begins to speak of the *ens extramundanum*. If this world is a contingent being, it is a *causatum alterius*. That it is contingent follows from this: the *mundus noumenon* is a *totum* of substances which stand in *commercium* with one another. By means of this *commercium* the substances depend reciprocally upon one another. Substances therefore have no necessary existence, for absolutely necessary existence presupposes an independent existence, and this the world does not have. Accordingly it is dependent upon another being. This being does not belong to the world. Otherwise it would be at once cause, in that it had produced the world, and at the same time effect, in that it belonged to the world; and that is quite inadmissible, for cause and effect must be distinct. Consequently the world, as effect, must also be distinct from its cause. The *causa mundi* is therefore an *ens extramundanum*.",["The Greek in the OCR ('peraßaaK ; etc ; aXAo yevoc;') is badly garbled; I read it as *metabasis eis allo genos*, which the sense (passage into another kind) clearly requires. Please verify against the Academy text.","'Fatum bedeutet ein einmal ausgesprochenes': the Latin etymology (*fatum* from *fari*) is left implicit, as in the German.","'das findt nur in moralischen Verhältnissen statt' is restated as a separate sentence; 'such a procedure' is supplied for the pronoun 'das'.","'V irkung' silently corrected to 'Wirkung'; 'wiedersprechendes' and 'wiedersinnisch' rendered as 'contradictory' and 'absurd'.","Latin 'caußatum' retained as printed (for *causatum*).","Bracketed German 'Schicksal', 'Zufall' and the gloss on the Greek are my additions."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-019":["*Causa mundi est ens supramundanum*, since the *causa* is nobler than the *effectus*, in that there lies in it the causality of still more and other *effectuum*.",["The transcript's 'super mundanum' is rendered as *supramundanum*; 'caußalitaet' kept as *causalitas* sense, translated 'causality'.","'Effectuum' is a genitive plural governed by 'causality'; Kant's Latin is kept as given."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-020":["What is necessary can subsist by itself, or is isolated; the *ens extramundanum* is therefore also isolated. It can indeed act upon other substances, but other substances cannot react upon it. The world of sense is certainly contingent, for appearances [Erscheinungen] are indeed nothing in themselves. The proximate cause of the world of sense is the *mundus noumenon*, which we do not now know, but into which, after our death, we may come as into an entirely different world. —",["'Sinnen Welt' rendered 'world of sense'; 'ia' read as 'indeed' (the emphatic particle *ja*).","'itzt' = 'jetzt', rendered 'now'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s23-021":["Second Section.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-001":["On the General Parts, or Monadology. First Chapter: On the General Simple Parts",["The heading is run together in the OCR; the division between section title and chapter title is my reading."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-002":["Egoism is the position of one who asserts that nothing exists outside himself, and that all we see is mere semblance; and he who asserts this is an egoist. Egoism may be dogmatic, and also sceptical. Scepticism of this kind has been seriously maintained by some, and it is admissible if one asserts merely that all the grounds against it are not yet sufficient. The egoist says: In a dream I also represent a world to myself, and am in it, and yet it is not so. May it not then be the same with me in waking? Against this, however, it may be said that dreams do not cohere with one another, but I dream now this, now that, whereas the appearances in waking cohere according to universal rules. Egoism is a mere problem, which has no ground at all in its favour, and yet it is very hard to prove or to refute. I cannot refute the egoist by experience, for this teaches us immediately only our own existence. Mediately we do indeed learn through the senses that other things are there; but the egoist says that these senses supply merely the ground for our becoming aware of the appearances, and that in themselves these appearances are nothing. Since nothing can be accomplished on this side, moral proofs are needed, and in particular the following. Man is limited, and therefore contingent. Accordingly he must have a cause. This highest cause must be unlimited, because it depends on nothing and therefore cannot be limited by anything. Hence it cannot be supposed that one should be quite alone.",["'sein' in 'hinreichend sein' (plural subject) kept as 'are'; 'wiederlegen' = 'widerlegen'.","'Schein' rendered 'semblance'; 'Erscheinungen' 'appearances'.","The long sentence on mediate experience is divided; 'these appearances' supplied in the clause 'in themselves nothing' to resolve the pronoun 'sie'.","'Zusammenhängen' (capitalised in the OCR) read as the verb 'zusammenhängen'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-003":["Opposed to egoism is dualism (pluralism). The dualist believes in thinking beings and bodies outside himself. In general it is so absurd [that is, egoism] that no one will ever let it occur to him to maintain this error in earnest, even if it were irrefutable.",["The German 'er' in the second sentence is ambiguous; by the sense (the error to be 'statuiert' is egoism) I take it to refer to egoism and say so in brackets. The transcript may be confused here, since 'dualism' would otherwise be the referent; please check.","'wol keiner niemals' is a double negative in the German, rendered as a single 'no one ... ever'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-004":["Idealism is the view that, besides oneself, there exist indeed thinking beings, but no bodies. This error also cannot be refuted either from experience or *a priori*, for it is not necessary that bodies exist. Here, too, a moral ground has been offered. It is said, namely, that God is the most perfect being and my author; consequently he has also created my senses, through which I constantly see bodies. The illusion, too, by which we are persuaded to take for something what is mere deception of our senses, would have to come from God. But that he would deceive us is not to be supposed. This ground is, however, very lame, for one may rightly object that we deceive ourselves if we believe what our senses teach us; it is surely rather in our power to believe this or not.",["'Blendwerk' rendered 'deception'; 'Urheber von mir' rendered 'my author'.","'Idealism ... außer einem' read as 'besides oneself' (außer einem = außer einem selbst)."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-005":["Idealism also has real grounds in its favour and is therefore more probable than egoism. Bishop Cloyd [Cloyne] in Ireland said, namely: If spiritual beings were so in *commercium* that their bodies were mere effects of their imagination, the world would lose nothing of its worth, since this rests properly upon thinking beings. It is therefore useless to assume a corporeal world, according to the principle: *Entia non sunt sine necessitate multiplicanda*. Bishop Berkeley in Ireland went still further, for he asserted that bodies are even impossible, because if one assumed them one would always contradict oneself. This is dogmatic, or crude, idealism: that no bodies exist outside us, but that appearances are nothing and lie merely in our senses and our imagination. There is, however, also a critical or transcendental idealism, which assumes that appearances are indeed nothing in themselves, but that something unknown nevertheless really lies at their ground. This is correct.",["'Cloyd' (as in the transcript) is an error for Berkeley's see of Cloyne; I give '[Cloyne]' in brackets as a supplied correction. Note that the transcript itself attributes both views to Irish bishops, confusing or doubling Berkeley.","'Körpeiwelt' silently corrected to 'Körperwelt'; 'neceßitate' normalised to *necessitate* in the Latin.","'Das hat seine Richtigkeit' rendered 'This is correct'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-006":["Materialism is the assumption that everything is material. Many people adopt this hypothesis in all seriousness. There is a difference between matter [Materie] and the material [material]. The material is that which is, or can be, a *pars constitutiva* of a matter; matter is the *extensum* that consists of such *partes*, or an impenetrable whole. Whoever maintains, then, that matter consists of simple parts or monads maintains also that these parts are indeed not matter, but are *materialia*. If, therefore, I say that the soul is simple, I cannot yet prove from this that it is immaterial, nor distinguish material from immaterial monads. No: if the soul is simple, it can also be material, though it does not on that account have to be matter. If the monads of bodies are real, they must, as much as the soul, have a representation, which Leibniz also asserts. The soul would then be such a monad as well, and so material. Thus if bodies consisted of simple parts, the materialist would not be wrong.\n\nThe grounds from which Leibniz sought to derive the existence of the monads and their power of representation are these. The world, he says, is a whole made up of simple parts, or *monades*; for whatever is composite presupposes something simple of which it is composed. There are, therefore, monads. But what powers do these have? All the powers which we know in things relate merely to external relations. Moving power, for example, is merely change of place, and impenetrability aims at persistence in place. Yet there must be inner realities, although we do not know them. They must be the causes of the outer ones. But we can become aware of such inner powers in no thing other than our own soul, since we perceive them not through outer sense but only through inner sense. And there we find that its inner power is thinking, from which willing, pleasure and displeasure arise. Since, then, the monads are also simple, like the soul, they can also have *vires repraesentativae*, and their influence can be nothing but a *modificatio virium repraesentativarum*. (They can also have a *facultas appetitiva* corresponding to the *repraesentativa*.) Accordingly the monads have a power of representation of all parts of the world by which they are affected. But because they are not alone, and are always combined with other monads, they are thereby hindered in their power of representation, and so have obscure representations; they are *sopitae*, slumbering monads. In this they are distinguished from the soul. Here Leibniz really meant merely the *mundus noumenon*; only he did not express himself quite clearly.",["'Monadatum' (OCR/transcript form) is taken as the genitive plural of a Latinised *monas*; rendered *monades* for clarity. Doubtful.","Restated the long sentence on Leibniz's grounds as several shorter sentences, and divided the passage into two paragraphs for clarity.","'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Vorstellungskraft' = power of representation; 'material' rendered 'material', with 'Materie' = matter, German supplied in brackets because the argument turns on the distinction.","Original 'Monadatum' and 'sopitae' left as in the transcript; 'sopitae' is italicised Latin = 'lulled, dormant'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-007":["That, then, is Leibniz's celebrated doctrine of monads. But it is a mere chimera. For Leibniz here committed the error of confusing the *mundus noumenon* and the *mundus phaenomenon* with one another; only of the *mundus noumenon* does it hold that bodies consist of monads. Of these, however, we know nothing but the bare name; their nature we do not know. (In regard to the *mundus noumenon*, bodies consist of simple parts. For if I remove the composition of the *compositum substantiale*, the parts still remain. Since these are then not composite, they must be simple. In the *mundus phaenomenon*, however, it is otherwise. If I here remove the composition, nothing remains to me. For space and time are here what is essential to composition; without them no thing can appear to me.)",["Parenthesis kept as in the transcript.","'Mundus noumenon/phaenomenon' kept in Latin; Kant's Latin forms are inflected (Mundi Noumenon, Mundo Noumeno) and are normalised to the nominative in English."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-008":["In regard to the *mundus phaenomenon*, however, it is manifestly false. For all these inferences, both Leibniz's own and those which the materialists draw from this proposition of Leibniz, are overthrown by the following proposition: matter, or rather the appearances [Erscheinungen] of it in the world of sense, do not consist of simple parts. For, first, no part of space is simple; and secondly, a body must consist of as many parts as the space which it occupies consists of. Hence a body is divisible to infinity, because space is divisible to infinity. Bodies themselves may perhaps consist of simple parts, but not the appearances, for these are not the bodies themselves. Now I can say: thinking beings are simple; matter, however, is not simple, nor is the material. Accordingly souls are immaterial. Impenetrability belongs to physics.",["'lstens' read as 'Istens' = 'erstens' (OCR slip for '1stens'); '2tens' = secondly.","'Haufen' idiom 'über den Haufen fallen' rendered 'are overthrown'.","'Impenetrabilitaet' rendered 'impenetrability' (cf. 'Undurchdringlichkeit')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s24-009":["Leibniz and the author distinguish mathematical and physical points. The latter are the monads, the former the places in space. They say: all monads are simple, and therefore not in space, since otherwise they would be divisible. They are therefore points, which are indivisible. But space does not consist of points. Hence neither does that which is in space consist of points.",["OCR 'I unkte' corrected to 'Punkte'; 'iene' = 'jene'; 'dei' = 'der'.","'Diese ... iene' resolved by sense: 'diese' (the nearer, physical points) = the monads; 'jene' (mathematical points) = places in space. In English, 'the latter ... the former'.","The final inference ('what is in space does not consist of points') is as in the transcript."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-001":["Second Chapter. On the First Origin of Bodies.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-002":["The author now speaks of the first possibility of the generation of bodies, and here proves various propositions *a priori*. (What is understood here is not physical but metaphysical possibility. The author explains the possibility of a body as a *monadatum* [composite of monads]. Change of space is always reciprocal. Of a ship that is going to Pillau, I say: it moves thither. But I can equally well say that Pillau moves towards the ship, ever nearer than the ship to it. This can be strictly proved, and from it I can also prove the possibility of *commercium* [community] *a priori*. The author seeks to explain the possibility of motion from the *vis repraesentativa*. But it is absolutely impossible to prove that what is an object of inner sense can be a cause of what is an object of outer sense.) (No concept of motion can be given *a priori*, because something empirical necessarily lies at its basis. The material is either that which is matter or that which is at least a *pars constitutiva materiae*. Those who make the human soul a monad cannot have the difficulty which follows from many souls making up one body; our elements are the fundamental parts of the body. *Elementa qualitativa* are those fundamental parts which cannot be further resolved into elements of different kind. *Elementa quantitativa* are those fundamental parts which can no longer be divided at all; they are entitled *atomi*.)",["'monadati' is doubtful (genitive of a Latinised form); my bracketed gloss 'composite of monads' is supplied by judgment.","'Commercii' rendered by the Latin nominative with English in brackets.","OCR 'dasienige', 'dieienigen', 'hegt' corrected silently to 'dasjenige', 'diejenigen', 'liegt'.","The sentence 'Pillau moves towards the ship, ever nearer than the ship to it' is obscure in the German ('immer näher als'); translated as it stands.","'unsere Elemente sind die Grundtheile des Körpers' is possibly corrupt or elliptical; kept literal."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-003":["The proposition that substances influence one another reciprocally through action and reaction is indeed correct. But it can be proved only in physics, from experience, and by no means *a priori* in metaphysics. Expressions such as *bellum omnium contra omnia* and the like are unsuitable. They signify only harmony; for example, the coachman acts on the horses, and the horses act on the carriage and draw it along. But *reactio* is an opposed effect, a resistance. The author wishes to prove this here *a priori* from the *nexus* of substances, but from that only *commercium* follows. The author errs, however, in taking *reactio* and impediment for [what is really] receptivity.",["'wol' = 'wohl'. 'reciptivitaet' read as 'receptivitaet' (receptivity, Empfänglichkeit); the sense of the last sentence is doubtful and the bracketed words are supplied.","'Nexu' normalised to nominative *nexus*.","'Harmonie' kept as 'harmony'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-004":["Now he wishes to prove *a priori* also that the monads are connected. But how can I see *a priori* that substance has a power? Contact is only one kind of immediate action and counteraction by virtue of impenetrability. This, however, is a physical concept.",["'Zusammenhängen' (OCR 'Zusammenhängen' with capital Z mid-sentence) taken as 'zusammenhängen' = are connected.","'Impenentrabilitaet' corrected to 'Impenetrabilitaet'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-005":["Moreover, attraction is quite different, since there one body acts on another in such a way that the other seeks to penetrate into the space in which the acting body is. [Contact] does not belong here, but pertains to matter and rests on impenetrability. This we can prove only *a posteriori*. Immediate action is a concept of the understanding. The author has therefore proved it very badly.",["'Der bei der Berührung' is garbled in the OCR; I read it as 'Der [Begriff] bei der Berührung' / 'Die Berührung' and supplied '[Contact]' in brackets. Doubtful.","'V erstandes Begriff' corrected to 'Verstandesbegriff', rendered 'concept of the understanding'.","The first sentence is obscure: the attraction described reads more like a pressure/penetration; translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-006":["An atom (the indivisible) is either so absolutely or *mathematice talis*, which no longer consists of parts but is simple; or *physice talis*, which cannot be divided further by any chemistry, although it still consists of parts.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-007":["An *atomus absolute talis* would be a monad, and that is not to be assumed. An *atomus physicus*, however, is assumed by all physicists. Figure, they say, constitutes the *differentia specifica* and can never be dissolved. For if atoms were dissolved into monads, their figure too would have to cease. There would then be no difference whatever among them, and all manifoldness in the things of the world would cease. But we see that gold remains gold even when it is dissolved into the most subtle parts.",["The German has no full stop after 'nicht anzunehmen'; I supplied one, dividing the sentence.","'Figur' is rendered 'figure' in the sense of shape."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-008":["The principle that assumes physical atoms (*atomos physicos*) as the first constituent parts is the *philosophia pigrorum* [philosophy of the lazy].",["'Prmcipien' is read as 'Principien' (OCR slip). The plural noun with singular 'ist' is kept in sense by rendering it as a single principle; the transcript is loose here.","The English beside *philosophia pigrorum* is supplied."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-009":["[The philosophy] that explains the phenomena from *corpuscula* (invisible parts) is the *philosophia corpuscularis*. (*Corpuscula* are *atomi physici*. According to the hypothesis of Descartes they are indivisible and constitute the difference of matter.) (With him the atoms are also the fundamental stuff of body. Body is that of which the limits of space for matter are determined.) This is assumed by Gassendi, Descartes, etc., etc. The atomistic of Epicurus is the same as this, except that he assumed in addition a *vacuum* or *inane*.",["The subject 'Die die Phaenomena … erklart' lacks a noun; I supply '[The philosophy]' from the predicate. 'erklart' is read as 'erklärt'.","The parenthetical remarks are Kant's own as transcribed and are kept in place. The clause on body ('Körper ist das, dessen Schranken des Raums der Materie bestimmt sind') is obscure and is rendered closely.","Cartesius = Descartes; Gaßendi = Gassendi; Epicurs = Epicurus."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-010":["He who explains the appearances from monads is a metaphysical atomist (*atomista metaphysicus*), and he who explains them from corpuscles or physical atoms (*atomis physicis*) is a physical atomist (*atomista physicus*).",["'Erscheinungen' = appearances; the Latin terms are given their English beside them."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s25-011":["The ancients entitled it *ignava ratio* [the lazy reason] when one passes at once from the *ratio proxima* to the most remote (*ad remotissimam*), without touching upon the intermediate grounds (*rationes intermedias*). That is, when one refers the explanation of all events straight to God, without investigating the nearest natural causes.",["'remotißimam' is normalised to *remotissimam*. The second German sentence is a fragment; I have joined it to the first with 'That is'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-001":["Third Chapter. On the Nature of Bodies",["'Koerper' is read as 'Körper'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-002":["The objective principles are the universal grounds of reason why things should happen. (Nature is the existence of a thing so far as it is inwardly determined according to universal laws.) We cannot see how a single created being can know things in themselves. We should not know things unless we knew them through action. These are the dynamical principles of nature. The ground of the determinability of appearance must lie in the understanding, and so also the ground of the determinability of essence. In their essence they are determined in space and time. The understanding is the faculty of rules; without it there is no experience. We must therefore premise, prior to experience, laws of nature which the understanding gives. Space and time make appearance possible, and thereby also the laws of nature of experience. — Our understanding accordingly prescribes laws to nature. But it will be asked: how are things to conform to my understanding? The appearances cannot become experiences unless they accord with the understanding. That is all that this means. These principles are therefore entitled the supreme dynamical principles. — The understanding does not prescribe, or have insight into, all the laws of nature, but only those which belong to the possibility of experience. 1. Only substance persists, and its states change. 2. Whatever happens has a cause. 3. In all appearances there is no action without reaction). Nature, then, is the first universal inner objective principle of all that belongs to the existence of the causality of a thing. By this it is distinguished from essence, which is the inner principle of the possibility of a thing. Nature is the first principle, and is therefore subordinate to no other. For example, the ground of inventions is reflection; this comes in turn from the understanding; this again from consciousness; and this is nature.",["Divided no sentences, but the transcript is loose; 'sie' in 'Ihrem Wesen nach sind sie' is taken to refer to appearances (or things), and I left it as 'they'.","'Das bedeutet dieses bloß' rendered 'That is all that this means'. 'Diese' in 'Diese heißen' is taken to refer to the three principles that follow (enumerated after), as the transcript anticipates them.","The closing parenthesis after '3. … Gegenwirkung' has no opening mark in the source; kept as it stands.","'Wesen' = essence; 'subalternirt' = subordinate; 'Bewußtsein' = consciousness."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-003":["Nature is, further, a universal principle from which I can explain all things. Finally, it is also an inner principle, one that is met with in the things themselves. By this it is distinguished from a miracle. This too is a principle, but an outer one. For example, if a sick man recovers by his nature, this comes from himself, from the constitution of his body. But if he is cured by a miracle, this comes from another cause outside him.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-004":["Nature, finally, is the principle of the existence of things. By this it is strictly distinguished from possibility. Even acute men have overlooked this distinction. Reimarus, for example, explains nature by essential force, which is false. For forces belong to nature because actuality belongs to nature. But if a substance is actual, then, in so far as it can be the ground of something else, it has force.",["'Kraft' is rendered 'force' throughout this passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-005":["But to nature there belong also formal conditions of the outer forces. For example, a wedge, since it is to split wood, must be pointed. This is the formal element in the force, and belongs to the nature of the wedge. What is merely possible has no nature; a triangle, for example. The definition of nature given by Reimarus is therefore, first, incomplete, for not forces alone belong to nature, but also their connection with one another. Secondly, it is confused with essence.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-006":["All objects of experience have their nature, for without this no experience is possible. Experience is not an aggregate of perceptions, but a whole of perceptions connected according to a principle. Hence in every thing there must be a principle according to which the perceptions are connected, and this is — nature.",["The dash before 'Natur' is in the source and kept (the OCR shows '— -')."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-007":["Nature can be taken in two senses. First, substantively or *materialiter*. This is entitled simply the nature of all things. It is the whole of all natures, the unity of all connected nature; in short, the universal nature of all things, or that which all things have in common with one another.",["'heißt' rendered 'is entitled'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-008":["Secondly, nature is also used as the particular nature of each thing, that is, *adjective vel formaliter*. Each thing has its own nature, by which it is distinguished from others. For example, the nature of body is extension and moving force; the nature of the soul is thinking and willing, etc.",["'Bewegungs Kraft' rendered 'moving force'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-009":["Finally, the term nature is also applied, and indeed most frequently, to the order of things according to certain universal laws.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-010":["It is reasonable to explain everything that happens in the world according to these laws; but this must not be done everywhere, where it is quite inapplicable. This is as great a fault as when we assume unknown forces as the ground of knowledge. For in that case I have really explained nothing. Nature is often used as a person, or as a form which is not a substance and yet is taken for one. Aristotle entitled it the *forma substantialis*, and with him this was also the soul.",["'des Forma substantialis' is doubtful; I read 'des' as an OCR or transcript slip for 'es' (or 'sie') and rendered 'entitled it'.","'Erkenntniß Grund' rendered 'ground of knowledge'. 'nicht angeht' rendered 'quite inapplicable'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-011":["Opposed to nature is, first, chance (*das Ohngefähr*). But since all things have a nature, nothing is by chance; everything is according to laws. Fate (*fatum*), however, is necessity without laws, without ground.",["'Ungefähr' rendered 'chance'. The sentence on *Fatum* lacks a verb in the source ('Das Fatum aber Nothwendigkeit'); I supplied 'is' only."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-012":["Freedom is also a nature, so far as it is determined not by others but by itself. This man has. Animals do indeed determine their instinct, but this has already been determined beforehand to that end by God. To call the beginning of nature (as the author wishes) life, and the end death, belongs only to living, not to lifeless, creatures. That would be too symbolic. A *machina* is not a *corpus secundum leges universales motus mobile* [a body movable according to universal laws of motion], as the author defines it, but [movable] *secundum leges particulares* [according to particular laws]. Otherwise every body would be a machine. Mechanism is the *modus compositionis* [mode of composition] of a machine. It is the formal element, just as the moving force is the material element of the law in nature.",["'parti culares' is read as *particulares* (OCR break). The bracketed '[movable]' is supplied to complete the ellipsis.","The 'author' is presumably the textbook author (Baumgarten), but the passage does not name him.","The last sentence is obscure ('des materiale Gesetz'); I rendered it as 'the material element of the law in nature' and the reading is doubtful.","'Thiere bestimmen zwar ihren Instinct' is rendered literally."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-013":["To explain something from the nature of bodies is entitled physical; to explain it from the nature of thinking beings, pneumatic. If I explain something not from nature but from a cause that goes beyond nature, it is explained hyperphysically. If, however, the explanation is drawn from *qualitates occultae* (occult qualities), it is hypophysical. A body stands in a *nexus mechanicus*, which can be explained from moving forces. There are, further, two kinds of physical explanation. The first is *philosophia mechanica*, which explains all phenomena (*Phaenomena*) from the shape of bodies and their general moving force. This is also entitled explanation *mechanice* or *physico mechanice*. Here I explain not the origin of motion but the alteration of motion from motions already present. For example, one explains mechanically how vinegar dissolves chalk, in this way: vinegar has in its atoms nothing but pointed particles, which look like spears. These pierce the chalk, and so it is dissolved. This force, which drives the vinegar-spears into the chalk, comes from the warmth of the outer air. But where does that come from? There we are stuck. The phenomena, then, cannot be explained mechanically after all.",["Restated the colon after 'Erklärungs Arten' as a full stop; 'Es giebt noch 2erlei' rendered 'two kinds'.","'qualitatibus occultis' kept in Latin (ablative restored to nominative plural in the English gloss for clarity); 'nexu mechanico' likewise.","'Phaenomene' rendered 'phenomena'; the term is Latinate in the transcript, not Erscheinung.","'Eßig' = vinegar; 'Atomis' rendered 'atoms'. The OCR gap before 'Eßig hat' was silently normalised.","'Dies ist auch mechanice ... erklärt': the adverbs are kept in Latin form as Kant's technical labels."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-014":["The second is the dynamical mode of explanation, in which one assumes certain fundamental forces from which one derives the phenomena. This was first devised by Newton, and is more satisfying and more complete than the former. To explain something mechanically, then, is to explain it according to the laws of motion; dynamically, from the forces of bodies. In every explanation one never comes to an end. The proper mode of explanation is the physico-dynamical, which includes both. That is the mode of explanation of the present time. The first is the mode of explanation of Descartes, the second that of the chemists. Thus Descartes, for example, explains the attractive force of bodies by assuming a matter that flowed around the bodies and held them together. But if one asks where this matter comes from, one can derive it from nothing other than God. Newton explained this appearance (*Erscheinung*) dynamically, holding that God had placed in substance a force to draw bodies to itself.",["'Cartesius' rendered 'Descartes'; 'Chymiker' as 'chemists'.","'flöße' (subjunctive of reported view) rendered 'flowed'.","'so daß Gott in die Substanz Kraft gelegt hatte' restated as 'holding that God had placed...'; 'an sich zu ziehen' ambiguous (to itself/to each other), rendered 'to draw bodies to itself' with the sense 'toward one another' possible."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-015":["Third Section",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-016":["On the Representation of the Whole",["'Vorstellung' = representation."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-017":["Chapter 1",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-018":["On the Best World",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-019":["(In the transcendental understanding (*Verstand*) all things are heterogeneous, and consequently one cannot say in this understanding that one thing is more perfect than another. One must therefore look to a certain similarity of things, and then one can draw the distinction between the good, the best and the most perfect. The good is that which is the objective ground of our choice, for what is the subjective ground of our choice is agreeable. The greatest objective ground of our choice is the best. Perfection is the ground of the greatest objective unity of knowledge. Thus the good is for the will, the perfect for the understanding. To the greatest perfection of the world belong: 1. The greatest multitude of substances, according to their extensive, intensive and protensive magnitude. 2. A complete connection of them according to the rules of the greatest unity. This latter belongs to it especially and is the chief point.) One can think a *summum bonum originarium* and *derivativum*. The world is the latter. For a *totum* is always contingent and therefore *causatum*. Here the talk is properly of the perfection of the world. The author has confused this with the *optimum*, although it is different from it. Perfection, in so far as it signifies a completeness of the whole and a reciprocal harmony and connection of the whole, is transcendental perfection. Here, then, the perfection of the world also signifies the perfection of the whole, to which no part is lacking that could still be added, and which stands in the best order and combination.",["'Verstände' (dative, 'im transcendentalen Verstände') rendered 'understanding'; possibly 'sense' is meant but the term is kept fixed.","'Erkenntnißes' = 'knowledge'.","'Der Autor' is Baumgarten, the textbook author; not named in the passage, so left as 'the author'.","'protensiven' kept as 'protensive' (duration); 'caußatum' normalised to *causatum*.","Parentheses carried over as in the transcript; the closing bracket appears after 'Vornehmste'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-020":["In the perfection of the world we have, then, two things to note: 1. a perfection with regard to the parts, or extensive magnitude; 2. the perfection in the connection, or intensive magnitude. No world is absolutely complete with regard to its parts. More substances can always be added, just as something can always be added to any multitude. But it is not the multitude of the parts that constitutes the perfection, only their manifoldness. Nevertheless a greatest is possible; for example, from the number of subjects in a country I cannot straightway infer the perfection of the state. (Breaks off.)",["'Untherthanen' (sic, for Unterthanen) silently corrected to 'subjects'.","'( bricht ab)' is the editor's remark and is rendered as '(Breaks off.)' in the parentheses where it stands."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-021":["I. Were there no hope of a future life, the vicious man, who sought by all sorts of means and intrigues to set himself in possession of earthly happiness, would be the happiest.",["Fragment belongs to a different section (the utility of metaphysics) than the preceding; no connection is supplied."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-022":["Hence one sees that metaphysics first arose from this practical interest of our reason.",[]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-023":["The utility of metaphysics is",["Breaks off; the enumeration continues in the following passage."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-024":["2. Logical, in that it extends our understanding; for in analysing the concepts of our understanding we thereby clarify it. But this utility is too insignificant to have given occasion to the invention of metaphysics, and if it were the only one, the whole of metaphysics would be dispensable.",["'erweitert' / 'aufklären' rendered 'extends' / 'clarify'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-025":["For the sake of physics and mathematics one would not need metaphysics. For the principles of the possibility of experience one sees daily from experience itself, and error in experience I can soon discover if I repeat it. Besides, metaphysics does not go to natural science but beyond it. The utility of metaphysics, considered in general, is either positive or negative. The positive utility of a thing always consists in this, how through it we can attain some further perfection; the positive utility of metaphysics, then, is the attainment of a knowledge (*Kenntniß*) of God and of the hope of a future life. The negative utility is to preserve us from errors.",["'Kenntniß' rendered 'knowledge' with the German added, since 'Erkenntnis' is the fixed term for knowledge proper.","'Natur Wissenschaft' rendered 'natural science'; 'geht nicht auf ... sondern über' rendered 'does not go to ... but beyond'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-026":["13»",["Stray page or line mark from the OCR; reproduced as it stands, meaning unclear."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-027":["The negative utility of metaphysics is the greater, since it consists in the prevention of imperfection. And it is surely more important to remedy a pain than to procure a pleasure. The negative utility of metaphysics is also almost the only one. For the existence of God and the hope of a future life can be known by every man, on some reflection upon nature and upon his own condition, through the healthy understanding [*gesunder Menschenverstand*], and he therefore has no need of speculative inquiry, which in any case can be undertaken by very few men. But this is merely a practical belief, in which a man can be led astray by any speculative doubt. Now these speculative doubts and investigations cannot be refuted by practical belief, and yet they are indispensable to our reason. To resolve them, speculative principles must be set against them, and this is what metaphysics does. Its chief utility, then, is to purge our knowledge of errors and to preserve it from them.\n\nThe chief ends of the whole of metaphysics are the knowledge of God and the hope of a future life. If metaphysics sought merely to discuss the question 'Is it true that there is a God and a future life?', it would not be needed at all; for this can be seen, with the greatest probability, in accordance with the rules of a reasonable practical belief. But it inquires rather whether we can arrive, by mere speculation, at knowledge of God and of a future life, and how far we are in a position to do so.\n\nThere are two ways of treating a science, the dogmatic and the critical. (1) The dogmatic method is that in which I lay down certain modes of knowledge as indemonstrable propositions as a foundation, and build the others upon them. (2) The critical method is that in which I investigate the principles themselves, as to their mode of origin (from which power of the mind they have sprung) and as to their possibility (how they could have arisen *a priori*).\n\nIn geometry one proceeds dogmatically, as in other sciences also; for example, the proposition that between two points only one straight line is possible. There is no need at all to investigate this, for it can be confirmed at once from experience. Hence indemonstrable propositions hold in mathematics. In philosophy, however, and especially in metaphysics, one cannot proceed dogmatically. For example, if I accept the principle 'Everything that comes to be is in time', I can infer from it many false and unprovable propositions and conclusions. I can say: if all things are in time, the first cause of them must also be in time; it must therefore have begun to act only in time. But why then could it not have acted earlier? This is insoluble for the understanding, and if the understanding pursues the matter further, it falls in the end into errors. Hence in philosophy it is not permissible to proceed from principles, since they are not secure and sufficient to explain all the consequences drawn from them.\n\nThe critical method is directed not upon knowledge itself, or upon the object, but upon the understanding. It is therefore not objective but subjective. If, then, I have adopted a principle in philosophy, I must first have proved it. If I cannot prove it, it is suspect and not to be used. For if the modes of knowledge inferred from it involve a contradiction, I cannot remove it, because metaphysics goes beyond experience and I cannot therefore test the principles by experience.\n\nIn metaphysics criticism is most indispensable, because otherwise one can very easily go astray, since, in mounting beyond the limits of experience, it has no clue to guide it. Hence the utility of metaphysics is also negative, and this utility is the greatest, because criticism is most necessary to metaphysics. Since, then, it is built upon criticism and preserves us from errors, it is a bulwark of religion against all speculative doubts.\n\nIn our exposition we shall combine both methods: first dogmatically, and then criticise it step by step. For if criticism does not accompany dogmatism, we have no touchstone of truth. If, for example, I speak of the beginning of the world, I cannot possibly have this from experience, nor can I ever confirm it through experience; it goes beyond all experience and is therefore a mere idea in my reason. — But how does my understanding come to it, and how far does it reach? This I must ask. It is strange that these questions have hardly been investigated at all. The questions of where our concepts come from have indeed been raised and investigated; but that does not belong here.",["Restated several long sentences as shorter ones (e.g. the 'Um nun diese speculative Zweifel…' sentence, and 'Es giebt 2erlei Art…') and numbered the two methods as in the source; the sentence on doubts is smoothed, since its relative clause is awkwardly attached in the German.","'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'; 'einige Erkenntniß' and 'die daraus gefolgerte Erkenntnisse' as 'modes of knowledge'.","'Gemüthskraft' rendered 'power of the mind'. 'Grundsätze' in the critical-method sentence is 'principles'; 'Satz/Sätze' 'proposition(s)'.","OCR corrections: 'Irrthiimern' → Irrthümern; 'Metyphysik' → Metaphysik; 'wiederlegt/wieder' left as the source's orthography, rendered by sense.","'Dasein Gottes … kann … erkennen': the full stop after 'nöthig' is followed by a lowercase 'welches' in the OCR; I read 'welches' as referring to speculative inquiry.","'Wenn ich … Grundsatz annehme: Alles was entsteht, ist in der Zeit' — 'alles was entsteht' rendered 'everything that comes to be'. 'Probierstein' rendered 'touchstone'.","'Daher gehts in der Philosophie nicht an, nach Grundsätzen zu verfahren' — 'principles' here means dogmatic first principles; the sense is as translated."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-028":["In every science that contains concepts of reason, one represents to oneself a metaphysics of that science, which must be carefully distinguished from this metaphysics. If, in a science of the understanding, I separate the pure knowledge of reason from the empirical, this collection — — is a metaphysics of that same science. It is thus the pure product of reason in the sciences. In a science this is very useful, in order to separate rational knowledge from the empirical and so to perceive the errors the more distinctly.\n\nMetaphysics is the spirit of philosophy. It stands to philosophy as the *spiritus vini* [spirit of wine] to wine. It purifies our elementary concepts and so makes us fit to understand all the sciences. In short, it is the greatest cultivation of the human understanding.",["The dash in 'Sammlung — - eine Metaphysik' is in the source (probably a lacuna or the transcriber's break); carried over as a dash and not filled in.","'Verstandes Wissenschaft' rendered 'science of the understanding'; 'Vernunft Erkenntniß' as 'knowledge of reason' / 'rational knowledge'.","'Cultur' rendered 'cultivation'."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-029":["History of metaphysics",["Heading only."]],"metaphysik-mrongovius-s26-030":["(breaks off)",["Editorial remark, translated as it stands."]]};
